export const CORPUS: Chunk[] = [{"ref":"01-founder-fit.md","label":"Founder fit (handbook ch 1)","heading":"Purpose","text":"Self-diagnosis before committing: do you have the traits, background and motivation to start\nand run a business? Load when a user asks \"should I start a business\", \"am I founder material\",\nor when onboarding a new user into a launch journey."},{"ref":"01-founder-fit.md","label":"Founder fit (handbook ch 1)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- Success is **personal qualities × quality planning**. Great qualities with a poor plan fails;\n  a great plan with weak motivation or fear of uncertainty also fails.\n- The three must-haves: **a solid plan, the ability to execute it, and high motivation** that\n  makes business success an important personal goal.\n- Not required: a genius \"killer idea\" (most successful startups begin with incremental\n  innovation), fearlessness (prosperous founders have a *healthy* aversion to risk), or technical\n  business know-how (learn as you go, or enlist an experienced co-owner).\n- Ideas alone are one step; the process also needs people skills, work style and financial savvy.\n- Self-scoring tests (e.g. the US SBA \"Is entrepreneurship for you?\" test) are a useful rough\n  gauge, not a verdict.\n- Entrepreneurship runs in families and social circles; exposure to a small business is a\n  meaningful predictor."},{"ref":"01-founder-fit.md","label":"Founder fit (handbook ch 1)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**Table 1-1 — Common entrepreneurial traits (five clusters)**\n\n| Cluster | Traits |\n|---|---|\n| Ideas and drive | Creativity; vision; ability to identify opportunities; passion |\n| People skills | Leadership; persuasion; influence; network building; ability to excite people by vision; ability to close a deal; ability to listen, trust, take advice |\n| Work style | Goal oriented; comfortable with uncertainty; self-challenging; solitary (prefer being own boss); rarely satisfied or complacent, can't sit still; driven to plan and be prepared; experimental mindset (OK starting small, moving past failures); perseverance in adversity; continuously looks for a better way |\n| Financial savvy | Comfortable with finance; comfortable with financial governance |\n| Entrepreneurial background | Family members started businesses; friends started businesses; worked at a small business or startup |\n\n**The entrepreneurial process** (Gergen & Vanourek): understand a problem → grasp its full\ncontext → connect previously unconnected dots → have the vision, courage, resourcefulness and\npersistence to see the solution through.\n\n**The investor's three questions** (Baehr & Loomis) — in any funding meeting the investor asks:\n1. Do I like you? 2. Do I trust you? 3. Do I want to do business with you?"},{"ref":"01-founder-fit.md","label":"Founder fit (handbook ch 1)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **Passion is not a predictor.** Research: passion doesn't correlate with success years out, and\n  it fades within the first months. Professional funders prefer strong preparation and a calm\n  demeanor; passion only helps with less experienced funders (relatives, semi-pro angels).\n  \"Passion is an emotion that blinds you\" (Isenberg): too much attachment stops you seeing\n  problems and correcting course.\n- **Start small and experiment** before locking in a business model to scale; test product,\n  price and service with real customers before spending large capital.\n- **Decide under uncertainty.** Corporate managers wait for more information; an entrepreneur\n  must make the best of uncertainty and move. Standing still is not an option.\n- **Fail fast beats failing long and slow.** \"Any fool can carry on, but only the wise man knows\n  how to shorten sail\" (Conrad, via Isenberg).\n- **Listen to complaints.** The most common selling mistake of new founders is discounting\n  customer criticism because of passion and ego.\n- **Know your deal limits.** Successful founders know exactly what they must give up and what\n  they can get away with when closing under pressure (Kuemmerle).\n- **Stretch rules creatively, not legally.** Freedom from normal rules is a competitive advantage\n  (Hoffman), but skirting regulation or tolerating misconduct produces long-lasting reputational\n  damage inside and outside the company."},{"ref":"01-founder-fit.md","label":"Founder fit (handbook ch 1)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. Take a self-assessment (SBA test or the Table 1-1 clusters).\n2. Weigh background: family/friends who founded businesses, time spent inside a small business.\n3. Confirm the three must-haves: plan, ability to execute, motivation.\n4. If a cluster is weak, decide the mitigation: learn it (finance → appendixes A and B), or\n   bring in a co-owner who has it."},{"ref":"01-founder-fit.md","label":"Founder fit (handbook ch 1)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"Founder-fit self-check (score each 1–5, group by cluster):\n- I can spot problems others miss and imagine new solutions.\n- I have a clear vision of the change I want to make.\n- I am comfortable reaching out to strangers (customers, investors, hires) with confident optimism.\n- I can persuade and lead; people get excited by my vision.\n- I can close a deal under pressure and know my walk-away points.\n- I listen to criticism and change course when the evidence says so.\n- I challenge and motivate myself without a boss.\n- I am comfortable acting with incomplete information.\n- I prefer starting small and testing to betting big up front.\n- I persevere through adversity but can recognise a dead end.\n- I am comfortable reading financial statements and governing finances.\n- Family or friends have started businesses; I have worked in a small business or startup.\nExternal: SBA \"Is entrepreneurship for you?\" quiz (sba.gov)."},{"ref":"01-founder-fit.md","label":"Founder fit (handbook ch 1)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Airbnb** (intro) — roommates rented air mattresses, funded via novelty cereal, joined Y\n  Combinator, learned by using their own service, fixed listing photos — iterate and learn.\n- **Webvan** — built a 330,000 sq ft automated warehouse, raised $850M+, started 26 more, never\n  broke even, bankrupt in two years — too much too fast; experiment first.\n- **Uber / Airbnb regulation** — rule-bending fuelled growth but drew lasting scrutiny — creativity\n  without disregarding civil society.\n- **Paul Newman, Jim Koch (Boston Beer), Dan Bricklin (VisiCalc)** — grew up in business-owning\n  families — background shapes entrepreneurial drive."},{"ref":"01-founder-fit.md","label":"Founder fit (handbook ch 1)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Treating passion as the main qualification.\n- Waiting for more information instead of deciding.\n- Ignoring customer complaints because of ego.\n- Carrying on with a failing project instead of shortening sail.\n- Mistaking rule-breaking for strategy."},{"ref":"01-founder-fit.md","label":"Founder fit (handbook ch 1)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`entrepreneur` — someone who perceives an opportunity **and creates an organization to pursue it** (Bygrave).\n`creative destruction` — Schumpeter's term for entrepreneurs sweeping away old technologies and ways of doing things."},{"ref":"01-founder-fit.md","label":"Founder fit (handbook ch 1)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- Ideas matter but are not sufficient; consider background, inclinations, motivation and skills.\n- Suitability tests exist; use them as a rough gauge only.\n- Entrepreneurship runs in families; children of business owners are more likely to start or buy one."},{"ref":"02-opportunity.md","label":"The opportunity (handbook ch 2)","heading":"Purpose","text":"Turn a raw idea into a tested, evaluated opportunity: define the customer problem, size the\nmarket, experiment before investing, then judge the idea against five criteria (value, profit,\nfit, durability, financeability). Load when a user says \"I have an idea\", \"is this a good\nopportunity\", \"how big is the market\", \"should I validate this first\", or when the journey\nmoves from founder-fit into idea evaluation."},{"ref":"02-opportunity.md","label":"The opportunity (handbook ch 2)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- A business opportunity (Timmons, 2004) is a product or service that **creates significant\n  value for customers and significant profit potential for the entrepreneur**. Start with the\n  *problem*, not the gizmo: be sure the problem exists and can be described in detail before\n  investing heavily in a solution.\n- The classic path (spot need → write plan and forecast → pitch → execute the document) usually\n  fails because of unknowns at the outset: the right business model, scale, competitor moves,\n  supply-chain glitches, and above all *is there a market and how big is it*.\n- The user is not always the payer (product for a user, paid by an advertiser); ask every\n  market question for both.\n- Guesses stay guesses until tested. Silicon Valley approaches (design thinking, **lean startup**)\n  expect pivots and run experiments to understand the market *before* proposing a solution; the\n  practitioners say this applies equally to ordinary small businesses.\n- Lean startup = business-model canvas (hypotheses on one page) + customer development (get out\n  and talk) + agile development (fast MVPs, fast iteration).\n- Evaluation is not a one-time event; it is a set of questions asked over and over as you\n  experiment and learn.\n- An opportunity worth pursuing scores on five characteristics: value to customers, significant\n  risk-adjusted profit, fit with the founding team, durability, and amenability to financing.\n- Risk and return travel together; reject anything that does not pay for the risk taken.\n- Every business rests on an economic structure (margin × volume; fixed vs variable costs) that\n  sets its breakeven and its profitability beyond breakeven.\n- Revenue projections without customers are \"the most dangerous trap\" — expenses can be\n  estimated from experience, revenue must be experimented into.\n- Durability dies two ways: fads, and visible demand with low entry barriers. Network effects\n  are a source of durability but require speed, trust, the right users and no disintermediation.\n- Always compare the opportunity with every alternative, including doing nothing."},{"ref":"02-opportunity.md","label":"The opportunity (handbook ch 2)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**Timmons' definition of a business opportunity (2004)** — a product or service that creates\nsignificant value for customers *and* offers significant profit potential to the entrepreneur.\nThe modern refinement: focus first on defining and refining the problem that creates that value.\n\n**Table 2-1 — Market evaluation for the Electric Car Care Center** (Cesar's worked example; the\nthree-column format *aspect / evaluation / confidence & unknowns* is the reusable worksheet)\n\n| Aspect of the market | Cesar's evaluation | Confidence and unknowns |\n|---|---|---|\n| Problem you are trying to solve | Help customers take care of their electric vehicles | Confident the need exists — but will customers see it, and why choose my shop over the dealer? |\n| Customer benefit from your solution | Lower price than equivalent dealer service; greater expertise (EV-only, all the right equipment) | Dealers are expensive so lower price is a likely benefit — but good enough to pull customers away? Test pricing with existing EV owners |\n| Market size | Over two million EVs on the road worldwide | Seeing more EVs locally, but growth trajectory unclear; understand before investing heavily |\n| Market growth rate | 32% compound annual growth in the US over the past four years; industry projections differ substantially | Will growth be sustained? Is local growth the same as national? |\n| Market share | 18% of service business within a twenty-mile radius during the first five years | A guess — must be tested |\n| Competitors | Dealers get most warranty-period business; other EV specialty shops likely to open; few neighbourhood garages have the training or equipment | Observations seem accurate or likely |\n| Customer awareness of need | Will become obvious as warranties expire and dealer prices bite | Definitely test this projection |\n| Customers | All EV owners, all makes and models | Need demographics — walk-ins skew wealthy early adopters; will that change if fuel prices rise? |\n| Reaching customers | Buy EV-owner list for direct email; social media; hyper-local ads; free \"Understanding Your Electric Vehicle\" clinics; partner with local environmental groups | Lots to test — e.g. start a Twitter/Facebook page of EV-care tips and count followers, then market to them |\n\n**The lean startup** (box, pp. 29–30; Eric Ries, as described by Steve Blank for HBR, May 2013)\n1. **Business-model canvas** — a one-page document of your hypotheses (what you cannot know in\n   advance). Seeing all unknowns on one page shows how the parts might fit. Standard framework by\n   Osterwalder & Pigneur, *Business Model Generation*; blank canvases free (with registration) at\n   strategyzer.com.\n2. **Customer development** — test hypotheses by interacting with customers, vendors and partners\n   on each canvas block. Secrecy before a splashy launch is over: \"customer feedback matters more\n   than secrecy\" and constant feedback beats cadenced unveilings (Blank).\n3. **Agile development** — build prototypes quickly to generate feedback. What is the *minimum\n   viable product* that tests the idea? How quickly and incrementally can you iterate without\n   building unnecessary elements?\n\n**Figure 2-1 — The business-model canvas** (Strategyzer; nine blocks and their guiding questions)\n\n| Block | Guiding questions |\n|---|---|\n| Customer segments | For whom are we creating value? Who are our most important customers? What are the customer archetypes? |\n| Value propositions | What value do we deliver? Which customer problem are we helping to solve? What bundles of products/services per segment? Which customer needs are we satisfying? What is the minimum viable product? |\n| Channels | Through which channels do segments want to be reached? How do other companies reach them now? Which work best? Which are most cost-efficient? How are we integrating them with customer routines? |\n| Customer relationships | How do we get, keep and grow customers? Which relationships have we established? How are they integrated with the rest of the model? How costly are they? |\n| Revenue streams | For what value are customers willing to pay? For what do they currently pay? What is the revenue model? What are the pricing tactics? |\n| Key resources | What key resources do our value propositions require? Our distribution channels? Customer relationships? Revenue streams? |\n| Key activities | What key activities do our value propositions require? Our channels? Customer relationships? Revenue streams? |\n| Key partners | Who are our key partners and suppliers? Which key resources do we acquire from partners? Which key activities do partners perform? |\n| Cost structure | What are the most important costs inherent to the model? Which key resources are most expensive? Which key activities are most expensive? |\n\n**The five characteristics of an opportunity worth pursuing** (Timmons 1–4; Alfred E. Osborne\nJr., UCLA Price Center, adds 5)\n1. Creates significant value for customers who will pay a premium to solve a significant problem\n   or fill an important unmet need.\n2. Offers significant profit potential to entrepreneur and investors — enough to meet their\n   risk-versus-reward expectations.\n3. Fits the capabilities (experience and skills) of the founder and management team.\n4. Is durable: profits persist and probably grow over a reasonable time; not a fad or a\n   quickly disappearing need.\n5. Is amenable to financing — promising ideas do not always find backing.\n\n**Figure 2-2 — The risk-versus-return trade-off** (return on the vertical axis, risk on the\nhorizontal, a diagonal line through the origin)\n- Point A: zero risk, very low return (e.g. Treasuries).\n- Points B, C, D: on the diagonal — rewards commensurate with risk.\n- Point E, and anything below the diagonal: reject — the return does not pay for the risk.\n\n**Profit structure spectrum** — low margin × huge volume (supermarkets) vs. high margin × few\nsales (custom furniture makers). Profit margin = profit ÷ sales revenue.\n\n[section truncated]"},{"ref":"02-opportunity.md","label":"The opportunity (handbook ch 2)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **Confirm the problem before building.** Cesar must prove EV drivers need his service, how\n  many there are, and how they behave — not just that the technology gap exists.\n- **Rate confidence per answer and plan a test for each.** Every \"guess\" in the confidence column\n  becomes an experiment (e.g. price-test with existing owners; count social followers).\n- **Ask who uses and who pays** — they may differ; answer the market questions for both.\n- **Expect pivots.** Blue River talked to 100+ customers in ten weeks, dropped golf courses for\n  farmers, and had a prototype ten weeks later.\n- **Feedback beats secrecy.** Show prototypes early; constant feedback beats big unveilings.\n- **Reject any opportunity below the risk-return diagonal.** Why accept a 5% return with\n  business risk when ten-year US Treasuries pay almost as much with no default risk?\n- **Know your margin-volume model.** Supermarket (thin margin, huge volume) or custom furniture\n  (fat margin, few sales) — and plan capacity and marketing to match.\n- **High fixed / low variable → high breakeven, high profitability after it. Low fixed / high\n  variable → low breakeven, low profitability after it.**\n- **Expense lines are credible when they come from your own experience** (labour, benefits,\n  interest, rent per square foot). **Revenue lines are assumptions until customers exist** —\n  experiment to make them real.\n- **Fit test:** do you have the managerial, financial and technical capabilities *and* the\n  personal commitment? If not, when can you hire them, will they command a high salary, and what\n  can be contracted out instead of brought in-house?\n- **Fads: wait.** It can be worth waiting to see if a fad has staying power before investing —\n  even in digital industries (Freek Vermeulen, London Business School).\n- **Visible opportunity + low entry barriers = deadly combination.** If barriers are high, know\n  how you will surmount them and whether they stay high.\n- **Network-effect businesses: move very, very fast** to be first at scale — but also build\n  trust, focus on the right users, and design against disintermediation.\n- **War-game the worst case:** name the single worst thing a competitor could do (e.g. cut\n  price 20%), model its effect on you, and decide your response in advance.\n- **Document every competition answer** — essential if you will seek outside capital.\n- **Financing windows close.** Biotech 2000–2004: plenty of good vaccine/therapy ideas, no\n  investor confidence, no money. A great idea is not automatically financeable.\n- **Compare against doing nothing.** A money-market fund at an anaemic 2% is a legitimate\n  alternative until an opportunity with all the right characteristics appears.\n- Non-US note: the \"risk-free\" benchmark is your local government bond yield, not US Treasuries."},{"ref":"02-opportunity.md","label":"The opportunity (handbook ch 2)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. **Recognise** the opportunity from inside your experience (Cesar: EV sales heading to 10% of\n   units in five years; dealers keep post-warranty service; independents cannot service EVs).\n2. **Define the problem** — answer the ten customer and market questions (below), separating\n   user from payer.\n3. **Grade each answer** — record what you know, your confidence, and the unknowns (Table 2-1\n   format); identify where you need more information.\n4. **Experiment** — put hypotheses on a business-model canvas; do customer development with\n   customers, vendors and partners; build a minimum viable product and iterate quickly.\n5. **Re-evaluate continuously** against the five characteristics as you learn.\n6. **Test profit** — answer the six economics questions, then build a pro forma income\n   statement for one to three years (lenders and investors will want it; see appendix A/B).\n7. **Test fit** — capabilities, commitment, hire-vs-contract plan.\n8. **Test durability** — sustained need? barriers to entry? network effects? defensibility?\n9. **Map the competition** — answer the six competitive questions with documentation.\n10. **Check financeability** — will this idea, in this climate, attract capital?\n11. **Ask the two final questions** — still attractive on risk-to-return? better than every\n    alternative available to you, including doing nothing?"},{"ref":"02-opportunity.md","label":"The opportunity (handbook ch 2)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Customer and market questions** (p. 25–26; rate confidence and note how you will test each)\n1. What is the problem you are trying to solve for your customers or users?\n2. How many people have this problem — what is the size of the market?\n3. Are potential customers/users aware of the problem, or is the need latent (undiscovered)?\n4. Is the market stable or growing? If growing, at what annual rate?\n5. How will your solution benefit customers or users?\n6. What percentage of the total market could you reasonably capture over the next few years?\n7. Is another product or service from competitors available to fill part of this demand?\n8. Who exactly are the potential customers? Can you name them? Can you describe them?\n9. How can you reach them and make a transaction — directly (own website or bricks-and-mortar),\n   through distributors (Apple/Google app stores), or through existing retail channels?\n10. How does the utility of your product compare with substitutes? (A tablet is easier to carry\n    than a laptop but lacks full functionality.)\n\n**Market evaluation worksheet** (Table 2-1 template) — for each row *Problem · Customer benefit\n· Market size · Market growth rate · Market share · Competitors · Customer awareness of need ·\nCustomers · Reaching customers*, fill in: your evaluation | your confidence | unknowns | test.\n\n**Economics of the opportunity** (p. 34–35; give a complete answer to each)\n1. Will the business be a price setter or a price taker? What constrains pricing?\n2. What is the supply-and-demand situation for your product or service?\n3. Is demand elastic (a price rise sharply cuts demand) or inelastic (demand barely moves) in\n   the short run?\n4. What substitutes do prospective customers have?\n5. Will the business be dominated by fixed or variable costs?\n6. To what extent can suppliers and employees enforce cost increases on the business?\n\n**Fit questions** (p. 36)\n- What expertise and skills will it take to run this business? Do you have them?\n- At what point can you hire for the gaps — and are those skills in high demand (high salary)?\n- What can be contracted out versus brought in-house?\n\n**Durability questions** (p. 38)\n- Is the need you identified likely to be sustained, or is it a fad?\n- Can you defend the solution? Can you take advantage of network effects?\n- Do other aspects of your offering make it hard for competitors to emulate or replace?\n\n**Competitive landscape questions** (p. 38–39; answer with documentation)\n1. How are customers currently satisfying the need (e.g. going to the dealer)?\n2. What are the strengths and weaknesses of the main competitors (quality, service, price)?\n3. How would a smart competitor respond to your entry (price cut, bundling, better experience)?\n4. Are barriers to entry high or low? Low → competitors keep entering until returns are driven\n   low. High → how will you surmount them, and will they stay high?\n5. Have current competitors shown themselves agile and responsive to customer needs and\n   technical change?\n6. What is the single worst thing a competitor could do to you (e.g. drop price 20%)? How would\n   it affect your prospects, how would you respond, and what pricing / positioning / service /\n   distribution / product strategy gives you a *sustainable* advantage?\n\n**Two questions to ask yourself** (p. 39)\n1. Is it still attractive on the risk-to-return relationship (figure 2-2; the book cites 2-1)?\n2. Is it more or less attractive than the other opportunities available to you?"},{"ref":"02-opportunity.md","label":"The opportunity (handbook ch 2)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Cesar / Electric Car Care Center** (fictional, runs through the chapter) — dealership\n  service manager spots post-warranty EV repair gap; sizes it (2M EVs, 32% CAGR, 18% local share\n  guess), plans tests, builds a 3-year pro forma (year-1 loss $21K → $140K after tax by year 3)\n  — a strong fit, but every revenue number is still a hypothesis; and if EV buyers turn out to\n  value convenience over price, the low-price positioning must be rethought before building.\n- **Blue River Technology** (Heraud & Redden, Stanford) — robotic mowers for golf courses;\n  100+ customer conversations in ten weeks showed golf courses did not care; farmers wanted\n  chemical-free weeding; prototype in ten weeks, $3M+ venture funding nine months later,\n  robotic lettuce thinner launched by 2017 — customer development finds the real problem.\n- **Pet rock (1970s), Second Life** — fads gone before requirements were met — durability test.\n- **Software industry** — the durable counter-example: long-lasting and growing.\n- **eBay, Etsy** — more buyers attract more sellers and vice versa — network effects reward the\n  first to scale.\n- **Supermarkets vs custom furniture makers** — same goal, opposite margin-volume structures.\n- **Auto engine plant vs technical service firm** — high-fixed vs low-fixed cost models and\n  their breakeven behaviour.\n- **Biotech 2000–2004** — good ideas starved of capital by lost investor confidence — a great\n  idea is not automatically financeable.\n- **10-year US Treasuries / 2% money-market fund** — the no-risk alternatives every opportunity\n  must beat."},{"ref":"02-opportunity.md","label":"The opportunity (handbook ch 2)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Falling in love with what the gizmo does and forgetting to assess its value to customers.\n- Treating the business plan as a straight line from need to solution to execution.\n- Assuming the customer's motive (low price) instead of discovering it (convenience).\n- Inventing revenue growth with no customers behind it.\n- Keeping the product secret until a big launch instead of gathering constant feedback.\n- Building unnecessary features instead of the minimum viable product.\n- Accepting returns below the risk-return line.\n- Ignoring the cost structure and therefore the breakeven point.\n- Chasing a fad, or a visible market with low barriers to entry.\n- Scaling a network business without trust, the right users, or protection from\n  disintermediation.\n- Skipping the competitor war-game and the \"worst thing they could do\" question.\n- Assuming a good idea will find financing regardless of the climate.\n- Never comparing the opportunity with alternatives, including doing nothing."},{"ref":"02-opportunity.md","label":"The opportunity (handbook ch 2)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`business opportunity` — product or service creating significant customer value and significant profit potential (Timmons).\n`latent need` — a problem customers have but have not yet recognised.\n`pivot` — a deliberate change of direction as experiments reveal what the market wants.\n`lean startup` — Ries's method: find a repeatable, scalable business model via canvas + customer development + agile development.\n`business-model canvas` — one-page grid of nine blocks holding your business hypotheses (Osterwalder & Pigneur).\n`customer development` — testing hypotheses by direct interaction with customers, vendors and partners from the start.\n`agile development` — fast, incremental prototyping to generate customer feedback.\n`minimum viable product (MVP)` — the smallest thing you can build that tests the idea.\n`profit margin` — profit ÷ sales revenue, as a percentage.\n`fixed costs` / `variable costs` — costs flat regardless of output vs. costs that move with output.\n`breakeven point` — units you must sell before earning a profit (appendix B).\n`price setter` / `price taker` — a business that can set its price vs. one that must accept the market's.\n`elastic` / `inelastic demand` — demand that falls sharply vs. barely with a price rise.\n`pro forma income statement` — best-estimate future revenues, expenses, taxes and profit for one or more years.\n`durable opportunity` — profits persist and grow over a reasonable period; not a fad.\n`barriers to entry` — obstacles that stop new competitors flooding in.\n`network effects` — offering's value rises with the number of users; first to scale is defensible.\n`disintermediation` — platform participants trust each other enough to bypass you as intermediary.\n`risk-versus-return trade-off` — higher risk must be matched by higher return; below the line is rejected."},{"ref":"02-opportunity.md","label":"The opportunity (handbook ch 2)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- A business opportunity (1) solves a real customer problem, (2) offers significant\n  risk-adjusted profit, (3) fits the leadership team's capabilities, (4) stays profitable over\n  a reasonable span, and (5) is amenable to financing.\n- Entrepreneurs spend too little time on the problem they are solving and on testing how users\n  and customers actually experience it.\n- An experimental or lean approach lowers risk and reveals customer needs and reactions before\n  significant investment.\n- Evaluate opportunities on the market, current and anticipated competition, underlying\n  economics, and the resources needed to succeed."},{"ref":"03-business-model-and-strategy.md","label":"Business model and strategy (handbook ch 3)","heading":"Purpose","text":"Once early findings and experiments suggest a real opportunity, how do you solidify (a) a\n**business model** — who your customers are and how you profitably serve them — and (b) a\n**strategy** — how you do better than competitors? Load when a user asks \"what's my business\nmodel\", \"how will I make money\", \"how am I different\", \"how will customers find me\", \"should I\njoin an incubator/accelerator\", \"when should I scale\", or when building the model/strategy\nmodule of the launch journey."},{"ref":"03-business-model-and-strategy.md","label":"Business model and strategy (handbook ch 3)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- **Model ≠ strategy.** A business model identifies customers and describes how you profitably\n  address their needs; strategy is how you beat competitors. You need both (p42).\n- Every founder must have concise answers to **five questions**: create value for customers,\n  make a profit for us and investors, differentiate, defend assets/position, be discovered (p41).\n- A business model is a variation of the value chain: **making** (design, purchasing,\n  manufacturing) + **selling** (finding/reaching customers, transacting, delivering) (Magretta, p42).\n- Two tests: the **narrative test** (does the model tell a logical story?) and the **numbers\n  test** (is the pro forma income statement profitable with reasonable projections?) (p43).\n- Many model elements are **hypotheses, not decisions** (Osterwalder's canvas). Test the ones\n  you're unsure of before building the product or approaching investors (p46–47).\n- **Marketing belongs at the centre of the model from day one**, not downstream. If you can't\n  reach customers or the product doesn't fit them, nothing else in the model works (p48).\n- Competitive advantage lives in **differences** (Henderson, Porter). But different alone isn't\n  enough — the difference must be **perceived as valuable** by customers (p50, 52).\n- Strategy is a **system of mutually reinforcing activities**, not a collection of parts (p54).\n- Platform businesses run on **demand-side economies of scale (network effects)**; the classic\n  five-forces \"moat\" logic treats external forces as depletive, but for platforms they can be\n  accretive (p55–56).\n- **Every startup is an experiment.** Expect initial strategy to miss; recognise bad news and\n  pivot quickly; then be a master of incrementalism (p57–58).\n- There is no sustainable competitive advantage any more (McGrath): build to exploit\n  **transient** advantages through deliberate, continuous change (p60).\n- Some argue a strong strategy helps more than an excess of lean-startup validation tests (p50)."},{"ref":"03-business-model-and-strategy.md","label":"Business model and strategy (handbook ch 3)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**The five questions every founder must answer (p41)**\n1. How will the business create value for customers?\n2. How will it make a profit for us and our investors?\n3. How will it differentiate itself from competitors?\n4. How will it defend its assets and position from competitors?\n5. How will it be discovered?\n\n**Magretta's two-part value chain and two tests (p42–43)**\n| Part | Activities |\n|---|---|\n| Making | Designing, purchasing raw materials, manufacturing, etc. |\n| Selling | Finding and reaching customers, transacting a sale, distributing/delivering |\n\n| Test | Question |\n|---|---|\n| Narrative test | Does the model tell a logical, sensible story? |\n| Numbers test | On a pro forma income statement with reasonable revenue/expense projections, is it profitable? |\n\n**Hamermesh & Marshall — four groups of business-model decisions (p43)**\n| Group | Meaning | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue sources | Where the money comes from | Sales, service fees, advertising |\n| Cost drivers | What creates cost | Labour, goods purchased for resale, energy |\n| Investment size | Measurable capital to launch, plus working capital to keep operating | — |\n| Critical success factors | What must go right | Sustained new-product rollout; reaching critical mass within a set time |\n\n**Table 3-1 — Business model analogies** (adapted from Mark W. Johnson, *Seizing the White\nSpace*, p45). Prompt: how might each be applied to the problem you're solving?\n\n| Analogy | How it works | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Affinity club | Pay royalties to a large organisation for the right to sell exclusively to its members | MBNA |\n| Brokerage | Bring buyers and sellers together; charge a fee per transaction | Century 21, Orbitz |\n| Bundling | Package related goods and services together | Fast-food value meals; iPod + iTunes |\n| Cell phone | Charge different rates for discrete service levels | Sprint, Better Place |\n| Crowdsourcing | Users contribute content free in exchange for access to others' content | Wikipedia, YouTube |\n| Disintermediation | Sell direct, sidestepping intermediaries | Dell, WebMD |\n| Fractionalization | Sell partial use of something | NetJets, time-shares |\n| Freemium | Basic service free; charge for premium | LinkedIn |\n| Leasing | Rent rather than sell high-margin, high-priced products | Cars, MachineryLink |\n| Low-touch | Lower prices by decreasing service | Walmart, IKEA |\n| Negative operating cycle | Lower prices by getting paid before delivering | Amazon |\n| Pay as you go | Charge for actual metered usage | Electric companies |\n| Razor/blades | Sell the companion product below cost to drive volume of the consumable | Printers and ink |\n| Reverse razor/blades | Sell the low-margin item below cost to drive the high-margin companion | Kindle; iPod/iTunes |\n| Reverse auction | Set a ceiling price; participants bid as the price drops | Elance.com |\n| Product to service | Sell the service the product performs, not the product | Zipcar |\n| Standardization | Standardise a previously personalised service to cut cost | MinuteClinic |\n| Subscription | Charge a recurring fee for a service | Netflix |\n| User communities | Grant network access; charge membership fees and advertising | Angie's List |\n\n**Porter — three sources of strategic position (p51–52)**\n| Position | Definition | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Variety-based | Choose a narrow subset of the industry's offerings; win by delivering faster, better or cheaper | Starbucks — premium coffee, convenient outlets, no breakfast/sandwiches at launch |\n| Need-based | Serve all or most needs of an identifiable customer set (price-sensitive, high-touch, or customised) | USAA — active/retired military officers and families; handles transfers and remote postings |\n| Access-based | Position on access to customers (location, channel) | Discount chain only in low-income neighbourhoods; Cracker Barrel along US expressways with a route trip-planner |\n\nGeneric bases of strategy: **low-cost leadership, technical differentiation, focus** (p51).\n\n**Porter's five forces of competition (p52)** — threat of new entrants; threat of substitute\nproducts/services; rivalry among existing competitors; bargaining power of suppliers; bargaining\npower of buyers. (The printed text repeats \"suppliers\"; buyers is the intended fifth force.)\n\n**Osborne's 4 S's (p55)** — \"structure follows strategy, and staffing follows structure, and\nyou hold the strategy together with systems\" (Alfred E. Osborne Jr., UCLA).\n\n**Box — Network effects and strategy** (Van Alstyne, Parker & Choudary, HBR April 2016, p56)\n| | Supply-side economies (pipelines) | Demand-side economies (platforms) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Source of market power | Control resources, ruthless efficiency, fend off the five forces | Network effects: bigger network → better supply/demand matches, richer matching data → higher value per transaction |\n| Goal of strategy | Build a moat that channels competition elsewhere | Grow participation; external forces are accretive, adding value |\n| Outcome | Defended position | Virtuous loop that produces monopolies |\nEvidence: Alibaba >75% of Chinese e-commerce transactions; Google 82% of mobile OS and 94% of\nmobile searches; Facebook the dominant social platform. Platform margins: eBay gross margin 70%,\nEtsy 60% (p55).\n\n**Incubator vs accelerator (p47–48)**\n| | Incubator | Accelerator |\n|---|---|---|\n| Structure | Less structured, less time-bound | Time-limited cohort program |\n| Money | Nonprofit or charges rent for shared coworking space | Funding in exchange for equity (see ch 6) |\n| Stage | Any; some specialise in later-phase growth | Almost exclusively early-stage |\n| Selectivity | Lower | More competitive, especially strong programs |\n| Sponsor | Independent, big firm, university, government arm, nonprofit | Independent programs |\nBoth provide space plus coaching, mentoring, networking, funding and education to test business\nmodels and assumptions quickly."},{"ref":"03-business-model-and-strategy.md","label":"Business model and strategy (handbook ch 3)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- If the model fails the narrative test **or** the numbers test, fix it before doing anything else.\n- Answer the four model groups unambiguously (revenue, costs, investment, success factors)\n  **before approaching any investor** (p46).\n- Know which costs are **fixed** and which **vary with sales volume**; calculate launch capital\n  and operating capital separately.\n- For each essential factor you're unsure about, find a way to test it **before building the\n  offering or pitching** — confidence in success rises sharply (p47).\n- For each hypothesis ask what could change it (e.g. no competitors today, competitors tomorrow)\n  → that is your risk list and mitigation plan.\n- App discovery: the app stores are the only scale channel (Apple 2.2M+ apps, Google Play\n  2.8M+); one in four apps is found via search; app-store optimisation and Facebook/Instagram\n  ads are **table stakes** — competitors do them too. Add creative reach, endorsements and\n  direct customer interaction outside those ecosystems (p48–49).\n- Two competitors cannot coexist doing business the same way; differentiate on price, product\n  mix, ambiance or another axis, or one perishes (Henderson, p50).\n- \"Different\" must be valued: lower cost, convenience, reliability, faster delivery, aesthetic\n  appeal, etc. Ask what value the strategy provides and whether you can deliver it (p52).\n- Resources constrain strategy choice: don't pick a strategy your capital and know-how cannot\n  execute — a startup with few people and assets must be honest here (p53).\n- Prioritise threats/opportunities first (Christensen's \"driving forces\"), then address each\n  in broad strokes (p53).\n- Generate **many alternatives**; the best parts of two strategies often combine into a stronger\n  third. Check every fact, question every assumption, fetch missing information, and vet the\n  leading choices with the wisest heads you know to avoid groupthink (p54).\n- Alignment test: every employee at every level (1) understands the strategy and (2) knows\n  their role in making it work. Also align marketing (right customers), compensation/bonuses\n  (strategy-advancing behaviour) and physical assets (p55).\n- A startup's clean slate is an advantage: hire, equip and structure around the strategy (p55).\n- **Don't scale a network business too early.** VRBO preceded Airbnb, eBay preceded Alibaba in\n  China; early scaling locks in an untested model and forfeits revenue, margin or customers\n  (Hagiu, p57).\n- **When to scale** (Hoffman): you have data, understand the competition and have ironed out\n  product-market fit. Typical transition: 10–100 → 100–1,000 employees; 100K–1M → 1–10M users;\n  revenue > $10M (p57).\n- If something works, **do more of it**; use a niche win to enter the next niche, adapting the\n  product as needed (p58).\n- Expect copycats: imagine a rival raising $10M from a VC while you're assembling round two\n  (p59). Expect incumbents: what if Amazon or Facebook adds your idea as a feature? (p59)\n- Big tech may pretend to acquire you \"just to screw up their business for 18 months\"\n  (Andreessen, p59–60).\n- Even a winning strategy has an end date: plan ahead for abandoning it (McGrath, p60).\n- Test and verify each assumption before launch; the market is the ultimate test. Explain the\n  model and strategy to as many trusted, experienced people as possible (p60).\n- Worked breakeven (Cesar): crew of five, 8,000 service-hours/year (~40 weeks); everything\n  above breakeven is profit (p44).\n- Asset-light pricing (Airbnb): no owned property → no big capital to scale → prices ~30% below\n  hotels; hosts carry maintenance, so risk and operating cost are lower (p47)."},{"ref":"03-business-model-and-strategy.md","label":"Business model and strategy (handbook ch 3)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"**Solidifying the business model (p41–49)**\n1. Write concise answers to the five questions.\n2. Describe the concept in the four groups (revenue sources, cost drivers, investment size,\n   critical success factors); split fixed vs variable costs; size launch and operating capital.\n3. Try each Table 3-1 analogy against your problem; adapt the one(s) that fit.\n4. Run the narrative test and the numbers test (build the pro forma income statement).\n5. Mark which elements are decisions and which are hypotheses; test uncertain factors before\n   building or pitching (an incubator/accelerator can speed this).\n6. Put the discovery/marketing plan inside the model, with unique features and approaches for\n   reaching customers (not just table stakes).\n\n**Six steps for formulating strategy (p52–55)** — built around the five forces\n1. **Look outside** to identify threats and opportunities (new entrants, demographic shifts,\n   suppliers cutting you off, substitutes, technology that obsoletes your solution or the\n   customer's problem, macro trends that reduce ability to pay, a rival with same quality at\n   much lower price or a much better product at the same price; opportunities such as\n   new-to-the-world technology or an unserved market).\n2. **Look inside** at resources, capabilities and practices — they constrain the strategy.\n3. **Consider strategies** for addressing threats and opportunities (prioritise, create\n   alternatives, check facts, fill information gaps, vet externally).\n4. **Build fit** among strategy-supporting activities so they reinforce each other and lock out\n   imitators (Southwest: rapid gate turnaround → frequent departures + aircraft utilisation →\n   low-cost/high-convenience; enabled by motivated gate/ground crews, no meals, no interline\n   baggage transfers).\n5. **Create alignment** between people, activities, marketing, compensation, assets and the strategy.\n6. **Be prepared to implement** — organise around the strategy (4 S's). Note: the chapter's\n   Summing up lists only steps 1–5; step 6 appears in the body.\n\n**Responding to a disappointing strategy (p57–58)**\n1. Recognise the bad news — admit the mistake.\n2. Search energetically for what went wrong.\n3. Revise the strategy (pivot) and get back into the game quickly."},{"ref":"03-business-model-and-strategy.md","label":"Business model and strategy (handbook ch 3)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Business model check (p46–47)**\n- How would you describe the concept in terms of revenue sources, cost drivers, investment\n  size and critical success factors?\n- Have you nailed down your revenue sources and the factors that drive costs?\n- Which costs are fixed and which vary with sales volume?\n- Have you calculated the capital needed to launch and to operate?\n- What factors are essential for success?\n- Of those essential factors, which are you unsure about? How can you test them before\n  building the offering or approaching investors?\n- What might change about your hypotheses (e.g. competitors appearing later)?\n- Which Table 3-1 analogies could apply to the problem you're solving?\n\n**Discovery plan questions (p48–49)**\n- How will customers find out about you? Do you know them well enough to reach them?\n- If you depend on others for distribution (e.g. app stores), how do you get discovered there?\n- Beyond table stakes (ASO, social ads), what creative reach, endorsements or direct\n  interactions will connect you with customers?\n\n**Strategy check (p52)**\n- What is your strategy for gaining competitive advantage?\n- Will it differentiate your company in ways that attract customers from rivals?\n- Will it draw new customers into the market?\n- Will it give you a tangible advantage?\n- What value does your strategy aim to provide? Can it deliver?\n\n**Step 1 — outside questions (p53)**\n- What is the economic environment we must operate in? How is it changing?\n- What opportunities are there for profitable action?\n- What are the risks associated with these opportunities?\n\n**Step 2 — inside questions (p53)**\n- What are our competencies as an organisation? How do they give us an advantage?\n- Which resources support or constrain our actions?\n\n**Step 3 — strategy-development checklist (p54)**\n- Create many alternatives (combine the best parts of two into a third).\n- Check all facts; question all assumptions.\n- Identify what information is missing; then get it.\n- Vet the leading choices with the wisest heads you know (avoid groupthink).\n\n**Step 5 — alignment checklist (p55)**\n- Does every employee understand the strategy and their role in it?\n- Is marketing focused on the customers the strategy defines?\n- Do compensation and bonuses reward strategy-advancing behaviour and performance?\n- Are physical assets deployed toward the organisation's highest goals?\n\n**Competition-readiness questions (p58–60)**\n- What if a copycat raised $10M from a VC while you were still preparing round two?\n- What if Amazon or Facebook added your idea as a feature?\n- What if a large company feigned an acquisition to distract you for 18 months?\n- (Cesar's set) What happens when the next technology shift arrives (self-driving cars)? Will\n  your team have the capabilities? What if a web platform inserts itself between you and your\n  customers? What if underlying demand declines (car ownership falling with ride-share)?\n- When your current advantage expires, what will replace it?"},{"ref":"03-business-model-and-strategy.md","label":"Business model and strategy (handbook ch 3)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Cesar's Electric Car Care Center** (box, p44; strategy box, p58–59) — repair/maintenance of\n  electric cars after warranty; four-part blueprint (local ads + owner mini-seminars, in-house\n  diagnostics for all makes, regional parts pipeline, outsourced body shop and A/C); breakeven\n  at 5 staff / 8,000 hours; differentiated as the only EV specialist in town; aligns tools,\n  trained mechanics, teaching-capable staff, seminars and social media; plans for self-driving\n  cars, platform intermediaries and falling car ownership — model + strategy + foresight.\n- **Airbnb** (box, p46–47) — more rooms than InterContinental or Hilton; 19.5% of NYC room\n  supply and 5.4% across 192 countries in 2016 (from 3.6% in 2015); owns no property, takes a\n  percentage of rent, prices ~30% below hotels — a new model is the interface between what\n  technology enables and what the market wants.\n- **Eat24** (p49) — rejected by Benchmark, Redpoint, Excel, Insight and Alibaba in a\n  GrubHub-dominated market; targeted small family restaurants, knocked on doors, gave free fax\n  machines, no charges on tiny orders, absorbed non-payment risk, bought cheap ads on sites\n  popular with young men; acquired by Yelp for $134M — creative discovery beats table stakes.\n- **Southwest Airlines** (p50, 54) — most profitable North American carrier via low fares,\n  frequent point-to-point departures, service; rapid gate turnaround as the hub of a fit system.\n- **Toyota Prius** (p50) — targeted buyers wanting green, cheap-to-run or cutting-edge cars, and\n  bought a learning lead in hybrid technology.\n- **Apple iPod/iTunes** (p50–51) — not first to market (Rio and Cabo failed); won with a\n  hardware + software + service ecosystem.\n- **Two men's clothing stores** (Henderson, p50) — formal vs leisure can share a block;\n  identical stores cannot.\n- **Starbucks / USAA / discount chain / Cracker Barrel** (p51–52) — variety, need and\n  access-based positions respectively.\n- **Alibaba, Google, Facebook** (p56) — network-effect monopolies.\n- **VRBO → Airbnb; eBay → Alibaba (China)** (p57) — the eventual winner was not first.\n- **Webvan** (p57) — web grocery delivery for affluent, time-starved households; customers\n  balked at higher prices; bankrupt 2001 — the experiment can surprise the best planners."},{"ref":"03-business-model-and-strategy.md","label":"Business model and strategy (handbook ch 3)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Using \"business model\" and \"strategy\" interchangeably, or having one without the other.\n- A model that tells a good story but doesn't add up — or adds up but makes no sense.\n- Treating assumptions as decisions; not testing the shaky ones before building or pitching.\n- Approaching investors with ambiguous answers on revenue, costs, capital or success factors.\n- Treating marketing/discovery as a downstream step.\n- Relying only on table-stakes discovery (ASO, social ads) that rivals can copy.\n- Substituting endless validation tests for a real strategy.\n- Being different in ways customers don't value.\n- Choosing a strategy your resources and capabilities can't support.\n- Considering only one strategy; groupthink; unchecked facts and assumptions.\n- Treating strategy as a list of parts instead of a mutually reinforcing system.\n- Stopping at strategy without creating alignment or preparing implementation.\n- Applying five-forces moat thinking to a platform whose value comes from external participants.\n- Scaling a network business before the model and offering are tested.\n- Refusing to admit a strategy missed, or responding slowly.\n- Chasing \"sustainable\" competitive advantage instead of planning the next one.\n- Keeping the model and strategy to yourself instead of vetting them widely."},{"ref":"03-business-model-and-strategy.md","label":"Business model and strategy (handbook ch 3)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`business model` — identifies customers and describes how the business will profitably address their needs; a variation of the value chain.\n`strategy` — a plan to differentiate the enterprise and give it a competitive advantage over rivals.\n`narrative test` / `numbers test` — Magretta's two tests: does the model tell a logical story, and is it profitable on a pro forma income statement?\n`pro forma income statement` — projected revenues and expenses used to model a business before launch.\n`business-model canvas` — Osterwalder's tool; frames model elements as hypotheses to test, not decisions.\n`incubator` — less structured, less time-bound support program (rent or nonprofit; any stage).\n`accelerator` — time-limited cohort program that invests for equity; early-stage; competitive.\n`app-store optimization` — understanding store search algorithms to rank higher; table stakes.\n`strategic position` — Porter's variety-based, need-based or access-based sources of position.\n`five forces` — Porter's competitive forces: new entrants, substitutes, rivalry, supplier power, buyer power.\n`fit` — activities combined into a chain that reinforce one another and lock out imitators.\n`alignment` — every employee understands the strategy and their role; resources deployed to it.\n`4 S's` — structure follows strategy, staffing follows structure, systems hold strategy together.\n`platform business` — enables exchanges between producers and consumers rather than producing itself.\n`network effects` / `demand-side economies of scale` — value rises with the number of participants; accretive external forces.\n`supply-side economies` — market power via resource control and efficiency; moat-building.\n`driving forces` — Christensen's term for prioritised threats and opportunities.\n`pivot` — a revised strategy adopted quickly after recognising the original missed.\n`incrementalism` — doing more of what works; moving niche to niche with what you've learned.\n`transient competitive advantage` — McGrath: advantages are temporary; build to create and abandon them continuously."},{"ref":"03-business-model-and-strategy.md","label":"Business model and strategy (handbook ch 3)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- A business model describes revenue sources, cost drivers, investment size and success factors.\n- Strategy differentiates the enterprise and gives it a competitive advantage.\n- Porter: strategic positions come from variety-based, need-based or access-based positioning.\n- Strategy formulation: (1) look outside for threats and opportunities; (2) look inside at\n  resources, capabilities, practices; (3) consider strategies for the threats/opportunities;\n  (4) build fit among strategy-supporting activities; (5) align people and activities with the\n  strategy (the body adds a sixth: be prepared to implement).\n- A startup is an experiment; if it doesn't produce the desired result, change — quickly."},{"ref":"04-legal-structure.md","label":"Legal structure (handbook ch 4)","heading":"Purpose","text":"Which legal form the venture should take — sole proprietorship, general or limited partnership,\nC corporation, S corporation or LLC — and how objectives, investors, taxation and liability drive\nthe choice. Load when a user asks \"should I incorporate\", \"LLC or corporation\", \"do I need a\npartnership agreement\", or before any fundraising, equity-compensation or first-hire module."},{"ref":"04-legal-structure.md","label":"Legal structure (handbook ch 4)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- The choice is driven **chiefly by your objectives and those of your investors**; taxation and\n  legal liability play a supporting part (p. 63).\n- The law builds in a trade-off: the most favorable tax treatment usually costs you **some\n  liability protection, some flexibility, or both**.\n- Two axes explain every form: **levels of taxation** (one = flow-through to owners; two =\n  corporate tax plus tax on dividends) and **exposure of personal assets** (unlimited vs limited).\n- Flow-through entities (proprietorship, partnership, LLC, S corp) are conduits: owners are taxed\n  on **income earned, not cash distributed** — earnings retained for growth still create a\n  personal tax bill.\n- Access to capital scales with form: own funds and borrowing (sole prop) → partners' pockets\n  (partnership) → a vast pool of individuals and institutions plus successive public offerings\n  (C corp).\n- Corporations are vastly outnumbered by sole proprietorships yet account for **over 60% of US\n  sales** because they include nearly all major companies (p. 70).\n- Outside equity investors and venture capitalists will not participate in a sole proprietorship,\n  cannot hold S corp shares (most VC firms are partnerships), and want a block of ownership — they\n  may not take an unincorporated venture seriously.\n- A business may pass through several forms in its life (sole prop → partnership → C corp; LP →\n  LLC → C corp); each transition costs considerable legal work and administrative burden.\n- High-potential, fast-track ventures should skip those hoops: the corporate form is almost always\n  best from the start.\n- Everything here is US law and not legal advice; always consult an attorney (box, p. 64)."},{"ref":"04-legal-structure.md","label":"Legal structure (handbook ch 4)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**Table 4-1 — Forms of business** (the chapter's own summary, p. 76)\n\n| Form | Key benefits | Key disadvantages |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sole proprietorship | Simple to organize and operate; one level of taxation | Full liability of the owner; cannot raise outside equity, limiting potential size |\n| General partnership | Brings in additional talent and personal capital; one level of taxation | Full liability of partners; capital limited to partners' pockets and borrowing; dissolves on death or withdrawal of any partner unless the agreement says otherwise |\n| Limited partnership | Limited liability (for limited partners); one level of taxation | Complex to set up |\n| C corporation | Can attract equity capital through share ownership; preferred form of venture capitalists; can deduct many employee benefit payments; shareholders have limited liability | Complex to set up and operate; income subject to double taxation |\n| S corporation | One level of taxation like a proprietorship/partnership; shareholders have limited liability | Complex to set up and operate; limited number of shareholders; venture capitalists cannot be shareholders |\n| Limited-liability company (LLC) | Simpler to set up and operate than a corporation; limited liability for members; one level of taxation; unlimited number of members | Cannot attract outside equity capital |\n\n**Full comparison of the six forms** (synthesised from pp. 63–75)\n\n| Form | Liability | Taxation | Ownership & control | Cost & complexity | Fundraising fit | Continuity |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Sole proprietorship | Unlimited — claimants can pursue the owner's personal property, not just business assets | One level: all income and expenses on Schedule C of the personal return; taxed whether or not cash is taken out; no way to shelter income; life/disability/medical premiums may be only partly deductible | One individual with complete control; income pocketed or reinvested at will | Lowest: no government filing or charter (some businesses need local licences, e.g. restaurants, day care; a trading name needs a DBA certificate); dissolves as informally as it began | Personal funds plus what the owner can borrow; no outside equity; cannot offer ownership to attract talent | Ends at the owner's death; assets pass to the estate |\n| General partnership | Unlimited and **joint and several** — any partner can be pursued for the whole claim, regardless of capital share or fault | One level: profits allocated per the agreement and taxed as each partner's personal income; some benefits may not be deductible | Two or more owners; shared judgment (ideally complementary skills) but slower decisions; disputes can tie it in knots | Easy apart from the time and legal cost of a written partnership agreement | More than one pool of personal capital plus borrowing; still no outside equity | Ends on withdrawal or death of any partner unless the agreement provides otherwise |\n| Limited partnership | General partner(s): unlimited; limited partners: capped at capital contributed plus debts specifically accepted | One level; profits and losses may be allocated differently (e.g. 20/80 profits but 99% of losses to LPs); losses deductible only up to capital at risk | General partner(s) manage and must hold at least 1% of profits and losses; limited partners have no voice in management | Complex to set up | Pairs operators with well-heeled passive investors; the vehicle of VC and real-estate funds | Per the partnership agreement |\n| C corporation | Shareholders limited to their investment; the veil is pierced only for fraud (officers remain personally liable for acts such as failing to withhold and pay corporate taxes) | **Two levels**: corporate tax on profits, then shareholders taxed on dividends; cost of certain officer/employee benefits deductible; salaries deductible but policed by IRS \"reasonable compensation\" rules | Unlimited number of owners holding transferable, giftable shares; managed for shareholders by a board of directors; stock and options attract and retain talent; every share sold dilutes control | Costly to incorporate; must adopt governance rules (stockholder meetings, board meetings, election of officers); monitored by federal, state and some local agencies; public corporations report quarterly | Best: sells stock to individuals and institutions (pension funds, mutual funds), can do successive public offerings, can borrow; the form VCs prefer; lets founders turn paper wealth into cash | Theoretically unlimited life |\n| S corporation | Shareholders limited to their capital contribution | One level (partnership-style flow-through to shareholders' personal returns) | Closely held: 35 or fewer stockholders (a married couple counts as one), one class of stock (voting differences allowed), individuals/estates/certain trusts only, all US citizens, domestic company | Complex to set up and operate (it is still a corporation) | Weak: no corporate or partnership shareholders, so VC firms are excluded | Corporate (perpetual) |\n| Limited-liability company (LLC) | Members limited (S-corp-level protection) | One level flow-through; operating agreement may split profits and losses in any ratio, not only by capital; members pay self-employment tax | Owners are \"members\"; no limit on number or type of members; law firms often use it | Simple to run: no statutory minutes, meetings or resolutions; state laws differ | Cannot attract outside equity capital (per Table 4-1) | Dissolves on the death of a member |\n\n### Non-US note (UK equivalents)\nThe forms above are US creatures (Schedule C, IRS, the S election, the 35-shareholder cap). Rough\nUK mapping: sole proprietorship → **sole trader** (register for Self Assessment; unlimited\nliability); general partnership → **ordinary partnership** (tax-transparent; joint and several\nliability); limited partnership → **limited partnership** (registered; the fund vehicle); LLC →\n[section truncated]"},{"ref":"04-legal-structure.md","label":"Legal structure (handbook ch 4)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **Large early losses** and investors who can use them to offset other income → partnership or LLC.\n- **Value created mainly by generating cash and distributing it** to investors → partnership or LLC.\n- **Long-term cash investment needed and value harvested through a sale or public offering** → C corp.\n- **High-potential, fast-track venture** → incorporate from the start; use stock and options to lure\n  an experienced team and conserve cash, even paying consultants in stock instead of cash.\n- **Any plan to raise venture capital** → C corp: VCs won't join a sole proprietorship, can't hold\n  S corp stock, and want a block of ownership.\n- **Want to share ownership with employees** → impossible in a sole proprietorship beyond simple\n  profit-sharing; requires a change of form.\n- **Two or more owners and no written agreement** → by default the partnership ends on any partner's\n  death or withdrawal, and every partner is exposed to the others' actions.\n- **Flow-through entity retaining earnings for growth** → owners still owe tax on that undistributed\n  income; budget for it.\n- **C corp owner-employee tempted to pay all profit as salary** (deductible, so not double-taxed) →\n  IRS reasonable-compensation rules limit this.\n- **Corporate veil** holds unless there is fraud; officers stay personally liable for duties such as\n  withholding and paying corporate taxes.\n- **Each share sold** dilutes ownership and personal control — price that against the growth\n  capital it buys.\n- **S corp caps to watch**: 35 shareholders, one class of stock, 25% passive revenue, 80% non-US\n  revenue, no entity shareholders.\n- **Corporations dominate**: over 60% of US sales despite being far fewer than sole proprietorships.\n- **Plan the evolution first**: choose for the stage you are entering *and* the next one; each\n  form change is a legal and administrative project.\n- **Before deciding**, consult a qualified tax attorney or accountant (p. 75)."},{"ref":"04-legal-structure.md","label":"Legal structure (handbook ch 4)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"The chapter gives no single numbered procedure; this sequence follows \"Which form makes sense for\nyou?\" (pp. 74–75) in the book's order.\n1. Write down your objectives and your investors' objectives (control, liability shelter, tax\n   treatment, exit).\n2. Project the venture's financial shape: early losses or profits? cash paid out to owners or\n   reinvested? long-term outside cash required? exit by sale or IPO?\n3. Map that shape to a form using the decision rules above (losses or cash-out → partnership or\n   LLC; long-term capital and exit → C corp; fast track → C corp now).\n4. Check eligibility and fit constraints (S corp requirements, VC involvement, employee equity,\n   number and type of owners, non-US owners, passive income).\n5. Sketch the likely evolution (e.g. sole prop → partnership → C corp) and weigh the cost of each\n   transition against starting in the later form now.\n6. Consult a qualified tax attorney or accountant.\n7. Execute the setup: DBA and licences (sole prop); written partnership or operating agreement\n   (partnership/LLC); charter, governance rules and officer elections (corporation); a separate\n   bank account and Schedule-C-aligned bookkeeping for everyone."},{"ref":"04-legal-structure.md","label":"Legal structure (handbook ch 4)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Form-choice diagnostic** (answer yes/no; derived from the chapter's decision factors)\n- Will more than one person own the business?\n- Will you raise outside equity from angels, funds or the public?\n- Will you seek venture capital specifically?\n- Do you intend to give employees or consultants a share of ownership (stock, options)?\n- Do you expect large losses in the early years — and could your investors use them against other income?\n- Is the business a cash generator that will pay out most profits, rather than reinvest for growth?\n- Will the business need cash investment over the long term?\n- Is the intended payoff a sale or public offering?\n- Does the business carry real liability exposure (products, premises, professional advice)?\n- Are any owners non-US citizens, or is a large share of revenue from outside the US?\n- Will passive income (interest, dividends, rents, royalties) exceed a quarter of revenue?\n- Are you willing to keep minutes, hold stockholder and board meetings and elect officers?\n- Do you want the business to outlive you or survive a co-owner's exit?\n- Do you need life, disability and medical premiums to be fully deductible?\n\n**Partnership agreement checklist** (pp. 67–68) — capital contributions; profit/loss allocation;\nsalaries and draws; management responsibilities; withdrawal/retirement/disability/death;\ndissolution and liquidation.\n\n**S corporation eligibility checklist** (p. 73) — one class of stock; domestic, US-citizen owners,\nno more than 80% non-US revenue; 35 or fewer stockholders; no more than 25% passive revenue;\nindividuals/estates/certain trusts only.\n\n**Sole proprietor launch checklist** (p. 65) — separate business bank account; accounting software\nwith Schedule C categories; receipt capture; name search and DBA filing; local licences (health\netc.); no Inc./Corp./Company/Co. in the name."},{"ref":"04-legal-structure.md","label":"Legal structure (handbook ch 4)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Mat Café (Matthew and Mathilde)** — 50/50 partnership earned $140,000; Matthew reports $70,000,\n  Mathilde $90,000 ($70,000 plus $20,000 from a part-time job) — a partnership is a conduit to\n  personal returns; and if Matthew harms a customer, the customer can sue Mathilde too (joint and\n  several liability).\n- **Deepwater Horizon (BP, Halliburton, Transocean, 2010)** — even had damages exceeded the\n  companies' net worth, courts could not pursue individual shareholders — the corporate veil.\n- **Amalgamated Hat Rack / Angus McDuff** — $647,500 pre-tax profit, a little over 46% ($300,000)\n  in state and federal corporate tax, $347,500 after tax; a $10,000 dividend to founder-CEO McDuff\n  is taxed again on his personal return — double taxation (minor exception: dividends a corporation\n  receives from other corporations).\n- **Surfside Management Consulting** — trading under a name other than your own triggers a DBA\n  filing and a name-availability check.\n- **Law firms** — commonly organized as LLCs: limited liability, flow-through tax, flexible profit split.\n- **VC and real-estate partnerships** — 99% of profits to LPs until capital is returned, then 30/70\n  — profit allocation in a partnership is a design choice, not a formula."},{"ref":"04-legal-structure.md","label":"Legal structure (handbook ch 4)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Optimising for tax alone and giving up liability protection the business actually needs.\n- Forgetting that flow-through income is taxed whether or not it is distributed — a growth-stage\n  tax bill with no cash to pay it.\n- Paying all corporate profit out as salary to dodge double taxation; the IRS polices reasonable\n  compensation.\n- Running a partnership on a handshake: no agreement means dissolution on any exit and no rules for\n  disputes, draws or contributions.\n- Underestimating joint and several liability — one partner can endanger the others without their\n  knowledge or consent.\n- Electing S corp status and then courting venture capital.\n- Corporation owners tripping over the formalities (minutes, meetings, resolutions) that LLCs avoid.\n- Officers assuming the veil covers them personally for unpaid withholding taxes.\n- Mixing household and business money, or losing receipts, in a sole proprietorship.\n- Using Inc., Corp., Company or Co. in an unincorporated business name.\n- A fast-track venture burning time and focus on successive form conversions instead of\n  incorporating once.\n- Limited partners expecting to deduct losses beyond their capital at risk.\n- Leaving a sole proprietorship's continuity unplanned — the entity dies with the owner."},{"ref":"04-legal-structure.md","label":"Legal structure (handbook ch 4)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`sole proprietorship` — a business owned by one individual; owner and business are one for tax and liability.\n`Schedule C` — the US personal-return schedule on which a proprietor reports business income and expenses.\n`general partnership` — two or more owners; treated like a proprietorship for tax and liability.\n`jointly and severally liable` — each partner can be pursued for the whole claim, regardless of fault or share.\n`limited partnership` — hybrid with managing general partner(s) (unlimited liability) and passive limited partners (liability capped at contribution).\n`C corporation` — state-chartered entity treated as a person in law; unlimited shareholders; taxed at the entity level.\n`corporate veil` — the shield limiting shareholder liability to their investment; pierced only for fraud.\n`double taxation` — profits taxed at the corporate level and again as dividends on shareholders' returns.\n`S corporation` — closely held corporation electing partnership-style flow-through tax while keeping limited liability.\n`limited-liability company (LLC)` — hybrid giving S-corp-level liability protection with proprietorship/partnership flow-through tax and no owner-count limits.\n`member` — an owner of an LLC (neither proprietor, partner nor shareholder).\n`operating agreement` — the LLC's equivalent of a partnership agreement; may allocate profits and losses in any ratio.\n`flow-through (conduit) entity` — one whose income is taxed on the owners' returns, not the entity's, whether or not cash is distributed.\n`DBA / fictitious name certificate` — local filing needed to trade under a name other than your own.\n`reasonable compensation` — IRS rules limiting how much corporate profit can be paid to owner-employees as deductible salary.\n`dilution` — the reduction in a founder's ownership share and control with every share sold."},{"ref":"04-legal-structure.md","label":"Legal structure (handbook ch 4)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"The chapter's summing up is Table 4-1 (above), condensed:\n- Sole proprietorship — simplest, one tax level; full personal liability, no outside equity.\n- General partnership — adds talent and capital, one tax level; full liability, capital limited to\n  partners, dissolves on exit unless the agreement says otherwise.\n- Limited partnership — limited liability for investors, one tax level; complex to set up.\n- C corporation — attracts equity, VC-preferred, deductible benefits, limited liability; complex\n  and double-taxed.\n- S corporation — one tax level with limited liability; complex, capped shareholders, no VCs.\n- LLC — simpler than a corporation, limited liability, one tax level, unlimited members; cannot\n  attract outside equity."},{"ref":"05-business-plan.md","label":"The business plan (handbook ch 5)","heading":"Purpose","text":"What a business plan is for, which format fits your audience (traditional plan, executive summary,\nelevator pitch, pitch deck, demo, canvas), exactly what goes into each section, and how to write it\nso a funder who gives you two minutes keeps reading. Load when a user asks \"write my business plan\",\n\"what goes in a pitch deck / executive summary\", \"how long should X be\", or whenever the app generates\nany funding or planning document."},{"ref":"05-business-plan.md","label":"The business plan (handbook ch 5)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- **Definition (p77):** a business plan explains a business opportunity, identifies the market to be\n  served, details how the organisation will pursue it, describes the unique qualifications of you and\n  your team, states the resources needed and forecasts financial results over a reasonable horizon.\n- **Funding is the obvious reason, not the only one.** Banks, angels, VCs — even relatives — won't\n  take you seriously without a solid plan (p78). Three further benefits: a deep reality check and\n  blueprint, advice from experienced outsiders, and projections that become your first budget (p78–79).\n- **The numbers are the weakest part.** \"No business plan survives first contact with customers\"\n  (Steve Blank). Padding plus enthusiasm make month-by-month projections years out unrealistic.\n  Sahlman (HBR, 1997): the best plans focus on the *people* and the *business model* (p79).\n- **Plans are evolving.** Tech founders use shorter, narrative, visual formats — pitch decks, demos,\n  elevator pitches, the business-model canvas (Baehr & Loomis) — that openly admit the future can't\n  be predicted. Pick the approach for your business type (p80).\n- **Whatever the format, the same core elements must be thought through:** the opportunity, the\n  solution, the market, the model, the team (p80).\n- **Readers are overloaded.** Many VC firms review 1,000+ plans a year and fund a few; banks and angels\n  are the same. Grab attention, keep it, and answer the reader's concerns in a well-organised way (p80).\n- **Show your work.** Because the numbers will be wrong, the plan must demonstrate professionalism,\n  expertise and trustworthiness — not just optimism (p80).\n- **Know your audience.** Nothing about the prototype format is sacred; tailor it to the reader's\n  interests the way you'd tailor a résumé to a job (p81).\n- **Style is half the plan.** Most founders shortchange it, making plans dull and hard to read. Use\n  words sparingly, keep sentences simple, use design elements, use graphics judiciously (p92).\n- **Tell readers how they get their money out.** Investors want an exit — sooner rather than later (p99)."},{"ref":"05-business-plan.md","label":"The business plan (handbook ch 5)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**Figure 5-1 — Prototype business plan format (Lo-Sugar Foods Company, p81)**\nImplied lengths are derived from the sample's page numbers (about 40 pages plus appendix).\n\n| # | Section | Sample pages | Implied length |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I | Executive summary | 2–4 | 2–3 pages |\n| II | The opportunity | 5–11 | ~7 pages |\n| III | The company and its offering and strategy | 12–14 | ~3 pages |\n| IV | The team | 15–17 | ~3 pages |\n| V | Marketing plan | 18–21 | ~4 pages |\n| VI | Operating plan | 22–24 | ~3 pages |\n| VII | Financial plan | 25–29 | ~5 pages |\n| — | Appendix: résumés of management team | 30–31 | 2 pages |\n| — | Appendix: supporting market research | 32–39 | ~8 pages |\n| — | Appendix: sales projections for initial products | 40+ | — |\n\nAlso belongs in the appendix: full financial statements, market-test details, any detailed data\nthat would otherwise bury the story (p83, p91).\n\n**Section-by-section specification (the generator schema)**\n\n*Contents (p81).* A table of contents so readers see at a glance what the plan offers and how to navigate.\n\n*I. Executive summary (p82).* The most important part for capturing backers; many readers never go\nfurther. Assume one or two minutes to convince them to keep reading.\n- Traditional: 2–3 pages. Not a preface or introduction — a snapshot of the entire plan that\n  explains the business to an intelligent reader in a few minutes.\n- Must explain (from the chapter's summary, p99): the opportunity; why it is timely; how the company\n  will pursue it; expected results; a thumbnail of the company and the management team.\n- Newer variants: an elevator pitch of ~100 words or a single sentence, often story-based to reach\n  the reader's emotions; in a pitch deck, an overview slide saying what the company is and what\n  problem it solves, big-picture; a separate 2-minute video elevator pitch on a private YouTube\n  channel for shareability.\n- Test: is it memorable enough that the reader can easily recount it to others?\n\n*II. The opportunity (p82–83).*\n- Outline the problem, its scope, and the market trends that will shape how the market experiences\n  the problem in the years ahead; then introduce your solution.\n- Describe the opportunity in clear, compelling terms; help readers see and appreciate it.\n- Highlight the economics underlying the problem and the factors that will drive your solution's\n  success (e.g. market penetration, product innovation).\n- State the magnitude of funding sought and how it will be used.\n- Keep it brief, focused and upbeat. Document with objective data but do not data-dump: summarise\n  the data, explain its implications for investors, push detail to the appendix.\n- Lo-Sugar example content: obesity figures for the US and Western Europe and their effect on\n  health, quality of life and care costs; research that 29 million Americans and 8 million Europeans\n  follow low-sugar diets (the chapter intro cites 4.5M / 1.2M — the company is fictional); independent\n  studies confirming effectiveness; consumer spending on health and weight-control foods; funding\n  ask of $2.75 million, mostly to exploit current success and a national high-protein/low-carb\n  vendor's interest in existing products.\n\n*III. The company and its offering and strategy (p83–86).* Four sub-parts.\n1. *Solution and company* — the product or service, the company and how it is organised, and any\n   traction data so far. Lo-Sugar: Colorado corporation founded 2018; three test-marketed products\n   (Mellow Mornings — whole-wheat/barley cereal with 90% less sugar than leading cereals; Crackle\n   Brackle — crisped steel-cut oatmeal for breakfast and baking; Yesgurt — dairy), differentiated by\n   being tasty and satisfying as well as low-sugar; sold through two regional health-food chains;\n   more in development; market-test details referenced to the appendix.\n2. *Goals* — how you plan to grow. If the company could become an acquisition target for a larger,\n   less innovative competitor, say so. Lo-Sugar's three goals: broaden the product line; expand\n   penetration through stores and a private-label agreement with a major diet company (in\n   negotiation); become the dominant healthy-food niche player or be acquired by a packaged-food\n   giant (small companies with one or two hit products are often bought at premium prices).\n   Product readiness: not yet market-ready → rollout plans, snapshots of viable prototypes, an\n   artist's rendering of the final product; market-ready → high-quality photos.\n3. *Business model and strategy* (its own section) — business model = how you make money; strategy\n   = differentiation and competitive advantage. Answer: What are your costs? Where will revenue come\n   from? What makes the product, service or delivery different and more desirable to customers? How\n   does that difference become a *sustainable* advantage producing profits and growing equity value?\n   Differentiation can sit in the product/service (a technically superior way of servicing electric\n   cars) or in the approach to customers (Uber's platform bypassing taxis). Describe competitors:\n   why customers will choose you, proof you understand how the industry operates, how competitors\n   will react to your success, what happens if they copy your best seller, how you keep loyalty.\n4. *Ownership* — current owners and percentages; how ownership is evidenced (common vs preferred\n   stock); options, warrants or convertible bonds that could expand ownership; which owners work in\n   the business day to day.\n\n*IV. The team (p86–89).* One of the most important sections. \"Without the right team, none of the\nother parts really matter\" (Sahlman).\n- No lengthy biographies; highlight the experiences and qualifications that make these the right\n  people for this mission. Résumés go in the appendix.\n- Say how the team must grow and what capabilities the strategy will need.\n- Add a table of names, titles and salaries (Table 5-1) and an org chart if reporting lines aren't\n  obvious.\n[section truncated]"},{"ref":"05-business-plan.md","label":"The business plan (handbook ch 5)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- Seeking any outside money → you need a coherent plan in *some* format; without one you look like\n  you haven't identified customers or how to serve them profitably.\n- Choose format by audience, business and industry: tech investors → deck/demo/story; banks and\n  traditional investors → fuller written plan. Tailor like a résumé.\n- Executive summary: 2–3 pages traditional; elevator pitch ~100 words or one sentence; video 2\n  minutes; assume the reader gives you one or two minutes.\n- Presentation deck: ≤1 sentence per slide. Reading deck: strategy statements ≤15 words; whole deck\n  scannable in 15 seconds.\n- Paragraphs should average no more than 200 words. Long blocks → headings, subheads, lists, white space.\n- Numbered list when sequence matters; bulleted list for items, parts of a whole, or three key ideas;\n  never long complex bullets (they're just another text block).\n- One message per chart. Decide the point *before* clicking \"insert chart\"; sketch by hand first;\n  drop distracting series and detailed labels.\n- Detailed data, full financial statements, résumés, market research → appendix. Body = summary +\n  implications for investors.\n- Every projection must carry its assumptions and the reasoning behind them.\n- Products not market-ready → rollout plan + prototype snapshots + artist's rendering; market-ready →\n  high-quality photos.\n- Corporation → name the board; non-obvious reporting lines → org chart.\n- If a larger, less innovative competitor might acquire you → say so in Goals.\n- Always answer \"how will investors cash out?\" — buyout by management, acquisition, IPO. Even if you\n  plan to stay for the long haul, investors want liquidity, the sooner the better.\n- Treat the plan as a thinking exercise, not fact (Blank); the numbers are not a strong predictor of success.\n- Use Word/PowerPoint features (bold, italics, fonts, colour, 3-D, clip art, animation) sparingly —\n  overuse looks amateurish.\n- \"Shorter is always better if it communicates the required information\" (p92)."},{"ref":"05-business-plan.md","label":"The business plan (handbook ch 5)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. Decide the format(s) for your audience: traditional plan, executive summary, elevator pitch,\n   pitch deck (presentation vs reading), video, demo, canvas.\n2. Think through the core elements regardless of format: opportunity, solution, market, model, team.\n3. Draft each section in the Figure 5-1 order (opportunity → company/offering/strategy incl. goals,\n   model, ownership, IP → team incl. board → marketing → operations → financials).\n4. Move detail into the appendix: résumés, market research, sales projections, full statements,\n   market-test data.\n5. Write the executive summary as a snapshot of the finished plan, then compress it further into an\n   elevator pitch / overview slide / video script.\n6. Style pass: omit needless words; split complex sentences; add headings, subheads, lists, white\n   space; convert numeric paragraphs into single-message charts.\n7. Reader pass: stop and ask — Is this a real opportunity? Do I understand the market and customers?\n   Can we really make this work? Confirm funding ask, use of funds and exit strategy are stated.\n8. Expose the draft to trusted, experienced outsiders to find missed opportunities, unsupportable\n   assumptions and over-optimistic projections; fix them on paper.\n9. After launch, use the projections as the initial budget; investigate and correct when actuals fall short.\n\nChart-making sub-process (Berinato, p97): decide the point → sketch formats by hand (pie, bar, tree\nmap) → pick the one that makes the point most clearly → include only the data needed → keep text minimal."},{"ref":"05-business-plan.md","label":"The business plan (handbook ch 5)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Executive summary must-answer list (p82, p99)**\n- What is the opportunity, and why is it timely?\n- How will the company pursue it?\n- What results do you expect?\n- Thumbnail: what is the company, who is the team?\n- Can a reader retell it to someone else after one read?\n\n**Opportunity section checklist (p82–83)**\n- Problem, its scope, and the market trends shaping it in coming years\n- The proposed solution\n- Underlying economics and the drivers of success (penetration, innovation)\n- Funding sought and use of funds\n- Data summarised with investor implications; detail in appendix\n\n**Business model & strategy questions (p85)**\n- What are your costs? Where will your revenue come from?\n- What is different about your approach to the marketplace?\n- What makes your product, service or delivery different and more desirable in customers' eyes?\n- How will that difference translate into a sustainable competitive advantage, profits and growing equity value?\n- Who are your competitors, and why will customers choose you instead?\n- How does your industry operate?\n- How might competitors react to your success? What if they copy your best-selling product?\n- How will you maintain customer loyalty?\n\n**Ownership questions (p86)**\n- Who are the current owners and what percentages do they control?\n- How is ownership evidenced (common vs preferred stock)?\n- Have you issued options, warrants or convertible bonds that could expand ownership?\n- Which owners are involved in the day-to-day workings of the business?\n\n**Intellectual property questions (p86)**\n- Is your competitive advantage based on a proprietary technology or process?\n- Is it patented or patentable?\n- Does the company own patents, copyrights or valuable trademarks? When do they expire?\n- What steps keep technical and market know-how inside the organisation?\n\n**Box — Questions about your team every business plan should answer (Sahlman, p88)**\n- Where are the founders and other key team members from?\n- Where did they go to school?\n- Where have they worked — and for whom? What are their current roles?\n- What have they accomplished — professionally and personally — in the past?\n- What is their reputation within the business community?\n- What aspects of their experience are directly relevant to the opportunity they are pursuing?\n- What skills, abilities and knowledge do they have?\n- How realistic are they about the venture's chances for success and the tribulations it will face?\n- Who else needs to be on the team?\n- Are they prepared to recruit high-quality people?\n- How will they respond to adversity?\n- Do they have the mettle to make the inevitable hard choices?\n- How committed are they to this venture?\n- What are their motivations?\nPlus: how will the team need to grow, and what capabilities does the strategy require? (p87)\n\n**Board of directors entry (p89)** — name; position on the board; professional background; history\nof involvement with the company.\n\n**Marketing plan checklist — be clear about all ten (p89–90)**\n1. Who your customers / primary market are — the customer type you must reach for full market potential\n2. Market size — number of potential customers and projected potential sales revenues\n3. Requirements of each customer segment (purchase convenience, rapid delivery, customisation, etc.)\n4. Ways to access each segment (distributors, e-commerce, captive sales force)\n5. Sales and promotion approaches (social media campaigns, content marketing, freemium, direct email)\n6. How your customer makes purchase decisions\n7. Customer price sensitivity\n8. Acquisition cost per customer and cost of retaining customers\n9. Competitors' strengths and weaknesses and their likely reaction when you enter\n10. Supporting market intelligence — summarised here, detailed in the appendix\n\n**Operating plan questions (p90–91)**\n- What supplier relationships do you have or envision?\n- How much inventory will be required?\n- Which day-to-day tasks are handled internally, and which are outsourced?\n- How, in detail, do inputs become outputs customers value?\n\n**Financial plan checklist (p91)**\n- Balance sheet, income statement, cash-flow statement (full versions in appendix)\n- Pro forma income statement: sales and expense projections\n- Revenue breakout by channel with growth and % of sales (Table 5-2 pattern)\n- Key expense categories with % of sales (Table 5-3 pattern)\n- Assumptions behind each projection and why you made them\n\n**Style checklist (p92–98)**\n- Every word tells; needless words omitted (Strunk & White rule 17)\n- No sentence beyond its \"optimal carrying capacity\"; extra information gets its own sentence\n- Headings and subheads carry the key ideas — a skimmer gets the gist from them alone\n- Paragraphs average ≤200 words; lists used for sequence, items, parts of a whole, three key ideas\n- Each chart makes one point; text in graphics minimal\n- Design features used sparingly; document looks professional, not decorated\n\n**Reader-lens self-check (p98–99)**\n- Is this a real opportunity with substantial growth possibilities and major upside?\n- Have I shown my homework — the market, the right customers, a sound strategy to transact profitably?\n- Does the team have the knowledge, experience and drive to turn the opportunity into profits?\n- Can we really make this thing work?\n- How will investors get their money out?"},{"ref":"05-business-plan.md","label":"The business plan (handbook ch 5)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Lo-Sugar Foods Company** (fictional prototype, p81–92) — unsweetened breakfast/snack maker; three\n  products in two regional chains; $2.75M ask; three growth goals ending in dominance or acquisition;\n  channel revenue from $112K to $520K in three years — the worked template for every section.\n- **Uber** (p85) — differentiation from the approach to customers (a platform connecting drivers and\n  riders, bypassing taxis), not from a superior product.\n- **Electric-car servicing** (p85) — differentiation residing in a technically superior product/service.\n- **Gunther Schwartz's credential** (p87) — twelve years at two named employers plus the extrusion\n  process behind two successful snack brands — how to write a team bio: concrete, relevant, short.\n- **Downtown transport survey** (p96–97) — four sentences of percentages vs one pie chart; the chart wins.\n- **Health-spending chart** (p98) — two series and dense labels obscure the message; one line, one\n  message (\"annual growth is declining\") lands.\n- **Weight-loss four steps** (p96) — eat less → exercise more → try diet programmes → settle on a\n  long-term diet — a numbered list makes a sequence unmissable.\n- **Steve Blank** (p79) — plans are full of untested assumptions; valuable for thinking, not fact.\n- **William Sahlman** (p79, p87) — judge plans by people and business model; the team is decisive."},{"ref":"05-business-plan.md","label":"The business plan (handbook ch 5)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Mistaking the plan for fact; padding numbers; detailed month-by-month projections years ahead.\n- Turning the opportunity section into a data dump that buries the story.\n- Lengthy biographies instead of relevant qualifications.\n- Ignoring how competitors will react or what happens if they copy you.\n- Omitting the funding ask, use of funds, or the exit strategy.\n- Projections without stated assumptions.\n- Empty impressive-sounding sentences; long complex sentences; long text blocks; long complex bullets.\n- Charts that carry several messages or distracting extra data.\n- Overusing fonts, colours, 3-D, clip art and animation — looks amateurish.\n- Concentrating on substance and ignoring style entirely, producing a dull, hard-to-read plan.\n- Sending a presentation deck to be read (too sparse) or presenting a reading deck (too wordy)."},{"ref":"05-business-plan.md","label":"The business plan (handbook ch 5)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`business plan` — document explaining an opportunity, its market, how the company will pursue it, the team's qualifications, resources needed and forecast results.\n`executive summary` — 2–3 page snapshot of the whole plan; the section most readers stop at.\n`elevator pitch` — ~100-word or one-sentence, often story-based statement of the business; also a 2-minute video variant.\n`pitch deck` — slide-based plan; a *presentation deck* supports a live talk (≤1 sentence/slide), a *reading deck* stands alone (point + evidence per slide, scannable in 15 s).\n`business model` — how you plan to make money (costs, revenue sources).\n`strategy` — how you differentiate and sustain competitive advantage.\n`pro forma income statement` — projected sales and expenses; the core of the financial plan section.\n`balance sheet` / `income statement` / `cash-flow statement` — what you own and how it's financed at a date / revenues, costs, interest and taxes over a period / sources and uses of cash over that period.\n`first-mover advantage` — being first to scale in a market; a strategic (non-legal) IP defence.\n`private-label agreement` — making product sold under another company's brand; a channel in Lo-Sugar's goals.\n`exit strategy` — how investors get liquidity: management buyout, acquisition, IPO."},{"ref":"05-business-plan.md","label":"The business plan (handbook ch 5)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- Choose a format that makes sense for your audience, your business and the industry.\n- Whatever the format, tell readers persuasively everything they need to make a decision.\n- Funding is only one reason to write a plan; the act of writing forces you to think through every key element.\n- The executive summary should compellingly explain the opportunity, why it is timely, how you'll\n  pursue it, expected results, and a thumbnail of the company and team.\n- State the company's goals and explain how investors will eventually cash out.\n- Pay attention to style: as few words as necessary; avoid long, complex sentences.\n- Make it skimmable: simple data visualisations, headings, subheads, white space, numbered and bulleted lists."},{"ref":"06-startup-financing.md","label":"Startup financing (handbook ch 6)","heading":"Purpose","text":"Where does launch money come from, and how much do you actually need? Maps the business\nlife cycle (startup → growth → maturity), sizes a startup's asset needs into an opening balance\nsheet, and surveys the bootstrap and seed sources. Load when a user asks \"how do I fund my\nstartup\", \"should I crowdfund / join an accelerator\", or is building a launch budget."},{"ref":"06-startup-financing.md","label":"Startup financing (handbook ch 6)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- Financing is needed at every size and stage — corner bookstore to Amazon; even a\n  billion-dollar mature company keeps financing to stay on the cutting edge.\n- US small businesses fall into three types (Mills & McCarthy, HBS/Fundera): **Main Street\n  (~70%)**, **supply-chain (~17%)**, **high-growth (~3%)**. Life cycle and financing needs\n  differ by type.\n- Every business passes through **startup, growth and maturity**; what varies is the scale of\n  growth and the length of the growth period. Rapid growers need successive rounds at\n  ever-higher levels.\n- Few companies make it through all three phases: many fail within a few years, others are\n  acquired before reaching full potential.\n- Startup money is mostly **personal (bootstrap)**, then **seed** from friends and family, small\n  banks and online lenders, supplier trade credit, crowdfunding and accelerators.\n- A team with a strong reputation or prior exits can attract angels, PE or even VC before it has a\n  marketable product. Financing has decentralised: VC was an exclusive club; angels, accelerators\n  and crowdfunding now widen the menu.\n- Equity has a price: you sell ownership, control and future profit. Worth it only if you must\n  raise quickly.\n- Early-stage equity is still rare. Kauffman: **40% of initial startup capital, even for fast\n  growers, is bank debt**, with an almost equal amount of owner equity.\n- Most businesses never grow large enough for accelerators or equity funding; a $10–20M revenue\n  ceiling is a fine business, just not a venture-scale one."},{"ref":"06-startup-financing.md","label":"Startup financing (handbook ch 6)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**Three types of US small business** (Mills & McCarthy, p. 104)\n\n| Type | Share of small-employer firms | Profile | Life cycle and financing need |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Main Street | ~70% | Dry cleaners, mechanics, clinics; serve the owner's personal/family income; sustain rather than grow once established | Startup → gradual growth → no/slow-growth maturity. Startup money buys or leases equipment, rents premises, stocks inventory and fixtures, funds working capital — or buys an existing business (see *HBR Guide to Buying a Small Business*) |\n| Supply-chain | ~17% | Niche firms serving a focused need in an industry, region or supply chain; ~37% of US employment and 80% of 2013 US patents | Keep pursuing growth after startup |\n| High-growth | ~3% | Mostly high-tech (Apple, Facebook, Amazon, Airbnb, Uber); disproportionate job creation | Successive financing phases at ever-higher levels |\n\n**The business life cycle**: startup → growth → maturity. This chapter covers startup; chapter 7\ncovers growth and maturity; chapter 8 covers angels/VC; chapter 9 covers IPOs.\n\n**Startup-phase sources** (p. 105)\n- *Bootstrap* (your own resources, no big money): personal savings; a second mortgage on the\n  home; credit-card lines of credit.\n- *Seed* (early outside money): loans from friends and relatives; bank debt from small banks and\n  online lenders (especially important for Main Street firms); 30–60 day trade credit from\n  suppliers; crowdfunding; equity from an accelerator.\n- *Reputation-driven*: angels, private equity, VC, hedge funds — realistic only when the founders\n  have a demonstrated \"Midas touch\".\n\n**Figure 6-1 — Sources of funding, Inc. 5000 fastest-growing US companies, 2014** (Kauffman)\n\n| Source | % of companies using it |\n|---|---|\n| Personal savings | 67.2% |\n| Bank loans | 51.8% |\n| Credit card | 34.0% |\n| Family | 20.9% |\n| Business acquaintances | 11.9% |\n| Angel investors | 7.7% |\n| Close friends | 7.5% |\n| Venture capitalists | 6.5% |\n| Government grants | 3.8% |\n| Have not used finance | 13.6% |\n\n**Tables 6-1 to 6-3 — Amalgamated Hat Rack opening balance sheet** (worked example, pp. 106–110)\n\nTable 6-1 Current asset requirements\n\n| Item | $ |\n|---|---|\n| Cash (to cover a 3-month startup period when costs outstrip revenue) | 8,000 |\n| Inventory — lumber | 4,000 |\n| Inventory — hardware | 2,500 |\n| Inventory — other | 1,000 |\n| *Total inventory* | *7,500* |\n| Prepaid — insurance (1 year) | 1,500 |\n| Prepaid — rent (3 months) | 5,000 |\n| *Total prepaid expenses* | *6,500* |\n| **Total current assets** | **22,000** |\n\nTable 6-2 Fixed asset requirements\n\n| Item | $ |\n|---|---|\n| Used panel truck | 7,500 |\n| Lathes (two, used) | 900 |\n| Other tools | 800 |\n| Shop fixtures | 800 |\n| *Total fixed assets* | *10,000* |\n| Total current assets (Table 6-1) | 22,000 |\n| **Total current and fixed assets** | **32,000** |\n\nTable 6-3 Liabilities and owners' equity (how the $32,000 was financed)\n\n| Item | $ |\n|---|---|\n| Current liabilities — current portion of 5-year loan | 1,000 |\n| Long-term liabilities — balance of 5-year loan | 4,000 |\n| *Total liabilities* | *5,000* |\n| Original owners' equity (personal savings) | 25,000 |\n| Crowdfunding proceeds | 2,000 |\n| *Total owners' equity* | *27,000* |\n| **Total liabilities and owners' equity** | **32,000** |\n\n**Table 6-4 — The four institutions that support startups** (Cohen 2013, adapted by Hathaway, p. 112)\n\n| | Incubators | Angel investors | Accelerators | Hybrid |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Duration | 1 to 5 years | Ongoing | 3 to 6 months | 3 months to 2 years |\n| Cohorts | No | No | Yes | No |\n| Business model | Rent; nonprofit | Investment | Investment; can also be nonprofit | Investment; can also be nonprofit |\n| Selection | Noncompetitive | Competitive, ongoing | Competitive, cyclical | Competitive, ongoing |\n| Venture stage | Early or late | Early | Early | Early |\n| Education | Ad hoc, HR, legal | None | Seminars | Various incubator and accelerator practices |\n| Mentorship | Minimal, tactical | As needed by investor | Intense, by self and others | Staff expert support, some mentoring |\n| Venture location | On-site | Off-site | On-site | On-site |"},{"ref":"06-startup-financing.md","label":"Startup financing (handbook ch 6)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **Size the raise from the balance sheet**: cash runway for the months costs exceed revenue\n  (McDuff: 3 months) + opening inventory + prepaid insurance/rent + fixed assets.\n- **Trade credit first.** 30–60 day supplier terms finance inventory at zero interest if you sell\n  through before the bill is due — better than any bank line.\n- **Credit cards and home-equity lines are legitimate but expensive** — use them last.\n- **Buy used fixed assets** from a former employer or industry contact (McDuff got a truck, two\n  lathes, tools and benches for $10,000).\n- **Bank debt has been tight since 2008** despite legislative fixes → crowdfunding, angel\n  syndication and online lending matter more for early needs.\n- **Crowdfund if the idea has broad consumer reach or deep reach into a narrow market.** Rewards\n  are early sales, and the campaign validates interest and tests messaging and pricing.\n  US crowdfunding exceeded $2B in 2015.\n- **Crowdfunding levels the field for women**: under 8% of VC-backed companies have female\n  cofounders, but women are 13% more likely than men to succeed on Kickstarter (Mollick).\n- **Equity crowdfunding is legal but suspect.** The JOBS Act (SEC rules, March 2015) lets nearly\n  anyone invest without accredited status, but Isenberg warns that equity is too complex to\n  extrapolate from rewards successes and the due diligence makes it too high-cost to work.\n- **Accelerators only for high-growth candidates, and only top programs.** Hathaway: graduates of\n  top programs raised VC, gained traction and exited faster; other programs showed no strong\n  effect — and every accelerator takes equity.\n- **Reputation buys early equity.** Financiers actively pursue founders with past successes; a\n  first-timer should plan on debt + own equity instead.\n- **Don't leave the job early.** McDuff went full-time only once the $32,000 was in hand."},{"ref":"06-startup-financing.md","label":"Startup financing (handbook ch 6)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"The McDuff launch sequence (the chapter's implied startup-financing procedure):\n1. Use the final year of employment to design the product line, cost it with real production\n   numbers, and learn the distribution channels.\n2. Compute current assets: cash runway, inventory by category, prepaid insurance and rent.\n3. Compute fixed assets: tools, vehicle, fixtures — sourced used where possible.\n4. Add them up to the total launch capital ($32,000).\n5. Stack sources cheapest first: personal savings ($25,000) → zero-interest family loan ($5,000,\n   repaid $1,000/year) → rewards crowdfunding ($2,000 plus product feedback).\n6. Write the opening balance sheet (assets = liabilities + owners' equity).\n7. Leave the job and go full-time.\n8. Once the bootstrapped company shows signs of success (the *seed stage*), add external\n   sources: trade credit, small-bank/online loans, crowdfunding, accelerators."},{"ref":"06-startup-financing.md","label":"Startup financing (handbook ch 6)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"Startup capital worksheet (Tables 6-1 and 6-2 as a form):\n- Cash to cover months when costs exceed revenue: ___ months × $___ per month = $___\n- Opening inventory, by category: ___\n- Prepaid expenses: insurance (1 year) ___; rent (first ___ months) ___; other ___\n- Fixed assets: equipment ___; vehicle ___; tools ___; fixtures ___ (new or used?)\n- Total assets required = $___\n\nSource stack worksheet (Table 6-3 as a form):\n- Personal savings available: $___\n- Second mortgage / home-equity line: $___ (rate ___)\n- Credit-card lines: $___ (rate ___ — expensive)\n- Friends and family loans: $___, interest ___, repayment ___ per year\n- Rewards crowdfunding target: $___\n- Supplier trade credit: ___ days on $___ of inventory\n- Small bank / online lender: $___\n- Accelerator equity: $___ for ___% ownership\n- Check: total sources = total assets required.\n\nCrowdfunding fit check:\n- Does the product have broad consumer reach, or deep reach into a narrow market?\n- Can you offer rewards that are effectively pre-sales of the product?\n- Will you use the campaign to test messaging and pricing and to collect product feedback?\n- Rewards or equity? If equity, can you carry the due-diligence cost and the complexity?\n\nAccelerator / support-institution fit check (Table 6-4):\n- Are you a high-growth business (the 3%) or a Main Street / supply-chain firm?\n- Does this program have a record of VC raises, customer traction and acquisitions?\n- Can you relocate on-site for 3–6 months and work in a cohort?\n- Are you willing to give away equity for the funding and mentorship?\n- How long do you need support (months vs years)? Do you need space, seminars, intense\n  mentorship, or just capital?"},{"ref":"06-startup-financing.md","label":"Startup financing (handbook ch 6)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Amalgamated Hat Rack (Angus McDuff)** — launched on $32,000: $25,000 savings, $5,000 uncle's\n  zero-interest loan, $2,000 Indiegogo — mostly self- and family-financed, like most small\n  businesses; may top out at $10–20M revenue and never suit an accelerator or VC.\n- **Apple** — Jobs and Wozniak's garage company, the archetypal 3% high-growth firm, now the\n  world's largest by market cap — same three phases, wildly different scale.\n- **Oculus Rift** (Mollick) — VCs shunned VR after the 1990s hype; a 2012 Kickstarter raised\n  nearly $2.5M; Facebook bought the company for $2B under two years later — crowdfunding can\n  bypass traditional funding entirely.\n- **Airbnb, Dropbox** — accelerator graduates — the path works for the right kind of company.\n- **Shoe-store owner** — $3,000 of shoes on 60-day terms, sold before payment is due — the\n  supplier finances inventory for free."},{"ref":"06-startup-financing.md","label":"Startup financing (handbook ch 6)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Assuming early equity investment is normal; debt and owner equity dominate startup capital.\n- Paying credit-card interest for launch capital when cheaper sources exist.\n- Treating rewards-crowdfunding success as evidence that equity crowdfunding will work.\n- Giving equity to a mediocre accelerator for little performance uplift.\n- Chasing VC or accelerators when you are a Main Street business.\n- Quitting the day job before the launch capital and plan are in place."},{"ref":"06-startup-financing.md","label":"Startup financing (handbook ch 6)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`bootstrap financing` — launch capital drawn entirely from the founder's own resources (savings, home equity, credit cards).\n`seed stage / seed investment` — the first outside money, sought once the bootstrapped business shows signs of success.\n`Main Street firm` — a locally serving small business run mainly to sustain the owner's income.\n`supply-chain firm` — a niche business serving a focused need in an industry, region or larger firm's supply chain.\n`high-growth firm` — the ~3% of small firms, mostly high-tech, that grow very rapidly and need successive rounds.\n`trade credit` — 30–60 day supplier terms that finance inventory without interest.\n`rewards crowdfunding` — backers give money for rewards (often early product); also validates demand.\n`equity crowdfunding` — backers buy actual ownership; open to non-accredited investors under the JOBS Act.\n`accelerator` — fixed-term, cohort-based, competitive program giving funding, education and mentorship for equity, ending in a demo day.\n`incubator` — longer-term (1–5 years), noncompetitive, rent-based on-site support.\n`demo day` — the accelerator's closing showcase where participants pitch to investors."},{"ref":"06-startup-financing.md","label":"Startup financing (handbook ch 6)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- Startup financing is bootstrapped from personal savings, credit cards and other personal\n  sources, then friends and family, and sometimes small bank loans.\n- Supplier trade credit is another low-cost source.\n- Other early sources: online banks, accelerators and crowdfunding."},{"ref":"07-growth-financing.md","label":"Growth financing (handbook ch 7)","heading":"Purpose","text":"How to fund expansion once the business has proven itself: bank debt and what bankers test, how\nmuch debt is safe (leverage ratios), the equity path to an IPO, what maturity-phase financing\nlooks like, and the rule for matching assets to financing. Load when a user asks about a bank\nloan, \"how much debt can I carry\", lender ratios, or wants an overview of growth → IPO → maturity\nfunding options."},{"ref":"07-growth-financing.md","label":"Growth financing (handbook ch 7)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- Growth needs capital for expansion, hiring and training, acquisitions and brand visibility.\n  Operating cash flow helps but rarely covers strong growth.\n- Proven credibility makes external capital easier. **Slow-to-moderate growth → bank debt;\n  likely to grow large quickly → equity** (chapter 8).\n- Banks lend **short and secured** to small firms — demand loans, seasonal lines of credit,\n  single-purpose equipment loans — and avoid long-term or unsecured loans because failure rates\n  are high. Small community banks approve more and satisfy more than big banks. 54% of small\n  firms hold under $100,000 of debt.\n- Bankers ask **three questions** (ability to repay, character, collateral) and rarely lend\n  without satisfactory answers to all three.\n- **Debt is the lowest-cost external capital** because interest is tax-deductible (US) — but\n  that helps only once there is taxable income, and interest is a contractual obligation payable\n  in bad years too.\n- **Leverage** magnifies owner returns and bankruptcy risk; lenders police it with the debt,\n  debt-to-equity and times-interest-earned ratios and write limits into loan covenants.\n- Growth-phase companies are usually too small or unproven for an IPO; VC or angel money\n  arrives only for hot industries or game-changing products. Most companies fail or are acquired\n  before either.\n- The **IPO** is the major milestone for the few: investment banks and their broker-dealer\n  syndicates open access to millions of investors and let early holders turn paper into cash.\n- **Maturity**: growth tapers, but financing continues — commercial paper, bank lines, term\n  loans, sale-and-leaseback, bonds and stock — and timing the market matters.\n- **Match assets to financing**: short-term assets with short-term money, long-term assets with\n  long-term debt or shareholders' capital."},{"ref":"07-growth-financing.md","label":"Growth financing (handbook ch 7)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**The banker's three questions** (p. 116)\n1. Will the borrower be able to pay me back? — judged from the entrepreneur's skills, the plan,\n   projected cash flow and (if operating) the current and acid-test ratios.\n2. Is the borrower's character such that he or she will pay me back? — judged from credit\n   history: evidence you pay bills on schedule.\n3. If the borrower fails, what marketable assets can I get my hands on? — collateral: current\n   assets (cash, inventory, receivables) and fixed assets (vehicles, buildings, equipment); an SBA\n   guarantee can stand in for the owner's assets.\n\n**Lender ratios** (pp. 117–120)\n\n| Ratio | Formula | What it tells a lender |\n|---|---|---|\n| Current ratio | Current assets ÷ current liabilities | Ability to pay bills as they come due. A business with steady, reliable cash inflows (utility, taxi firm) can live on ~1.10; a manufacturer with a long development and production cycle needs more |\n| Acid-test (quick) ratio | Quick assets (cash, marketable securities, receivables) ÷ current liabilities | Absolute liquidity — inventory excluded because it can't be sold on short notice |\n| Debt ratio | Total debt ÷ total assets | Degree of leverage |\n| Debt-to-equity ratio | Total liabilities ÷ owners' equity | Leverage; as it rises, owner returns and risk both rise; creditors set limits beyond which loans are called |\n| Times-interest-earned ratio | EBIT ÷ interest expense | How far income could fall before interest is uncovered — a test of staying power under adversity more than of solvency |\n\n**Table 7-1 — Sources of capital for growth-stage financing** (p. 122)\n\n| Source | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| Internal cash flow from operations | Cost-free if shareholders aren't anxious for dividends | May not be enough to finance substantial growth in the productive base |\n| Debt capital | Interest deductible from taxable income (if any); rate set by prevailing rates, loan term and creditworthiness | Costly; increases the riskiness of the enterprise |\n| Venture capital | Comes with business advice that may be valuable — unlike any other capital | The most expensive capital: a significant share of ownership and of future prospects; the entrepreneur must share power |\n| Initial public offering | Perhaps the only way to round up a large bundle of money | Dilutes the entrepreneur and earlier investors; public-company duties are onerous |\n\n**Financing instruments across growth and maturity** (pp. 115–128)\n\n| Instrument | Typical user | Term | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Demand loan / seasonal line / equipment loan | Growth-stage small firm, local bank | Short | What banks prefer to offer small firms |\n| Asset-based loan or lease | Growing firm with a track record | Medium | Pledge assets as collateral; move upstream to a money-center bank once you outgrow the local one |\n| SBA-guaranteed loan | US small business | Varies | See box below |\n| Online lender / peer-to-peer | Small firms shut out of banks | Short | Kabbage, OnDeck, Funding Circle; Lending Club, Prosper |\n| Term loan | Mature, creditworthy | Medium/long | From banks, insurance companies, pension funds; existing assets and cash flow as collateral lower the cost |\n| Sale-and-leaseback | Mature | Long | Sell an owned asset, lease it back, release significant funds |\n| Commercial paper | Large corporations with high credit ratings | 2–270 days | Short-term debt security sold at a discount, redeemed at face value; cheaper than short-term bank borrowing; bought by investors with idle cash |\n| Bonds | Mature, public-market access | 1–30 years | IOU with fixed rate, usually paid semiannually, redeemed at face on maturity |\n| Preferred stock | Mature | Perpetual | Equity paying a stated annual dividend; trades like a bond with rates and issuer credit |\n| Common stock (IPO, follow-on) | Exceptional growth firms | Permanent | Via investment bank + syndicate; opens the door to individuals, mutual funds, pension funds |\n\n**Box — SBA loans** (p. 119)\nThe SBA runs three loan programs for small businesses owned by US citizens. It does not lend; it\nsets guidelines and its partners (lenders, community development organisations, microlenders)\nmake the loans. The SBA guarantees repayment up to certain levels, removing part of the lender's\nrisk; post-2008 legislation made the loans more attractive to both sides. Details at sba.gov.\nNon-US note: look for the local government-guaranteed small-business loan scheme (e.g. the UK's\nStart Up Loans / British Business Bank guarantees).\n\n**Why growth tapers — four causes** (p. 122)\n1. Success and profitability draw competitors into the market.\n2. Demand is largely satisfied (market saturation).\n3. A shift in the technology of the product — or the technology customers use.\n4. The larger organisation loses ambition, agility or the ability to innovate.\n\n**Table 7-2 — eBay's cash flow, 1998–2000 (thousands of dollars)** (p. 125)\n\n| | 2000 | 1999 | 1998 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Net cash provided by operating activities | 100,148 | 62,852 | 6,041 |\n| Net cash used in investing activities | (206,054) | (603,363) | (53,024) |\n| Net cash provided by financing activities | 85,978 | 725,027 | 72,159 |\n| Net increase (decrease) in cash and equivalents | (19,928) | 184,516 | 25,176 |\n| Cash and equivalents at year end | 201,873 | 221,801 | 37,285 |\n\n**The matching principle** (p. 128): finance current assets with short-term financing and\nlong-term assets with long-term or permanent financing. Trade credit against inventory that sells\nin 60 days is a match; office space, systems and equipment against long-term debt or\nshareholders' capital is a match; municipalities fund bridges and stadiums with 20–30 year bonds\nthat roughly match the assets' productive life."},{"ref":"07-growth-financing.md","label":"Growth financing (handbook ch 7)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **Growth speed picks the instrument**: slow/moderate → bank debt; likely to grow large quickly\n  → equity.\n- **Start with a small community bank or credit union** — higher approval and satisfaction than\n  big banks or online lenders. Most small firms borrow small: 54% hold under $100,000.\n- **Expect short-term, secured, single-purpose loans**; do not plan on a long-term unsecured\n  bank loan as a young company.\n- **Current ratio target depends on cash reliability**: ~1.10 can be fine for a utility- or\n  taxi-like cash cycle; long production cycles need a larger cushion.\n- **Creditors don't take payment in used delivery trucks.** A company rich in buildings, fleets\n  and inventory can still go insolvent if quick assets can't cover bills as they fall due.\n- **TIE stress test**: if EBIT were cut in half by a recession, could you still cover interest?\n- **Deductibility is worthless without taxable income** — young loss-making firms get no debt\n  tax shield.\n- **Leverage cuts both ways**: higher debt/assets or debt/equity → higher owner returns when\n  profitable, but interest is contractual and can force bankruptcy when cash flow falls.\n- **Watch covenant limits**: lenders write maximum debt levels into loans and call them if\n  breached.\n- **Online lenders**: by 2015 ~20% of US small-employer firms had applied; most were approved\n  for some credit but reported lower satisfaction than with a small bank or credit union.\n- **Fast growers pay no dividends** — plow everything back (eBay returned \"not even a cent\").\n- **When operating cash covers less than the investment outflow** (eBay 2000: slightly under\n  half), the gap must come from external financing.\n- **Outgrown your local bank?** Move upstream to a large money-center bank.\n- **Mature-company timing**: sell bonds when interest rates are low, sell shares when prices\n  are high.\n- **Growth never fully stops**: a $1B company growing 3% still needs financing; few companies\n  sustain high growth for more than a decade.\n- **Never fund a long-term asset with a short balloon loan** (refinance risk) **and never fund a\n  short-lived asset with long debt** (payments outlast the benefit).\n- **Every borrowed dollar must buy assets whose incremental revenue or savings exceed the\n  financing cost** over the period — the same test applies to owners' capital."},{"ref":"07-growth-financing.md","label":"Growth financing (handbook ch 7)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"Preparing a bank-loan application (built from the banker's three questions):\n1. Demonstrate you understand the market and have a feasible plan for satisfying it.\n2. Show the experience or knowledge (or both) needed to operate this type of business.\n3. Present a business plan that is realistic, complete and based on reasonable assumptions.\n4. Provide conservative revenue and cost projections — above all projected cash flow, because\n   repayments come from cash flow.\n5. If already operating, compute and present the current, acid-test, debt, debt-to-equity and\n   times-interest-earned ratios.\n6. Assemble evidence of credit history (bills paid on schedule).\n7. List pledgeable collateral (current and fixed assets); consider an SBA-style guarantee.\n\nThe growth-financing ladder (eBay's sequence, pp. 123–127):\n1. Self-finance from operating cash flow.\n2. VC private placement.\n3. IPO.\n4. Follow-on public offering while the stock is high.\n5. (Maturity) bank lines, term loans, commercial paper, bonds, preferred stock, sale-and-leaseback."},{"ref":"07-growth-financing.md","label":"Growth financing (handbook ch 7)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"Banker's question 1 — ability to repay (near-verbatim, p. 116):\n- Does the applicant understand the market and have a feasible plan for satisfying it?\n- Does the entrepreneur have the experience or knowledge — or both — required to operate this\n  type of business?\n- Is the business plan realistic, complete and based on reasonable assumptions?\n- Are the plan's revenue and cost projections realistic and conservative? Is projected cash flow\n  sufficient to service the loan?\n\nLoan-readiness checklist:\n- Credit history clean (personal and business bills paid on time)?\n- Collateral list with resale values: cash, inventory, receivables, vehicles, buildings, equipment?\n- Government guarantee (SBA or local equivalent) explored?\n- Applying to a community bank / credit union before big banks or online lenders?\n- Loan type matches the need: seasonal line for working capital, equipment loan for machinery,\n  demand loan for short bridges?\n\nDebt-capacity worksheet:\n- Current assets ___ ÷ current liabilities ___ = current ratio ___\n- Quick assets ___ ÷ current liabilities ___ = acid-test ratio ___\n- Total debt ___ ÷ total assets ___ = debt ratio ___\n- Total liabilities ___ ÷ owners' equity ___ = debt-to-equity ___\n- EBIT ___ ÷ interest expense ___ = times-interest-earned ___; with EBIT halved: ___\n- Covenant limits on any existing loan: ___\n- Can the business survive a bad year at this interest load?\n\nAsset–financing matching audit:\n- For each major asset: useful life ___; funding source ___; term of that funding ___.\n- Flag: long-lived asset on short money (balloon/refinance risk)? Consumable on long money?\n- Will the asset's incremental revenue or savings exceed its financing cost over the period?\n\nMaturity-phase financing menu (for a creditworthy company):\n- Short-term need: commercial paper, bank line of credit, term loan (bank, insurer, pension fund).\n- Lower the cost: pledge existing assets and cash flow; sale-and-leaseback.\n- Public markets: bonds (when rates are low), stock (when prices are high).\n- Strategic events with financing implications: mergers, acquisitions, restructuring."},{"ref":"07-growth-financing.md","label":"Growth financing (handbook ch 7)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **eBay (1995–2000)** — Omidyar started on a free website with a filing cabinet, a school desk\n  and a laptop; he and Jeff Skoll paid themselves $25,000 a year; early growth was self-financed\n  from transaction fees; by December 2000: 22M registered users, 8,000+ categories, 6M auction and\n  8M fixed-price listings a day; product development spend $4.6M (1998) → $24.8M (1999) → $55.9M\n  (2000); FY2000 balance sheet: cash $202M, short-term investments $354M, long-term investments\n  $218M, total assets $1,182M — growth financed first by operating cash flow, then mostly by\n  selling shares.\n- **Benchmark Capital** — $5M private placement for 22% of eBay; after the 1998 IPO its stake\n  showed a 49,000% return — what early equity costs a founder, and why VCs chase it.\n- **Pierre Omidyar** — held 44M+ shares pre-IPO; the offering and run-up made him a billionaire\n  four times over; a 1999 follow-on netted eBay over $700M for expansion.\n- **The $200,000 home on a 3-year loan** — 8% nonamortizing: $16,000 a year in interest, then the\n  full $200,000 due in year three; refinancing depends on finding a lender and on rates not\n  jumping to 10–12% — two \"ifs\" that end in foreclosure. Businesses die the same way.\n- **Second mortgage for a dream vacation** — the vacation ends, the payments go on — borrowing\n  long for a short-lived asset.\n- **Asset-rich, cash-poor company** — office buildings, delivery fleets, warehouses full of stock,\n  and still insolvent because quick assets can't meet bills."},{"ref":"07-growth-financing.md","label":"Growth financing (handbook ch 7)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Over-leveraging so that contractual interest crushes the business in a downturn.\n- Counting on the interest tax shield before the business has taxable income.\n- Mistaking a big asset base for liquidity.\n- Breaching loan covenants and having the loan called in.\n- Financing long-term assets with short-term money, or short-lived spending with long-term debt.\n- Expecting VC or an IPO when most companies fail or are acquired before either.\n- Applying only to large banks (or only online) and skipping community banks.\n- Paying dividends during hypergrowth instead of reinvesting."},{"ref":"07-growth-financing.md","label":"Growth financing (handbook ch 7)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`current ratio` — current assets ÷ current liabilities; short-term bill-paying ability.\n`acid-test (quick) ratio` — quick assets (cash, marketable securities, receivables) ÷ current liabilities; liquidity without inventory.\n`collateral` — an asset pledged to the lender until the loan is satisfied.\n`leverage` — the degree to which a company is funded by liabilities and long-term debt rather than owners' capital.\n`debt ratio` — total debt ÷ total assets.\n`debt-to-equity ratio` — total liabilities ÷ owners' equity.\n`times-interest-earned ratio` — EBIT ÷ interest expense; how many times pretax earnings cover interest.\n`EBIT` — earnings before interest and taxes.\n`private placement` — sale of shares to a private investor (e.g. a VC) rather than the public.\n`IPO` — initial public offering; the transition from private to public company, managed by investment banks.\n`investment bank / syndicate` — the firm(s) and broker-dealers that run a share issue and reach investors.\n`money-center bank` — a large bank a company graduates to after outgrowing its local lender.\n`commercial paper` — short-term (2–270 day) debt security sold at a discount by highly rated corporations.\n`bond` — fixed-rate debt security with a stated maturity (1–30 years), interest usually semiannual.\n`preferred stock` — equity paying a stated annual dividend; prices move like bonds.\n`sale-and-leaseback` — selling an owned asset and leasing it back to release cash.\n`peer-to-peer lending` — loans funded by individuals through networks such as Lending Club and Prosper.\n`cash-flow statement` — totals cash entering and leaving through operations, investing and financing (see appendix A)."},{"ref":"07-growth-financing.md","label":"Growth financing (handbook ch 7)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- Growth-phase entrepreneurs draw on internally generated cash flow, asset-based loans and\n  external equity.\n- Bankers look at ability to repay, character and collateral before lending.\n- The current, acid-test and times-interest-earned ratios give lenders insight into repayment ability.\n- Debt is generally the lowest-cost capital because interest is tax-deductible, but it makes the\n  enterprise riskier.\n- An IPO is a major milestone for the few firms that reach it and a major cash infusion for growth.\n- Maturity-phase financing for creditworthy companies includes bank loans, commercial paper,\n  bonds and stock.\n- Finance short-term assets with short-term money, long-term assets with long-term debt or\n  shareholders' contributions."},{"ref":"08-angels-and-vc.md","label":"Angels and venture capital (handbook ch 8)","heading":"Purpose","text":"Which outside equity source, if any, fits a venture — angel investors or venture capital — how to\nfind and pitch them, what they take in return (ownership, convertible preferred stock, board\ninfluence) and what it really costs. Load when a user asks \"should I raise money\", \"angel vs VC\",\n\"how do I find investors\", \"what is a term sheet / preferred stock / dilution\", or when the journey\nreaches the seed, Series A or Series B step."},{"ref":"08-angels-and-vc.md","label":"Angels and venture capital (handbook ch 8)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- Most businesses never need outside equity: internally generated cash plus loans can grow them to\n  a size the founders find satisfactory, with no ownership sold. Only firms with broader growth\n  opportunities must seek outside equity, because debt and internal cash rarely fund that growth (p. 131).\n- Equity capital buys ownership rights, a share of future fortunes and, in most cases, a voice in\n  how the business is run. Accepting it means ceding some measure of control (p. 131).\n- Silicon Valley vocabulary: **seed stage** (startup phase) → **Series A** (gearing up to release\n  the first product; often angels) → **Series B** (continued growth; probably VC) (p. 132).\n- Angels, not VCs, are the realistic source for most founders: fewer than 1% of US companies have\n  ever raised VC; angels fund 16× as many companies and the VC share is shrinking (p. 132).\n- Angels are high-net-worth, usually self-made businesspeople or professionals investing their own\n  money as debt, equity or both; they also bring advice, feedback and local networks (pp. 132–134).\n- VCs are high-risk investors who take a significant ownership stake plus a board seat in\n  high-potential companies, expect ~10× within 5–10 years, and harvest at IPO or sale (pp. 136–141).\n- Both audiences weigh **people over idea**: founder credibility, character, trustworthiness and\n  coachability outrank competence and traction (pp. 136, 138, 144).\n- VC is the most expensive capital you can use — especially if you succeed (eBay) — and it comes\n  with loss of control; self-fund or delay wherever you can (pp. 145–147).\n- The more solid and less desperate the business when you negotiate, the better the terms (p. 147)."},{"ref":"08-angels-and-vc.md","label":"Angels and venture capital (handbook ch 8)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**Funding-round vocabulary** (p. 132)\n\n| Round | When | Typical source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Seed | Startup phase of fund-raising | Founders; friends and relatives (considered before angels) |\n| Series A | Gearing up to release the first product | Angel investors — the first stage of serious equity |\n| Series B | Company keeps growing and needs scale capital | Venture capital |\n\n**Angels vs. VCs at a glance** (compiled from pp. 132–143)\n\n| | Angel investors | Venture capitalists |\n|---|---|---|\n| Who | High-net-worth individuals, usually successful businesspeople/professionals; often self-made millionaires investing their own money | Firms structured as limited partnerships investing other people's money (pension funds, endowments, corporations, insurers, wealthy individuals) |\n| Round | Series A | Series B |\n| Fit | Firms too small for VCs, too limited in revenue at maturity, or too risky for banks and most VCs; any industry, any region | Risky firms with proven management and substantial growth potential, mostly tech-linked (software, hardware, biotech, medical devices, media/entertainment); often pre-product; IPO-bound |\n| Scale (2014) | ~$24.1B across 73,400 deals | ~$48.3B across 4,356 deals |\n| Instrument | Debt, equity or both | Convertible preferred stock (or convertible debt with warrants) |\n| What they take | An ownership interest; offer advice and a local network | A significant % of ownership plus a board seat; a role in strategic management; may hold enough control to fire you |\n| Return goal | Exceptional returns; want a credible exit | ~10× within 5–10 years; harvest at IPO/acquisition after 4–5 years |\n| Visibility | Don't advertise; keep activity inside trusted circles | Highly visible; hunt deals at MIT labs, Stanford, accelerators/incubators, Silicon Valley, Research Triangle, San Diego biotech |\n| Decision style | Solo angels decide fast; angel groups are more professional but more bureaucratic and slower | Syndicates: the lead investor does due diligence and takes the board seat; others contribute smaller passive cheques |\n| How to find | AngelList; Angel Capital Association directory; referrals via lawyer, accountant, entrepreneurs, patent attorneys | Pratt's Guide; VCgate; Forbes Midas List; top accelerators; entrepreneurial forums; warm intros |\n| What sways them | The people: founder profile (school, prestigious prior employers), coachability, character over competence | A first-rate person with a good idea beats a good idea with second-rate management; \"to the moon\" potential |\n\n**The three kinds of firms angels finance** (p. 133)\n1. Too small to get the attention of VCs.\n2. Too limited in revenue potential at maturity to interest VCs.\n3. Too risky for bank loans and for most VC appetites.\n\n**What angels actually decide on** (Bernstein, Korteweg & Laws study of AngelList, p. 136)\n- Ranking of signals: **founders' profiles** (school, previous work at prestigious firms) > initial\n  traction > initial investors.\n- Credibility hypothesis: if a Harvard MBA is betting a career on this rather than other attractive\n  options, there must be something to it.\n- Related research: angels weigh **coachability**, and **trustworthiness and character over competence**.\n\n**Figure 8-1 — The flow of venture capital** (Bygrave & Timmons, p. 140)\n\n| Actor | Role | Receives |\n|---|---|---|\n| Limited partners — pension funds, individuals, corporations, insurance companies, foreign investors, endowments | Provide most of the capital; venture is a small slice of their portfolios | Return of principal + 75–85% of capital gain |\n| Gatekeepers | Intermediaries between LPs and funds | 1% annual fee |\n| VC firm (general partner: new MBAs, securities lawyers, experienced deal makers) | Identify and screen opportunities; transact and close deals; monitor and add value; raise additional funds | 2–3% annual fee + 15–25% of capital gains |\n| Portfolio companies (entrepreneurs) | Opportunity creation/recognition and execution; value creation; harvest | Equity capital; return cash to the fund via IPOs, mergers, alliances |\n\n**Portfolio math** (p. 140): a VC diversifies across many deals knowing some will fail, others only\nbreak even, and maybe **1 in 15** is the bonanza. Syndicates spread the bets further.\n\n**Convertible preferred stock — what the VC gets** (pp. 140–141)\n\n| Feature | Meaning for the founder |\n|---|---|\n| Voting rights | The VC gains a measure of control over the enterprise and its officers |\n| Conversion right | VC may convert to common at its discretion, at 1:1 or another stipulated ratio — so it is really taking ownership |\n| Cumulative dividends | Must be paid before any dividend reaches common shareholders |\n| Liquidation preference | Preferred stands ahead of common if the company is liquidated — downside protection |\n| Net effect | \"Best of both worlds\": protection if you fail, full upside if you succeed |\n| Common alternative | Convertible debt with warrants |\n\n**The three things a VC does after investing** (p. 141)\n1. Monitors the progress of its portfolio companies.\n2. Uses its network of contacts to strengthen their technical and management teams.\n3. Shapes company plans and strategies through its influence on their boards.\n\n**Balachandra's three presentation rules** (Babson research, p. 144)\n\n| Rule | Why |\n|---|---|\n| Maintain a calm demeanor | Professional funders equate equanimity with leadership strength; passion helps only with less formal sources |\n| Build trust | They assess character more than competence — skills are teachable or hirable, personality barely changes; projected trustworthiness raised funding odds by 10% |\n| Listen actively; show openness to new ideas | Early-stage investors want to coach and mould you; ego is a red flag |\n\n**Mullins' four downsides of venture capital** (p. 145)\n1. **Distraction from your day job** — fund-raising is another full-time job.\n[section truncated]"},{"ref":"08-angels-and-vc.md","label":"Angels and venture capital (handbook ch 8)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **Don't sell equity if you don't have to.** If internal cash plus loans can reach the size you\n  want, stay self-funded (p. 131).\n- **VC only for \"to the moon\" ventures.** If you cannot credibly return ~10× in 5–10 years, the\n  VC search will be fruitless (pp. 137–138); don't pursue VC unless you have *all* the characteristics\n  they look for (p. 147).\n- **Plan on angels.** <1% of US companies raise VC; angels fund 16× more companies; ~300,000 US\n  angels put ~$25B into 71,000+ ventures in 2015 (pp. 132–134).\n- **Angels are getting pickier.** Deal count dipped in 2015–16 as selectivity rose — arrive with\n  your act together (p. 135).\n- **Angel investing is growing abroad**: Europe's market doubled 2011–16; Canada's tripled (p. 134).\n- **AngelList gate**: post your profile only once at least a third (better, half) of your target is\n  committed by quality investors; their due diligence draws the rest (Baehr & Loomis, p. 134).\n  Startups raise >$10M a month through the platform.\n- **Local or regional business → find a local angel** (p. 134).\n- **Target angels whose wealth came from your industry**: a Sun Microsystems founder wrote Google a\n  $100,000 cheque after a prelaunch demo because he could see the technology's potential; a\n  medical-device startup should get the word out to local physicians (p. 135).\n- **Angel groups**: easier to find and contact, deeper expertise and more money, but more\n  bureaucratic and slower than solo angels (p. 135).\n- **Lead time**: line up venture money **6–8 months** before it is needed; never wait until you\n  really need the cash (p. 142).\n- **Send the summary, not the plan**: executive summary, YouTube pitch link or brief deck; the full\n  plan only when asked — investors don't have time to read it (p. 142).\n- **Blind emails ≈ blind résumés**: you are one of thousands; get a personal meeting or a\n  recommendation from someone the VC respects (p. 142).\n- **Accelerators**: graduates of *top* programmes raise VC faster; not true across the board — be\n  selective (p. 142).\n- **Forum pitch = 5–10 minutes per VC table**: bring a brief, compelling elevator speech and be\n  totally prepared (pp. 142–143).\n- **Choose team, advisers, board and attorney partly for their financier connections**; all else\n  equal, pick board members with personal ties to financiers (p. 143).\n- **Calm beats passion** with professional funders (p. 144).\n- **Trustworthiness is worth +10% odds** of being funded (p. 144).\n- **VC economics**: 1-in-15 bonanza thinking; LPs get principal + 75–85% of gains; GPs get 15–25% of\n  gains + a 2–3% annual fee; gatekeepers 1% (p. 140).\n- **Harvest horizon**: VCs typically exit after 4–5 years via IPO or sale (p. 141).\n- **IPO ambition → a VC is almost essential** for the sophistication, connections and terms (p. 137).\n- **Always get your own valuation** before accepting the VC's number (p. 146).\n- **Read the legal agreement, not just the term sheet** — the details may be worse than the summary (p. 145).\n- **Negotiate from strength**: a viable business that isn't desperate gets much better terms and\n  keeps more of the company and control (p. 147)."},{"ref":"08-angels-and-vc.md","label":"Angels and venture capital (handbook ch 8)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"**A. Finding local angels — the referral chain** (pp. 134–135)\n1. Join AngelList (subject to the one-third/one-half gate) and check the Angel Capital Association's\n   directory of angel groups and platforms by region.\n2. Work into the local angel network through your lawyer, accountant and entrepreneur\n   acquaintances; talk with patent attorneys and share your business plan.\n3. Ask successful local entrepreneurs: \"Whom should I approach about private financing?\"\n4. If that person cannot help, ask them: \"Do you know anyone who might want to invest in my company?\"\n5. Follow every lead until you connect with the right person.\n\n**B. Persuading an angel** (pp. 135–136)\n1. Target angels in professions related to your enterprise.\n2. Have your act together: a working prototype, a well-managed lean operation, or at minimum a\n   rock-solid business plan.\n3. Prepare a well-rehearsed, right-to-the-point verbal presentation that explains clearly and\n   specifically how the money will fuel profitable growth.\n4. Offer a credible exit plan — angels want paper ownership converted to real money.\n5. Focus on the team: founders' profiles, coachability, trustworthiness and character.\n\n**C. The venture-capital process** (pp. 139–141)\n1. Limited partners commit capital to the VC partnership.\n2. The VC firm identifies, screens and closes deals; it forms a syndicate in which the lead does due\n   diligence and takes the board seat.\n3. Capital arrives as convertible preferred stock (or convertible debt with warrants).\n4. Post-investment: monitor progress; use the network to strengthen teams; shape strategy via the board.\n5. Harvest at IPO or sale, usually after 4–5 years; proceeds split between LPs and GPs per the\n   partnership terms.\n\n**D. Connecting with VCs** (pp. 141–143)\n1. Start 6–8 months before the cash is needed.\n2. Shortlist firms that specialise in your industry (Pratt's Guide, VCgate, Midas List) and read\n   each firm's application instructions.\n3. Email the executive summary, pitch-video link or brief deck — not the whole plan; an intrigued\n   VC will ask for the thorough version.\n4. Work for a warm route: a top accelerator; entrepreneurial forums (5–10-minute table pitches);\n   team members, board members or an attorney the VC knows and respects.\n5. On any contact, ask for an office meeting to pitch; deliver the highlights, hold details for questions.\n\n**E. Making the presentation** (pp. 144–145)\n1. Assume they have read your materials — the meeting is to answer questions, assuage concerns and\n   let them get to know you.\n2. Stay calm; project trustworthiness; listen actively and show openness to coaching.\n3. Rehearse until you have it down cold; convey control of the facts and confidence in the future.\n4. Anticipate pointed questions and prepare rock-solid answers for each.\n\n**F. If you would rather not raise** (pp. 138–139): bootstrap → pace growth to financing → stay\ntightfisted → outsource non-essentials."},{"ref":"08-angels-and-vc.md","label":"Angels and venture capital (handbook ch 8)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Do I need outside equity at all?**\n- Can internal cash plus loans grow the business to a size I would find satisfactory?\n- Is my growth opportunity so broad that debt and internal cash cannot fund it?\n- Am I willing to give an outsider ownership, a share of future profits and a voice in decisions?\n- Have I exhausted bootstrapping, growth pacing, cost discipline and outsourcing?\n- Could customers fund the growth (the GoViral model)?\n\n**Angel-readiness checklist**\n- [ ] Solid business plan and the know-how to launch and operate the company.\n- [ ] Working prototype, lean operation, or a rock-solid plan.\n- [ ] Rehearsed, right-to-the-point verbal pitch stating exactly how the money will be used.\n- [ ] Credible exit plan for investors.\n- [ ] Team story: schools, prior employers, evidence of coachability and character.\n- [ ] Target list of angels whose wealth came from my industry or region.\n- [ ] Referral chain started (lawyer, accountant, entrepreneurs, patent attorneys).\n- [ ] AngelList profile ready, posted only once a third to a half is committed.\n\n**VC-fit checklist — do I have *all* of these?**\n- [ ] Potential to return ~10× within 5–10 years.\n- [ ] Proven management (first-rate person plus a good idea).\n- [ ] Substantial growth potential in a VC-favoured industry (software, hardware, biotech, medical\n      devices, media/entertainment) or an equally scalable one.\n- [ ] Willing to accept significant dilution, a board seat and strategic involvement.\n- [ ] Comfortable with a 4–5-year harvest via IPO or sale.\n- [ ] Aligned with a fast flip rather than a long private build.\n- [ ] Started 6–8 months ahead of need.\n\n**Pitch-meeting prep**\n- [ ] Executive summary / video link / brief deck sent per the firm's instructions.\n- [ ] Elevator speech (the opportunity and how the team will exploit it) for 5–10-minute forum slots.\n- [ ] Presentation customisable for length and audience; highlights first, details on demand.\n- [ ] Rehearsed cold; calm delivery; trust signals; active listening.\n- [ ] List of anticipated pointed questions with rock-solid answers.\n- [ ] Well-connected team member, board member or attorney to secure the intro.\n\n**Questions to ask a VC before signing** (pp. 145–147)\n- How exactly did you arrive at the valuation? (Then commission your own professional valuation.)\n- What ownership percentage does the instrument imply after conversion, and at what ratio?\n- What voting rights, cumulative dividends and liquidation preferences come with the preferred stock?\n- Which term-sheet lines will expand into onerous legal detail?\n- Who takes the board seat and what control rights follow — including the power to replace me?\n- What is your exit horizon, and does it match my plan to stay private and build long-term?\n\n**Networking script**\n- \"Whom should I approach about private financing?\"\n- \"Do you know anyone who might want to invest in my company?\""},{"ref":"08-angels-and-vc.md","label":"Angels and venture capital (handbook ch 8)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Jack, Minneapolis** (p. 133) — 62-year-old owner of a $43M-revenue trucking and maintenance\n  company, minority stakes in two local firms, invests locally in what he understands, often with\n  two wealthy friends (\"Three minds are better than one\") — the typical angel is a self-made local\n  operator, not a Valley tech investor.\n- **Google / Sun Microsystems founder** (p. 135) — a $100,000 cheque after a prelaunch demo because\n  he grasped the technology — target angels from your industry.\n- **Google, Amazon, Starbucks, PayPal** (p. 133) — all began with angel money.\n- **Arthur Rock / Apple** (p. 138) — $1.5M invested, valued at $100M at the IPO three years later —\n  the payoff VCs live for; without that potential, VC won't bite.\n- **eBay / Benchmark** (pp. 145–146) — $6.7M for 22% in 1997; a bank would have earned ~$2M in\n  interest over three years, Benchmark made more than $2B; the CEO recruiting it provided could be\n  bought for under $200K and IPO advice for a consulting fee — VC is most expensive precisely when you succeed.\n- **GoViral** (p. 139) — sold for $97M in 2011 with zero investment capital — customer cash can fund\n  a category leader.\n- **Corporate VC** (p. 137) — 1,068 → 1,501 US corporate VCs, $75B invested — a growing but\n  increasingly return-driven source."},{"ref":"08-angels-and-vc.md","label":"Angels and venture capital (handbook ch 8)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Chasing VC without VC-grade potential or the full profile VCs demand.\n- Sending the full business plan, or blind emails, to VCs.\n- Waiting until cash is critical instead of starting 6–8 months ahead.\n- Posting on AngelList before anchor investors have committed.\n- Pitching with passion instead of calm; letting ego signal uncoachability.\n- Treating the pitch as a first exposure rather than a Q&A on materials already read.\n- Accepting the VC's valuation without an independent assessment.\n- Signing a legal agreement whose details are worse than the term sheet.\n- Underestimating control loss: preferred voting rights, board influence, the power to fire the\n  founder, pressure to flip via IPO or sale (\"venture vultures\").\n- Negotiating from desperation.\n- Assuming any accelerator will open VC doors.\n- Letting fund-raising swallow the day job."},{"ref":"08-angels-and-vc.md","label":"Angels and venture capital (handbook ch 8)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`seed stage` — the startup phase of fund-raising.\n`Series A` — the round raised while gearing up to release the first product; often angels.\n`Series B` — the growth round after Series A; probably venture capital.\n`angel investor` — a high-net-worth individual, usually a successful businessperson, providing early-stage capital as debt, equity or both.\n`angel group / network` — a formal organisation of angels with professional screeners; more depth and capital, slower decisions.\n`venture capital (VC)` — an individual or firm seeking large capital gains through early-stage equity or equity-linked financing of high-potential firms.\n`corporate venture capital` — a large company investing in startups for strategic insight and acquisition options, increasingly for returns too.\n`limited partner (LP)` — passive capital provider to a VC fund (pension fund, endowment, corporation, insurer, wealthy individual).\n`general partner (GP)` — the VC firm that manages the fund and invests; earns a 2–3% annual fee plus 15–25% of gains.\n`gatekeeper` — intermediary between LPs and funds; ~1% annual fee.\n`portfolio company` — a firm a VC has invested in.\n`syndicate` — a group of VC firms co-investing; the lead investor does due diligence and takes the board seat.\n`due diligence` — the investor's investigation of the company before committing.\n`convertible preferred stock` — preferred shares with voting rights, cumulative dividends, liquidation priority and the right to convert to common at a set ratio.\n`conversion ratio` — the stipulated preferred-to-common exchange rate (e.g. 1:1).\n`cumulative dividends` — dividends owed to preferred holders that accrue and must be paid before any common dividend.\n`liquidation preference` — preferred holders are paid before common holders if the company is wound up.\n`convertible debt with warrants` — a loan convertible into equity, packaged with rights to buy shares; the common alternative to convertible preferred.\n`harvest` — when the VC cashes out via IPO or sale of the company, typically after 4–5 years.\n`dilution` — the reduction in the founders' ownership share when equity is issued to investors.\n`term sheet` — the concise summary of deal terms that later expands into the detailed legal agreement.\n`valuation` — the estimated value of the firm that determines what share a given investment buys; part science, part art.\n`bootstrap financing` — funding growth from internal cash and frugality rather than outside equity."},{"ref":"08-angels-and-vc.md","label":"Angels and venture capital (handbook ch 8)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- The most likely source of outside venture funding is angel investors — high-net-worth individuals\n  providing early-stage capital to startups.\n- Networking is often the best way to connect with angels.\n- Venture capital comes from an individual or firm seeking large capital gains through early-stage\n  equity or equity-linked financing of high-potential enterprises.\n- Don't waste time pursuing VC unless you have all the characteristics VCs look for.\n- Most venture capital takes the form of convertible preferred stock or something similar, such as\n  convertible debt with warrants.\n- Venture capital is nice to have but costly — economically and in loss of control.\n- When pitching an angel or VC, choose calm over passion and build trust in your character and coachability."},{"ref":"09-going-public.md","label":"Going public (handbook ch 9)","heading":"Purpose","text":"Should a growing company raise money through an IPO, is it even a candidate, what does the\ndeal involve, and what is the alternative? Load when a user asks about \"going public\", \"IPO\",\n\"listing\", \"underwriters\", \"private placement\", or when a late-stage business (~$100M revenue,\nprofitable quarters) is weighing its next financing. Fewer than 5% of founders will ever need\nthis directly (p. 149) — for everyone else it sets expectations and points to alternatives."},{"ref":"09-going-public.md","label":"Going public (handbook ch 9)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- An IPO swaps paper ownership shares for hard cash from individual and institutional investors\n  (pension funds, mutual funds). It signals the company has \"made it\" and makes it accountable\n  to a far wider set of stakeholders, analysts and regulators (p. 149).\n- Conventional bar: **~$100M annualized revenue plus several consecutive profitable quarters**.\n  Only a tiny fraction of startups get there; \"an IPO is a pipe dream for all but a few\".\n- Post-WWII norm: prove a record of sales and earnings first. Exception: proprietary technology\n  plus a tested management team. The dot-com boom let \"clever idea\" companies float; many\n  failed and the hangover still shapes the process (p. 153).\n- Ability to launch = **investor mood and expectations × ability to meet regulatory requirements**.\n- Public status is a mixed blessing: cash, liquidity and stability versus expense, management\n  time, scrutiny, dilution and pressure for short-term results.\n- Successful IPO companies **act like public companies long before the deal** (Ernst & Young).\n- Timing is a judgement: too soon and you under-exploit potential; too late and you miss the\n  bullish window. VC-backed companies now stay private longer (Figure 9-1), partly because\n  Sarbanes-Oxley (2002) made being public more expensive.\n- You cannot do this in-house: an investment bank (an agent and deal-maker, not a deposit-taking\n  lender) gets you through registration and distributes the shares for 6–10% of the offering price.\n- Insiders do not get full liquidity on IPO day — US rules and lockup agreements restrict\n  insider sales.\n- If an IPO is infeasible or unwanted, a **private placement** (or selling the company — covered\n  in a later chapter) brings large capital without SEC registration or public scrutiny.\n- Non-US note: written for US SEC rules; a UK founder would look at LSE Main Market or AIM (FCA\n  prospectus rules, a Nominated Adviser on AIM), a Nigerian one at NGX / SEC Nigeria — same\n  logic, local rulebook."},{"ref":"09-going-public.md","label":"Going public (handbook ch 9)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**Table — Pros and cons of going public (pp. 150–152)**\n\n| Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|\n| Personal wealth for founders, key employees and early investors — and (restricted) liquidity for it | **IPO expense**: legal, accounting, printing, advisory, SEC and state filing fees, exchange fees, underwriter commission, process upgrades — expect **~$2M out of pocket, up to $100M** on large deals |\n| Pay off costly interest-bearing debt | **Management time and attention**: months of preparation, the road show, then ongoing investor and analyst inquiries; may need an investor relations manager |\n| Financial capacity to develop new products and the marketing to sell them | **Public scrutiny**: results and executive pay are public; the 10-K hands competitors key suppliers, product-development plans and strategy |\n| Improved debt-to-equity ratio → debt on better terms later | **Loss of control**: founder and managers usually lose a major portion of ownership; institutions hold blocks, thousands of small holders own the rest |\n| Cash plus marketable stock as currency for strategic acquisitions | **Pressure for short-term gain**: analysts expect predictable year-to-year earnings; long-term moves get deferred |\n| Financial stability that attracts talent, suppliers and joint-venture partners | |\n| Opens the door to future stock and bond rounds | |\n\n**The IPO candidate profile — seven characteristics (pp. 153–154)**\n1. **Reasonable deal size** — given the cost, there is little point seeking less than **$10M**, and you need a solid plan for using it.\n2. **Evidence of growth** — double-digit sales growth with earnings growing faster (or all signs pointing to substantial profitability ahead); enough to support a **P/E multiple above the historical S&P 500 or Russell 2000**.\n3. **Outstanding products or services that are difficult to copy.**\n4. **A credible CEO** who can communicate the vision to cautious outsiders.\n5. **At least three years of audited financial statements** (a \"look-back\" audit works if records are solid).\n6. **High-quality employees.**\n7. **A logical growth strategy and a predictable revenue stream.**\n\n**Hallmarks of \"already acting public\" (Ernst & Young study of successful IPOs, p. 154)** —\npre-IPO improvements in: employee incentive programs; strategic planning; internal controls;\nfinancial accounting and reporting; executive compensation; investor relations policies.\nInvestors were buying a firm that already looked professionally managed.\n\n**Figure 9-1 — Waiting game (p. 156)**: median time from founding to IPO for VC-backed companies\n(y-axis 0–8 years, 1995–2015) climbs steadily across the two decades; higher Sarbanes-Oxley\nrequirements are partly responsible. Reference points from the box: Netscape 16 months; Google\n~6 years; Facebook 8 years; Pure Storage 6 years and six private rounds.\n\n**What the investment banker does for its 6–10% (pp. 164–165)**\n1. **Gets the regulatory act together** — steers the stringent hurdles of issuing securities, including developing the prospectus (preliminary form = the *red herring*: company, business, finances, use of proceeds).\n2. **Sets the price** — no market-clearing price exists yet; the company wants the highest price the market will bear, investors expect a bargain versus seasoned securities; the banker mediates.\n3. **Arranges distribution** — assembles a syndicate of broker-dealers, typically buys the shares from the issuer at a set price, marks them up, and sells through its own and partners' channels to pension funds, mutual funds and individuals — i.e. it *underwrites* the selling risk.\n\n**Pricing inputs at step 6 (p. 160)**: the range printed in the prospectus (e.g. $15–$20);\nobserved demand; prices comparable companies got in recent IPOs; the company's projected\nearnings; a deliberate discount below expected after-market trading so first buyers have a\nbetter-than-even chance of a gain. \"The answer is based as much on art as on science.\"\n\n**Private placement instruments (pp. 166–167)**: senior debt; subordinated debt; asset-backed\ndebt; equity; negotiated hybrids — e.g. below-market-rate debt (good for the company) with\nwarrants attached (good for the investor). Buyers are usually sophisticated institutions\n(insurers, pension funds, endowments) seeking higher returns than public markets."},{"ref":"09-going-public.md","label":"Going public (handbook ch 9)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **Revenue bar**: ~$100M annualized revenue and several consecutive profitable quarters before an IPO is necessary or feasible.\n- **Minimum raise**: do not go public for less than $10M — the fixed costs swamp the proceeds.\n- **Cost**: budget ~$2M out of pocket; large deals reach $100M. Banker fee 6–10% of offering price.\n- **Timeline**: 4–5 months from underwriter selection to close. Pure Storage's readiness work (board, CFO, systems, bank relationship) ran roughly two years before that.\n- **Audited history**: three years minimum, or commission a look-back audit now.\n- **Growth test**: double-digit sales growth, earnings growing faster; the story must justify a P/E above S&P 500 / Russell 2000 history.\n- **Banker selection**: invite 3–5 candidates to present; pick on industry fit, distribution and analyst coverage, not just valuation flattery.\n- **Price**: the issuer can halt the offering if it dislikes the final price — that veto is your leverage.\n- **Quiet period**: filing the S-1 with the SEC triggers strict limits on public statements; competitors may spread \"fear, uncertainty and doubt\" while you cannot answer. Time the filing for when rivals are distracted if you can.\n- **Lockups**: vested insiders cannot sell for several months after the IPO; paper wealth can evaporate before it is realised (Akamai, 1999–2000).\n- **Window**: markets cool quickly; complete every step so you can \"hit while the IPO window is open\". At least five companies planned around Pure's IPO delayed or pulled.\n- **Valuation trap**: nobody wants to go public below the last private round. If the next private round would set a price the IPO could not match, IPO instead. A CEO's job is a fair deal with investors who will be good advisers, not the maximum valuation every round (Dietzen).\n- **Stay private longer when**: you want to keep control and fend off M&A (harder as a small public company); Sarbanes-Oxley costs bite; late-stage mutual-fund money (Fidelity, T. Rowe Price) is available and brings future public-market holders as advisers.\n- **Go sooner when**: customers prefer to buy from public companies (visible financials, higher scrutiny = trust); employees and investors need liquidity; growth needs capital you cannot raise privately.\n- **Employee retention**: offer selective liquidity in private rounds (a set % of vested shares sold to institutions) to relieve pressure for an early IPO; after the IPO, re-sell the vision so newly wealthy staff stay.\n- **Control**: adopt a two-class share structure before filing so founders and management can resist a hostile buyer.\n- **Private placement**: exempt from SEC registration (some states have requirements), so sizeable capital without the time, expense or scrutiny of a public offering — often the better route.\n- **Always get an experienced investment banker**, whichever route you take; expensive, but they hold the technical expertise and investor contacts (box, p. 165)."},{"ref":"09-going-public.md","label":"Going public (handbook ch 9)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"**The eight steps of an IPO (pp. 158–161)** — some sequential, some parallel:\n1. **Select an underwriter** — the investment bank handles the details with management; larger deals have one lead underwriter plus one or more comanagers.\n2. **Prepare the registration statement for the SEC** — required by federal law; discloses past results, company information and intended use of proceeds.\n3. **Conduct due diligence** — verify every fact and risk statement in the registration statement and that nothing relevant is omitted; the responsibility of those who prepare and sign it.\n4. **Print and distribute the prospectus** — the preliminary prospectus (red herring) is part of the registration statement and goes to prospective investors to build interest.\n5. **Prepare and conduct a road show** — meetings in major cities where potential investors grill the CEO and/or CFO on the company and the offering.\n6. **Agree the final price and number of shares** — the most important step (see pricing inputs above); the issuer can put the brakes on if the price is wrong.\n7. **Commence trading** — once price is set and regulatory loose ends are tied, shares trade on the chosen exchange.\n8. **Close the purchase and sale** — certificates delivered to shareholders; underwriter delivers proceeds less fees and expenses; the company has its money.\nAfterwards the underwriter supports the stock with ongoing research for investors. Beyond these\nsteps sit publicity restrictions before, during and after filing, and lockup agreements — the\nrules are \"many and arcane\", so professional help is essential (p. 161).\n\n**Getting ready — Pure Storage's preparation sequence (pp. 157–158)**\n1. Expand the board with large-company operating experience; recruit a public-company finance executive to chair the audit committee (Mark Garrett, Adobe CFO).\n2. Build an investment-bank relationship — ideally the bank that led the late private rounds (Allen & Company).\n3. Create a two-class stock structure to protect founder/management control.\n4. Hire a CFO with public-company experience (Tim Riitters, ex-Google) and install systems for financial visibility.\n5. When another private round adds no advantage (Pure: >$3B valuation in 2014), start IPO planning.\n6. Accept that filing the S-1 opens the quiet period and makes you vulnerable — pick the moment.\n\n**Inside the deal — the IPO-day view (Annie Bourne, Akamai, pp. 162–164)**: bankers and lawyers\nwrite the business-and-risk document → build a financial model of existing and expected revenue\n→ file with regulators, wait for comments, respond → two-week road show across Europe and the US\n→ bankers line up buyers for the \"book\" of shares → pricing decided \"in a seemingly unscientific\nfrenzy in a paneled room on Wall Street\" → ticker goes live; staff watch bid/ask numbers change;\nvested employees locked up for months."},{"ref":"09-going-public.md","label":"Going public (handbook ch 9)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Go / no-go: is an IPO right for us?** (pp. 150–152)\n- Do we need more than $10M, and do we have a solid plan for using it?\n- Which pros apply — debt payoff, product and marketing capacity, cheaper future debt, acquisition currency, stability for talent/suppliers/partners, future rounds?\n- Can we absorb ~$2M+ in costs and 6–10% underwriting fees?\n- Are we ready for financial results, executive pay, suppliers, product plans and strategy to be public via the 10-K?\n- Can the founders accept losing a major portion of ownership? Have we structured share classes for control?\n- Can the CEO and CFO give months to preparation and the road show, then ongoing investor time? Who will run investor relations?\n- Can we resist sacrificing long-term moves to hit predictable quarterly earnings?\n\n**IPO candidate checklist** (yes/no; all seven plus the E&Y hallmarks should be yes)\n- ~$100M annualized revenue and several consecutive profitable quarters\n- Double-digit sales growth; earnings growing faster or clearly imminent\n- Growth story supports a P/E above S&P 500 / Russell 2000 history\n- Products or services that are outstanding and hard to copy\n- A CEO who can communicate the vision to cautious outsiders\n- Three years of audited statements, or a look-back audit is feasible\n- High-quality employees\n- A logical growth strategy and a predictable revenue stream\n- Already acting public: employee incentives, strategic planning, internal controls, financial reporting, executive compensation, investor relations policy\n\n**Timing questions (from the \"When to go public\" box, pp. 155–159)**\n- Are we big enough now? Would waiting increase or decrease value?\n- Can we carry Sarbanes-Oxley compliance costs?\n- Do we want to stay independent long term — could we fend off M&A as a small public company?\n- Are customers asking for the transparency and security of a public supplier?\n- Would another private round set a valuation the IPO could not match?\n- Do employees and investors need liquidity — can selective liquidity in private rounds buy time?\n- Is the IPO window open now, and what if it cools while we prepare?\n- Who could attack us with FUD during the quiet period — are they distracted?\n- Do we have the board, audit chair, CFO, systems, bank relationship and share structure in place?\n\n**Due-diligence questions (step 3, p. 160)**\n- Is the company using an unorthodox accounting convention?\n- Is it involved in any current lawsuits?\n- Have patents been granted, or are patents pending?\n- Is every fact and risk statement in the registration statement accurate, with nothing relevant omitted?\n\n**Choosing an investment banker (p. 165; 3–5 candidates present)**\n- Good fit with our industry?\n- The sales and distribution capabilities we need?\n- Good analyst coverage for us once public?\n- Their take on the current market?\n- What they think our valuation should be?\n- Do they confirm we are ready to go public?"},{"ref":"09-going-public.md","label":"Going public (handbook ch 9)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **eBay / Pierre Omidyar (1998)** — held ~44M common shares with no market, rented house, old Jetta; next day they traded on NASDAQ and climbed past $4B — an IPO converts paper into wealth, but the process has real challenges.\n- **Pure Storage / Scott Dietzen (Oct 2015, NYSE)** — six years old, six private rounds, ~1,200 employees, ~$500M annualized revenue; could have listed in 2013 but waited; IPO at $17 a share, just over $3B valuation, still reporting losses — waiting worked, and luck (Dell buying EMC, HP splitting) muted competitor FUD during the quiet period.\n- **Google (~6 yrs), Facebook (8 yrs), Netscape (16 months)** — the first two staying private longer worked out so well it changed the conventional wisdom from \"IPO as soon as you can\" to \"many choose to wait\".\n- **Akamai (1999)** — priced at $26, opened at $114.50, closed at $145.19 on day one; most staff not yet vested and all locked up; the bust months later took much of the paper wealth away — day-one price is not money.\n- **Danny Lewin (Akamai cofounder, 29, >$1B on paper)** — pulled everyone out of the IPO party into a conference room to whiteboard the future — the same idea that makes investors buy must make employees stay.\n- **Dot-com boom** — companies with nothing but a clever idea sold public shares, then failed to prove their worth — the reason investors now demand a working \"money-making machine\".\n- **Fidelity / T. Rowe Price** — mutual funds that missed Facebook-style growth now do pre-IPO private placements — a new way to stay private longer and build public-market relationships early.\n- **At least five companies near Pure's IPO date** — delayed or pulled their offerings as the market cooled — windows close."},{"ref":"09-going-public.md","label":"Going public (handbook ch 9)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Treating the IPO as a goal in itself rather than one financing option among several.\n- Going too early (under-exploiting potential, exposed to M&A) or too late (missing the window).\n- Underestimating the out-of-pocket cost, the 6–10% fee and the months of CEO/CFO time.\n- Forgetting that the 10-K publishes suppliers, product plans and strategy to competitors.\n- Taking a private round at a valuation the IPO market will not match.\n- Filing the S-1 without a plan for the quiet period — rivals spread FUD while you are gagged.\n- Chasing the highest valuation instead of fair terms with investors who will be good advisers.\n- Letting newly rich employees drift away after the IPO; not re-selling the vision.\n- Mistaking day-one paper wealth for realised wealth while locked up.\n- Sacrificing long-term investment to protect predictable quarterly earnings.\n- Attempting the deal without an experienced investment banker."},{"ref":"09-going-public.md","label":"Going public (handbook ch 9)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`IPO (initial public offering)` — first sale of a company's shares to the public, exchanging ownership for cash.\n`underwriter` — the investment bank that manages the offering, prices it and takes on the risk of selling the shares; `comanagers` assist on larger deals.\n`investment bank` — agent and deal-maker for capital seekers (not a deposit-and-loan bank); fee 6–10% of offering price.\n`registration statement (Form S-1)` — federally required SEC filing disclosing past results, company information and use of proceeds.\n`due diligence` — verifying that the registration statement's facts and risk statements are accurate and complete.\n`prospectus` / `red herring` — the disclosure document for investors; the preliminary version sent to build interest.\n`road show` — investor meetings in major cities where the CEO/CFO are grilled on the offering.\n`book` — the bank's ledger of investor orders for the shares.\n`quiet period` — post-S-1 window with strict limits on public statements.\n`lockup agreement` — restriction stopping insiders selling shares for several months after the IPO.\n`10-K` — the annual public filing that discloses results, pay, suppliers, plans and strategy.\n`P/E ratio` — price-earnings multiple; the growth story must justify one above the S&P 500 / Russell 2000.\n`Sarbanes-Oxley` — 2002 US act raising public-company controls and compliance costs.\n`two-class stock` — share structure giving founders/management voting control against hostile buyers.\n`syndicate` — group of broker-dealers assembled by the underwriter to distribute shares.\n`bid / ask` — what a buyer will pay and what a seller will accept for a share.\n`look-back audit` — retroactive audit of prior years built from existing records.\n`investor relations manager` — role created to handle investor and analyst inquiries post-IPO.\n`private placement` — sale of stock (or debt) to one or a few private investors, exempt from SEC registration.\n`warrant` — right to buy common shares at a stated price for a stated period, usually set above current valuation."},{"ref":"09-going-public.md","label":"Going public (handbook ch 9)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- An IPO is a pipe dream for all but a few corporations.\n- It brings much-needed cash and lets owners liquidate and diversify their wealth.\n- Downside: expense, absorbed management time, ownership dilution, ongoing public scrutiny and pressure for short-term gains.\n- Choose the time carefully, weighing market conditions and your interest in keeping control.\n- Do not consider an IPO without: a CEO who communicates, deal size of $10M+, double-digit revenue and earnings growth (or earnings clearly to follow), outstanding hard-to-copy products, quality employees, a logical growth strategy.\n- Eight US steps: select underwriter → registration statement to SEC → due diligence → red herring → road show → price and share count → trading → close.\n- An investment bank supplies two necessities: technical knowledge to clear registration and a sales network to distribute the shares.\n- A private placement is often a good alternative to an IPO."},{"ref":"10-sustaining-growth.md","label":"Sustaining growth (handbook ch 10)","heading":"Purpose","text":"What must change in the *business* (strategy, markets, capacity, hiring rules, structure) once it\nhas broken out of the startup phase and revenue is growing. Load when a user says \"we're growing\nfast\", \"how do we scale\", \"should we outsource\", \"how do we keep competitors out\", or when a\njourney enters the scaling stage. Chapter 11 covers the matching changes in the founder's leadership."},{"ref":"10-sustaining-growth.md","label":"Sustaining growth (handbook ch 10)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- **Growth is a mixed blessing, especially rapid growth.** Keeping pace with demand normally needs\n  outside capital, and every dollar of it has a cost: debt raises fixed expenses (more risk);\n  outside equity dilutes the founders' ownership — and control.\n- For platform and web-marketplace businesses fast scaling can be existential (Hoffman's\n  **blitzscaling**): scale creates value for users (LinkedIn's professional base, eBay's buyers\n  and sellers, Amazon's low margin / high volume) and wins **first-mover advantage** — securing\n  customer loyalty in a segment before anyone else.\n- Growth in sales triggers growth in everything that supports sales: customer service, marketing,\n  transaction accounting, after-sales service; for physical product also purchasing, inventory,\n  manufacturing and logistics. It also forces you to study new channels, product-line extensions\n  and new markets, and to build retention strategies for new customers.\n- Head count must grow with sales, plus the HR capacity to recruit, comply with labor law and run\n  benefits — and finance: without a knowledgeable CFO and accounting staff the enterprise \"could\n  easily capsize and sink\". Many promising ventures fail by not managing their way through\n  initial success.\n- Rule number one (learned by Hewlett and Packard): **management is getting results through\n  people**; the founders' own skills are insufficient — you must marshal many employees' talent.\n- Few strategies are sustainable long term. Regulation, deregulation, a superior technology, a\n  faster/cheaper process, or entrants into a market you created will erode the advantage. Keep\n  looking several steps ahead and bar the door to new competitors.\n- Sustained growth does not always require invention: new uses, new niches and new geographies\n  recharge the growth engine at far lower risk and cost than new-product development.\n- Capacity scales differently by model: service firms scale by hiring scarce, expensive skilled\n  people; product firms commit capital a year or more ahead of sales, which needs conviction that\n  demand will actually be there.\n- Outsourcing lets you scale without fixed assets — but never outsource the links to customers.\n- As the business scales it must change: strategy, offering, structure and how you hire."},{"ref":"10-sustaining-growth.md","label":"Sustaining growth (handbook ch 10)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**The three post-startup growth questions** (p. 174) — revisit and recalibrate once revenue grows:\n1. Is our strategy sustainable?\n2. Do we have unique advantages that would let us expand into other markets?\n3. Is scaling up the business a practical possibility?\n\n**Why fast scale matters for platforms** (Hoffman, blitzscaling, p. 171–172):\n- Scale itself creates value for users (network and volume effects).\n- Reaching customers faster than competitors gives first-mover advantage — loyalty locked in\n  before anyone else arrives.\n\n**Growth triggers — what must scale with sales** (p. 173–174)\n\n| Trigger | What must grow |\n|---|---|\n| More customers and transactions | Customer service, marketing, transaction accounting, after-sales service, retention strategies |\n| Physical product | Materials purchasing, inventory management, manufacturing, logistics |\n| Growing sales | Study of new distribution channels, product-line extensions, entry into new markets |\n| Head count | Developers, security engineers, marketers, operations, sales, sales support, customer service; an HR function to recruit, comply and run benefits |\n| Money flow | A knowledgeable CFO plus accounting staff keeping payments, collections and spending on an even keel |\n\n**Four mechanisms for sustaining growth / barring the door to competitors** (p. 175–176)\n\n| Mechanism | How it works |\n|---|---|\n| Exploit the learning curve | If first to market, continual improvement in design and manufacturing efficiency lets you sell for less at the same profitability; late entrants that can't catch up are doomed to slim or no profits |\n| Don't price for maximum profit | High margins attract rivals; if you and your investors accept a modest margin, would-be competitors stay away |\n| Continually refresh the offer | New features, colour choices, lower price, easier purchase, fewer quality problems, amazing service — and reinvent the product for an as-yet unmet need |\n| Be constantly vigilant about competition | Who will notice you and try to stop you? How can you change course or refine strategy to avoid or beat their challenge? |\n\n**Three routes into new markets without invention** (p. 176–177)\n\n| Route | Condition / method | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Geographic expansion | Needs in unserved regions are the same or similar; via your own sales & marketing, or indirectly via distributors or a sales-representation arrangement | — |\n| New uses for the same product | Raise consumption by existing customers and attract new ones | Baking soda: open box in the fridge, changed monthly; mixed into cat litter |\n| Customise the product for other niches | Same core, different exterior for each segment, at very low cost | Swatch: dozens of watches on identical internal movements; only the case changes |\n\n**Scaling capacity by business model** (p. 177–179)\n\n| Model | Constraint | Scaling lever |\n|---|---|---|\n| Service (e.g. consulting) | Production = employee output; people with the unique skills are scarce and expensive; training may be needed | Hire people who can both sell and deliver; ask \"can we scale human assets fast enough?\" |\n| Product / manufacturing | Substantial capital committed a year or more before the first unit ships; requires conviction that demand will exist then | Contract out to manufacturers selling unused capacity (short-run antidote), as Boston Beer did |\n\n**Blitzscaling hiring hack** (Hoffman on Uber, p. 178): ask each newly hired engineer \"who are the\nthree best engineers you've worked with?\" — then send those three offer letters. No interview,\nno reference check. Creative rule-bending lets you grow faster than predetermined process allows\nwhile still trying to hire quality and keep the culture.\n\n**Box — Tips on outsourcing** (p. 179). Outsourcing helps you scale without fixed assets you can't\nafford (or that would drag you down if demand faltered) and frees managerial attention for what\nreally differentiates the company. Two cautions:\n- **Never outsource activities that connect you directly with customers** — sales, customer\n  service, market research, product or service development. These links let you learn about\n  customers and them about you; outsource them and your customers become your partner's customers.\n- **Never depend too much on any single outsource partner.** Ask what happens if a manufacturing,\n  assembly or distribution partner fails or stops doing business with you; hedge by diversifying."},{"ref":"10-sustaining-growth.md","label":"Sustaining growth (handbook ch 10)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- If you run a platform or marketplace where scale creates user value → speed of scaling is a\n  strategic necessity; plan capital and hiring for it rather than growing organically.\n- Every dollar of outside capital has a negative side: debt → higher fixed cost and risk; equity →\n  diluted ownership and control. Weigh both before financing growth.\n- Robust revenue growth proves the strategy works *today*; the question is how much longer. Test it\n  against competitors improving, technology maturing, new technology, and changed context.\n- If you created a profitable, expanding market, assume other entrepreneurs will enter — build\n  barriers now, not later.\n- If first in market: ride the learning curve and price for a modest margin rather than maximum\n  profit, so late entrants face slim profits and stay away.\n- Prefer new uses, niche customisation and geographic expansion before betting the growth engine on\n  risky, expensive new-product development.\n- Expand geographically only where customer needs are the same as or similar to those you serve now.\n- Manufacturers: plan facilities a year or more before first output. If the capital or the\n  conviction about demand is missing, buy contract capacity instead of building.\n- Outsource peripheral tasks; keep sales, customer service, market research and product\n  development in-house.\n- Diversify outsource partners; model the failure of any single one before signing.\n- When growth outruns process, deliberately relax guidelines (e.g. referral offers without\n  interviews) — but only where you can still protect quality and culture.\n- Put a knowledgeable CFO and accounting staff in place before growth strains payments,\n  collections and spending."},{"ref":"10-sustaining-growth.md","label":"Sustaining growth (handbook ch 10)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. Confirm you have broken out of the startup phase and revenue is growing.\n2. Re-ask the three growth questions (strategy sustainable? unique advantages for other markets?\n   scaling practical?) and recalibrate the plan.\n3. Strategy: name what could undermine your advantage; choose barriers — learning curve, modest\n   pricing, refreshed offer, competitor vigilance.\n4. Markets: list unserved geographies with similar needs; list new uses and niche variants of the\n   existing product; write the plan for recharging the growth engine.\n5. Capacity: services — plan hiring and training of sell-and-deliver staff; products — decide build\n   vs outsource with lead times and capital timing.\n6. Support functions: scale service, marketing, accounting, HR and finance in step with sales.\n7. Revisit strategy, offering, structure and hiring rules as scale changes them; then move to\n   chapter 11 for the leadership changes."},{"ref":"10-sustaining-growth.md","label":"Sustaining growth (handbook ch 10)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"Growth readiness (p. 174):\n- Is our strategy sustainable?\n- Do we have unique advantages that would let us expand into other markets?\n- Is scaling up the business a practical possibility?\n\nStrategy stress-test (p. 174–176):\n- What is our advantage based on — a new/superior product or technology, a lower price, or\n  extreme convenience?\n- What happens if competitors improve their offerings, the technology matures, new technology\n  arises, or the context changes in some other way?\n- Could what happened to Blockbuster happen to our business?\n- Which patterns in our industry let us anticipate the solutions competitors will offer?\n- As we grow, who is going to notice us and try to stop us? How can we change course or refine\n  strategy to avoid or beat that challenge?\n- Which barriers can we build: learning curve, modest pricing, a continually refreshed offer?\n- How can we reinvent our product to solve an as-yet unmet customer need?\n\nNew-market scan (p. 176–177):\n- Which geographic regions have no distribution from us, and are needs there similar to ours?\n- For those regions: own sales and marketing, distributors, or sales representation?\n- What new uses could existing customers find for the same product?\n- Which niches could a customised version serve at low cost?\n- What plan does our enterprise have for recharging the growth engine?\n\nCapacity check (p. 177–179):\n- Service: can we scale our human assets fast enough to satisfy demand and our own growth\n  expectations?\n- Product: what capital must be committed how far ahead of sales, and how certain is demand then?\n- Which activities can be outsourced without touching customer contact?\n- What happens if any one outsource partner fails or walks away?\n- Do we have HR capacity to recruit, comply with labor law and manage benefits at scale?\n- Do we have a CFO and accounting staff able to keep payments, collections and spending steady?"},{"ref":"10-sustaining-growth.md","label":"Sustaining growth (handbook ch 10)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Hewlett-Packard** — 1938 garage, two employees, $5,369 revenue; Disney's eight oscillators for\n  *Fantasia*; 111 people and ~$1M by 1943; 19 years to 1,000 employees; IPO only in 1957; grew to\n  ~90,000 employees — deliberate pace, \"results through people\", the HP Way built to attract talent.\n- **Amazon** — revenue $5.1M in 1996, $1.64B three years later — explosive scale.\n- **Airbnb** — founded Aug 2008; 47,000 summer guests in 2010, 17 million by 2015.\n- **Uber** — founded 2009; ride-share bookings $688M (2013) to $10.84B two years later.\n- **Blockbuster vs Netflix** — market leader by 1993; Netflix's 1999 mail service (no late fees,\n  far more titles), then Redbox and on-demand cable, ate its business; stores closed from 2006,\n  operations ceased 2013 — few strategies last.\n- **Netflix** — used the DVD base to introduce customers to streaming, then built original\n  content: owns both content and distribution, a virtuous circle rivals can't break — bar the door.\n- **Baking soda** — a leading supplier promoted fridge deodorising (change monthly) and cat-litter\n  use — new uses grew sales to existing customers and created new ones.\n- **Swatch** — dozens of watches for men, women, teens, sports fans on the same movement; only the\n  case design changed — niche customisation at very low cost.\n- **Uber engineering hiring** — offer letters to each new hire's three best former colleagues, no\n  interview or references — hack the process to scale hiring.\n- **Boston Beer / Jim Koch** — sixth-generation brewer, left consulting; R&D in an abandoned Boston\n  brewery; brewing and bottling contracted to a quality Pennsylvania brewery under his own\n  brewmaster; repeated as US distribution grew — keep the distinctive features, outsource the\n  capital-intensive capacity."},{"ref":"10-sustaining-growth.md","label":"Sustaining growth (handbook ch 10)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Failing to manage through initial success — no CFO, no HR, no accounting discipline.\n- Assuming a strategy that works now will keep working.\n- Pricing for maximum profit and inviting competitors into the market.\n- Betting the growth engine on new-product invention alone.\n- Expanding into geographies where customer needs differ.\n- Building fixed assets a year ahead of unproven demand.\n- Outsourcing sales, customer service, market research or product development.\n- Depending on a single outsource partner.\n- Ignoring the control cost of equity and the risk cost of debt when funding growth."},{"ref":"10-sustaining-growth.md","label":"Sustaining growth (handbook ch 10)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`blitzscaling` — Hoffman's term for growth at the aggressive rate platform and marketplace businesses require.\n`first-mover advantage` — connecting with customers and securing their loyalty in a segment before anyone else does.\n`strategy` — what differentiates a business in a way that confers a competitive advantage.\n`learning curve` — falling unit cost from continual improvement in design and production efficiency as cumulative output grows.\n`outsourcing` — buying other firms' unused capacity to scale output without owning the fixed assets."},{"ref":"10-sustaining-growth.md","label":"Sustaining growth (handbook ch 10)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- Growth forces companies through transitions.\n- Continued growth depends on a sustainable strategy, the ability to expand into other markets,\n  and mechanisms for scaling up output.\n- Three mechanisms sustain growth: exploit the learning curve for a cost advantage; don't price for\n  maximum profit (high profits attract competitors); continually refresh the offer to customers.\n- To scale, businesses often change guidelines around processes like hiring to become more nimble.\n- Outsourcing peripheral tasks can meet rising demand; outsourcing core tasks — especially those\n  that put the partner in direct contact with customers — can have very bad consequences."},{"ref":"11-leadership.md","label":"Leadership (handbook ch 11)","heading":"Purpose","text":"How the founder's role and way of working must change as the company grows: which leadership\nmode fits which size, how to tell whether you can and will adapt, where to get objective feedback\n(funders, advisory board, executive coach), and when to bring in professional management or step\naside. Load when a user says \"I'm the bottleneck\", \"should I hire a CEO\", \"how do I delegate\",\n\"advisory board\", or when a journey enters scaling after chapter 10."},{"ref":"11-leadership.md","label":"Leadership (handbook ch 11)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- Organizational issues often eclipse sales as the biggest growth challenge; the founding team must\n  periodically reinvent the organization to cope with changing circumstances.\n- Bhidé: sustainability requires broadening the capabilities of the **firm**, not the entrepreneur —\n  more qualified people, more specialised functions, decentralised decisions, systems for a larger\n  and more complex organization, and employees oriented to a common long-term purpose.\n- Founders must reinvent themselves: from **doing things yourself to doing things through other\n  people**. Many find this hard, fail to change, and become liabilities to the companies they founded.\n- Growth strains each founding contribution — vision, technical skills, management skills, and\n  personal energy and time.\n- Roberts' four leadership approaches — **content, behaviors, results, context** — map to enterprise\n  size and complexity. There is no best way to manage a startup, but there is a best mode for a\n  particular company at a particular point, and the transitions between modes need extra attention.\n- Modes can be mixed: hands-on with a newly appointed manager while running the overall operation\n  on results.\n- Some founders adapt through years of growth (Page & Brin, Gates, Kelleher, Cook, Branson). Others\n  cannot change habits that served them in a small setting, or actively resist changes that dilute\n  their control.\n- If you are willing but struggling to adapt, recruit people who will criticise your leadership\n  objectively: the management team, the board, funders, an advisory board, an executive coach.\n- If you cannot or will not adapt, change role or step aside — there is no shame in either. Bring in\n  professional management, with the founder staying on as chair.\n- Professional managers know how to build the systems and processes every growing firm eventually\n  needs (Flamholtz & Randle). Many firms recognise the need too late to avoid a crisis, and some\n  founders only give \"the illusion of turning the organization over\"."},{"ref":"11-leadership.md","label":"Leadership (handbook ch 11)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**Four founding contributions strained by growth** (p. 182)\n\n| Contribution | Strain |\n|---|---|\n| Common vision | Must be instilled in newly hired employees |\n| Technical skills | Become relatively less important as operational and management skills matter more |\n| Management skills | May not be up to the challenge of a larger organization |\n| Personal energy and time | Finite, while the need to direct and control keeps growing |\n\n**Roberts' four leadership approaches** (p. 182–185)\n- **Managing content** — do it yourself or directly supervise those who do (hiring, product design,\n  moving goods to the stockroom). Maximum control, which appeals to founders motivated by control;\n  fails when the founder's time and energy can't keep pace and new challenges need skills or\n  experience they lack. Not recognising when this mode is no longer appropriate can kill the business.\n- **Managing behaviors** — specify how people should act; codify the behaviors that lead to success\n  in policies, rules, procedures, job design and behavior-auditing systems (e.g. train lab staff to\n  run specific tests, then audit compliance). Best when employees are inexperienced or need clear\n  direction. Frees the founder's time while keeping control.\n- **Managing results** — define what the outcome must look like and hand over responsibility for\n  how (military vehicle: 25 mpg on paved roads, rough terrain, protects driver plus five passengers\n  from small-arms fire). The leader supplies resources, training and motivation. Needed where\n  problems have no clear guidelines (science, engineering) and talented people must find the answer.\n- **Managing context** — also results-focused, but achieved by shaping culture, values and\n  structure so highly competent people are attracted, retained and do their best work; select and\n  develop people, communicate broadly, spend little or no time saying what or how.\n\n**The sales-team illustration across modes** (p. 183–185)\n\n| Mode | Instruction to salespeople |\n|---|---|\n| Behaviors | Talk with 20 prospects a day → about one new account a day, five a week; a solid commission base after six months. Manager monitors the 20-contact rule and helps where needed |\n| Results | Produce at least EUR 150,000 in commission revenue this year — by whatever method works |\n| Context | Decide both how to win accounts and what the goals should be (e.g. win back lapsed accounts, measure account profitability to choose leads) |\n\n**Table 11-1 — Four leadership approaches** (Roberts, p. 186)\n\n| | Content | Behavior | Results | Context |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Situation | Young, small, simple enterprise | Somewhat larger, more involved enterprise | Large, complex organization | Very large, very complex, mature organization |\n| Driving assumptions | Insufficient knowledge and experience to plan; subordinates not capable of independent action or decisions | Too little time to do everything; subordinates can act independently but per managerial prescription | Too little time; subordinates achieve better outcomes with their own means | Too little time and knowledge; the right people in the right environment with the right mission will succeed |\n| Behavior | On the front lines; barking orders; pitching in to help out | Developing process and procedure; observing | Attending meetings and reviews; studying plans, papers, reports; writing memos | Lots of time on key hires and promotions; tone-setting events |\n| Key skills, tools | Action; decisions | Policies; procedures; behavior audit | Plans; budgets; organizing structure and systems | Communication; leadership by example |\n\n**Three damaging consequences of a founder who won't adapt** (p. 187)\n1. Employee initiative is smothered by the founder controlling all activities and decisions; the\n   best employees leave in frustration.\n2. Opportunities are missed because the organization can only run at the overworked founder's pace.\n3. The enterprise's scope is limited to the founder's knowledge and vision.\n\n**Sources of objective feedback and help** (p. 187–189)\n\n| Source | What it offers | Caveat |\n|---|---|---|\n| Management team and board | Tell you if your grip is too tight or too loose, where you need help, and when it's time to go | Must not be afraid to say so |\n| Funders (VCs) | Experienced entrepreneurial professionals; Andreessen: CEO skills can be taught (innovator skills are more innate), so his firm trains CEOs | Mulcahy: VCs differ widely in how much they actually coach — verify with portfolio CEOs |\n| Advisory board | Sounding board for ideas, plus skills, mentorship and a broader network | Hard to choose members before you know which expertise you lack |\n| Executive coach | One-on-one, customised behavior change to improve on-the-job performance | Expensive, but may be worth it |\n\n**Two executive-coaching approaches** (p. 188–189)\n\n| Approach | Character |\n|---|---|\n| Diagnosis and development | Traditional, rooted in psychology; deeper method; takes longer to deliver |\n| Prescriptive | Like the everyday coaching managers give subordinates; faster and more direct |\n\n**Box — How to build a board** (adapted from MacPherson, p. 190–191)\n1. **Look outside your existing network.** Not friends and fans: diverse experience, expertise,\n   viewpoints and skills; people who have built successful businesses; a constructively critical\n   audience; access to customers, suppliers, strategic partners, financiers, publicists and\n   professional-service vendors.\n2. **Recruit a well-known community member or industry influencer first.** Like film producers\n   lining up bankable talent — a star's commitment attracts others and gives credibility from day one.\n3. **Invest time in the relationships.** Most members are unpaid; their reward is the satisfaction\n   of helping, so show appreciation. If one insists on pay, judge how uniquely valuable they are and\n   consider remuneration where a long-term business relationship is possible.\n[section truncated]"},{"ref":"11-leadership.md","label":"Leadership (handbook ch 11)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- Young, small, simple → content mode; larger organizations call for behaviors, then results, then\n  context in turn. Match the mode to size and complexity, not to personal preference.\n- Inexperienced staff who need clear direction → manage behaviors. Complex problems with no clear\n  guidelines (science, engineering, creative work) → manage results or context.\n- Behavior mode assumes the prescribed behavior produces the result and is the only way to it; when\n  either assumption fails, move to results.\n- Rigid, by-the-book rules work for McDonald's and would be disastrous for IDEO — the mode must fit\n  the work, not just the size.\n- You may run more than one mode at once (hands-on with a new manager; results for the whole).\n- Transitions between modes are where things break; give them extra attention.\n- Behavior-mode sales heuristic: 20 prospect conversations a day ≈ 1 new account a day ≈ 5 a week;\n  six months to a solid commission base.\n- \"If you want advice for your startup, ask for money. If you want money, ask for advice.\"\n  (Baehr & Loomis) — you will need both.\n- Before taking VC money, get the names of the firm's other portfolio CEOs and ask them: is the\n  partner accessible, do they add to boardroom discussion, have they given constructive help?\n- Advisory board: meet in person every 3–6 months, agenda with specific goals, yearly assessment,\n  term limits of 2–3 years, recruit the bankable influencer first, no friends-and-fans.\n- An advisory board carries no fiduciary duty; a board of directors does.\n- If any of the seven professional-management signals sound familiar → act before the crisis.\n- If you cannot or will not adapt → change role (founder as chair, professional CEO) or step aside.\n- A handover must be real: hiring managers and then controlling everything is the illusion of\n  letting go."},{"ref":"11-leadership.md","label":"Leadership (handbook ch 11)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. Diagnose the mode you use today (Table 11-1) and whether it fits your current size and growth.\n2. Identify the mode the next stage needs and the tools it requires (policies and audits; plans,\n   budgets and structure; communication, key hires and tone-setting).\n3. Reflect: is the business at a transition point? Can you adapt? Are you willing to?\n4. If willing but struggling: recruit objective critics — management team, board, funders, an\n   advisory board (built per the box), or an executive coach (choose diagnosis-and-development or\n   prescriptive).\n5. Run the \"Do you need professional management?\" checklist.\n6. If you cannot or will not adapt, or the firm needs systems you can't build: bring in\n   professional management with the founder as chair (or cash out) — and genuinely hand over."},{"ref":"11-leadership.md","label":"Leadership (handbook ch 11)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"Leadership-mode self-check (p. 185):\n- Which management method am I using today?\n- Is it appropriate for my current state of development and growth?\n- As volume and scope grow, how much less time do I have for hands-on involvement?\n\nAdaptation check (p. 187):\n- Is my business at a transition point where my style of leading and managing must change?\n- Can I adapt?\n- Am I willing to adapt?\n- Who will tell me objectively if my grip is too tight (or too loose), where I need help, and when\n  it's time for me to go?\n\nVC-mentoring due diligence (p. 188) — ask the CEOs of the VC's other portfolio companies:\n- How effective is the firm's mentoring?\n- Is the VC partner accessible?\n- How much do they add to boardroom discussion?\n- Have you received constructive help in dealing with company problems?\n\nAdvisory-board setup (p. 190–191):\n- Who outside my inner circle has built a successful business and would be constructively critical?\n- Who can open doors to customers, suppliers, partners, financiers, publicists, service vendors?\n- Who is the bankable first member?\n- Meeting cadence and place, agenda, yearly assessment, term limits — written down?\n- Have I stated clearly what the board is and is not?\n\nProfessional-management scenario (p. 192) — tick each that applies:\n- Every decision must be made at the top.\n- Policies for routine functions are almost nonexistent.\n- Human resources are not being developed.\n- I make decisions, but no one follows through with action.\n- Accounting is haphazard and amateurish.\n- We have trouble recruiting competent people.\n- People spend a lot of their time putting out fires.\n\nFinal reflection (p. 193):\n- Is a lack of systems and processes holding the firm back?\n- Am I personally up to the challenge of building the business, or would the company be better off\n  if I stepped aside in favour of experienced managers?"},{"ref":"11-leadership.md","label":"Leadership (handbook ch 11)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Page & Brin (Google), Gates (Microsoft), Kelleher (Southwest), Cook (Intuit), Branson\n  (Virgin)** — each adapted his management mode as the business grew — adaptation is possible.\n- **McDonald's vs IDEO** — McDonald's thrives on behavior-mode procedures and job design and would\n  never tell crews to \"figure it out\"; the same rules would wreck a creative design firm — fit the\n  mode to the work.\n- **Pierre Omidyar / eBay** — kept the chairman's job, handed management to Meg Whitman as CEO;\n  had brought in a VC partly to recruit management expertise — change role without shame.\n- **Esther / \"ChickenFix\"** (fictional) — molecular biologist; university took a 25 percent share;\n  seed money from university and a VC; content as CEO while lab-based, out of her element once\n  testing, a product manager, VCs and MBAs arrived; \"everything seems anticlimactic\" — heart not in\n  the executive role → professional CEO, founder as chair.\n- **Flamholtz & Randle's founder** — hired two experienced managers, made a big deal of turning\n  over the reins, then kept controlling everything — the illusion of letting go.\n- **Andreessen vs Mulcahy** — CEO skills can be taught and some VCs train them, but coaching\n  quality varies widely — verify with portfolio CEOs before you sign."},{"ref":"11-leadership.md","label":"Leadership (handbook ch 11)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Staying in content mode past the size where it works; the business fails when the founder can't\n  see it.\n- Assuming prescribed behaviors guarantee results, or are the only route to them.\n- Resisting change to protect control — smothering initiative and losing the best people.\n- Filling the advisory board with friends and fans; letting it drift without goals, agenda,\n  assessment or term limits.\n- Confusing an advisory board with a legal board of directors.\n- Taking VC money assuming mentoring comes with it, without checking.\n- Recognising the need for professional management only after a crisis.\n- Pretending to hand over to professional managers while still controlling everything."},{"ref":"11-leadership.md","label":"Leadership (handbook ch 11)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`managing content` — leading by doing the work yourself or directly supervising those who do.\n`managing behaviors` — leading by prescribing and auditing how people act (policies, rules, procedures, job design).\n`managing results` — leading by defining outcomes and giving people responsibility for the means.\n`managing context` — leading by shaping culture, values, structure and hiring so the right people succeed on their own.\n`advisory board` — unpaid volunteers with knowledge you lack; no fiduciary, legal or financial responsibility (unlike a board of directors).\n`executive coach` — one-on-one behavior-change professional; diagnosis-and-development (deep, slow) or prescriptive (fast, direct).\n`professional management` — experienced managers who build the systems and processes a growing firm needs."},{"ref":"11-leadership.md","label":"Leadership (handbook ch 11)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- Growth challenges the founding team, who may lack the skills, experience or temperament to lead\n  a larger, more complex organization.\n- Roberts' four modes — real-time management of content, behavior, results, context — must be\n  matched to circumstances, and the team must know when to change from one to another.\n- A few entrepreneurs adapt with their companies' growth; others must change themselves (often\n  difficult), change roles by bringing in professional management, or cash in their equity and\n  move on to new challenges."},{"ref":"12-entrepreneurial-spirit.md","label":"Keeping the entrepreneurial spirit (handbook ch 12)","heading":"Purpose","text":"How a growing company keeps innovating once it has customers, headcount, functions and approval\nprocesses. Load when a user says \"we've stopped innovating\", \"the company feels bureaucratic\",\n\"how do I balance running the business with building the next thing\", or when a scaling business\nis setting its innovation strategy, hiring plan or reward system."},{"ref":"12-entrepreneurial-spirit.md","label":"Keeping the entrepreneurial spirit (handbook ch 12)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- Entrepreneurial ventures *are* innovation: a technical advance (a thin, high-performance\n  monitor), a new service (smartphone taxi hailing) or an invisible process innovation (Ford's\n  assembly line; cheaper, denser semiconductor processes). Newness customers value is the wedge\n  into a profitable niche; it is hard to name a successful entrepreneurial firm that is bad at it.\n- Big firms are seen as slow and rigid, yet Honda/Toyota (the hybrid car), 160-year-old Corning\n  (smartphone glass) and 3M keep innovating. For every one of them, dozens of large firms have\n  forgotten how and **buy innovation** by acquisition or licensing — usually from startups.\n- Growing firms lose the spark for three reasons: **size**, **the existing-customers problem**\n  and **complacency**. A new company starts free of all three; success and growth reintroduce them.\n- Size forces specialization, coordination overhead and review/approval systems that frustrate\n  creative people. What the founders settled over coffee now needs formal meetings; the more\n  people involved, the slower the agreement and the more likely it is a compromise.\n- Serving existing customers diligently has two side effects: customers protect their installed\n  base and push you toward incremental upgrades (the \"tyranny of served markets\"), and\n  leadership attention drifts to people, marketing, finance, operations and service.\n- Success breeds complacency (Pascale's **paradox of success**): success plants the seeds of\n  failure. Threatened incumbents typically invest *more* in the mature technology.\n- Seven remedies, all leadership jobs: innovation-friendly culture, vision and strategic\n  direction, personal involvement, a continually improved idea-to-commercialization process,\n  portfolio thinking, hiring entrepreneurial attitudes, and an ambidextrous organization.\n- Innovation must be **bounded by strategy**: creative energy and money are scarce; ideas outside\n  the boundaries eat resources, get rejected before commercialization and demoralise idea people.\n- Today's business runs on certainty, efficiency and cost control; tomorrow's depends on an\n  uncertain, inefficient, costly innovation process. Few executives can live in both worlds, and\n  the immediate business usually wins — so separate them structurally."},{"ref":"12-entrepreneurial-spirit.md","label":"Keeping the entrepreneurial spirit (handbook ch 12)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**The three challenges to innovation in growing firms** (pp. 196–198)\n\n| Challenge | Mechanism | Symptom |\n|---|---|---|\n| Size | Specialization of functions; communication/coordination gaps; review boards and approval requirements | Coffee-table decisions become multi-party meetings; compromises replace optimal solutions |\n| Existing customers | (a) Tyranny of served markets: customers urge parts and incremental upgrades to protect their investments; (b) leadership focus shifts to operations | Only incremental innovation; breakthroughs left to rivals; innovation slips off the radar; customers eventually defect to more innovative rivals anyway |\n| Complacency | Paradox of success: \"keep doing what we're doing and all will be well\" | Doubling down on the mature technology as the new one gets better and cheaper monthly (sail vs steam; gas vs electric light; piston vs jet) |\n\n**Seven levers for keeping the spirit alive** (chapter structure, restated on p. 210)\n1. Preserve an innovation-friendly culture.\n2. Establish vision and strategic direction.\n3. Be personally involved with innovation.\n4. Continually improve the idea-to-commercialization process.\n5. Apply portfolio thinking.\n6. Hire people with entrepreneurial attitudes.\n7. Create an ambidextrous organization.\n\n**Three culture levers** (p. 199): the physical environment; risk taking and learning;\nincentives and rewards.\n\n**Physical-environment tactics** (pp. 199–200)\n- Notice where people already gather informally (coffee machine, kitchen, copy room) and make\n  those spots inviting: comfortable chairs, staircases wide enough to stop and chat, beanbags in\n  conference rooms, snacks every week or two with an invitation to just talk.\n- Put creativity tools in unexpected places: whiteboards, markers and flip charts in the\n  kitchen; crayons and white paper on conference tables to invite doodling and diagrams.\n- Build in play (foosball, stress relievers) — the conscious mind takes a break and returns with\n  a fresh approach.\n- Balance open spaces with quiet zones: a silent company library, rooms whose doors shut out\n  distraction.\n\n**Risk-and-learning tactics** (pp. 200–201)\n- Communicate that reasonable risks are not only acceptable but necessary; innovation is normal\n  business, not an exception.\n- Share knowledge across the organization; tightly controlled information cannot recombine.\n- Foster communities of interest across functions; send people to professional meetings,\n  conferences, customer visits and experts.\n\n**Four types of rewards that sustain inventive effort** (pp. 201–202)\n\n| Reward | Nature | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Recognition | Progress is a powerful motivator | Public announcement or award; a senior executive thanks the team; recognise people who worked outside their preferred style or function |\n| Control | Autonomy and involvement | Involve people in decisions that affect them; let the team choose the next marketing opportunity; assign progressively harder projects matching their interests |\n| Celebration | Shared applause | Small party; toast a product launch; team dinner after the redesigned website ships |\n| Rejuvenation | Time away | Extra vacation days for a win (e.g. taking the core cereal brand into a new international market); industry conferences to build skills and return energised |\n\nExtrinsic rewards (pay, vacation, recognition) target a goal distinct from the work; intrinsic\nrewards target self-actualization, challenge, deep interest, curiosity and enjoyment. Use both.\n**Idea champions**: leaders' visible attention signals importance, and executive backing gives a\nproject moral support, protection and resources.\n\n**Attributes of a good innovation process** (pp. 204–205)\n1. Generates a sufficient number of good ideas.\n2. Free of bottlenecks that impede development and frustrate innovators.\n3. Free of politics.\n4. Encourages calculated risk taking.\n5. Not arbitrary.\n6. Creates cheap failures.\n7. Channels resources to the worthiest projects.\n8. Involves people who understand the company's capabilities, strategy and customers.\n\n**Figure 12-1 — Innovation portfolio map** (pp. 205–207)\n- Horizontal axis: market/technology characteristics, **Mature → New**.\n- Vertical axis: technical challenge, uncertainty and economic opportunity, **Low → High**.\n- Each circle is a funded project; circle size = resources currently committed.\n- Lower-left = cautious: mature tech and market, least challenge, least risk, least upside.\n- Upper-right = aggressive: new markets, high technical risk, greatest economic opportunity.\n- Typical picture: the biggest circles sit cautiously in the lower-left; the small ones in the\n  upper-right carry the future.\n\n**Qualities of entrepreneurial new hires** (p. 208)\n- Comfortable with change.\n- View unmet needs as opportunities.\n- Adopt appropriate time horizons.\n- Comfortable with failure.\n- Experimental mindset.\n- Enjoy collaborative work.\n- Think and act like entrepreneurs.\nPlus: think beyond their own role, understand industry patterns, internalise the strategy and\nconnect it to their work. Managers and supervisors must be comfortable with participative\nmanagement.\n\n**The ambidextrous organization** (Tushman & O'Reilly, p. 209) — an organization that can\n\"get today's work done more effectively and anticipate tomorrow's discontinuities\". Christensen\nand Govindarajan: house discontinuous innovation in a separate area with its own goals, values\nand processes, so high-performing operators are not distracted and innovators are not judged by\noperating metrics."},{"ref":"12-entrepreneurial-spirit.md","label":"Keeping the entrepreneurial spirit (handbook ch 12)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **Any \"yes\" on the seven creative-edge questions** (below) → the culture needs a serious\n  evaluation and adjustment; start with environment, risk/learning and rewards.\n- **Portfolio skew**: most projects and resources in the lower-left → too risk-averse, doing too\n  little about future opportunities, technologies and markets. Most in the upper-right → very,\n  perhaps dangerously, aggressive. Define and articulate the balance you want.\n- **Set strategy boundaries for ideas** (e.g. an active-women's-apparel e-commerce site welcomes\n  ideas inside \"better connections with our customers\" and \"fast and accurate order\n  fulfillment\"). Set them right and creative energy focuses itself where the payoff is highest.\n- **Cannibalize yourself before someone else does.** Apple let the iPhone eat iPod revenue; Apple\n  as a whole gained.\n- **Don't let operational humdrum detach you from innovation.** Leaders can't make R&D decisions\n  in a vacuum or treat innovation as a mysterious force; know the technical hurdles and the\n  pipeline.\n- **Ad hoc innovation is waste.** Once you have grown past the informal stage you need a process\n  for generating, experimenting, evaluating, selecting and commercializing ideas.\n- **No friends or yes-people** on the leadership team or board; you need blunt feedback before\n  investing in an idea.\n- **Cut losses like a poker player**: you can't win without playing, but don't play every hand to\n  the end.\n- **Double-check assumptions** — a rosy case is a disaster if they are unrealistic.\n- **Hire managers who accept participative management**; anything else grows the hierarchy and\n  bureaucracy that kill the spirit. Liberal-arts backgrounds (big ideas, complexity, ambiguity,\n  writing, communication) can suit innovative roles.\n- **Separate the future business from the current one** structurally rather than asking one\n  team to be certain and efficient *and* exploratory.\n- Too many ideas outside strategy = money lost and idea generators' energy dissipated; the fix is\n  a clear mission, not fewer ideas. Reid Hoffman: blitzscaling orgs are chaotic, held together by\n  \"excitement about what's happening and the vision of a great future\"."},{"ref":"12-entrepreneurial-spirit.md","label":"Keeping the entrepreneurial spirit (handbook ch 12)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"**Culture check and repair** (p. 199)\n1. Answer the seven creative-edge questions honestly.\n2. If any is yes, audit the physical environment, risk taking/learning, and incentives/rewards.\n3. Apply the tactics above; establish a reward system using all four reward types.\n\n**Portfolio thinking** (pp. 205–207)\n1. List every funded project with its market/tech newness, technical challenge and resources.\n2. Plot them on the Figure 12-1 map, sizing circles by resources.\n3. Read the distribution (lower-left heavy = risk-averse; upper-right heavy = over-aggressive).\n4. Decide the risk-to-reward balance that suits the company.\n5. Articulate that balance to employees and investors; rebalance funding.\n6. Make each investment decision using the \"Tips for making good innovation decisions\" box.\n\n**Leader involvement routine** (pp. 203–204)\n1. Visit the research/product people regularly.\n2. Have lunch with project teams.\n3. Get to know key people one-on-one.\n4. Understand the technical hurdles between appealing ideas and commercialization.\nPayoffs: signals that innovation matters; lets you restate strategic direction and boundaries;\nkeeps you current on technology, customer and market trends."},{"ref":"12-entrepreneurial-spirit.md","label":"Keeping the entrepreneurial spirit (handbook ch 12)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Is the company losing its creative edge?** (p. 199; any yes = act)\n- Is our current success making us self-satisfied and complacent?\n- Are we inwardly focused?\n- Do we punish risk takers who fail?\n- Are creative people and new ideas unwelcome or unappreciated here?\n- Do we fail to reward acts of creativity?\n- Do we handle new ideas too bureaucratically?\n- Are hierarchy and its symbols creeping into our culture?\n\n**Innovation process scorecard** — rate the current process on each of the eight attributes\n(sufficient ideas; no bottlenecks; no politics; calculated risk; not arbitrary; cheap failures;\nresources to the worthiest; involves people who know capabilities, strategy and customers).\n\n**Portfolio questions** (p. 207)\n- Where do most projects and most resources sit on the map?\n- What would constitute a suitable risk-to-reward balance for this company?\n- Can you articulate that balance to employees and investors?\n\n**Box — Tips for making good innovation decisions** (p. 207)\n- Exclude friends and \"yes people\" from the leadership team and board.\n- Surround yourself with complementary skills and different ways of analysing; listen even when\n  you disagree.\n- Learn when to cut your losses.\n- Double-check your assumptions.\n\n**Entrepreneurial-hire screen** (p. 208) — score candidates on the seven qualities, on thinking\nbeyond their role, and (for managers) on comfort with participative management.\n\n**IBM warning signs** (Tushman & O'Reilly, pp. 198–199): inward focus; extensive consensus and\n\"push back\" procedures; arrogance bred by past success; a sense of entitlement to jobs without a\nquid pro quo. If these describe you, your best innovators are already looking elsewhere."},{"ref":"12-entrepreneurial-spirit.md","label":"Keeping the entrepreneurial spirit (handbook ch 12)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Honda and Toyota** — introduced the hybrid car, matching a real need for emissions and fuel\n  savings — large firms can still make the biggest innovation of a half-century.\n- **Corning / 3M** — decade-after-decade serial innovators — age is not the problem; culture is.\n- **IBM before Gerstner** — inward focus, consensus procedures, arrogance, entitlement — the\n  culture on which innovation \"fell on infertile soil\".\n- **Sail vs steam, gas vs electric lighting (1800s), piston vs jet (1940s)** — incumbents kept\n  improving the old technology — the complacency reflex.\n- **Apple iPhone vs iPod** — deliberate self-cannibalization; iPod revenue fell, Apple won.\n- **PayPal, Google, eBay, Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter** (Hoffman) — chaotic blitzscaling orgs\n  held together by excitement and vision — vision is the glue when roles are fuzzy.\n- **Henry Ford's assembly line; semiconductor process advances** — innovation customers never\n  see still builds the advantage."},{"ref":"12-entrepreneurial-spirit.md","label":"Keeping the entrepreneurial spirit (handbook ch 12)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Slavishly giving existing customers what they ask for and ceding breakthroughs to rivals.\n- Letting operations consume all leadership attention until innovation drops off the radar.\n- Punishing failed risk-takers; rewarding nothing; handling ideas bureaucratically.\n- Allowing hierarchy and its symbols to creep in; hiring command-and-control managers.\n- Running innovation ad hoc, politically or arbitrarily; funding by who shouts loudest.\n- A portfolio that is all lower-left (safe) or all upper-right (reckless).\n- Ideas without strategic boundaries: wasted money and demoralised idea people.\n- A board of friends; playing losing hands to the end; unchecked assumptions.\n- Asking the operating team to also own discontinuous innovation."},{"ref":"12-entrepreneurial-spirit.md","label":"Keeping the entrepreneurial spirit (handbook ch 12)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`tyranny of served markets` — existing customers' pressure to keep supplying the old business, which traps a vendor in incremental innovation.\n`paradox of success` — Pascale's observation that success plants the seeds of eventual failure via complacency.\n`idea champion` — an influential insider whose visible backing gives a project attention, protection and resources.\n`intrinsic vs extrinsic rewards` — motivation from the work itself (challenge, curiosity) vs from goals distinct from it (pay, vacation, recognition).\n`communities of interest` — cross-organization groups with shared passions who exchange ideas.\n`portfolio thinking` — managing all funded projects as a set with risk-versus-return characteristics.\n`ambidextrous organization` — one that executes today's business efficiently while anticipating tomorrow's discontinuities (Tushman & O'Reilly).\n`discontinuous innovation` — radically new concepts aimed at creating new markets, as opposed to incremental improvement."},{"ref":"12-entrepreneurial-spirit.md","label":"Keeping the entrepreneurial spirit (handbook ch 12)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- Growth challenges the entrepreneurial spirit: size brings specialization, communication\n  problems and control systems that frustrate creativity.\n- Once a company has customers, the tyranny of served markets can block innovation.\n- Success breeds complacency, the opposite of the entrepreneurial spirit.\n- Set the strategic direction within which innovation should happen.\n- Leaders keep the spirit alive by (1) preserving an innovation-friendly culture, (2) setting\n  strategic direction, (3) staying personally involved, (4) continually improving the\n  idea-to-commercialization process, (5) applying portfolio thinking, (6) hiring entrepreneurial\n  attitudes, and (7) building an ambidextrous organization that runs today and anticipates\n  tomorrow."},{"ref":"13-harvest-and-exit.md","label":"Harvest and exit (handbook ch 13)","heading":"Purpose","text":"When and how to turn the value locked inside the business into personal wealth: why founders\ncash out, the harvesting mechanisms (IPO, merger/acquisition, ESOP, management buyout, sale to a\nnew owner, partial \"shearing\"), timing, and how the business gets valued. Load when a user asks\n\"should I sell\", \"what's my company worth\", \"what are my exit options\", \"how do I hand over the\nbusiness\", or when a mature business enters a \"looking to the future\" stage."},{"ref":"13-harvest-and-exit.md","label":"Harvest and exit (handbook ch 13)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- Most entrepreneurs eventually harvest the monetary value locked in the enterprise; a minority\n  pass the business to family members (mentioned only in passing — the chapter does not detail\n  family succession).\n- Three dominant motivations: **diversify wealth** (net worth dangerously concentrated in one\n  basket), **the business has reached the end of its line** (at least under this leadership, or\n  the competitive wind has turned), and **the urge to begin anew** (loving the build phase, not\n  the operating phase). Retirement and an offer \"too good to refuse\" also trigger exits.\n- Choose the mechanism that is most timely and appropriate; each has distinct trade-offs. If you\n  only want *some* capital out, shear cash flow instead of selling.\n- **IPO**: few firms qualify; insiders face Rule 144 caps and underwriter lockups (up to six\n  months); costs, scrutiny and reporting deter founders even when VCs want the fast exit; the\n  upside is usually a higher price, especially when investor appetite is high.\n- **M&A**: far more harvests happen this way than by IPO. Three issues decide the outcome — how\n  the deal is valued, how payment is structured, and the seller's post-deal relationship.\n- **ESOP**: a tax-qualified employee retirement plan that buys the owner's shares gradually over\n  years — a market for shares where none exists, at the cost of annual appraisals and eventual\n  employee majority ownership, and with concentration risk for employees.\n- **Management buyout**: managers know the cash-generating potential best; typically financed as\n  a leveraged buyout on the company's own assets, or with the seller acting as lender.\n- **Sale to a new owner**: brokers connect you to qualified buyers; the asset-vs-stock choice\n  decides who keeps the liabilities.\n- **Timing**: buyer and investor mood swings between optimism and fear; the same business sells\n  for much more when buyers are giddy. Only the ESOP (sales spread over many years) is exempt.\n- **Valuation** sits at the heart of every mechanism except shearing. If you cannot answer \"what\n  is this company really worth\", you negotiate from weakness. Two reliable methods:\n  earnings-multiple and discounted cash flow; DCF is forward-looking and fairer to growing firms."},{"ref":"13-harvest-and-exit.md","label":"Harvest and exit (handbook ch 13)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**Why entrepreneurs cash out** (pp. 213–214)\n\n| Motivation | What it looks like |\n|---|---|\n| Diversify wealth | Most net worth is in one company; a technology shift, a powerful new competitor or a setback could wipe it out |\n| End of the line | The business has gone as far as it can under you; growth needs investment you won't make; or the competitive environment turns (hardware-store owner facing a national chain's buying power) |\n| Begin anew | You love creating something from nothing; once operations dominate your time you are happy to move on |\n| Also | Retirement; an offer too good to refuse |\n\n**Harvesting mechanisms — pros and cons** (pp. 214–220)\n\n| Mechanism | How it works | Advantages | Drawbacks |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| IPO | Public market for shares; founders and investors sell over time within regulatory limits (see ch. 9, app. D) | Often the highest price, especially with strong investor appetite; liquidity over time; VCs get a fast, high-return exit | Few firms qualify (too small, limited potential, moribund industry); Rule 144 monthly caps on restricted shares; lockup up to 6 months; deal costs, public scrutiny, reporting burden |\n| Merger / acquisition | Strategic merger or purchase by a company wanting your patents, product lines or capabilities | Most common harvest by far; can be a full cash exit | Complex; needs experienced legal and financial advisers; private-to-private deals need formal valuations; payment may be tied up in acquirer stock; seller rarely keeps a free hand |\n| ESOP | Tax-qualified retirement plan buys the owner's shares gradually over years; company may contribute part of the price | Creates a market for closely held shares; retirement plan and ownership stake for employees; gradual, tax-favoured cash-out | Independent appraisal every year (costly for a small firm); employees eventually own the majority; employees are doubly non-diversified (job + retirement in one company — Enron) |\n| Management buyout / LBO | Senior managers buy the company, borrowing against its assets; debt-to-equity can reach 10:1 | Buyers already know the business and industry; continuity of experienced management | Post-deal: unit sell-offs, asset sales, headcount and inventory cuts to service debt; junk-bond funding seldom available now, so the seller often takes a collateral-backed note and is paid over many years |\n| Sale to a new owner | Business broker finds qualified buyers (typically businesses valued up to $20M); commission mostly paid at close | Access to buyers you can't find yourself; clean break possible | Must negotiate asset sale vs stock sale (liabilities); broker fee |\n| Shearing (box, p. 219) | Pocket the cash flow not needed to maintain or expand the business instead of reinvesting it | Periodic capital for living standards, retirement income or diversification without selling; no valuation needed | Limits internally funded growth; if growth still matters, substitute debt for internal cash (changes debt-to-equity) |\n| Family transfer | Pass the business to family members | Keeps the business in the family | Not covered in this chapter beyond a mention |\n\n**Three issues in any merger or acquisition** (p. 216)\n1. **How the deal is valued** — different methods give different numbers.\n2. **How payment is structured** — cash, cash + acquirer stock, or debt; in a merger you may end\n   up holding stock of a newly formed company. Cash is ideal; every other form ties your capital\n   to the other company for a period. Not all stock deals are bad (Hotmail → 2.7M Microsoft\n   shares), but other acquirers' shares may be far less solid.\n3. **The seller's relationship with the merged/acquired company** — many deals require a period\n   of managerial involvement; approach with care, the acquirer won't give you a free hand.\n\n**Asset sale vs stock sale** (pp. 219–220)\n\n| | Asset sale | Stock sale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyer gets | Physical elements: employees, buildings, equipment, plus intangibles (trademarks, goodwill) | The company itself |\n| Liabilities | Stay with you (e.g. a lawsuit from an employee you hired) | Shift to the new owner |\n| Better for seller? | Usually not | Usually yes |\n\n**Best candidates for a leveraged buyout** (p. 218)\n- High levels of predictable free cash flow.\n- Few capital-spending requirements.\n- Little existing debt.\n- Substantial nonessential assets (can be sold to pay down debt).\n\n**Valuation methods** (pp. 220–222; detail in appendix C)\n\n| Method | Mechanics | Strength | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| IPO pricing | Underwriter weighs investor mood, comparable public P/E ratios, current and forecast performance, proprietary technology, growth potential; issues slightly below anticipated trading level | Captures market appetite | Unscientific; deliberate discount rewards first investors, not you |\n| Earnings-based (multiple) | EBIT (or EBITDA = EBIT + depreciation + amortization, the non-cash charges) × a multiple drawn from recent comparable sales. e.g. EBIT $2M × 5 = $10M | Simple; anchored to what buyers actually paid | Not forward-looking — shortchanges a fast-growing firm; multiples float with investor mood |\n| Discounted cash flow (DCF) | Forecast several years of cash flows; discount to present value with time-value-of-money maths | Future-oriented; \"captures all the elements that affect the value of the company\" (Copeland, Koller & Murrin) | Needs a credible forecast and usually professional help |"},{"ref":"13-harvest-and-exit.md","label":"Harvest and exit (handbook ch 13)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **Sell when investors and buyers are giddy with optimism**; the pendulum swings to fear and\n  the same business fetches far less. Watch the mood before selling shares or the company.\n- **Prefer cash**; stock or debt payment ties your capital to the acquirer. Judge the acquirer's\n  shares as an investment before accepting them.\n- **Never enter an M&A deal without experienced legal and financial advisers** — the typical\n  founder has done zero of these transactions.\n- **Post-deal management role**: accept only with eyes open; you will not run it the way you did.\n- **IPO reality check**: expect Rule 144 monthly sale caps on restricted shares plus a lockup of\n  up to six months; you cannot dump shares on day one.\n- **ESOP fit**: good when there is no public market and you want a gradual, tax-favoured exit\n  plus employee ownership; accept an annual independent appraisal and eventual employee majority.\n- **MBO/LBO fit**: strong when the business has predictable free cash flow, low capex, little\n  debt and nonessential assets. Debt-to-equity after the deal can hit 10:1; expect the buyers to\n  sell non-core units, aircraft and property and to cut heads, costs and inventory.\n- **If buyers can't borrow**, you become the lender: a collateral-backed note paid as principal\n  and interest over many years — your harvest is spread out and carries the buyers' risk.\n- **Broker threshold**: business brokers usually handle companies valued up to ~$20M; fees are\n  commission, mostly paid at closing. Find them via IBBA, ACG, AM&AA or APMAA listings.\n- **Sell stock, not assets, where you can** — an asset sale leaves liabilities with you.\n- **Multiples must come from recent comparable transactions**, never \"from outer space\"; the\n  multiple swings the value enormously (5× on $2M EBIT = $10M).\n- **Use EBITDA** rather than EBIT when depreciation/amortization are material, so the multiple\n  attaches to cash actually available to the owner.\n- **Fast-growing firm → insist on DCF**; a multiple of current earnings undervalues it.\n- **Learn enough valuation to work with an appraiser** — it matters only a few times in a career\n  but its impact on the harvest is huge (box, p. 222).\n- **Shearing rule**: if the company is as big as you can comfortably handle, pocket excess cash;\n  if you still want growth, replace the pocketed cash with debt and accept the higher leverage.\n- **LBO scale**: US leveraged buyouts still totalled $70.5B of company sales in 2016 — a\n  mainstream route, not a relic of the 1980s.\n- Non-US note: Rule 144, lockups, ESOP tax qualification and the broker associations named are\n  US-specific; a founder elsewhere needs the local equivalents (e.g. UK employee-ownership trusts,\n  local listing rules and lock-in periods)."},{"ref":"13-harvest-and-exit.md","label":"Harvest and exit (handbook ch 13)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"**Harvest planning sequence** (chapter flow)\n1. Name your motivation: diversify, end of the line, begin anew, retire, or an unrefusable offer.\n2. Decide full harvest vs partial: if you only want some capital, shear cash flow instead.\n3. Pick the mechanism that is timely and appropriate (table above); check the fit rules.\n4. Assemble advisers: legal, financial, a business appraiser, a broker if selling outright.\n5. Value the business (multiple and/or DCF) so you can negotiate from knowledge.\n6. For M&A: settle valuation method, payment structure and your post-deal role.\n7. Negotiate asset vs stock sale.\n8. Time the sale to buyer/investor optimism.\n\n**ESOP mechanics** (Macmillan Metal Works example, pp. 216–217)\n1. Attorney sets up the plan.\n2. Business appraiser values the company's shares.\n3. Owner sells a first tranche to the plan (Macmillan: 2,000 shares in year one).\n4. Qualified employees commit to buying specified numbers of shares each year; the company\n   contributes part of the purchase price.\n5. Proceeds go to the owner, who diversifies personal assets; repeat annually with a fresh\n   appraisal.\n\n**Post-LBO playbook the buyers will run** (p. 218): sell underperforming or non-fitting units;\nsell aircraft and nonessential property to pay down debt immediately; raise free cash by cutting\nheadcount, expenses and inventory."},{"ref":"13-harvest-and-exit.md","label":"Harvest and exit (handbook ch 13)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Motivation check**\n- Is most of my net worth concentrated in this one company?\n- Has the business gone as far as it can under my leadership? Would the next stage need\n  investment I don't want to make?\n- Is the competitive environment turning against us?\n- Do I prefer building to operating? Am I happy to move on now that operations dominate?\n- Do I want all my capital out at once, or periodic withdrawals (shearing)?\n\n**Mechanism selector**\n- Is there (or could there be) a public market for the shares? If not, rule out IPO.\n- Would a strategic buyer want our patents, product lines or capabilities?\n- Do we have a management team able and willing to buy? Does the company have predictable free\n  cash flow, low capex, little debt and nonessential assets (LBO fit)?\n- Do I want employees to own the company over time and can we afford annual appraisals (ESOP)?\n- Is the business below ~$20M in value (broker territory)?\n\n**M&A deal checklist** (p. 216)\n- Which valuation method is being used, and what do the alternatives say?\n- Payment mix: cash / acquirer stock / debt / new-company stock? How solid is the acquirer's stock?\n- What managerial involvement is required of me, for how long, with what authority?\n- Do I have experienced legal and financial advisers on the deal?\n\n**Sale structure checklist** (pp. 219–220)\n- Asset sale or stock sale? Which liabilities stay with me?\n- Broker engaged? Commission terms and timing?\n\n**Valuation prep checklist** (pp. 220–222)\n- Current EBIT and EBITDA (add back depreciation and amortization).\n- Recent comparable transactions and the multiples paid.\n- Multi-year cash-flow forecast and an appropriate discount rate for DCF.\n- Is the industry in or out of favour right now (multiple direction)?\n- Have I learned enough to brief and challenge a professional appraiser?\n\n**Timing check** (p. 220)\n- Are buyers/investors currently optimistic or fearful? What are comparable deals fetching?\n\n**Broker associations** (p. 219): International Business Brokers Association; Association for\nCorporate Growth; Alliance of Merger & Acquisition Advisors; Association of Professional Merger\nand Acquisitions Advisors."},{"ref":"13-harvest-and-exit.md","label":"Harvest and exit (handbook ch 13)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Sabeer Bhatia / Hotmail** — sold to Microsoft for 2.7 million Microsoft shares — stock\n  payment can be excellent when the acquirer's shares are solid.\n- **Macmillan Metal Works (hypothetical)** — 80 employees, founder owned 100%, wealth trapped;\n  ESOP gave employees a retirement plan and ownership while he cashed out gradually — ESOP as a\n  market for closely held shares.\n- **Enron employees (early 2000s)** — lost jobs and retirement savings at once — the double\n  non-diversification risk of employee ownership.\n- **Hardware-store owner vs a national chain** — competitive wind turning — a valid \"end of the\n  line\" signal.\n- **1980s raiders and junk bonds** — bought companies whose parts were worth more than the\n  whole; that debt source is seldom available now, so sellers often finance the buyers.\n- **Copeland, Koller & Murrin** (\"the bible of valuation\") — endorse DCF as the comprehensive\n  method — use it for growth businesses."},{"ref":"13-harvest-and-exit.md","label":"Harvest and exit (handbook ch 13)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Negotiating without knowing what the company is really worth.\n- Doing an M&A deal without experienced advisers.\n- Accepting acquirer stock without judging it as an investment; assuming a free hand post-deal.\n- Underestimating IPO restrictions (Rule 144, six-month lockup), costs and scrutiny.\n- Choosing an ESOP without budgeting annual appraisals or accepting employee majority ownership;\n  ignoring employees' concentration risk.\n- Financing a management buyout yourself without weighing a decade of repayment risk.\n- Selling assets and unknowingly keeping the liabilities.\n- Using an arbitrary multiple, or valuing a fast-growing company on current earnings only.\n- Selling during a period of buyer fear because you did not watch the market mood."},{"ref":"13-harvest-and-exit.md","label":"Harvest and exit (handbook ch 13)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`harvest` — converting the value locked in the enterprise into personal wealth via an exit mechanism.\n`shearing` — pocketing excess cash flow instead of reinvesting it; a partial harvest without a sale.\n`lockup agreement` — underwriter-imposed ban on pre-IPO shareholders selling for a period after the IPO (up to six months).\n`SEC Rule 144` — US rule capping how many restricted shares an insider may sell in any one-month period (see appendix D).\n`ESOP` — employee stock ownership plan: a tax-qualified retirement plan that buys the owner's shares on behalf of employees over years.\n`management buyout (MBO)` — purchase of the company by its senior managers.\n`leveraged buyout (LBO)` — a buyout financed largely with debt secured on the company's own assets.\n`junk (high-yield) bonds` — the outside debt that funded 1980s LBOs; seldom available to buyer groups today.\n`business broker` — intermediary who finds qualified buyers, usually for businesses valued up to ~$20M, paid by commission.\n`asset sale / stock sale` — selling the business's assets (liabilities stay with the seller) vs selling the shares (the whole company transfers).\n`EBIT / EBITDA` — earnings before interest and taxes / plus depreciation and amortization added back (non-cash).\n`multiple` — the number applied to EBIT/EBITDA, taken from recent comparable transactions.\n`discounted cash flow (DCF)` — valuation by forecasting future cash flows and discounting them to present value."},{"ref":"13-harvest-and-exit.md","label":"Harvest and exit (handbook ch 13)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- Entrepreneurs harvest to diversify wealth, to take the business to a higher level under\n  someone else, or to try something new.\n- An IPO gives the team liquidity over time but is available to very few enterprises.\n- For most companies, sale by acquisition is far more likely than an IPO; watch how the deal is\n  valued, how payment is structured (cash, stock, debt) and how any ongoing relationship is defined.\n- An ESOP is a tax-qualified retirement plan that buys the owner's shares over years — in effect,\n  a sale to the employees.\n- Management groups often buy out the founder, via a leveraged buyout or a debt arrangement with\n  the seller.\n- The earnings-multiple method values the firm at EBIT × a multiple, which must match what\n  investors recently paid for comparable companies.\n- DCF gives a better measure of value because it is future oriented."},{"ref":"A-financial-statements.md","label":"Financial statements (handbook appendix A)","heading":"Purpose","text":"A primer on the three essential financial statements — balance sheet, income statement and\ncash-flow statement — plus working capital, financial leverage and the debt/equity structure of\na firm. Load when a user asks \"what do these statements mean\", needs projected financials for a\nplan or pitch, confuses profit with cash, or wants a ratio explained or computed. Skip for\nusers with a business degree or senior finance experience (the book says so itself)."},{"ref":"A-financial-statements.md","label":"Financial statements (handbook appendix A)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- Statements answer four questions: what the company owns and owes; where revenue comes from\n  and how money was spent; how much profit it made; how financially healthy it is.\n- Three statements, three lenses: **balance sheet** = position at one moment; **income\n  statement** = results over a period; **cash-flow statement** = why cash changed over a period.\n- Readers: managers (performance, where to intervene), shareholders (capital stewardship),\n  investors (opportunities), lenders and suppliers (creditworthiness). Formats are standard\n  enough to compare one business with another.\n- Public companies must publish them (SEC full-disclosure rule); private ones need not, but\n  owners and bankers expect them. *Non-US note:* the filing body differs (Companies House,\n  CAC, etc.) but the same three statements are the norm everywhere.\n- Every transaction keeps **Assets = Liabilities + Owners' Equity** in balance.\n- Balance-sheet values are **historic cost**, not market value — the lesser of two evils versus\n  annual appraisals nobody would trust. Only cash, receivables and payables sit near cash value.\n- **Profit is not cash.** A November sale booked as revenue but paid next period raises profit\n  and lowers cash flow. The cash-flow statement translates net income into a cash basis.\n- Insight comes from **multi-period comparison** (trends, turnarounds), not one statement.\n- **Leverage** magnifies returns on owners' capital in both directions; the CEO's job is a\n  realistic debt/equity balance, not maximum debt.\n- A startup's statements will be simple; learn the rest with your accountant as you grow\n  (further reading: *HBR Guide to Finance Basics for Managers*)."},{"ref":"A-financial-statements.md","label":"Financial statements (handbook appendix A)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"### The three statements at a glance\n| Statement | Question answered | Time frame | Governing equation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Balance sheet | What do we own, what do we owe, what is left for owners? | Snapshot at a date (month/quarter/fiscal-year end) | Assets = Liabilities + Owners' Equity |\n| Income statement (P&L) | Did we make a profit, and what did it cost to make it? | Cumulative over a defined period | Revenues − Expenses = Net Income (or Net Loss) |\n| Cash-flow statement | Why did cash change, and can we pay bills as they fall due? | Over a period: opening cash → closing cash | Cash Flow from Profit + Other Sources of Cash − Uses of Cash = Change in Cash |\n\n### Table A-1 — Amalgamated Hat Rack balance sheet as of December 31, 2017\n| Line | 2017 | 2016 | Increase (Decrease) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| **Assets** | | | |\n| Cash and marketable securities | $652,500 | 486,500 | 166,000 |\n| Accounts receivable | 555,000 | 512,000 | 43,000 |\n| Inventory | 835,000 | 755,000 | 80,000 |\n| Prepaid expenses | 123,000 | 98,000 | 25,000 |\n| Total current assets | 2,165,500 | 1,851,500 | 314,000 |\n| Gross property, plant, and equipment | 2,100,000 | 1,900,000 | 200,000 |\n| Less: Accumulated depreciation | 333,000 | 290,500 | (42,500) |\n| Net property, plant, and equipment | 1,767,000 | 1,609,500 | 157,500 |\n| **Total assets** | $3,932,500 | 3,461,000 | 471,500 |\n| **Liabilities and owners' equity** | | | |\n| Accounts payable | $450,000 | 430,000 | 20,000 |\n| Accrued expenses | 98,000 | 77,000 | 21,000 |\n| Income tax payable | 17,000 | 9,000 | 8,000 |\n| Short-term debt | 435,000 | 500,000 | (65,000) |\n| Total current liabilities | 1,000,000 | 1,016,000 | (16,000) |\n| Long-term debt | 750,000 | 660,000 | 90,000 |\n| Total liabilities | 1,750,000 | 1,676,000 | 74,000 |\n| Contributed capital | 900,000 | 850,000 | 50,000 |\n| Retained earnings | 1,282,500 | 935,000 | 347,500 |\n| Total owners' equity | 2,182,500 | 1,785,000 | 397,500 |\n| **Total liabilities and owners' equity** | $3,932,500 | $3,461,000 | $471,500 |\n\nBook's reading: owners' equity grew **$397,500** in a year — the company is moving in a positive\ndirection. Net working capital 2017 = 2,165,500 − 1,000,000 = **$1,165,500** (2016: $835,500).\n\n**Balance-sheet anatomy (order and meaning of the blocks)**\n| Block | Contents | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Current assets | Cash & marketable securities, receivables, inventory, prepaid expenses | Convertible to cash within one year; listed first, most liquid first |\n| Fixed (plant) assets | Gross PP&E − accumulated depreciation = net book value | Hard to turn into cash; everything except land depreciates |\n| Goodwill / intangibles | Price paid above fair value of an acquired company's assets; brands, reputation, patents | Goodwill is \"strictly an accounting fiction\"; brands and patents may have real value |\n| Total assets | Current + fixed (+ intangibles) | |\n| Current liabilities | Accounts payable, accrued salaries and expenses, accrued income taxes, short-term IOUs, **this year's instalment of long-term loans** | Claims due within a year |\n| Long-term liabilities | Bonds, mortgages | Contractual obligation for interest and principal |\n| Owners' equity | Contributed capital (cash received for shares) + retained earnings (accumulated net profit after dividends) | Also shareholders'/stockholders' equity; = total assets − total liabilities |\n\n### Table A-2 — Amalgamated Hat Rack income statement, period ending December 31, 2017\n| Line | Amount |\n|---|---:|\n| Retail sales | $2,200,000 |\n| Corporate sales | 1,000,000 |\n| Total sales revenue | 3,200,000 |\n| Less: Cost of goods sold | 1,600,000 |\n| **Gross profit** | 1,600,000 |\n| Less: Operating expenses | 800,000 |\n| Less: Depreciation expenses | 42,500 |\n| **Earnings before interest and taxes (EBIT)** | 757,500 |\n| Less: Interest expense | 110,000 |\n| **Earnings before income taxes** | 647,500 |\n| Less: Income taxes | 300,000 |\n| **Net income** | $347,500 |\n\n**Income-statement anatomy (the revenue waterfall)**\n| Line | What it contains | Formula |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenues | Sales of products/services; plus other revenue (investment or interest income on cash) | Sum of revenue lines |\n| Cost of goods sold (COGS) | Raw materials (the lumber) + cost of turning them into finished goods incl. direct labor | |\n| Gross profit | \"Roughest estimation\" of profitability | Revenue − COGS |\n| Operating expenses | Admin salaries, rent, sales & marketing — costs not directly attributable to making the product (advertising yes, lumber no) | |\n| Depreciation | Non-cash expense recognising the \"consumption\" of an asset over time (laptop ≈ one-fifth of value per year → expensed over 5 years, not all in year 1) | Asset cost ÷ useful life |\n| EBIT (operating earnings) | | Gross profit − operating expenses − depreciation |\n| Interest expense | Interest on loans | |\n| Earnings before income taxes | | EBIT − interest |\n| Income taxes | Government levy on corporate income; the final charge | |\n| Net income (net earnings, net profit) | The bottom line; positive = profit | EBT − income taxes |\n\n### Table A-3 — Amalgamated Hat Rack multiperiod income statement, 2015–2017\n| Line | 2017 | 2016 | 2015 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Retail sales | $2,200,000 | 2,000,000 | 1,720,000 |\n| Corporate sales | 1,000,000 | 1,000,000 | 1,100,000 |\n| Total sales revenue | 3,200,000 | 3,000,000 | 2,820,000 |\n| Less: Cost of goods sold | 1,600,000 | 1,550,000 | 1,400,000 |\n| Gross profit | 1,600,000 | 1,450,000 | 1,420,000 |\n| Less: Operating expenses | 800,000 | 810,000 | 812,000 |\n| Less: Depreciation expenses | 42,500 | 44,500 | 45,500 |\n| EBIT | 757,500 | 595,500 | 562,500 |\n| Less: Interest expense | 110,000 | 110,000 | 150,000 |\n| Earnings before income taxes | 647,500 | 485,500 | 412,500 |\n| Less: Income taxes | 300,000 | 194,200 | 165,000 |\n| Net income | $347,500 | 291,300 | 247,500 |\n\nBook's reading: retail sales grow steadily (+16.3%, +10.0%); corporate sales stagnate and slip\n[section truncated]"},{"ref":"A-financial-statements.md","label":"Financial statements (handbook appendix A)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **Always compare with prior periods.** The \"Increase (Decrease)\" column and a 3–5 year P&L\n  are where the story is; one statement says little.\n- **Rising owners' equity** = moving in a positive direction. **Flat operating expenses while\n  sales grow** = management holding the line on cost. **Falling interest expense** = a loan paid off.\n- **Working capital: neither too little nor too much.** Too little → can't pay bills or seize\n  opportunities. Too much → carrying cost (financed by interest-bearing loans) eats profit.\n  The right level depends on industry and plans.\n- **Inventory: buffer vs. burden.** Stock fills orders instantly and cushions stoppages, but\n  every excess widget adds financing cost and obsolescence risk — PC finished goods lose\n  about **2% of value per day**.\n- **Read balance-sheet values as historic cost** except cash, receivables and payables; land\n  bought in downtown San Francisco in 1992 is carried at what was paid. **Discount goodwill.**\n- **Cash tight → budget conservatively; cash flush → look for new investments.**\n- **Solvency = paying bills as they come due**; what matters is whether receivables are turning\n  into cash. Small-company owners feel cash flow \"almost every day\".\n- **Depreciation is an expense but not a cash outflow** — add it back when converting profit to cash.\n- **Leverage at 4× asset/equity turns a 25% move into ±100% on your capital.** Use it when\n  returns are reliable and interest is deductible; never maximise debt.\n- **Expect to pay for risk:** high debt-to-equity → ~14% borrowing cost vs 10–12%, plus a higher\n  return demanded by equity investors."},{"ref":"A-financial-statements.md","label":"Financial statements (handbook appendix A)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"**Read a balance sheet**\n1. Confirm Total assets = Total liabilities + Owners' equity.\n2. Walk current assets (most liquid first) → fixed assets net of depreciation → intangibles;\n   then current liabilities (incl. this year's loan instalment) → long-term debt → contributed\n   capital and retained earnings.\n3. Compute net working capital and debt-to-equity; compare both with the prior year.\n\n**Build an income statement (revenue waterfall)**\n1. Revenue by stream (retail, corporate…) → total sales revenue.\n2. − COGS → gross profit.\n3. − operating expenses − depreciation → EBIT.\n4. − interest → earnings before taxes.\n5. − income taxes → net income.\n\n**Derive the cash-flow statement from net income (indirect method)**\n1. Start with net income; add back depreciation.\n2. Operating assets (receivables, inventory, prepaid): subtract each increase, add each decrease.\n3. Operating liabilities (payables, accrued, tax payable): add each increase, subtract each decrease.\n4. Sum → cash flow from operations.\n5. Investing: + asset sales − capital expenditures. Financing: + borrowings + stock issued − repayments − dividends.\n6. Sum the three sections → change in cash; check it equals the balance-sheet cash delta.\n\n**Compute a leveraged return** — equity E, borrowed B, asset A = E + B, exit value V:\nowner's proceeds = V − B; return on equity = (V − B − E) ÷ E vs asset return (V − A) ÷ A."},{"ref":"A-financial-statements.md","label":"Financial statements (handbook appendix A)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Minimum line items for a startup's statements**\n- Balance sheet: cash; receivables; inventory; prepaid expenses; gross fixed assets; accumulated\n  depreciation; payables; accrued expenses; tax payable; short-term debt; long-term debt;\n  contributed capital; retained earnings.\n- Income statement: revenue by stream; COGS; operating expenses; depreciation; interest; taxes.\n- Cash-flow: opening cash; the change in each balance-sheet line; capex; asset sales;\n  borrowings/repayments; stock issued; dividends; closing cash.\n\n**Statement health check (each period)**\n- Does the balance sheet balance, and does closing cash tie to the cash-flow statement?\n- Did owners' equity rise or fall, and why (profit, dividends, new capital)?\n- Is net working capital enough to pay bills and fund growth — without idle, financed surplus?\n- Is inventory growing faster than sales? What does holding it cost, and is it ageing?\n- Are receivables turning into cash, or is profit piling up as unpaid invoices?\n- Which revenue streams grow, which stagnate? Are operating expenses held flat?\n- What is the debt-to-equity ratio, what rate is it costing, and would equity survive a 25% fall?\n- Which balance-sheet values are historic cost and materially off market value?"},{"ref":"A-financial-statements.md","label":"Financial statements (handbook appendix A)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Ken Olsen, Digital Equipment Corporation (late 1950s)** — engineer with no finance; copied\n  balance-sheet and P&L layouts from Samuelson's economics textbook for his VC plan and got\n  funded — the layouts are learnable; don't let them block a pitch.\n- **Smartphone-case maker** — $1M of plastic on 30-day terms: inventory +$1M, payables +$1M;\n  borrows $100,000: cash and liabilities both +$100,000 — every transaction stays balanced.\n- **Fire example** — $3M assets − $2M liabilities = $1M equity; $500,000 of uninsured assets\n  burn → equity $500,000, liabilities unchanged — owners absorb losses first.\n- **XYZ's San Francisco land** — carried at the $700,000 paid in 1992 — historic cost hides value.\n- **Personal-computer industry** — finished goods lose ~2% of value per day — inventory melts.\n- **Amalgamated Hat Rack** — retail growth, flat opex, falling interest, equity +$397,500, cash\n  +$166,000 — a healthy small manufacturer to benchmark against.\n- **Highly leveraged borrower** — pays 14% vs 10–12% — the market charges for balance-sheet risk."},{"ref":"A-financial-statements.md","label":"Financial statements (handbook appendix A)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Treating profit as cash (and being surprised by insolvency while \"profitable\").\n- Reading historic-cost balance-sheet values as market values.\n- Counting goodwill as a real asset.\n- Letting working capital or inventory drift too high (financing cost, obsolescence) or too low\n  (missed bills, missed opportunities).\n- Maximising debt because interest is deductible; forgetting interest is due in bad years too.\n- Judging from one period instead of a multi-period view.\n- Ignoring the cash-flow statement — \"the least used and understood\" of the three."},{"ref":"A-financial-statements.md","label":"Financial statements (handbook appendix A)","heading":"Key terms","text":"(Statement line items are defined in the anatomy tables above.)\n`accounts receivable` / `accounts payable` — money owed to the company by customers / owed by it to suppliers.\n`current assets / current liabilities` — convertible to cash / due within one year.\n`net working capital` — current assets − current liabilities; money tied up in short-term operations.\n`accumulated depreciation` — cumulative reduction in the stated value of fixed assets (all but land).\n`goodwill` — purchase price paid above fair value of an acquired company's assets.\n`contributed capital` / `retained earnings` — capital received for shares / net profits accumulated after dividends.\n`historic cost` — recording items at what was paid, not market value.\n`depreciation` — non-cash expense estimating an asset's consumption over its useful life.\n`EBIT` — earnings before interest and taxes; operating earnings.\n`profit margin` — net income ÷ revenue.\n`capital expenditures` — cash invested in plant, property and equipment.\n`solvency` — the ability to pay bills as they come due.\n`financial leverage` — using borrowed money to acquire an asset; magnifies return on owners' capital.\n`financial structure` — the balance of debt and equity on the balance sheet.\n`debt-to-equity ratio` — the leverage measure creditors and investors price into rates and required returns."},{"ref":"A-financial-statements.md","label":"Financial statements (handbook appendix A)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- The three statements are the essential documents of business; managers, owners, investors\n  and lenders all read them. Assets = Liabilities + Owners' Equity; Revenues − Expenses = Net\n  Income; cash from profit + other sources − uses = change in cash.\n- Watch working capital, understand leverage, and seek a realistic debt/equity balance.\n- There is far more to interpretation than this primer, but a startup's statements will be\n  simple — learn the rest with your accountant or financial officer as the company grows."},{"ref":"B-breakeven.md","label":"Breakeven analysis (handbook appendix B)","heading":"Purpose","text":"How many units (or how much revenue) must a new business, product line or investment sell\nbefore it starts making money — and how does the fixed/variable cost mix shape the risk? Load\nwhen a user asks \"how much do I need to sell\", tests a price, sizes a fixed investment, or\ncompares a high-fixed-cost model with a pay-as-you-go one. Pairs with appendix A (income\nstatement) and the business-plan financials chapters."},{"ref":"B-breakeven.md","label":"Breakeven analysis (handbook appendix B)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- Breakeven is the volume at which total contribution from a product line or investment covers\n  its total fixed costs; after that, every unit's contribution goes straight to profit.\n- Three prerequisite concepts: **fixed costs, variable costs, contribution margin**.\n- The number is not the decision: compare breakeven volume with market demand and competitors'\n  shares to judge whether selling that much is realistic — and how quickly. It doubles as a\n  what-if tool for price/volume relationships (discounts, volume tiers).\n- The simple model assumes costs are cleanly fixed or variable and constant across volume;\n  real businesses have step costs, hybrid costs and price erosion — adjust for them.\n- **Operating leverage** (fixed relative to variable cost) drives both the upside past breakeven\n  and the losses if it is never reached. Breakeven is the floor; the goal is profit."},{"ref":"B-breakeven.md","label":"Breakeven analysis (handbook appendix B)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"### Cost classification\n| Concept | Definition | Book examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fixed costs | Stay mostly the same no matter how many units are sold | Insurance, management salaries, rent or lease payments — the same at 10,000 or 20,000 units |\n| Variable costs | Change with the number of units produced and sold | Utilities, labor, raw materials, sales commissions |\n| Contribution margin (per unit) | What every sold unit contributes toward paying fixed costs | Net unit revenue − variable (direct) cost per unit |\n\n### Core formulas (book)\n| Name | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Unit contribution margin | Net revenue per unit − Variable cost per unit |\n| Breakeven volume (units) | Fixed costs (or investment amount) ÷ Unit contribution margin |\n| Contribution to profit (each unit after breakeven) | Unit net revenue − Unit variable cost |\n\n### Worked example — Amalgamated Hat Rack plastic wall-mounted hat rack (p. 242–243)\n| Input | Value |\n|---|---:|\n| Price per unit | $75 |\n| Variable cost per unit | $22 |\n| Fixed cost (plastic extruder) | $100,000 |\n| Unit contribution margin | $75 − $22 = **$53** |\n| Breakeven volume | $100,000 ÷ $53 = 1,886.8 → **1,887 units** (round up) |\nThe decision the book then poses: is 1,887 *additional* hat racks realistic, and how quickly?\n\n### Derived formulas (standard extensions not in the book's text; needed for a calculator)\n| Name | Formula | Hat rack |\n|---|---|---:|\n| Contribution margin ratio | Unit contribution margin ÷ Price | 53 ÷ 75 = 70.7% |\n| Breakeven revenue | Fixed costs ÷ CM ratio (= exact breakeven units × price) | $141,509 |\n| Profit at volume Q | Q × UCM − Fixed costs | 3,000 units → 159,000 − 100,000 = $59,000 |\n| Volume for target pre-tax profit P | (Fixed costs + P) ÷ UCM | P = $50,000 → 2,831 units |\n| Volume for target after-tax profit P at tax rate t | (Fixed costs + P ÷ (1 − t)) ÷ UCM | P = $50,000, t = 30% → 3,235 units |\n| Margin of safety | (Planned units − Breakeven units) ÷ Planned units | plan 3,000 → 37.1% |\n| Degree of operating leverage at Q | (Q × UCM) ÷ (Q × UCM − Fixed costs) | 3,000 units → 159,000 ÷ 59,000 = 2.7× |\n| Step fixed cost | Recompute breakeven per volume band with that band's fixed costs (e.g. rent × 1.5 above the first facility's capacity) | — |\nThe book phrases breakeven as \"after-tax contribution\" covering fixed costs; at breakeven profit\nand tax are both zero, so the pre-tax formula gives the same answer — tax only matters for\ntarget-profit volumes.\n\n### Operating leverage spectrum\n| | High operating leverage | Low operating leverage |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cost structure | Fixed costs high relative to variable | Fixed costs low relative to the total cost of each unit |\n| Book example | Pharmaceuticals: a bottle costs < $1 to make and pack, sells for $100 → $99 contribution per bottle, but ~$400 million of fixed product-development cost up front (≈4.04 million bottles to break even) | Consulting: minimal equipment and fixed expense; most cost is consultant fees that vary with hours billed |\n| After breakeven | Profits \"can be extraordinary\" | Steady, modest profit per unit |\n| If breakeven is never reached | Substantial losses — risky | Losses limited |"},{"ref":"B-breakeven.md","label":"Breakeven analysis (handbook appendix B)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **Round breakeven units up** — 1,886.8 units means 1,887 sales.\n- **Use *net* revenue per unit** (after discounts and returns; commissions are variable cost).\n- **If breakeven exceeds a realistic share of demand, don't invest** — or change price, unit\n  cost or the size of the fixed investment until it does. Ask \"how quickly?\" as well as \"how many?\".\n- **Lower unit variable cost → larger contribution** → faster breakeven, fatter profit after it.\n- **Step costs:** rent fixed up to a volume, then ~50% more for a second facility — model per band.\n- **Hybrid costs:** labor is often part fixed, part variable — split it before classifying.\n- **Volume discounts cut contribution per unit** — recompute breakeven at the discounted price.\n- **High operating leverage is great after breakeven and dangerous before it** — balance fixed\n  and variable costs against how certain demand is."},{"ref":"B-breakeven.md","label":"Breakeven analysis (handbook appendix B)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. List fixed costs, including the investment being evaluated (equipment, development, launch).\n2. Estimate variable cost per unit (materials, direct labor, utilities, commissions).\n3. Set net revenue (price) per unit.\n4. Unit contribution margin = price − variable cost per unit.\n5. Breakeven volume = fixed costs ÷ unit contribution margin; round up.\n6. Compare with market demand and competitors' shares: is the volume achievable, and how fast?\n7. Stress-test: step fixed costs, hybrid costs, price discounts at higher volume.\n8. Project profit at the planned volume and check the operating-leverage exposure."},{"ref":"B-breakeven.md","label":"Breakeven analysis (handbook appendix B)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Breakeven inputs** — fixed costs (rent/lease, insurance, management salaries, the one-off\ninvestment); variable cost per unit (materials, direct labor, utilities, commissions); net price\nper unit after expected discounts; planned volume; addressable demand; competitors' shares.\n\n**Questions before committing**\n- Which costs are truly fixed at every plausible volume, and where do they step up?\n- Which \"fixed\" costs (labor especially) actually flex with output?\n- At what volume will we have to discount, and what does that do to contribution per unit?\n- Is the breakeven volume a realistic slice of demand given competitors' shares?\n- How many months to breakeven, and can we fund the losses until then?\n- If demand comes in 25% under plan, how far below breakeven are we (operating leverage)?"},{"ref":"B-breakeven.md","label":"Breakeven analysis (handbook appendix B)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Amalgamated Hat Rack** — $75 price, $22 variable cost, $100,000 extruder → 1,887 units to\n  break even — the calculation is easy; the judgement is whether 1,887 extra sales are realistic.\n- **Pharmaceutical company** — $99 contribution per $100 bottle but ~$400M fixed development\n  cost — high operating leverage: extraordinary profit after breakeven, ruinous losses before.\n- **Consulting firm** — low fixed cost, fees vary with hours billed — low operating leverage: safer, less explosive."},{"ref":"B-breakeven.md","label":"Breakeven analysis (handbook appendix B)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Treating every cost as cleanly fixed or variable when it is hybrid or stepped.\n- Assuming price and contribution stay constant as volume rises (ignoring discounting).\n- Reading breakeven as the goal rather than the floor.\n- Taking on high fixed costs without demand certainty — operating leverage cuts both ways.\n- Answering \"how many?\" without \"how quickly?\" or whether the market can absorb it."},{"ref":"B-breakeven.md","label":"Breakeven analysis (handbook appendix B)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`fixed costs` — costs that stay roughly constant regardless of units sold.\n`variable costs` — costs that rise and fall with units produced and sold.\n`contribution margin` — net unit revenue − variable cost per unit; what each sale contributes toward fixed costs, then profit.\n`breakeven volume` — fixed costs ÷ unit contribution margin; the units needed to cover fixed costs.\n`operating leverage` — the ratio of fixed to variable costs; high when fixed costs dominate."},{"ref":"B-breakeven.md","label":"Breakeven analysis (handbook appendix B)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- Breakeven volume = fixed costs ÷ (net unit revenue − unit variable cost).\n- Compare it with market demand and competitors' shares before committing; ask how quickly.\n- Adjust the simple model for step costs, hybrid costs and price erosion.\n- Operating leverage amplifies profit past breakeven and loss before it; find the right balance\n  between fixed and variable costs."},{"ref":"C-valuation.md","label":"Valuation (handbook appendix C)","heading":"Purpose","text":"What a business is worth and how appraisers reach a number: four asset-based methods, three\nearnings-multiple methods and discounted cash flow, each with its bias and its inputs. Load when a\nuser is buying a business, selling their own, pricing shares for an ESOP or a round, or asks\n\"what is my company worth / how do multiples work\". Every worked example reuses the Amalgamated\nHat Rack statements from appendix A (tables A-1 and A-2)."},{"ref":"C-valuation.md","label":"Valuation (handbook appendix C)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- Every entrepreneur eventually values a business — buying one or selling their own. Overpay and\n  the return disappoints; undervalue what you sell and you shortchange yourself without knowing it.\n- Valuation is neither easy nor exact and is normally expert territory; the founder's job is to know\n  each method's strengths and weaknesses well enough to negotiate with the experts.\n- Two structural reasons value is never certain: (1) different methods consistently give different\n  answers even when meticulously calculated; (2) every method is only as good as its inputs, which\n  are often incomplete, unreliable or projections.\n- A business is worth different amounts to different parties (the Hemingway first edition that\n  completes a collector's set). Acquirers rationally pay above the market value of existing shares\n  when the target completes something they already own — e.g. a small high-tech firm whose\n  technology leverages the acquirer's other operations.\n- Public markets (NASDAQ, NYSE) make value transparent: share price × shares outstanding\n  approximates value at a point in time. Valuation problems arise where no public price exists —\n  closely held businesses (a few owners) and operating units of public companies.\n- Three families: **asset-based** (what it owns), **earnings-based** (what it earned, capitalised by a\n  comparable multiple) and **DCF** (what it will earn, discounted to today).\n- Equity value ≠ enterprise value. Enterprise value = equity + debt — a house sells for the\n  mortgage plus the owner's equity, not the equity alone.\n- Because the methods disagree, most appraisers run more than one and triangulate."},{"ref":"C-valuation.md","label":"Valuation (handbook appendix C)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**The valuation map — three approaches, eight methods**\n\n| Family | Method | Formula / approach | Key inputs | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Asset-based | Equity book value | Total assets − Total liabilities | Balance sheet | Quick floor; owner's \"at least what we invested\" |\n| Asset-based | Adjusted book value | Book value with assets and intangibles restated to appraised market values | Balance sheet + appraisals | Asset-heavy firms, LBOs, stale property/inventory values |\n| Asset-based | Liquidation value | Net cash from a quick sale of assets − all liabilities settled | Balance sheet + fire-sale haircuts | Distress, wind-down, lender's floor |\n| Asset-based | Replacement value | Cost to reproduce the business's assets | Current cost to rebuild each asset | Build-vs-buy sanity check; usually a ceiling for a buyer |\n| Earnings-based | P/E (earnings) multiple | Equity value = Net income × comparable P/E; EV = Equity + interest-bearing debt | Normalised net income; comparables' P/E | Comparable public firms or recent private deals exist |\n| Earnings-based | EBIT multiple | Value = EBIT × comparable EBIT multiple | Normalised EBIT | Comparables differ in leverage or tax position |\n| Earnings-based | EBITDA multiple | Value = (EBIT + D&A) × comparable EBITDA multiple | Normalised EBITDA | Comparables also differ in depreciation/amortisation |\n| Cash-flow-based | Discounted cash flow | PV of forecast cash flows + PV of terminal value at the buyer's cost of capital | Multi-year forecast, terminal value, discount rate | Buyer valuing what it could earn under new ownership |\n\n### Asset-based methods (book pp. 247–249)\n\n**1. Equity book value**\n- Formula: `Equity book value = Total assets − Total liabilities` (identical to total owners'\n  equity on the balance sheet).\n- Worked example (Amalgamated Hat Rack, 2017, table A-1): $3,932,500 − $1,750,000 = **$2,182,500**.\n- Strength: quick, easy, a common industry shorthand; an owner argues the firm is worth at least\n  book because that is what was invested.\n- Weakness: assets sit at historical cost. Receivables may be uncollectible; inventory may be\n  spoiled or obsolete (or undervalued); land booked in 1995 in the heart of San Francisco may be\n  worth 10–20× its book figure.\n\n**2. Adjusted book value**\n- Approach: restate each balance-sheet asset to a realistic market level and appraise listed\n  intangibles (goodwill, patents) at what a buyer would actually pay.\n- Worked example (leveraged buyout of a major retail chain): equity book value $1.3 billion →\n  **$2.2 billion** after inventory and property were restated to appraised values — **+69%**.\n- Goodwill caution: usually an accounting fiction created when one company buys another at a\n  premium to book value; to the next buyer it may be worth nothing.\n\n**3. Liquidation value**\n- Approach: the net cash realised if assets were disposed of in a quick sale and every liability\n  paid off or settled.\n- Bias: inventory and fixed assets fetch less in a hurried sale than a deliberate one → a floor.\n\n**4. Replacement value**\n- Approach: estimate the cost of reproducing the business's assets.\n- Bias: a buyer may not want to replicate every asset in the package, so replacement value often\n  exceeds what the buyer would pay → a rough ceiling.\n\n**Shared verdict on asset-based methods**\n- Strengths: easy, inexpensive to calculate, easy to understand.\n- Weaknesses: equity book value and liquidation value fail to reflect actual market values; all four\n  ignore the intangible value of a going concern — human knowledge, skill and reputation.\n\n### Earnings-based methods (book pp. 249–253)\n\nPrinciple: **capitalise earnings** — multiply an income-statement earnings figure (net income,\npre-tax earnings, EBIT or EBITDA) by a multiple observed in comparable companies.\n\n**5. Price-earnings (P/E) multiple**\n- Public company: `Equity value = Share price × Shares outstanding`; `Enterprise value = Equity\n  value + Debt`.\n- Read the multiple off a comparable: `Multiple = Share price ÷ Current earnings per share`.\n  XYZ Corp: $50 ÷ $5 = **10** (\"trading at ten times earnings\").\n- Apply it: `Share price = Current earnings × Multiple`; for a whole private firm\n  `Equity value = Net income × Comparable multiple`.\n- Worked example (Amalgamated): comparable public firm Acme Corp trades at P/E **8** (similar in\n  industry and size); a private firm in the same industry was recently acquired at ~**8** → the\n  multiple is in the ballpark. Net income (table A-2) $347,500 × 8 = **$2,780,000 equity value**.\n- Enterprise value: `EV = Equity value + Interest-bearing debt` (short- plus long-term debt).\n  Book: $2,780,000 + $1,175,000 = **$3,955,000**. (The prose quotes 2017 interest-bearing debt as\n  $1,185,000, which would give $3,965,000 — a book inconsistency; a calculator should read the\n  figure straight from the balance sheet.)\n- Derived, not in the book: the 8× value sits ~27% above equity book value; implied EV/EBIT ≈ 5.2×\n  ($3,955,000 ÷ $757,500).\n- Reliability rests on (a) truly comparable firms — no two are alike, so valuation is \"part science\n  and part art\" — and (b) a normalised earnings figure (checklist below).\n\n**6. EBIT multiple**\n- Problem it fixes: two firms with identical operating results can report very different net income\n  because of **financing** (interest expense) and **taxes**, neither of which says much about future\n  wealth-producing capacity. A P/E lifted from a differently financed comparable misprices the target.\n\n**Table C-1 — Hypothetical income statements of Amalgamated Hat Rack and Acme Corporation** (book p. 253)\n\n| Line | Amalgamated | Acme |\n|---|---|---|\n| Earnings before interest and taxes (EBIT) | $757,500 | $757,500 |\n| Less: interest expense | $110,000 | $350,000 |\n| Earnings before income tax | $647,500 | $407,400* |\n| Less: income tax | $300,000 | $187,000 |\n| Net income | $347,500 | $220,500 |\n\n*Printed as $407,400; the arithmetic ($757,500 − $350,000, and $220,500 + $187,000) gives\n[section truncated]"},{"ref":"C-valuation.md","label":"Valuation (handbook appendix C)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- If comparable public companies or recent private deals with known multiples exist → use an\n  earnings-based method; it is generally superior to asset-based methods.\n- If comparables differ from the target in leverage or tax → use EBIT, not P/E. If they also differ\n  in depreciation/amortisation or asset age → use EBITDA.\n- If you are the buyer and can forecast cash flows under your ownership → run a DCF at your own\n  cost of capital; it is the only method that prices what *you* could make of the business.\n- If the business is asset-heavy or the balance sheet is stale (old land, under-recorded\n  inventory) → adjust book value before trusting any book figure; the retail LBO moved 69% on\n  appraisal alone.\n- Treat liquidation value as the floor and replacement value as the buyer's ceiling.\n- Always normalise earnings before applying a multiple: strip one-off write-offs and windfall\n  sales, restate owner/family salaries to market, check maintenance and depreciation are neither\n  starved nor padded.\n- Always add interest-bearing debt to an equity value to quote enterprise value (and subtract it to\n  go the other way). Never compare an EV multiple with an equity multiple.\n- Goodwill on the seller's balance sheet is worth nothing to you unless you can say what it buys.\n- Expect methods — and two professionals using the same method — to disagree; run at least two and\n  negotiate inside the range.\n- Strategic value exceeds market value when the buyer completes a \"set\" (technology, distribution,\n  a collection); price that premium consciously on either side of the table.\n- For the sale of a closely held company or an ESOP share price, hire a professional appraiser;\n  large firms use the M&A departments of accounting, investment-banking or consulting firms."},{"ref":"C-valuation.md","label":"Valuation (handbook appendix C)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"The book presents methods rather than a numbered procedure; this is the order it implies:\n1. Fix the context — buyer or seller, closely held firm or public unit, strategic or financial\n   buyer — and who bears the estimation risk.\n2. Pull the latest balance sheet and income statement (appendix A format).\n3. Asset-based pass: equity book value; restate assets and intangibles to market (adjusted book);\n   estimate liquidation (floor) and replacement (ceiling) values.\n4. Normalise earnings: remove non-recurring items, restate owner salaries, review maintenance and\n   depreciation; produce normalised net income, EBIT and EBITDA.\n5. Find comparables — public peers by industry and size plus recent private acquisitions; record\n   their P/E, EV/EBIT and EV/EBITDA multiples.\n6. Earnings-based pass: apply the multiples; convert between equity and enterprise value with\n   interest-bearing debt.\n7. DCF pass (buyer's view): forecast cash flows under new ownership, set a terminal value, discount\n   at the buyer's cost of capital.\n8. Lay the results side by side, explain the spread, and bring in a professional appraiser for any\n   transaction or ESOP."},{"ref":"C-valuation.md","label":"Valuation (handbook appendix C)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Earnings-normalisation checklist (before applying any multiple)** — book pp. 251–252\n- Remove one-time write-offs (e.g. obsolete inventory) that depress the latest year.\n- Remove one-time gains (e.g. sale of a subsidiary) and non-recurring windfall sales that inflate it.\n- Are expenses normal — neither extraordinarily high nor low? Inordinately low maintenance inflates\n  near-term earnings and stores up deferred-maintenance cost.\n- Closely held company: are owner-manager and family salaries unreasonably high or low? Adjust to market.\n- Are depreciation rates valid? If not, adjust reported earnings.\n\n**Balance-sheet adjustment checklist (adjusted book value)** — book pp. 247–248\n- Accounts receivable: how much is actually collectible?\n- Inventory: spoilage, obsolescence — or undervaluation?\n- Property, plant and equipment: market value today versus depreciated historical cost, especially land?\n- Intangibles (goodwill, patents): what would a buyer really pay for them?\n\n**Method-selection questions**\n- Are there publicly traded companies similar in industry and size with known P/E multiples?\n- Has a similar private company been acquired recently, and at what multiple?\n- Do comparables differ from the target in debt load or tax position? (→ EBIT) In depreciation\n  policy or asset age? (→ EBITDA)\n- Can future cash flows and a terminal value be forecast with reasonable confidence? (→ DCF)\n- Is the buyer strategic (completes a set) or purely financial?\n\n**Data inputs a valuation calculator needs**\n- Balance sheet: total assets, total liabilities, short- and long-term interest-bearing debt,\n  per-asset market adjustments.\n- Income statement: EBIT, interest expense, pre-tax earnings, income tax, net income, depreciation\n  and amortisation, list of non-recurring items, owner-salary adjustment.\n- Comparables: P/E, EV/EBIT, EV/EBITDA per peer or transaction.\n- DCF: cash-flow forecast by year, terminal value, discount rate."},{"ref":"C-valuation.md","label":"Valuation (handbook appendix C)","heading":"Examples & cautionary tales","text":"- **Amalgamated Hat Rack** — book value $2,182,500 vs 8× earnings $2,780,000 vs enterprise value\n  $3,955,000 — one company, three numbers, depending on method and equity-vs-enterprise framing.\n- **Acme Corporation (table C-1)** — identical EBIT to Amalgamated, but $350,000 interest instead of\n  $110,000 leaves net income $220,500 vs $347,500 — leverage distorts P/E; use EBIT/EBITDA.\n- **XYZ Corporation** — $50 share ÷ $5 earnings = 10× — how a multiple is read off a public comparable.\n- **Retail-chain LBO** — equity book $1.3B became $2.2B (+69%) once inventory and property were\n  appraised — stale book values hide value.\n- **1995 San Francisco land** — worth 10–20× its book figure — historical cost is not market value.\n- **Hemingway first editions** — the volume that completes a set is worth more to that collector —\n  strategic buyers pay more than the market value of the shares."},{"ref":"C-valuation.md","label":"Valuation (handbook appendix C)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Trusting a single method or a single professional's number.\n- Taking book value as market value (stale land, dead inventory, uncollectible receivables,\n  fictional goodwill).\n- Applying a P/E multiple from a comparable with a different capital structure or tax position.\n- Capitalising un-normalised earnings (one-offs, deferred maintenance, off-market owner salaries,\n  invalid depreciation).\n- Confusing equity value with enterprise value when quoting or comparing multiples.\n- Believing multi-year cash-flow forecasts and terminal values are precise.\n- Paying replacement value for assets you would never rebuild.\n- Closing a sale or setting an ESOP price without a professional appraiser."},{"ref":"C-valuation.md","label":"Valuation (handbook appendix C)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`equity book value` — total assets minus total liabilities; equals owners' equity on the balance sheet.\n`adjusted book value` — book value with assets and intangibles restated to appraised market values.\n`liquidation value` — net cash from a quick sale of all assets after settling all liabilities.\n`replacement value` — the cost of reproducing the business's assets.\n`goodwill` — the premium over book value paid in a past acquisition, carried as an intangible; often worthless to a new buyer.\n`capitalising earnings` — valuing a business by multiplying an earnings figure by a multiple.\n`P/E multiple` — share price ÷ earnings per share of a comparable public company.\n`enterprise value` — equity value plus (interest-bearing) debt; what the whole business is worth to all capital providers.\n`EBIT` — earnings before interest and taxes. `EBITDA` — EBIT plus depreciation and amortisation (non-cash charges).\n`discounted cash flow (DCF)` — present value of forecast cash flows plus terminal value, discounted at the buyer's cost of capital.\n`terminal value` — the value of cash flows beyond the explicit forecast horizon, discounted back as a lump sum.\n`closely held business` — a company with only a few owners and no public market for its shares.\n`ESOP` — employee stock ownership plan; a common trigger for a formal appraisal of private shares."},{"ref":"C-valuation.md","label":"Valuation (handbook appendix C)","heading":"Summing up (book)","text":"- **Asset-based** (equity book, adjusted book, liquidation, replacement): easy to calculate and\n  understand; except adjusted book and replacement they miss market values, and all miss the\n  intangible value of a going concern — knowledge, skill, reputation.\n- **Earnings-based** (P/E, EBIT, EBITDA): generally superior to asset-based, but depends on\n  comparable businesses whose multiples are known.\n- **DCF**: includes the time value of money, is future-looking, estimates what a new owner could\n  achieve and recognises the buyer's cost of capital; its weakness is producing reliable cash-flow\n  estimates.\n- The approaches will produce different outcomes — even the same method in two experienced hands —\n  so most appraisers use more than one."},{"ref":"D-rule-144.md","label":"Selling restricted stock, US (handbook appendix D)","heading":"Purpose","text":"When can a founder, early investor, employee or insider sell pre-IPO stock on the public market?\nLoad when a user holds shares acquired privately (founder stock, seed round, exercised options, a\nRegulation D placement) or is an officer/director/large holder of a public company and asks about\nselling, lock-ups, \"restricted stock\" or the legend on their certificate.\n\n**Non-US note:** Rule 144 is US federal securities law; a founder elsewhere needs the local\nequivalent (e.g. UK/EU prospectus-exemption and lock-up rules, Nigeria's SEC rules under the ISA)\n— confirm with local securities counsel."},{"ref":"D-rule-144.md","label":"Selling restricted stock, US (handbook appendix D)","heading":"Core ideas","text":"- Stock acquired before an IPO is restricted by the SEC; selling it publicly needs registration or\n  an exemption. Rule 144 is a **safe-harbour exemption** — not the only route, but the standard one.\n- Two categories: **restricted securities** (how you got them — an unregistered private sale) and\n  **control securities** (who you are — an affiliate with control).\n- Five conditions: holding period, current public information, volume limit, ordinary brokerage\n  handling, Form 144 notice. The last three bind affiliates only.\n- Non-affiliates who have held for a year are free of every condition; affiliates are never fully free.\n- Meeting Rule 144 is not enough: the restrictive legend must be removed by the transfer agent,\n  which needs the issuer's consent. Disputes are the issuer's discretion under state law; the SEC\n  will not intervene."},{"ref":"D-rule-144.md","label":"Selling restricted stock, US (handbook appendix D)","heading":"Frameworks & tables","text":"**Restricted vs control securities (book p. 258)**\n\n| | Restricted securities | Control securities |\n|---|---|---|\n| Defined by | How acquired: unregistered, private sale from the issuer or an affiliate (Rule 144(a)(3)) | Who holds them: an affiliate of the issuer |\n| Typical sources | Private placements, Regulation D offerings, ESOPs, stock for professional services, seed/startup capital | Executive officers, directors, large shareholders — anyone with power to direct management and policies (via voting stock, contract or otherwise) |\n| Certificate | Stamped with a restrictive legend | Usually no legend |\n| Note | Buying from an affiliate makes the shares restricted in your hands even if they were not in theirs | Affiliate shares bought in the open market have no holding period but still face the other conditions |\n\n**The five conditions of Rule 144 (book pp. 258–260)**\n\n| # | Condition | Binds | Rule |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Holding period | Restricted securities only | Reporting company (subject to Exchange Act 1934 reporting): ≥ 6 months. Non-reporting company: ≥ 1 year. Clock starts when bought and fully paid for. |\n| 2 | Current public information | All sellers | Reporting company: up to date with periodic Exchange Act filings. Non-reporting: nature of business, identity of officers/directors and financial statements publicly available. |\n| 3 | Trading-volume formula | Affiliates | In any 3-month period sell no more than the greater of 1% of outstanding shares of the class or, if exchange-listed, the average reported weekly trading volume over the 4 weeks before filing Form 144. OTC stocks (OTC Bulletin Board, Pink Sheets): 1% test only. |\n| 4 | Ordinary brokerage transactions | Affiliates | Routine trades; broker earns no more than a normal commission; neither seller nor broker solicits buy orders. |\n| 5 | Notice of proposed sale | Affiliates | File Form 144 with the SEC if the sale exceeds 5,000 shares or $50,000 aggregate in any 3-month period. |\n\n**Holding-period start rules (book p. 259)**\n\n| Situation | Clock starts |\n|---|---|\n| Bought from the issuer | When bought and fully paid for |\n| Later purchases from the issuer, same class | Do not reset the clock on earlier shares |\n| Bought from a non-affiliate | Tack on the non-affiliate's holding period |\n| Gift from an affiliate | When the affiliate acquired the shares, not the gift date |\n| Stock option (incl. employee options) | On exercise, not grant |\n| Affiliate buys in the public market | No holding period (not restricted), but conditions 2–5 still apply to resale as control securities |\n\n**Non-affiliate resale matrix (book p. 260)** — \"non-affiliate\" = not an affiliate now and not for the past 3 months\n\n| Held for | Reporting issuer | Non-reporting issuer |\n|---|---|---|\n| < 6 months | No Rule 144 sale | No Rule 144 sale |\n| 6–12 months | Sell if the current-public-information condition is met | No Rule 144 sale |\n| ≥ 1 year | Sell free of all Rule 144 conditions | Sell free of all Rule 144 conditions |"},{"ref":"D-rule-144.md","label":"Selling restricted stock, US (handbook appendix D)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- Not an affiliate (for ≥ 3 months) and held ≥ 1 year → sell freely.\n- Not an affiliate, reporting issuer, held 6–12 months → sell only while the issuer is current on its filings.\n- Affiliate → every sale, indefinitely, is subject to the volume cap, ordinary-brokerage handling\n  and (above 5,000 shares or $50,000 per 3 months) a Form 144 filing.\n- Volume cap per 3 months (affiliate, listed stock) = max(1% × shares outstanding, average weekly\n  volume of the prior 4 weeks); OTC = 1% only.\n- Options: the clock starts at exercise, not at grant or vesting.\n- A legend on the certificate blocks trading until the transfer agent removes it, whatever your\n  Rule 144 status."},{"ref":"D-rule-144.md","label":"Selling restricted stock, US (handbook appendix D)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Assuming a met holding period alone permits a sale (public-information test and legend removal still apply).\n- Affiliates ignoring the 1%/weekly-volume cap or the Form 144 trigger.\n- Counting an option's holding period from grant instead of exercise.\n- Buying shares from an affiliate and expecting them to be freely tradable.\n- Expecting the SEC to arbitrate a legend dispute."},{"ref":"D-rule-144.md","label":"Selling restricted stock, US (handbook appendix D)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`restricted securities` — shares acquired in an unregistered private sale from the issuer or an affiliate.\n`control securities` — shares held by an affiliate of the issuer.\n`affiliate` — a person (officer, director, large shareholder) in a relationship of control with the issuer.\n`control` — the power to direct a company's management and policies, via voting securities, contract or otherwise.\n`reporting company` — an issuer subject to the periodic reporting requirements of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934.\n`restrictive legend` — the stamp on a certificate stating the shares cannot be resold unless registered or exempt.\n`Form 144` — the SEC notice of proposed sale an affiliate files above the 5,000-share / $50,000 threshold.\n`transfer agent` — the only party that can remove a restrictive legend, with the issuer's consent.\n`tacking` — adding a prior non-affiliate holder's holding period to your own."},{"ref":"modern/S01-sales.md","label":"Sales (2026 layer)","heading":"What changed since 2018","text":"- **Founder-led sales is now the named default, not a stopgap.** Bessemer (2025), SaaStr (2024),\n  Techstars (2026) and Kazanjy (2020-26) all say the founder personally closes the first 10-25\n  customers before anyone is hired [9][4][46][2][47].\n- **Win rates and quota attainment fell.** Ebsta × Pavilion's 655K-opportunity dataset shows\n  B2B win rates dropping from 29% (2024) to 19% (2025) [20]; Bridge Group finds only 48% of AEs at\n  quota (2026, from 51% in 2024) and 60% of SDRs (2025, lowest on record) [22][23].\n- **\"No decision\" is the main competitor.** Dixon & McKenna's 2.5M-call study: 40-60% of lost\n  deals end in no decision, not in a rival win [13]; Dunford builds the whole pitch around it [16].\n- **Buyers self-serve first.** Gartner (survey of ~650 B2B buyers, Aug-Sep 2025): 67% prefer a\n  rep-free experience and 45% used AI during a recent purchase [27][28].\n- **Product-led motions matured into product-led *sales*.** Verna's segmentation: self-serve for\n  1-100-employee firms, product-led sales for 101-1,000, classic sales-led above that [11].\n- **Outbound got regulated by the inbox, not the law.** Google/Yahoo bulk-sender rules (Feb 2024)\n  and Microsoft (May 2025) require SPF/DKIM/DMARC, one-click unsubscribe and <0.3% complaints;\n  non-compliant mail is now refused outright [31][32]. Average cold-email reply fell to 3.43% [30].\n- **Expansion is the growth engine.** Expansion is 52% of new revenue in Ebsta × Pavilion 2025\n  [19] and 40% of new ARR in Benchmarkit 2025 [24]; 38% of AEs now own renewals [22].\n- **AI entered the sales floor.** 54% of sellers have used AI agents; average seller still spends\n  only 40% of time selling (Salesforce, 4,050 sellers, 2026) [29]. Effects are mixed — see AI notes.\n- **The first hire moved down-market.** \"Founding AE\" / half-AE-half-PM profiles replaced the early\n  VP Sales; a VP is justified around $1-2M ARR with two reps already hitting quota [5][7][9]."},{"ref":"modern/S01-sales.md","label":"Sales (2026 layer)","heading":"Core ideas (current consensus)","text":"- **Sales is consulting with a bias** — Kazanjy (GTMnow, 2026): the seller helps a buyer understand\n  the problem, believe it matters, and judge fit; founders are uniquely equipped for that [2].\n- **Selling *is* product development at this stage** — Walsh/Techstars (2026): a hired rep becomes a\n  buffer that filters the customer signal the founder needs; lead sales yourself for 1-2 years [46].\n- **Readiness is retention, not revenue** — Roberge (Stage 2 Capital, *Science of Scaling*, 2025):\n  product-market fit is \"true\" when P% of customers hit a leading-indicator event within T time;\n  scale only when it holds, and pace hires so you can stop if it breaks [7][8].\n- **Narrow the ICP until it is uncomfortable** — Bessemer (2025): first 10-20 customers by cold\n  outreach, at least one won from a total skeptic; ACV decides how many logos $1M ARR needs [9].\n- **Discovery beats persuasion** — Ebsta × Pavilion (2025): top reps are 55% better at discovery\n  and 24% more likely to decline non-ICP deals early; ICP focus lifts quota attainment 90% [20].\n- **The buyer fears messing up more than missing out** — Dixon & McKenna (2022): once intent\n  exists, the customer optimises for \"not failing\"; 73% of average reps respond by pushing harder\n  on status quo and lose 84% of those deals [13].\n- **A pitch is an argument about alternatives, not features** — Dunford (*Sales Pitch*, 2023): set\n  up the market insight, the alternatives (including do-nothing), the \"perfect world\"; then your\n  differentiated value with proof [15][16].\n- **Usage is the pipeline** — Verna (2023-24): in product-led sales, end-user usage creates the\n  pipeline; score product-qualified accounts daily and route at a threshold [11][12].\n- **Free-to-paid is a design choice** — Poyar with ChartMogul/ProductLed (2026, 200 products):\n  median free-to-paid is 8%, but the top and bottom quintiles differ 10x; card-required trials\n  convert ~30%, more than 5x card-free [10].\n- **Hire two, not one** — Lemkin (SaaStr): a single rep cannot tell you whether the person or the\n  process failed; hire in pairs and only after 10+ founder-closed customers [4][5].\n- **Health is GRR before NRR** — SaaS Capital and Optifai warn that NRR above 100% can mask an\n  eroding gross retention base; read both [25][26]."},{"ref":"modern/S01-sales.md","label":"Sales (2026 layer)","heading":"Frameworks & playbooks","text":"| Framework | Author / year | What it says | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Founder-led selling maturity stages | Pete Kazanjy, *Founding Sales* (2020) / GTMnow (2026) | Stage 0 hypothesis testing → Stage 1 5-10 customers from 30-50 prospects (30-50% win) → Stage 2 documented motion + first executor (15-25% win on qualified pipeline) → Stage 3 2-4 reps + leader; buyer journey = awareness, prioritisation, solution preference, agreement, championship [2][3] | Deciding what to document and whom to hire next |\n| The Mom Test | Rob Fitzpatrick (2013, classic) | Talk about their life not your idea; ask about specific past behaviour, not hypotheticals; listen more than pitch [49] | Every early discovery conversation |\n| SPICED | Jacco van der Kooij, Winning by Design | Situation, Pain, Impact, Critical Event, Decision — one qualification model shared by sales, CS and marketing, inside the Bowtie (pre- and post-sale funnel) [17][18] | Default discovery template for SMB/mid-market; CRM fields |\n| MEDDIC / MEDDICC / MEDDPICC | Dick Dunkel & Jack Napoli (PTC, 1990s); MEDDPICC adds Paper Process | Metrics, Economic buyer, Decision criteria, Decision process, Identify pain, Champion (+Competition, +Paper process) [50] | Enterprise deals with procurement, legal, security |\n| Challenger | Dixon & Adamson (2011, 6,000-rep study) | Teach, tailor, take control; relevant where buyers call purchases \"very complex\" (Gartner 2025: 77%) [50] | Category-creating or insight-led pitches |\n| JOLT | Matt Dixon & Ted McKenna, *The JOLT Effect* (2022) | Judge the indecision, Offer a recommendation, Limit exploration, Take risk off the table [13][14] | Deals stalling at \"I need to think about it\" |\n| Sales Pitch structure | April Dunford, *Sales Pitch* (2023) | Setup: market insight → alternatives (pros/cons incl. do-nothing) → perfect world. Follow-through: differentiated value → proof → objections → ask [15][16] | Building the founder's narrative; onboarding rep #1 |\n| Science of Scaling | Mark Roberge, Stage 2 Capital / Wiley (2025) | PMF = \"P% achieve E within T\" (P 60-80%, T 1-3 months); GTM fit = LTV/CAC > 3, payback < 12 mo, magic number > 1; hire AE/PM hybrid; pay 50% on signature, 50% on retention indicator; pace 2 hires/month [7][8] | Deciding *whether* and *how fast* to add sellers |\n| Product-led sales (PQA scoring) | Elena Verna (2023, 2024) | Add sales to PLG only with self-serve PMF, organic hand-raisers and deals above a ~$10K \"sales floor\"; score accounts on volume, velocity, breadth (+behaviour) [11][12] | SaaS with a free tier; storefront-style usage data |\n| $1M ARR playbook | Bessemer Atlas (Feb 2025) | Founder cold outreach → narrow ICP → one motion (PLG, direct or channel) → one segment → playbook → avoid early VP Sales, use fractional leaders [9] | Seed-stage B2B |\n| Customer advisory board → paid | Sahil Mansuri / Expa (2023) | Recruit 5-10 target customers as advisors, pilot 2 weeks-2 months, ask each for one referral, convert at a 50% early-adopter discount [48] | First 5-10 logos with no brand |\n| Predictable Revenue (classic) | Aaron Ross & Marylou Tyler (2011) | Separate prospecting (SDR) from closing; \"Cold Calling 2.0\" via referrals and email [51]. No 2024-26 update verified here | Understanding why SDR/AE splits exist; treat volumes as dated |\n| Two-rep test + 10-point hire checklist | Jason Lemkin, SaaStr (2024-25) | Close 10-20 yourself → hire 2 reps → head of sales at $1-2M ARR; hire only someone you'd buy from, who sold at your price point without a brand; interview ~30 [4][5][6] | Any first sales hire |"},{"ref":"modern/S01-sales.md","label":"Sales (2026 layer)","heading":"Benchmarks & numbers (2024-2026)","text":"| Metric | Value | Segment / context | Source | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| B2B win rate | 19% (from 29%) | 655K opportunities, $48B pipeline | [20] | 2025 |\n| Sellers missing quota | 76% | H1 2025, Ebsta × Pavilion (secondary summary) | [21] | 2025 |\n| Win rate, deals closed ≤ 50 days | 47% vs ~20% for longer deals | same dataset (secondary summary) | [21] | 2025 |\n| Early decision-maker involvement | +55% win rate | Ebsta × Pavilion | [19] | 2025 |\n| Pipeline coverage required | 5.3x | Ebsta × Pavilion | [20] | 2025 |\n| Revenue concentration | 14% of sellers → 80% of revenue | Ebsta × Pavilion | [19] | 2025 |\n| Expansion share of new revenue | 52% / 40% | Ebsta × Pavilion / Benchmarkit | [19][24] | 2025 |\n| Founder Stage 1 funnel | 30-50 prospects → 10-20 customers | Kazanjy | [2] | 2026 |\n| Prospecting base before hiring | 75-100 target accounts | Kazanjy | [2] | 2026 |\n| Founder-closed customers before hire | 10-20 (SaaStr, BVP); 10-25 (DDVC) | B2B SaaS | [4][9][47] | 2024-25 |\n| VP/Head of Sales timing | ~$1M ARR (BVP); $1-2M ARR + 2 reps at quota (SaaStr) | B2B SaaS | [9][5] | 2024-25 |\n| AE median OTE | $200K (from $190K in 2024) | 158 B2B cos, US-heavy | [22] | 2026 |\n| AE quota / quota:OTE | $960K / 4.6x | same | [22] | 2026 |\n| AEs at quota / ramp | 48% / 6.2 months (record high) | same | [22] | 2026 |\n| SDR median OTE | $80K (68:32 → $55K base + $25K) | 351 B2B cos | [23] | 2025 |\n| SDR activity → output | 112 activities/day (44 dials, 41 emails, 19 LinkedIn) → 4.1 quality conversations/day; ~10 meetings/month quota | same | [23] | 2025 |\n| SDRs at quota / ramp / tenure | 60% / 3.0 mo / 1.9 yrs | same | [23] | 2025 |\n| AE OTE by segment | SMB $110-160K; mid-market $160-220K; enterprise $230-270K+ | secondary aggregator | [53] | 2026 |\n| Funnel (aggregated) | MQL→SQL 39% / 31%; SQL→opp 42% / 36%; opp→close 39% / 31% | SMB-MM / enterprise; 40+ studies pooled, not primary | [38] | 2025 |\n| Sales cycle | ~84 days avg; SMB <$5K 30-90 (median 40); mid-market 60-120; enterprise 170+ | aggregated | [38] | 2025 |\n| Median free-to-paid | 8%; top vs bottom quintile 10x | 200 B2B products, mostly $1-10M ARR | [10] | 2026 |\n| Card-required trial conversion | ~30% (>5x card-free) | same | [10] | 2026 |\n| Primary PLG entry | 57% free trial, 26% freemium, 7% reverse trial | same | [10] | 2026 |\n| Sales floor for PLS | ~$10K/yr; rep costs $100-300K and must return 3-5x | Verna | [11] | 2024 |\n| NRR median | 101% (GRR 88%) | Benchmarkit private SaaS | [24] | 2025 |\n| NRR by ACV $25-50K | median 102%, top quartile 111%, bottom 97% | SaaS Capital survey, >$1M ARR | [25] | 2025 |\n| NRR by segment | enterprise 118%, mid-market 108%, SMB 97% | Optifai 939 cos + ChartMogul 2,100 | [26] | 2026 |\n| New-customer CAC ratio | $2.00 median (+14% YoY); payback +12.5% since 2022 | Benchmarkit | [24] | 2025 |\n| Annual logo retention \"world class\" | > 90% | Roberge | [7] | 2025 |\n| Cold email reply rate | 3.43% avg; >10% top; 58% of replies from email 1 | Instantly, billions of sends | [30] | 2026 |\n| Cold email reply rate (managed infra) | 3.1-3.7% avg; 5%+ strong; 8-12% elite | Lacleo | [32] | 2026 |\n| Deliverability thresholds | bounce <3% (best <1.5%); spam complaints <0.3% hard cap, <0.1% target | Amplemarket / Google | [31][32] | 2026 |\n| Domain warm-up | 5-10 emails/day rising over 4-6 weeks | Lacleo | [32] | 2026 |\n| Buyers preferring rep-free | 67%; 45% used AI in a recent purchase | ~650 B2B buyers | [27][28] | 2026 |\n| Seller time actually selling | 40% (Gen Z 35%) | 4,050 sellers | [29] | 2026 |\n| Human vs AI SDR reply | 4.7% vs 2.9%; hybrid pod 18.3 meetings/mo vs 9.4 human, 11.7 AI | \"Digital Applied\" blend, secondary | [33] | 2026 |\n| AI SDR domain-reputation failures | 47% of deployments within 90 days | secondary | [33] | 2026 |\n| DTC conversion rate | median 1.17% (IQR 0.92-1.52%) | 19 DTC stores, 17M sessions, Jul 25-Jun 26 | [36] | 2026 |\n| DTC conversion rate (larger stores) | median 2.07%, 75th pct 2.59% | 21 Shopify stores, $417M rev | [37] | 2026 |\n| Add-to-cart / checkout completion | 5.95% / 48.4% | same | [37] | 2026 |\n| Mobile vs desktop CVR | 2.29% (86% of traffic) vs 3.74% | same | [37] | 2026 |\n| Returning-customer effect | stores with 50%+ returning: 3.28% vs 1.61% | same | [37] | 2026 |\n| Shopify CVR tiers | avg 1.4-1.8%; good 2.5-3%; top 10% > 4.7% | secondary | [54] | 2025 |"},{"ref":"modern/S01-sales.md","label":"Sales (2026 layer)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **If you have < 10 paying customers, the founder sells.** Do not hire sales before 10-20\n  founder-closed deals and a written playbook (Lemkin; Bessemer; Data Driven VC) [4][9][47].\n- **If you hire, hire two at once** and compare them; if neither converts like the founder did,\n  the process is broken, not the people (Lemkin) [5]. Kazanjy's transition test: rep conversion\n  metrics match the founder's [2].\n- **If two reps hit quota and ARR is $1-2M, hire a head of sales**; earlier and they will spend\n  90-180 days on decks and headcount plans instead of closing (Lemkin; Bessemer) [5][9].\n- **If your leading-indicator retention event is not met by 60-80% of customers, do not scale\n  sellers** (Roberge). GTM fit before pacing hires: LTV/CAC > 3, payback < 12 months [7].\n- **If ACV is enterprise, 5-10 deals can reach $1M ARR; if SMB, plan for hundreds** and design\n  the motion (self-serve or high-velocity) accordingly (Bessemer) [9].\n- **If deals average under ~$10K, do not attach a human salesperson** — the sales floor; route\n  below it to self-serve or success (Verna) [11].\n- **If a prospect says \"let me think about it\", switch from status-quo pressure to JOLT**: judge\n  indecision, recommend, limit options, de-risk; high-indecision deals close 6% of the time [13].\n- **If a deal passes 50 days, treat it as at risk** — win rate roughly halves after that (Ebsta\n  summary) [21]; ask for the critical event (SPICED) — 76% of B-player deals lack one [20].\n- **If the economic buyer is not in the first meetings, get them in**: +55% win rate [19].\n- **If your cold-email complaint rate nears 0.1%, stop and fix targeting**; 0.3% is Google's\n  hard ceiling and mail is now rejected, not filtered [31][32].\n- **If you run a free trial and want conversion over volume, require a card** (~30% vs <6%) —\n  but expect fewer signups; choose by CAC and sales floor (Poyar) [10].\n- **If NRR > 100% but GRR < ~85%, you have a churn problem hidden by upsells** (SaaS Capital;\n  Optifai) [25][26].\n- **If you must discount early, discount hard and grandfather**: Roberge's example is the first\n  20 customers at 90% off for learning, not margin [7]; Expa's advisory-board conversion uses\n  50% [48]. Never discount without a documented reason (reference, case study, logo).\n- **If a candidate has not sold at your price point without a brand, pass** (Lemkin's checklist);\n  interview ~30 to find one or two stage-appropriate fits [6].\n- **If a rep shows no pipeline momentum by month 3-6, diagnose people/product/process, then act**\n  (Data Driven VC) [47]."},{"ref":"modern/S01-sales.md","label":"Sales (2026 layer)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. **Write the ICP as three nested answers** (Kazanjy): which companies, who inside them, and what\n   trigger makes it urgent now (funding, new hire, launch, missed quota) [2].\n2. **Build a prospect base of 75-100 target accounts** with named contacts; source from your\n   network, advisors, communities and warm intros before cold channels [2][48].\n3. **Run 20-30 Mom-Test conversations** about their current workflow, cost and past attempts;\n   no pitching; capture SPICED fields (situation, pain, impact, critical event, decision) [49][17].\n4. **Write the narrative** in Dunford's order (insight → alternatives → perfect world → your\n   differentiated value → proof) and produce it as talk track, deck, email and one-pager [15][2].\n5. **Recruit 5-10 design partners / advisory customers**; pilot 2 weeks-2 months, scoped to one\n   measurable outcome; agree the success metric and the price you will ask for up front [48].\n6. **Outbound only after warm sources are exhausted**: authenticate the domain (SPF, DKIM, DMARC),\n   one-click unsubscribe, warm up 4-6 weeks, send from a secondary domain, micro-segment, and\n   judge by positive replies not opens [31][32][30].\n7. **Qualify every opportunity** on SPICED (SMB/mid-market) or MEDDPICC (enterprise); log the\n   critical event and economic buyer; drop non-ICP deals early [17][50][20].\n8. **Close with a recommendation, not a menu** (JOLT): one option, a limited evaluation window,\n   and risk removal (pilot terms, opt-out, guarantee, phased rollout) [13].\n9. **Define the retention leading indicator** (\"P% of customers do E within T\") before hiring\n   anyone; instrument it in the product or ops console [7].\n10. **Document the playbook**: ICP, prospecting, discovery guide, demo script, objections, pricing\n    rules, hand-off to success — then hire two reps who match the checklist [2][6].\n11. **Pay for retention, not just signatures** (50/50 on signature and indicator) and pace hiring\n    so you can stop if the indicator slips [7].\n12. **Run expansion as a motion**: quarterly business reviews, usage-triggered upsell, C-suite\n    relationships (Ebsta: engaged C-suite ↑ upsell potential 189%) [19]."},{"ref":"modern/S01-sales.md","label":"Sales (2026 layer)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**ICP and trigger worksheet**\n- Which company types (size, sector, stack, region) have paid or piloted? Which said no and why?\n- Who inside owns the pain, who signs, who blocks, who uses? (buying-group map) [2]\n- What event makes this urgent this quarter? How would we detect it? [2]\n\n**Discovery call sheet (SPICED)** [17]\n- Situation: how do they do it today, with what tools, people, budget?\n- Pain: what breaks, how often, who feels it?\n- Impact: what does it cost in money, time, risk; what does fixing it unlock?\n- Critical event: what dated deadline or consequence forces a decision?\n- Decision: who decides, on what criteria, through what process and paper?\n\n**Mom Test self-check** [49]\n- Did I ask about their past behaviour, not my idea? Did I avoid \"would you…?\" hypotheticals?\n- Did they give me a commitment (time, reputation, money) or only compliments?\n\n**Indecision (JOLT) deal audit** [13]\n- Is the stall about *which* option, *too much* information, or *fear of being blamed*?\n- Have I made one recommendation? Set an evaluation end date? Removed the downside?\n\n**Outbound compliance checklist** [31][32][39]\n- SPF, DKIM, DMARC aligned; one-click unsubscribe header; physical address; true sender identity.\n- Complaint rate < 0.1%; bounce < 2-3%; volume ramped over 4-6 weeks per new domain.\n- Jurisdiction check: opt-out (US), corporate exemption (UK), legitimate-interest assessment (EU),\n  opt-in (Germany, Spain, Italy), consent (Canada).\n\n**First-sales-hire readiness gate** [4][7][9][47]\n- 10-20 founder-closed customers; written playbook; retention indicator met by 60-80%.\n- Budget for two reps; founder still on calls for 3-6 months; 30-day and 90-day checkpoints set.\n- Candidate: sold at our price point, without a brand, thrived in chaos, I would buy from them.\n\n**DTC funnel review** [36][37]\n- Sessions → add-to-cart (benchmark ~6%) → checkout completion (~48-65%) → CVR (1.2-2.1% median).\n- Mobile vs desktop gap; branded/email vs cold paid social; returning-customer share."},{"ref":"modern/S01-sales.md","label":"Sales (2026 layer)","heading":"Regional notes","text":"- **US** — CAN-SPAM is opt-out: honest headers, postal address, working unsubscribe honoured\n  within 10 business days; penalties up to $53,088 per email [39]. Comp benchmarks above are\n  US-weighted (Bridge Group) — scale for local pay. Buyers expect self-serve first [27].\n- **UK** — PECR Regulation 22 exempts *corporate subscribers* (Ltd, LLP work addresses) from the\n  consent rule if the sender is identified and opt-out offered; sole traders and partnerships need\n  consent; fines up to £500K [39]. Check ICO guidance before campaigns.\n- **EU** — B2B cold email rests on GDPR legitimate interest with a documented assessment, but\n  ePrivacy varies by state: France and the Netherlands allow B2B without opt-in; Germany, Spain and\n  Italy require consent even for B2B [39]. Germany's UWG §7 has no B2B exemption and competitor\n  cease-and-desist letters are the usual enforcement; the ECJ *Inteligo Media* ruling (Nov 2025)\n  narrowed the existing-customer exception — free-trial sign-ups do not automatically qualify [40].\n- **Nigeria / Africa** — WhatsApp is the dominant B2B conversion channel; Lagos decision-makers\n  answer on WhatsApp before cold email [41]. Expect long, relationship-driven cycles, currency\n  volatility affecting naira vs dollar pricing, and heavy verification (WhatsApp, LinkedIn, physical\n  visits, peer references) because of fraud history [42]. Distribution often runs through\n  retailer/distributor networks — OmniRetail links 150,000 retailers, 145 manufacturers and 5,800\n  distributors across 12 cities (₦1.8trn processed) [45]; chat-native payments are arriving (Xara:\n  10,000 users, ₦135M within weeks) [43]. Pan-African B2B startups sell as \"symbiotic\" partners of\n  banks and corporates and embed WhatsApp into the product (e.g. Jem HR) [44]. Check the Nigeria\n  Data Protection Act 2023 for outreach consent rules (not researched here — unverified).\n- **Canada / Australia** — consent-based regimes (CASL express/implied; Spam Act inferred consent)\n  with 5-10 day unsubscribe honouring [39].\n- **India, Southeast Asia, Middle East, LatAm** — not researched in this pass; treat all figures\n  above as US/UK/EU-centric and re-benchmark locally (unverified)."},{"ref":"modern/S01-sales.md","label":"Sales (2026 layer)","heading":"AI-era notes","text":"- **Adoption is real; impact is uneven.** 54% of sellers have used AI agents and ~9 in 10 plan to\n  by 2027; expected savings are 34% on research and 36% on drafting [29]. Bridge Group: firms with\n  high AI engagement report 57% of AEs at quota vs 39% — but 10 of 11 AI use cases are rated \"hit\n  or miss\" by the same respondents [22]. Best-evidenced uses: post-call summaries, account\n  research, email personalisation [22].\n- **AI SDRs: hype ahead of evidence.** Only 1% of Bridge Group's 2025 SDR respondents ran an \"AI\n  SDR\" [23]. Secondary 2026 data puts AI-SDR raw reply rates below human (2.9% vs 4.7%), with 47%\n  of deployments hitting domain-reputation trouble within 90 days and vendor churn estimated at\n  50-70% a year; hybrid pods (one human + AI seats) out-produced both [33]. Artisan's CEO admitted\n  the first generation \"barely worked\" with \"extremely bad hallucinations\" [34]. Vendor case\n  studies (7x ROI, 5x meetings) exist but are unaudited [34][35].\n- **AI-generated volume degraded the channel for everyone.** Instantly attributes the reply-rate\n  drop to inbox saturation from AI outbound plus stricter sender rules [30]; the winning response is\n  precision (micro-segments, problem-led lines), with elite teams letting agents do ~80% of research\n  and sequencing while humans write the argument [30].\n- **Buyers use AI too.** 45% of B2B buyers used AI in a recent purchase and 67% prefer no rep [27];\n  your product pages, pricing and proof must be legible to an AI research agent as well as a human.\n- **Frameworks are being encoded, not replaced.** Winning by Design now certifies \"AI for GTM\" with\n  every output grounded in SPICED and written to the CRM [17]; the practical founder move is an AI\n  note-taker that fills SPICED/MEDDPICC fields, then a human decides.\n- **Where hype outruns evidence:** fully autonomous closing, \"10x pipeline\" claims, and AI voice\n  agents for cold calls — no independent benchmark found in this research; Gartner's own data\n  shows regret rises when complex purchases go rep-free (secondary summary) [55]."},{"ref":"modern/S01-sales.md","label":"Sales (2026 layer)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Hiring a salesperson before the founder has a repeatable, documented motion [46][47][4].\n- Hiring one rep and drawing conclusions from n = 1 [5]; hiring a big-company VP who needs a team [47].\n- Treating \"no decision\" as a competitor loss and doubling down on status-quo fear [13].\n- Pitching features before establishing alternatives and the customer's perfect world [16].\n- Buying an AI SDR to replace prospecting rather than to assist it; burning the primary domain [33].\n- Cold-emailing consumers or German/Spanish/Italian businesses without consent [39][40].\n- Scaling on top-line growth while the retention leading indicator is unmet [7].\n- Reading NRR without GRR [25][26]; celebrating logos below the sales floor that cost more to\n  serve than they pay [11].\n- Accepting non-ICP deals to hit a number — top reps decline them early [20].\n- Comparing a DTC store to a mean inflated by low-AOV outliers; use the median [36]."},{"ref":"modern/S01-sales.md","label":"Sales (2026 layer)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`ICP` — ideal customer profile: which companies, which people, which trigger [2].\n`trigger / critical event` — dated reason the buyer must decide now; the C and E of SPICED [17].\n`SPICED` — Situation, Pain, Impact, Critical Event, Decision (Winning by Design) [17].\n`MEDDPICC` — Metrics, Economic buyer, Decision criteria, Decision process, Paper process, Identify pain, Champion, Competition [50].\n`JOLT` — Judge, Offer, Limit, Take risk off the table — the anti-indecision playbook [13].\n`FOMU` — fear of messing up; dominates once purchase intent exists [13].\n`no-decision loss` — deal that ends with the buyer doing nothing; 40-60% of losses [13][16].\n`product-led sales (PLS)` — sales pipeline built from self-serve usage signals [11].\n`PQA / PQL` — product-qualified account / lead: usage-scored readiness for sales [12].\n`sales floor` — minimum deal size that justifies a human seller (~$10K) [11].\n`reverse trial` — start on the paid tier, drop to free at trial end [10].\n`NRR / GRR` — net / gross revenue retention; read together [25][26].\n`CAC ratio` — sales & marketing spend per $1 of new ARR [24].\n`pipeline coverage` — open pipeline ÷ quota; ~5.3x needed at 2025 win rates [20].\n`OTE` — on-target earnings (base + variable at 100% quota) [22].\n`quota:OTE` — quota divided by OTE; ~4.6x median [22].\n`ramp time` — months to full productivity; 6.2 (AE), 3.0 (SDR) [22][23].\n`founding AE` — first, full-stack seller who prospects, closes and writes process [47].\n`leading indicator (retention)` — \"P% of customers achieve E within T\" [7].\n`DMARC / one-click unsubscribe` — sender authentication and RFC 8058 header now required by Gmail, Yahoo, Outlook [32].\n`hybrid pod` — one human seller plus AI seats for research and sequencing [33].\n`bowtie` — Winning by Design's funnel extended through onboarding, retention and expansion [17]."},{"ref":"modern/S02-marketing.md","label":"Marketing (2026 layer)","heading":"What changed since 2018","text":"- **Search stopped sending clicks.** 68.0% of US Google searches ended without a click in\n  Jan-Apr 2026, up from 60.5% in 2024 and ~45% in 2016; AI Overviews appear on 20%+ of\n  searches and cut click-through by roughly 60% where they show [1]. The \"SEO → traffic →\n  leads\" plan in a 2018 business plan no longer describes reality.\n- **Answer engines are a channel.** ChatGPT alone has ~800M weekly users and ~70% of AI-search\n  usage [52]; LLM answers cite only 2-7 domains each [51]. Being cited is the new page one.\n- **Paid acquisition got structurally dearer.** Meta CPMs are ~89% above 2020 levels [11];\n  Shopify puts average US DTC customer acquisition cost at $226 in 2024, up 60% in five\n  years [10]. Apple's 2021 App Tracking Transparency (initial opt-in ~16%) removed the cheap\n  signal that made 2018-era Facebook ads work [43]; Google then reversed cookie deprecation in\n  April 2025, so the web is now a permanent patchwork of consented and unconsented data [42].\n- **Owned channels flipped from \"nice to have\" to the profit centre.** Email flows are 5.3% of\n  sends but ~41% of email revenue; SMS flows 7.6% of sends but 45.2% of SMS revenue [28][29].\n  In most of the Global South the owned channel is WhatsApp, not email (see Regional notes).\n- **Creators replaced ad agencies for small brands.** The influencer industry reached ~$32.6B\n  in 2025 [8]; 87% of brands expect to raise creator budgets and the money is going to nano and\n  micro creators, not celebrities [7].\n- **Social commerce collapsed the funnel in Asia.** TikTok Shop went from $4.4B (2022) to\n  $45.6B (2025) GMV in Southeast Asia; Indonesia alone did $13.1B, +111% in a year [33].\n- **The growth model replaced the funnel.** Reforge's \"growth loops\" (2018) reframed marketing\n  from a linear pipe you keep paying to fill into compounding systems [13][14].\n- **AI produces the content; distribution is the scarce thing.** 66% of marketers use AI\n  (74% in the US) and only 4% let it write whole pieces [38]; Google's March 2024 \"scaled\n  content abuse\" policy punishes volume without value regardless of who wrote it [40].\n- **Budgets flatlined.** Large-company marketing budgets sit at 7.7% of revenue for the second\n  year, half of CMOs at 6% or less, and 59% say it is not enough [5][6]. Founders compete with\n  incumbents who are also cutting."},{"ref":"modern/S02-marketing.md","label":"Marketing (2026 layer)","heading":"Core ideas (current consensus)","text":"- **Positioning is the input to everything else** (April Dunford, *Obviously Awesome*, 2019;\n  *Sales Pitch*, 2023). The buyer's real question is \"why you over the alternatives?\", so\n  positioning is defined relative to competitive alternatives, not in a vacuum [18][19].\n- **Narrative beats features** (Andy Raskin, \"The Greatest Sales Deck I've Ever Seen\", 2016).\n  Open with an undeniable shift in the world, not with your product; the strongest narratives\n  are deliberately \"wrong\" — they trade completeness for memorability [16][17].\n- **One channel carries the company** (Gabriel Weinberg & Justin Mares, *Traction*, 2015).\n  Most companies get 70%+ of customers from a single channel at any stage; the job is to find it\n  fast and cheaply, then go deep [15]. Peter Thiel's version: you won't have several equally\n  good distribution strategies [15].\n- **Loops, not funnels** (Brian Balfour with Casey Winters, Kevin Kwok, Andrew Chen, Reforge,\n  2018). A funnel needs constant external input; a loop reinvests its output (users, content,\n  revenue) as the next input, so growth compounds [13][14].\n- **Earned before paid, and evolve the model** (Elena Verna, 2025). Paid channels buy access\n  to someone else's audience; virality, word of mouth and UGC build your own. Every channel\n  decays — expect to layer a new one roughly every 18 months and keep 20-25% of effort on\n  exploration [20].\n- **Penetration beats loyalty** (Byron Sharp, *How Brands Grow*, 2010, Ehrenberg-Bass).\n  Small brands suffer double jeopardy — fewer buyers who also buy less often — so growth comes\n  from reaching all category buyers and being easy to notice and easy to buy, not from\n  deepening loyalty among a few [25]. Evidence from decades of panel data; the startup caveat is\n  that Sharp studied established categories.\n- **Brand and activation are both required, in roughly 60/40** (Les Binet & Peter Field,\n  *The Long and the Short of It*, IPA, 2013; 996 IPA cases 1980-2016). Activation pays fast and\n  fades; brand pays slowly and compounds. WARC's 2024 \"Multiplier Effect\" work finds moving from\n  performance-only to brand-plus-performance lifts ROI ~90%, and the reverse cuts it ~40% [12].\n- **Zero-click is a strategy, not a defeat** (Amanda Natividad, SparkToro, 2022; Rand Fishkin\n  & Natividad, *Zero Click Marketing*). Platforms reward content that keeps people on-platform;\n  deliver the whole idea there and earn recall, then convert later [27].\n- **Smallest viable audience** (Seth Godin, *This Is Marketing*, 2018). Pick the smallest\n  market that can sustain you, serve it so well they spread it; \"specificity is the way\" [26].\n- **Marketing is fuel × engine** (Emily Kramer, MKT1, 2025). Product marketing is the\n  foundation, content and brand the fuel, growth the engine; avoid \"random acts of marketing\"\n  copied from other companies [22].\n- **Test before you launch** (Nik Sharma, 2021). Run cold-traffic ads to a one-page landing\n  page with a real conversion event before you build inventory; a 2-6% click-through against a\n  0.89% average tells you the message works [23]."},{"ref":"modern/S02-marketing.md","label":"Marketing (2026 layer)","heading":"Frameworks & playbooks","text":"| Framework | Author, year | What it says | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Five components of positioning | April Dunford, *Obviously Awesome* (2019) [18] | Competitive alternatives → unique attributes → value those attributes enable → target customers who care most → market category you compete in (plus relevant trends). Work the components in that order; category is chosen last, not first. | Before any website, deck, ad or hire; whenever \"who is this for?\" gets three answers |\n| Sales-pitch storyboard | April Dunford, *Sales Pitch* (2023) [18][19] | Setup (your insight about the market, the alternatives and their trade-offs, the \"perfect world\") then follow-through (intro, differentiated value, proof, objections, ask). Written for sales, usable for the homepage. | First sales conversations; founder-led sales |\n| Strategic narrative (five elements) | Andy Raskin (2016, Zuora deck) [16] | 1 Name the big relevant change in the world; 2 show there will be winners and losers; 3 tease the promised land; 4 introduce features as \"magic gifts\"; 5 present evidence you can make the story true. | Pitch deck, homepage hero, investor and recruiting stories; CEO owns it [17] |\n| Category design | Ramadan, Peterson, Lochhead & Maney, *Play Bigger* (2016) [53] | Design the problem, the category and the company together; \"lightning strike\" launches. Claim: category kings capture ~76% of category market cap; a 2021 cohort of 35 kings grew from $465B to $1.92T market cap 2014-2021 [53]. Opinion with selective evidence — VC-backed, US-tech sample. | Genuinely new categories with a war chest; not for a \"better X\" |\n| Bullseye | Weinberg & Mares, *Traction* (2015) [15] | Brainstorm all 19 channels → rank into three rings → run cheap parallel tests on the inner ring → focus on the one that wins. 19 channels: viral, PR, unconventional PR, SEM, social/display ads, offline ads, SEO, content, email, engineering-as-marketing, targeting blogs, BD, sales, affiliates, existing platforms, trade shows, offline events, speaking, community. | Any launch; re-run at each growth stage |\n| Growth loops | Balfour, Winters, Kwok, Chen, Reforge (2018) [13][14] | Input → action → output → reinvested as input. Four families: paid/acquisition loops (revenue funds ads), viral/referral, content (UGC indexed by search), product-experience (more users make the product better). Funnels are fine for step metrics, wrong as the growth model. | Designing the growth model; choosing what to build into the product |\n| Growth-model evolution | Elena Verna (2025) [20] | Layer a new channel every ~18 months; 20-25% of resources on exploration; no growth hire before PMF, ~$1M ARR, real data and self-serve revenue. | Planning the year; deciding when to hire growth |\n| Fuel & engine, 70/20/10 | Emily Kramer, MKT1 (2025) [22] | Three sub-functions (product marketing / content & brand / growth). Grade every project green (proven, 70%), yellow (emerging, 20%), red (experiment, 10%). First hire a \"π-shaped\" marketer: deep in two, fluent in a third. | First marketing hire; quarterly planning |\n| Eleven laws of growth | Byron Sharp (2010) [25] | Double jeopardy; retention double jeopardy; Pareto is 50/20 not 80/20; buyer moderation; natural monopoly of light buyers; customer bases seldom differ; attitudes follow behaviour; duplication of purchase; 80%+ distribution needed but not sufficient; NBD-Dirichlet. | Any consumer category; sanity-check loyalty-programme plans |\n| 60/40 brand-activation | Binet & Field, IPA (2013; refined 2017-2024) [12] | ~60% brand / ~40% activation maximises combined long and short-term profit; practitioners adjust to ~70:30 for new entrants and ~40:60 for mature brands; B2B ~46:54 (LinkedIn B2B Institute, 2019). Excess share of voice: +10 ESOV points ≈ +0.5-0.7 share points a year (Danenberg et al., 2016). | Setting budget split once you have any budget at all |\n| Zero-click content | Amanda Natividad, SparkToro (2022) [27] | Deliver the complete idea in the post (≤200 words, bullets, a strong take); expect no click; measure brand search, mentions and DMs instead. | LinkedIn, X, TikTok, newsletters, and now AI answers |\n| GEO / AEO 10 steps | Profound (2025) [51] | Align to KPIs → audit AI visibility → map real prompts → structure for summaries → schema and technical signals → citation authority → E-E-A-T → multimedia and data assets → prompt testing → quarterly benchmark. | Any business whose buyers research with ChatGPT/Gemini/Perplexity |\n| Test-launch playbook | Nik Sharma (2021) [23][24] | Competitive analysis → one landing page → a conversion event worth an email → cold ad creative with varied visuals and one message → report and decide. Sharma's teams test ~17 creatives × 7 pages × 37 audiences to find message-audience fit [24]. | DTC pre-launch; any new product line |\n| Supply-first marketplace | Lenny Rachitsky on andrewchen.com (2019); Andrew Chen, *The Cold Start Problem* (2021) [54][55] | Seed the harder side (usually supply) by hand: direct sales, meetups, piggy-backing existing networks (Craigslist), referrals, converting demand into supply, reaching an \"atomic network\" before spending on demand. | Two-sided products; local services; creator platforms |\n| Product Hunt launch | Waitlister guide (2026) [35] | 4-6 weeks of prep; 400+ warm waitlist minimum (1,000+ better); launch 12:01 AM Pacific Tue-Thu; 150-200 upvotes in the first 4 hours; aged accounts weigh ~10x new ones; email → vote converts 15-25%. Vendor guidance, not audited data. | Dev tools, SaaS, indie products with a US early-adopter audience |"},{"ref":"modern/S02-marketing.md","label":"Marketing (2026 layer)","heading":"Benchmarks & numbers (2024-2026)","text":"| Metric | Value | Segment / context | Source | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Zero-click Google searches (US) | 68.0% | Similarweb panel, Jan-Apr 2026; 60.5% in 2024, ~45% in 2016 | [1] | 2026 |\n| Clicks to the open web per 1,000 searches | 360 (US), 374 (EU); ~30% of US clicks go to Google-owned properties | Datos panel, Sep 2022-May 2024 | [2] | 2024 |\n| Organic CTR when an AI Overview shows | 0.52% not cited / 0.70% cited / 1.45% no AIO | 3,119 informational queries, 42 orgs, 25.1M impressions | [3] | 2025 |\n| Paid CTR when an AI Overview shows | 4.14% not cited / 7.89% cited / 13.88% no AIO | Same panel; cited brands +35% organic, +91% paid CTR | [3] | 2025 |\n| Top-ranking page click loss with AIO | -34.5% | 300,000 keywords, Mar 2024 vs Mar 2025; a 2026 follow-up reports a larger gap (unverified) | [4] | 2025 |\n| Domains cited per LLM answer | 2-7 | vs ~10 blue links | [51] | 2025 |\n| Americans using AI search to research purchases | 71% | Profound survey | [51] | 2025 |\n| Overlap of Google top results and AI citations | fell from ~70% to <20% | Brandlight, via LLMrefs | [52] | 2025-26 |\n| Marketing budget, % of revenue | 7.7%; half of CMOs ≤6% | ~400 CMOs, NA/UK/EU, mostly >$1B revenue | [5][6] | 2025 |\n| Budget split | Paid media 31% / martech 22% / labour 22% / agencies 21% | Same survey; 39% plan agency cuts | [6] | 2025 |\n| B2B SaaS spend | Sales 15% (18% high-growth), marketing 10%, CS 7% of revenue | SaaS Capital, ~1,000 companies; VC-backed spend ~2x bootstrapped on marketing | [44] | 2025 |\n| Marketing spend by stage | Seed 15-25% of ARR; Series A 15-20%; B+ 10-15% | Agency synthesis, US SaaS | [46] | 2025 |\n| S&M efficiency (revenue per S&M $) | 3.19x median, down from ~6x | 155 private B2B SaaS, US/CA/AU | [45] | 2025 |\n| SaaS growth, median | 75-100% (<$1M ARR); 40-110% ($1-5M); 15-40% (>$50M) | 800+ companies, High Alpha / Growth Unhinged; AI-native grow ~3x faster | [21] | 2025 |\n| DTC customer acquisition cost | $226.38 average; +7% YoY, +60% in 5 years | Shopify, US retail DTC | [10] | 2024 |\n| Meta CPM | $22.98 average Q4 2025, $25.22 BFCM peak; ~89% above 2020 | Triple Whale / Eightx panels; Q1 2026 reset ~11% lower | [10][11] | 2025-26 |\n| Meta DTC ad panel | CPM $13.52, CPC $0.57, CTR 2.68%, ROAS 2.96, CPA $49 | 15 managed DTC accounts, $2.5M spend, Jul 2025-Jun 2026 | [9] | 2026 |\n| CAC by channel (ranges) | Meta platform CPA $38-58 (fully loaded $212-230); Google Search $50-130; TikTok Ads $90-129; TikTok Shop $30-80; micro-influencer $40-120; referral $40-65; SEO fully loaded $200-300 | Eightx aggregate of agency and vendor data | [11] | 2026 |\n| Google Shopping CPC | +33.7% YoY | US e-commerce | [10] | 2025 |\n| LTV:CAC / payback | 3:1; payback 3-6 months healthy, 6-12 acceptable | DTC rule of thumb | [10] | 2025 |\n| Email flows vs campaigns | Click 5.58% vs 1.69%; RPR ~18x; flows 5.3% of sends, ~41% of revenue; top-decile flows $7.79 RPR | 183,000+ Klaviyo accounts | [28] | 2026 |\n| SMS flows | Click ~10% (top >16%); 7.6% of sends, 45.2% of SMS revenue; 64.4% of flow revenue from new buyers | Same | [29] | 2026 |\n| AI product recommendations in email | Click 3.75% average, 8.79% top performers | Same | [28] | 2026 |\n| Influencer industry size | ~$32.6B (2025) from $24B (2024) | Influencer Marketing Hub estimate | [8] | 2025 |\n| Creator budgets | 87.5% expect increases; 51% (nano) and 53% (micro) plan expansion vs 21% macro; TikTok primary for 31% | 600+ marketers | [7] | 2026 |\n| Creator engagement | Nano 2.71% (Instagram), micro ~1.8%; nano on TikTok 10.3% | IMH | [8] | 2025 |\n| Influencer ROI claim | $5.78 per $1 | Digital Marketing Institute, vendor-adjacent, treat as upper bound | [8] | 2025 |\n| Community value | 84% report positive impact; only 17.2% can quantify it (24% in 2025); CRM-connected teams rate success 57.6% vs 30.6% | CMX, n≈174 | [36][37] | 2026 |\n| Marketers using AI | 66% (US 74%); 4% let AI write whole pieces; 1-2 hours saved a day | 1,000+ marketers | [38] | 2025 |\n| AI content editing | 7% publish unedited; 56% substantially rewrite | HubSpot | [39] | 2025 |\n| WhatsApp Business | 200M+ businesses; 98% open-rate claim (vendor) | Meta/vendor compilations | [30] | 2024-25 |\n| TikTok Shop SEA GMV | $4.4B (2022) → $45.6B (2025); Indonesia $13.1B, +111%, 35% of its e-commerce | Momentum Works via Digital in Asia | [33] | 2025 |\n| App tracking opt-in after ATT | ~16% initially; Meta est. $12.8B revenue loss in 2022 | Secondary coverage | [43] | 2021-22 |"},{"ref":"modern/S02-marketing.md","label":"Marketing (2026 layer)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **If you cannot name the competitive alternative, you do not have positioning yet** — start\n  with \"what would they do if we didn't exist?\" (Dunford [18]).\n- **If the homepage opens with the product, rewrite it to open with the shift** (Raskin [16]).\n- **Pre-launch: run the Bullseye once, test three channels for two weeks each with a fixed\n  small budget, then put 70%+ into the winner** (Weinberg & Mares [15]; Kramer's 70/20/10 [22]).\n- **If ad CTR on cold traffic is under ~1% and landing-page conversion under industry\n  average, the message is wrong, not the channel** — fix positioning before scaling spend\n  (Sharma: target 2-6% CTR vs 0.89% average [23]).\n- **If a channel needs constant cash to keep moving, it is a funnel; find or build the loop\n  that reinvests its output** (Reforge [13]).\n- **Do not hire a growth lead before PMF, ~$1M ARR and self-serve revenue; do not hire one to\n  reverse a decline** (Verna [20]).\n- **Expect any working channel to decay; budget a new channel every ~18 months** (Verna [20]).\n- **Never let brand fall below ~30% of spend, even at launch; new entrants should be nearer\n  70:30 brand-heavy if they have any budget, mature brands 40:60** (Binet & Field practitioner\n  ranges [12]). Founders with no media budget substitute founder-led content and community.\n- **If SEO is in the plan, plan for citations, not clicks:** a cited brand keeps 35% more\n  organic and 91% more paid CTR than an uncited one on the same query [3]; assume 60-70% of\n  searches send no click at all [1].\n- **Build flows before campaigns:** welcome, browse/cart abandonment, post-purchase and\n  win-back flows return ~18x the revenue per recipient of blasts [28].\n- **Prefer nano and micro creators, pay for content rights, and track with codes:** engagement\n  is higher [8], budgets are moving there [7], and 45.9% of brands still track with promo\n  codes because attribution is unsolved [7].\n- **In a two-sided business, seed supply by hand first; do not spend on demand until the\n  atomic network exists** (Rachitsky / Chen [54][55]).\n- **CAC must be judged fully loaded** (platform CPA $38-58 vs fully loaded $212-230 on Meta\n  [11]); use LTV:CAC ≥ 3 and payback ≤ 6-12 months as the gate [10].\n- **Publishing AI content at scale without a value gate risks a manual action** — Google's\n  March 2024 policy is method-neutral, and roughly 1,446 sites were hit in the first wave [41].\n- **Marketing spend as a share of revenue is a lagging norm, not a target:** 7.7% is a\n  large-company average [5]; early-stage software spends 10% on marketing plus 15% on sales\n  [44], seed-stage 15-25% of ARR on marketing alone [46]."},{"ref":"modern/S02-marketing.md","label":"Marketing (2026 layer)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. **Position (1-2 weeks).** List competitive alternatives (including \"do nothing\" and\n   spreadsheets), unique attributes, the value each enables, the customers who care most, and\n   the category you will claim. Draft the one-sentence position and the Raskin shift statement.\n2. **Prove the message with cold traffic (1-2 weeks, small budget).** One landing page, one\n   conversion event (waitlist, pre-order, sample), 5-10 creative variants with the same message.\n   Gate: CTR and conversion above channel averages [23].\n3. **Choose the channel with Bullseye (2-4 weeks).** Rank the 19 channels, test the top three\n   in parallel with equal budgets, pick the one that wins on CAC and quality of customer [15].\n4. **Design the loop.** Write the input → action → output → reinvest cycle for the winning\n   channel (e.g. customer → referral → customer; usage → content → search → user; revenue →\n   ads → revenue) and build the product feature or content system that closes it [13][14].\n5. **Build the owned base before you scale paid.** Email or WhatsApp capture on every page;\n   welcome, abandonment, post-purchase and win-back flows live before the first big campaign\n   [28][29].\n6. **Launch as an event.** 4-6 weeks of waitlist building; Product Hunt or equivalent\n   community launch; founder posts as zero-click content; creators seeded with product [35][27].\n7. **Instrument.** Track blended and channel CAC (fully loaded), payback, LTV:CAC, branded\n   search and direct traffic (brand proxies), AI-answer citations for your top prompts, and\n   flow revenue share. Review monthly.\n8. **Rebalance quarterly.** 70/20/10 across proven, emerging and experimental projects [22];\n   add a channel when the winner's marginal CAC rises for two consecutive quarters [20]."},{"ref":"modern/S02-marketing.md","label":"Marketing (2026 layer)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"Positioning canvas (Dunford):\n- What would our best customers use if we didn't exist?\n- What can we do that those alternatives cannot?\n- What value does each of those attributes unlock, in the customer's words?\n- Which customers care about that value most, and how would we recognise them?\n- What market category makes our value obvious? What trend makes it urgent now?\n\nNarrative check (Raskin):\n- What undeniable change in the world do we open with? Who wins and who loses if they ignore\n  it? What does the promised land look like? Which features are the \"magic gifts\"? What proof\n  shows we have taken someone there?\n\nChannel selection (Bullseye):\n- Rank all 19 channels: inner ring (3), promising (6), long shot (10).\n- For each inner-ring test: budget, duration, success metric, kill threshold, owner.\n- After the tests: which channel delivered the best customers at the lowest fully loaded CAC?\n\nGrowth-loop worksheet (Reforge):\n- Input → action → output. What is reinvested? What is the cycle time? What is the loop's\n  conversion at each step? What would double it?\n\nOwned-channel readiness:\n- Capture on every entry page; welcome flow; abandonment flow; post-purchase flow; win-back\n  flow; consent recorded per jurisdiction; WhatsApp Business profile and catalogue where relevant.\n\nAI-search readiness:\n- Ten real prompts a buyer would type; are we cited? Who is? Do we have an \"about\", named\n  authors, structured lists, first-hand data, and schema on the pages that answer them? [51]\n\nBudget sanity:\n- Planned spend as % of revenue vs stage norms [44][46]; brand vs activation split [12];\n  fully loaded CAC per channel [11]; payback ≤ 12 months [10]."},{"ref":"modern/S02-marketing.md","label":"Marketing (2026 layer)","heading":"Regional notes","text":"- **US.** CAN-SPAM is opt-out, so email is legally easy but deliverability-policed [47]. Paid\n  social is the default first channel and the most inflated: Meta Q4 2025 CPM $22.98 [10]; ATT\n  broke cross-app attribution in 2021 [43]. Product Hunt, Reddit and creator seeding are the\n  standard launch stack.\n- **UK.** PECR sits on top of GDPR: soft opt-in for existing customers, opt-in otherwise;\n  fines to £500k under PECR and up to 4% of turnover under GDPR [47]. Binet & Field's IPA\n  evidence base is UK-born and the 60/40 norm is most cited here [12].\n- **EU.** GDPR opt-in consent (fines to €20M or 4% of global turnover) [47]; regulation also\n  shows up in the data — a smaller share of EU search clicks flow to Google's own properties\n  than in the US [2]. Expect cookie banners, consent-mode analytics and higher form friction.\n- **Africa, Nigeria especially.** WhatsApp is the owned channel: Nigeria has ~51M users [30]\n  and vendor compilations put adoption near 95% and marketing-message cost around $0.05 [31];\n  small-business usage across Sub-Saharan Africa is reported near 78% (vendor-compiled,\n  unaudited) [31]. The Nigeria Data Protection Act 2023 requires consent for direct marketing;\n  on 11 June 2026 the Federal High Court, Abuja ordered GTCO to stop unsolicited marketing to\n  non-customers and to disclose the data source — possession of a number is not consent [48].\n  Instagram plus WhatsApp catalogue plus bank-transfer checkout is the working DTC stack; card\n  ad payments and Meta ad-account restrictions are recurring frictions.\n- **India.** 535.8M WhatsApp users [30]; the DPDP Act requires explicit opt-in with penalties\n  up to ₹250 crore [47]. Volume, low ARPU and vernacular content favour creator and WhatsApp\n  channels over search.\n- **Southeast Asia.** Social commerce is the store: TikTok Shop SEA GMV $45.6B in 2025, 71% of\n  its global total; Indonesia $13.1B (+111%), Vietnam +150% [33]; social commerce was ~80% of\n  Indonesian digital transactions in 2024 and ~60% of Indonesians buy via livestream [34].\n  TikTok's paid ad reach has fallen 30-40% in several markets [58], so organic and shop-native\n  affiliate content matter more than ad buys.\n- **Middle East / GCC.** Digital ad market ~$13.6B in 2025, mobile-first, growing ~7% a year\n  [49]; Arabic creator content and WhatsApp/Instagram commerce dominate SMB marketing.\n- **Latin America.** Highest WhatsApp penetration anywhere (Brazil 93-99%, Mexico 93%);\n  businesses spent $3.6B on the platform in 2024 and in-WhatsApp transactions grew 85% [32];\n  digital ad spend ~$45B in 2025, growing ~11% a year [50]. Marketing messages cost $0.01-0.09\n  each depending on country [32].\n- **Everywhere:** \"check local law\" on consent, cooling-off rights, influencer disclosure and\n  price-display rules before the first campaign."},{"ref":"modern/S02-marketing.md","label":"Marketing (2026 layer)","heading":"AI-era notes","text":"- **Search behaviour moved first.** Zero-click reached 68% in early 2026, \"the fastest\n  acceleration in the last decade\" [1]; even queries without an AI Overview lost ~46% organic\n  CTR year on year as users click less everywhere [3]. The measurable job of content is now\n  to be *cited* (by Google's AI Overviews and by ChatGPT/Gemini/Perplexity) and to be\n  *remembered*, not to be clicked.\n- **What being cited requires** (evidence from Seer, Profound and LLMrefs): first-hand data,\n  named authors, direct answers in the first 200 words, structured lists (30-40% more AI\n  visibility in one 10,000-query study [52]), fresh pages (citations drop after ~3 months [52]),\n  schema and consistent entity facts across the web [51]. Only 2-7 domains are cited per answer\n  [51], so this is a winner-takes-most contest — but the overlap with Google's top ten has\n  fallen below 20% [52], which is an opening for new brands.\n- **AI-referred traffic is small and converts well** — Vercel reports 10% of new sign-ups\n  from ChatGPT referrals [52]; broader claims of 1% of all web traffic at higher conversion than\n  organic circulate but are vendor-sourced (unverified).\n- **Production is cheap; the moat is distribution and taste.** Two-thirds of marketers use AI\n  and most rewrite what it drafts [38][39]; Klaviyo's AI product recommendations lift email\n  clicks to 3.75% [28]; 93% of community professionals now use AI tools [36]. Ad platforms\n  (Advantage+, Performance Max) automate targeting and creative assembly, so the founder's\n  leverage sits in the offer, the creative concept and the first-party data fed in.\n- **Hype vs evidence.** Programmatic AI content at scale is a documented penalty risk [40][41].\n  \"AI agents will run your marketing\" is ahead of the data: HubSpot finds AI saving 1-2 hours a\n  day, not replacing the function [38]. GEO tooling is a new vendor category whose benchmarks\n  are mostly self-published; treat conversion claims as upper bounds. Attribution has got worse,\n  not better, since ATT and the cookie reversal [42][43] — expect to rely on incrementality\n  tests and brand proxies rather than platform dashboards.\n- **What a founder should do now:** write the ten prompts a buyer would type and check who is\n  cited; publish first-hand data the LLMs cannot get elsewhere; keep human editing on anything\n  that carries the brand; use AI for research, variants and personalisation (Verna's warning:\n  micro-optimisations rarely move the number [20])."},{"ref":"modern/S02-marketing.md","label":"Marketing (2026 layer)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Choosing a category before doing the positioning work; leading with features (Dunford [18]).\n- Spreading a launch across six channels at once; never finding the one that carries 70% [15].\n- Scaling paid before the owned flows exist, then discovering fully loaded CAC is 4-5x the\n  platform's CPA [11][10].\n- Reading platform-reported ROAS as truth after ATT and the cookie reversal [42][43].\n- Cutting brand to zero because it is hard to measure; long-term effects decay silently [12].\n- Planning \"SEO traffic\" as if it were 2018; two-thirds of searches send nothing [1].\n- Publishing hundreds of AI pages without a value gate — a policy violation regardless of\n  authorship [40][41].\n- Hiring a growth lead to fix a retention problem, or rebranding to fix acquisition [20].\n- Running a community you cannot connect to CRM or revenue — 83% cannot quantify it [36].\n- Blasting purchased lists: unlawful under GDPR, PECR, DPDP and, since the 2026 GTCO ruling,\n  enforceable in Nigeria [47][48].\n- Treating a creator campaign as an ad buy: no content rights, no codes, no whitelisting, so\n  nothing is learnt [7]."},{"ref":"modern/S02-marketing.md","label":"Marketing (2026 layer)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`positioning` — the deliberate definition of how a product is the best in the world at something a defined market cares about, set relative to competitive alternatives (Dunford).\n`strategic narrative` — a company story that opens with a change in the world and casts the customer as the hero who must adapt (Raskin).\n`category design` — creating and naming a new market category so the company that defines it becomes its default (Play Bigger).\n`growth loop` — a closed system where the output of one cohort (users, content, revenue) becomes the input that acquires the next (Reforge).\n`Bullseye` — the Traction method of ranking 19 channels, testing the inner three cheaply and focusing on the winner.\n`zero-click search` — a query that ends with no click to any website; 68% of US Google searches in early 2026.\n`AI Overview` — Google's generated answer block at the top of results; present on 20%+ of queries.\n`GEO / AEO` — generative (answer) engine optimisation: earning citations in LLM answers rather than rankings.\n`zero-click content` — platform-native content that delivers its full value without a link out (Natividad).\n`mental / physical availability` — being thought of in buying situations and being easy to find and buy (Sharp).\n`double jeopardy` — small brands have fewer buyers who are also slightly less loyal (Sharp).\n`60/40` — Binet & Field's brand-building to activation budget heuristic.\n`ESOV` — excess share of voice: share of category advertising minus share of market; predicts share growth.\n`flow` — an automated, behaviour-triggered email/SMS/WhatsApp sequence (welcome, abandonment, post-purchase), as opposed to a one-off campaign.\n`fully loaded CAC` — acquisition cost including creative, tooling, agency and people, not just platform spend.\n`ATT` — Apple's App Tracking Transparency prompt (2021) that collapsed cross-app ad attribution.\n`atomic network` — the smallest self-sustaining group of users on one side of a network (Chen).\n`smallest viable audience` — the narrowest market you can delight enough that it spreads (Godin)."},{"ref":"modern/S03-hiring-and-people.md","label":"Hiring and people (2026 layer)","heading":"What changed since 2018","text":"- **Co-founder splits are converging on equal, and solo founding is common but funded less.** Two-founder\n  teams splitting 50/50 rose from 31.5% (2015) to 45.9% (2024); solo founders were 35% of new startups\n  in 2024 but only 17% of those that closed a VC round [1][2].\n- **Equity became a benchmarked market.** Carta (45,000+ cap tables), Pave (8,000+ companies) and Index\n  Ventures' OptionPlan (20,000+ grants, 1,650+ startups) replaced folklore with medians [3][10][61].\n  Four-year/one-year-cliff is still the default, but private-company ongoing grants now vest in ~3.5\n  years and refresh grants are performance-gated, not automatic [28].\n- **Selection science was revised.** Sackett et al. (2022) re-ran the classic meta-analyses with better\n  corrections: structured interviews (r = .42) now top the table and cognitive ability fell to .31 [13].\n- **Hiring is global by default.** An employer-of-record (EOR) turns a compliant hire in 150+ countries\n  into a ~$499-599/month subscription [37]; Deel processed 1M+ worker contracts across 37,000+ companies in\n  2025 [35]. Classification enforcement tightened in parallel (US DOL economic-reality rule, California\n  ABC test, UK IR35 thresholds, Nigerian courts on substance-over-form) [32][33][34].\n- **Remote settled into structured hybrid, then met a 2025-26 return-to-office push.** 66% of US firms\n  still offer location flexibility (Flex Index Q3 2025), but required office days rose ~12% since early\n  2024 and fully on-site job postings jumped from 65% (Q4 2025) to 87% (Q2 2026) in Robert Half's\n  series [40][41].\n- **AI sits on both sides of the table.** 39% of organisations used AI in HR by Dec 2025 (26% in 2024)\n  [51]; ~40% of candidates use AI to apply and 6% admit to interview fraud [48]. Regulators answered:\n  NYC Local Law 144 (bias audits, 2023) and the EU AI Act, which classes recruitment AI as high-risk from\n  2 Aug 2026 [43][47].\n- **Startups run leaner.** Average Series A headcount fell from ~21 to ~15.9 [3]; startup hiring slowed in\n  every sector but hardware in H1 2025 while salaries rose and equity stayed flat [4].\n- **UK EMI expanded (6 Apr 2026): company cap £3m → £6m, gross assets £30m → £120m, headcount 250 →\n  500** [29]; India's DPIIT startups defer ESOP tax to exit; SEBI (Jun 2025) let founders keep ESOPs\n  through IPO [59][60].\n- **Performance management moved to a quarterly rhythm** — 39% of high-performing HR teams review\n  quarterly vs 10% of low performers [54]."},{"ref":"modern/S03-hiring-and-people.md","label":"Hiring and people (2026 layer)","heading":"Core ideas (current consensus)","text":"- **Scorecard before search.** Define the mission, 3-8 measurable outcomes and the competencies of the\n  role, then hunt for an \"A player\": someone with a 90% chance of achieving outcomes only the top 10% of\n  candidates could (Smart & Street, *Who*, 2008) [17]. Mochary (2020) makes it a 90-day roadmap [18].\n- **Structure beats intuition.** Structured interviews are the single most valid tool (r = .42), ahead of\n  job-knowledge tests (.40), biodata (.38), work samples (.33) and cognitive ability (.31) — evidence, not\n  opinion (Sackett et al. 2022) [13]. Google's data: four interviews predict with 86% confidence; each\n  extra interviewer adds ~1% (Bock, *Work Rules!*, 2015) [16].\n- **References are the real interview.** Run 6-8 (Altman) to 10-20 (Zappacosta) calls, half of them\n  backchannel, and ask \"who else should I talk to that might tell me this could be a mistake?\" (First\n  Round Review, 2024) [22]. Mochary: check references you choose, not the candidate's list [18].\n- **Hire for the next 12-18 months, not forever.** Shorter and they can't scale; longer and you over-hire\n  a bored executive (Gil, *High Growth Handbook*, 2018) [19].\n- **Hiring is a network effect.** \"The first 100 hires define the next 200\"; the best predictor of a\n  hire's quality is who referred them (Graham, First Round, 2015) [20]. Mochary's sourcing loop: ask every\n  strong person \"who are the three most talented people you know?\" [18].\n- **Founder-led sales first, engineers first.** Across 20 top B2B startups, >2/3 hired an engineer first,\n  100% had one in the first three, and sales became the second most common role only inside the first ten\n  (Rachitsky, 2023) [23].\n- **Split founder equity near-equally, vest everyone, never on performance metrics.** \"All the work is\n  ahead of you\" (Y Combinator / Seibel); vesting and a cliff apply to founders too [11][12]. Carta data\n  shows teams drifting this way [1].\n- **Pay is a system, not a negotiation.** Benchmark salary and equity by role, level and location; tie\n  refresh grants to rating (Pave 2025: ~100% of promoted staff refreshed vs ~20-30% of \"meets\") [28].\n- **Adequate performance gets a generous severance** — the keeper test (McCord/Netflix, HBR 2014;\n  *Powerful*, 2018). Opinion, contested, and culturally loaded outside the US [25][26].\n- **Give away your Legos.** Leaders who grow with the company hand their job away every few months;\n  30-50 people is where \"a family becomes a company\" and things must be written down (Graham) [20].\n- **Onboarding is the manager's job.** New hires are 3.4x more likely to call onboarding exceptional when\n  the manager is involved (Gallup, via [55]); 86% decide how long they'll stay within six months [55].\n- **Write the operating system down.** Founding documents, hiring plans, role definitions, feedback and\n  performance mechanisms as one toolkit with templates (Hughes Johnson, *Scaling People*, 2023) [27]."},{"ref":"modern/S03-hiring-and-people.md","label":"Hiring and people (2026 layer)","heading":"Frameworks & playbooks","text":"| Framework | Author, year | What it says | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| The A Method (*Who*) | Geoff Smart & Randy Street, 2008 [17] | Scorecard → Source → Select → Sell; four interviews: screening (30 min phone), \"Who\" chronological walk-through (Topgrading lineage, Bradford Smart), focused (outcomes), reference | Every hire from employee #1 |\n| Rule of four + structured loop | Laszlo Bock, *Work Rules!*, 2015 [16] | Fixed questions, scoring rubric, hiring committee; four interviewers is the plateau; Google cut hiring time per employee from 4-10 hrs/week to 1.5 | Designing the interview loop |\n| Sackett validity hierarchy | Sackett, Zhang, Berry & Lievens, 2022/23 [13][14] | Structured interview .42 > job knowledge .40 > biodata .38 > work sample .33 > GMA .31; wide credibility intervals (.18-.66 for interviews) so quality of structure matters [15] | Choosing assessment tools |\n| 12-18 month executive rule + six traits | Elad Gil, 2018 [19] | Hire for the next 12-18 months; look for functional expertise, team-building, collegiality, communication, owner mentality, strategic thinking; \"give yourself permission to make hiring mistakes\" | First VP/exec hires |\n| Give Away Your Legos / growth stages | Molly Graham, First Round 2015 (upd. 2025) [20][21] | 30-50: family → company; 50-200: hiring is a network effect, fire low performers fast; 200-750: personality sets; 750+: politics | Every headcount doubling |\n| 90-day roadmap, buddy, PIP ladder | Matt Mochary, *The Great CEO Within*, 2020 [18] | Written 90-day goals shared before day 1; a buddy for 15 min daily for two weeks; feedback as fact/feeling/story/request; PIP with 7/30/60/90-day milestones and documentation | Onboarding and underperformance |\n| Reference-call protocol | First Round Review, 2024 [22] | 6-20 calls incl. backchannel; \"compare to the best you've seen\", \"rate 1-100 — why not 100?\", \"if they left in 3 months, why?\" | Before every offer |\n| First-10-hires pattern | Lenny Rachitsky, 2023 [23] | Engineers first; CS/support and designers in the first three; sales, PM and a recruiter inside the first ten; channels: friends/ex-colleagues > cold LinkedIn/GitHub outreach > job boards > employees' networks | Sequencing the first ten |\n| Co-founder equity rules | Y Combinator (Michael Seibel) [11][12] | Near-equal splits; 4-year vesting with 1-year cliff for all founders; no metric-based founder equity | Before incorporation |\n| OptionPlan / ESOP rules | Index Ventures (Rewarding Talent; tool updated) [7][8][9][10] | Pool 10% at seed (20% for some accelerators), ~12% Series A, 14% B, 16% C; benchmarks per role/level; leaver, strike-price and change-of-control rules | Sizing the pool, first grants |\n| Refresh matrix and geo tiers | Pave, 2025 [28] | Refresh by rating; equity geo-discounts (29-36%) run larger than cash (11-16%) | Comp reviews after ~20 people |\n| Keeper test | Patty McCord / Reed Hastings, 2014-2018 [25][26] | \"Would I fight to keep this person?\"; if not, pay severance now | Performance calls in senior teams (use with care) |\n| Tour of duty | Reid Hoffman, Ben Casnocha & Chris Yeh, *The Alliance*, 2014 (classic; not fetched) | Frame each role as a 2-4 year mission with explicit mutual promises instead of pretending at lifetime employment | Job descriptions, retention conversations |\n| Operating-system toolkit | Claire Hughes Johnson, *Scaling People*, 2023 [27] | Founding docs, hiring plan, team structure, feedback and performance systems with 100+ pages of templates | Getting from 20 to 200 |"},{"ref":"modern/S03-hiring-and-people.md","label":"Hiring and people (2026 layer)","heading":"Benchmarks & numbers (2024-2026)","text":"| Metric | Value | Segment / context | Source | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Two-founder teams splitting equity equally | 45.9% (31.5% in 2015) | US startups on Carta, 45,000+ cos | [1] | 2025 |\n| Three- / four-founder teams splitting equally | 27.3% / 16.7% | up from 21% / 10.8% a year earlier | [1] | 2025 |\n| Solo-founder share of new startups | 35% (29% in 2023, 17% in 2017) | but 17% of startups that closed a VC round | [2] | 2025 |\n| First employee equity grant | ~1.5%, tapering fast | Peter Walker, Carta | [3] | 2025 |\n| Advisor equity | asked ~1%; median granted ~0.25% (range 0.1-1.0%) | Carta | [3][61] | 2025 |\n| Seed grants by level (US) | senior eng/product 1.00%; mid 0.45%; junior 0.15% (eng) / 0.05% (product); senior BD 0.35%; senior marketing 0.9% | of fully diluted equity | [7] | Index handbook |\n| Total equity to first ten hires at seed | ~5% FDE (US average) vs 3-4% (Europe) | at a $6m post-money seed | [7] | Index |\n| Option pool by stage | 10% seed (some accelerators 20%); 12% Series A (10-15%); 14% B; 16% C | Index OptionPlan model, Europe | [8] | Index |\n| Early-stage pool range | 10-20%; 10% at seed standard | Carta guide | [5] | 2025 |\n| ESOP size, APAC & Middle East | 7% pre-seed → 11% seed → 11-13% later; US median 18% at Series C+ | Carta APAC/ME | [6] | 2025 |\n| Employee ownership, late stage | ~10% Europe vs ~20% US; Europe gives 2/3 of options to execs, US the reverse | Index | [7][8] | Index |\n| Vesting length | 4.0 yrs new-hire (private); ~3.5 yrs ongoing grants (private); ~3.0 yrs public | Pave | [28] | 2025 |\n| Refresh grant probability by rating | ~100% promoted; 50-75% \"above\"; 20-30% \"meets\" | Pave merit-cycle data | [28] | 2025 |\n| Equity geo discount | 29% (tier 1→2), 36% (tier 1→3) vs cash 11% / 16% | Pave | [28] | 2025 |\n| Startup new-hire salary | +5.8% vs Apr 2022; ~+5% since Jan 2024; equity flat | Carta | [4] | H1 2025 |\n| Dilution per priced round | ~20% | Carta (AI and non-AI similar) | [3] | 2025 |\n| Average Series A headcount | ~21 → ~15.9 | Carta | [3] | 2025 |\n| Structured interview validity | r = .42 (80% CI .18-.66) | vs GMA .31, work sample .33 | [13][15] | 2022 |\n| Interviews needed | 4 → 86% confidence; +1% per extra | Google | [16] | 2014/15 |\n| Reference calls per exec hire | 6-8 (Altman) to 10-20 (Zappacosta) | First Round | [22] | 2024 |\n| First hire is an engineer | >2/3 of 20 B2B startups; 100% in first three | Lenny's Newsletter | [23] | 2023 |\n| EOR cost | Deel $599 / Remote $599 (annual) / Oyster $499 per employee-month; contractors $29-49 | 150-200+ countries | [37] | Oct 2025 |\n| Payroll/HRIS, 20-person US team | Gusto ~$160/mo ($40 + $6/emp); Rippling ~$195 ($35 + $8); Deel ~$380 | US-only | [38] | May 2026 |\n| Mid-market HRIS | HiBob $16-25 PEPM; Workday $34-42 PEPM + 150-200% setup | 50+ / 500+ staff | [39] | Feb 2026 |\n| US firms offering location flexibility | 66% (34% require full-time office) | Flex Index Q3 2025 | [40] | 2025 |\n| Remote-capable US workers | 52% hybrid / 26% remote / 22% on-site; 2.6 office days/week | Gallup | [40] | 2026 |\n| All US full-time workers | 12% remote / 27% hybrid / 61% on-site; ~25% of paid days at home | Stanford SWAA / Bloom | [40] | Jan 2026 |\n| Fully on-site job postings | 65% (Q4 2025) → 87% (Q2 2026); hybrid 10%, remote 3% | Robert Half postings | [41] | 2026 |\n| Orgs using AI in HR | 39% (Dec 2025) vs 26% (2024); 60% at 5,000+ staff, 33% under 100 | SHRM | [51] | 2025 |\n| US companies using AI in hiring | 51%; 82% of them for CV screening; 21% let AI reject at every stage without review | ResumeBuilder, n=948 | [52] | Oct 2024 |\n| Candidates using AI to apply | ~40% (n=3,290); 6% admit interview fraud (n=3,000) | Gartner | [48][49] | 2025 |\n| Candidate trust in AI evaluation | 26%; 62% likelier to apply if interview is in person | Gartner | [48][49] | 2025 |\n| Fake candidate profiles | 1 in 4 by 2028 (forecast) | Gartner | [48] | 2025 |\n| Ghost jobs | 27.4% of US LinkedIn listings (one analysis) | Clarify Capital | [53] | 2025 |\n| AI in performance reviews | 48% use AI to write reviews; 45% to summarise a year's feedback; 23% for bias checks | Lattice SoPS | [54] | 2026 |\n| Quarterly review cadence | 39% of high-performing HR teams vs 10% low; Europe 51% vs US 25% | Lattice | [54] | 2026 |\n| Early attrition | 22% left a job inside 90 days; 86% decide tenure in first six months | Insight Global / Enboarder via AIHR | [55] | 2025 |\n| Cost of a failed hire | $25,000 (HR managers) to $50,000 (CPOs) estimates | Enboarder survey | [55] | 2025 |\n| UK EMI limits from 6 Apr 2026 | £250k per employee (unchanged); £6m company; £120m gross assets; 500 staff; 15-year life | HMRC via Orrick/Saffery | [29][30] | 2025-26 |\n| EMI tax | no income tax/NIC at exercise if strike ≥ market value; BADR CGT 18% from 6 Apr 2026 (14% before, 24% standard); 24-month hold from grant | Saffery / Vestd | [30][31] | 2026 |\n| Nigeria tech pay (Lagos) | back-end dev ₦1.2-2.5m/mo ($800-1,667 at ₦1,500/$); TPM ₦1.5-2.8m; CTO ₦3-5m+ | TechCabal | [56] | Sep 2025 |\n| Africa engineer salaries | Lagos $7.5-11k/yr; Nairobi $14-14.7k; Cairo $25k; Cape Town $55k | Tech In Africa | [57] | Aug 2025 |\n| India ESOP pool | 10-15% early stage; 4-yr/1-yr cliff standard; DPIIT tax deferral to exit | PedalStart | [59] | 2026 |\n| Cross-border hires by top-funded startups | 1,400+ in 2025; 28% software developers; from UK 12.2%, Canada 11.9%, Germany 8.8% | Deel | [35][36] | 2026 |\n| Argentine workers choosing USD pay | 84.6% | Deel | [35] | 2026 |"},{"ref":"modern/S03-hiring-and-people.md","label":"Hiring and people (2026 layer)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **If you cannot write the scorecard, you are not ready to hire.** Mission, outcomes, competencies first;\n  job descriptions are marketing, scorecards are the contract (Smart & Street) [17].\n- **Split founder equity equally unless one founder has already de-risked the business** (product, revenue,\n  capital); then adjust by a few points, never by \"idea credit\". Vest all founders 4 years / 1-year cliff\n  (YC) [11][12]; the data trend supports it [1].\n- **Solo founders: expect a funding penalty** (35% of startups, 17% of VC rounds) and recruit a co-founder or\n  a senior first hire with meaningful equity [2].\n- **Hire #1 is almost always an engineer unless the founders are technical; hire sales only once founder-led\n  sales has closed repeatable deals** (Rachitsky) [23]. Hire a recruiter by ~hire 8-10 if you plan 20+ in a\n  year — it recurs across top startups [23].\n- **Four structured interviews, then stop.** Each extra interviewer buys ~1% predictive power at a large\n  candidate-experience cost (Bock) [16]. Replace one interview with a paid work sample when the role\n  produces artefacts [13].\n- **Never extend an offer without references you chose.** If a candidate will not enable backchannel calls\n  before the offer, delay the offer (First Round; Mochary) [22][18].\n- **Exec hire size test:** would this person be the right leader for a company 2-3x today's size in 18\n  months? If they are right for 5 years out, they are too senior (Gil) [19].\n- **Pool sizing:** create ~10% at seed and top up to 12-15% at Series A rather than over-reserving early —\n  every unused option is founder dilution (Index) [8]. In APAC/ME, 11% at seed is the median [6].\n- **First ten grants total ~5% (US) or 3-4% (Europe); a senior engineer ≈ 1%, mid ≈ 0.45%, junior ≈\n  0.15%** (Index) [7]. Below-market cash → above-benchmark equity, and say so in the offer [9].\n- **Refreshes are earned:** budget them for promotions and \"above\" ratings; a \"meets\" refresh is the\n  exception (Pave) [28].\n- **Contractor vs employee: if you control how, when and where the work is done, they work only for you,\n  and the work is core to the business, they are an employee in the US, UK and Nigeria alike**\n  [32][33][34]. Use an EOR ($499-599/month) before that line is crossed abroad [37].\n- **Remote policy: pick structured hybrid (3 days) or fully remote with in-person weeks — mandates without\n  attendance enforcement raise required days 12% and actual days 1-3%** (Flex Index) [40].\n- **Onboarding: the manager runs day 1 to day 90.** Ship the 90-day roadmap and a buddy before start;\n  manager involvement is worth 3.4x on perceived quality [18][55].\n- **Performance: quarterly written check-ins, continuous feedback, a documented 7/30/60/90 PIP before any\n  termination** (Mochary; Lattice) [18][54].\n- **AI in hiring: use it to draft, schedule, transcribe and summarise; never to reject.** Keep a trained\n  human with override authority on every decision, tell candidates, and log it — required in the EU from\n  Aug 2026 and in NYC now [43][47]."},{"ref":"modern/S03-hiring-and-people.md","label":"Hiring and people (2026 layer)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. **Choose and paper the co-founder(s).** Test the relationship with a 4-8 week build sprint; agree roles,\n   decision rights, time commitment and exit terms; split near-equally; sign vesting (4/1) and IP\n   assignment at incorporation; pick the pool size for the first round [11][12][8].\n2. **Plan the first ten.** Map the next 12-18 months of outcomes → roles in sequence (engineering → design\n   /CS → sales/PM → recruiter); decide location and legal route per role (local employee, EOR, contractor)\n   [23][19][37].\n3. **Write the scorecard** (mission, 3-8 outcomes, competencies) and the 90-day roadmap; convert the\n   scorecard into a structured interview kit with rubrics and one work sample [17][18][16].\n4. **Source.** Ask every strong contact for their three most talented people; cold outreach on LinkedIn/\n   GitHub; post to startup boards; pay referral bonuses; publish a one-page \"how we work\" doc as the\n   employer brand [18][23][27].\n5. **Select.** Screening call (30 min) → \"Who\" chronological interview → focused interviews (4 total,\n   independent scores) → work sample → decision meeting against the scorecard [17][16].\n6. **Reference.** 6-8 calls minimum, half backchannel; ask the comparison, 1-100, criticism and \"why would\n   they leave in three months\" questions; kill the offer on a pattern, not one voice [22].\n7. **Offer.** Benchmark base (Carta/Pave), set equity from the level table, state strike price, vesting,\n   cliff, exercise window and refresh policy in plain language; UK: grant under EMI where eligible [7][28][29].\n8. **Contract and comply.** Employee vs contractor test per country; EOR or local entity; PAYE/pension/\n   NSITF-type registrations; keep a written classification memo [32][33][34].\n9. **Onboard (day −7 to day 90).** Roadmap and buddy before day 1; laptop and access on day 1; manager\n   30/60/90 check-ins; explicit \"you are on track / not\" at day 90 [18][55].\n10. **Run performance.** Written quarterly check-ins, continuous feedback, comp review once a year with a\n    refresh matrix, PIP ladder with documentation, generous severance when it is not working [54][28][18][25].\n11. **Re-plan at every doubling** (10 → 20 → 50): write down mission, values and decision rights at 30-50;\n    add a recruiter and a people lead; give away your Legos [20]."},{"ref":"modern/S03-hiring-and-people.md","label":"Hiring and people (2026 layer)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Co-founder compatibility (answer separately, then compare)**\n- What does each of us own, and who decides when we disagree?\n- Hours per week for the next 24 months; other commitments; runway each can survive without salary?\n- What is each founder's number for \"we should stop\"? For \"we should sell\"?\n- What did each of us de-risk before today (code, customers, cash)? Does that justify an unequal split?\n- Vesting 4 years, 1-year cliff, acceleration on change of control — agreed for all founders? [11][12]\n\n**Scorecard template (one page)** — Mission (one sentence) · Outcomes (3-8, measurable, dated) ·\nCompetencies (5-8 behaviours, incl. culture) · 90-day roadmap · Who interviews for what [17][18].\n\n**Interview-loop checklist** — 4 interviewers max, each owns distinct outcomes · fixed questions and a\n1-4 rubric written before the loop · scores submitted before the debrief · one work sample, paid ·\ndecision meeting ends in yes/no, not \"maybe\" [16][17].\n\n**Reference-call script (eight questions)** — best you've seen in this role? · 1-100 and why not 100? ·\na time you gave critical feedback? · how do they handle disagreement? · will they thrive in *this* role?\n· what did you see them build? · fit with my management style? · if they left in 3 months, why? · who\nelse should I talk to? [22]\n\n**Offer checklist** — base vs benchmark · equity % and share count · strike price and 409A/valuation\nbasis · vesting, cliff, acceleration · post-termination exercise window · refresh policy · location tier\n· EMI/DPIIT status where relevant · start date and 90-day roadmap attached [7][28][29][59].\n\n**Contractor-or-employee test (any country)** — Do we control how/when/where? · Can they send a\nsubstitute? · Do they work mainly for us? · Is the work core to the business? · Do we supply the tools?\n· Is there ongoing mutual obligation? Three or more \"yes\" → treat as employee or use an EOR [32][33][34].\n\n**AI-in-hiring compliance checklist** — inventory every tool that ranks, filters or scores people ·\nis any output \"heavily influencing\" who advances? (then high-risk in the EU) · human with authority and\ntraining reviews every reject · candidates notified before use; NYC: opt-out notice + annual bias audit\npublished · logs kept ≥ 6 months · no emotion recognition · vendor's technical documentation on file\n[43][45][46][47].\n\n**First-90-days checklist** — roadmap shared pre-start · buddy assigned (15 min/day, two weeks) · week-1\ncustomer exposure · day-30 written check-in · day-60 first outcome shipped · day-90 explicit verdict [18]."},{"ref":"modern/S03-hiring-and-people.md","label":"Hiring and people (2026 layer)","heading":"Regional notes","text":"- **US:** at-will employment; options need a 409A valuation; ISOs vs NSOs; DOL six-factor economic-reality\n  test and California's ABC test with $25k-100k per-contractor fines for misclassification [33]; NYC LL144\n  bias audits for any automated screening tool used on NYC candidates [47]; US seed hires expect options\n  immediately (Index) [9]; salaries and equity benchmarks: Carta, Pave [4][28].\n- **UK:** EMI is the default early-stage scheme — £250k per employee, £6m company cap, ≤500 staff and\n  £120m gross assets from 6 Apr 2026, no income tax/NIC at exercise if strike ≥ market value, BADR CGT 18%\n  after a 24-month hold, ≥25 hrs/week or 75% working-time eligibility [29][30][31]. IR35: small companies\n  (turnover ≤ £15m, balance sheet ≤ £7.5m, ≤ 50 staff, from 6 Apr 2025) leave status to the contractor;\n  above that you issue a Status Determination Statement; penalties 30-100% of lost tax with 4/6/20-year\n  lookbacks [32]. Personio is the common UK/EU SME HRIS [39]. Check local law on the Employment Rights\n  Act 2025 changes (unverified here).\n- **EU:** high-risk AI obligations for recruitment, promotion, allocation and monitoring apply from 2 Aug\n  2026 (Digital Omnibus may defer to no later than Dec 2027/Aug 2028 — plan as if it holds) [42][43];\n  workplace emotion recognition banned and AI-literacy duty live since 2 Feb 2025 [45]; inform and consult\n  workers' representatives before deployment [42]; Commission draft guidelines (30 Jun 2026) put CV\n  screening, ranking and background checks in scope and employer-branding tools out [46]. European employees\n  own roughly half what US employees do (~10% vs ~20% late stage) and options skew to executives — a\n  recruiting lever if you break the pattern [7][8].\n- **Africa / Nigeria:** courts apply control, integration, economic-reality, mutuality and exclusivity\n  tests; misclassification exposes you to Labour Act entitlements, National Industrial Court claims,\n  permanent-establishment tax and Immigration Act sanctions [34]. Lagos back-end developers earn ₦1.2-2.5m\n  per month (~$800-1,667), CTOs ₦3-5m+; naira weakness pushes senior talent to dollar-paying remote roles,\n  tightening local supply [56][57]. EORs with Nigerian coverage: Deel, Remote, Multiplier, Bento Africa,\n  Native Teams [58]; Deel's top-funded startups already source from Kenya and Nigeria for AI-training roles\n  [35]. Pay in USD/stablecoin is a retention lever across volatile currencies (84.6% of Argentine Deel\n  workers chose USD) [35]. Check local law: PAYE, pension (PenCom) and NSITF registrations (unverified here).\n- **India:** ESOP pools 10-15% early, 4-year/1-year cliff; DPIIT-recognised startups defer perquisite tax\n  to sale/exit; SEBI (Jun 2025) lets founders keep ESOPs granted ≥1 year before the DRHP through IPO\n  [59][60]. Carta's APAC/ME data: pools 7% pre-seed → 11% seed [6].\n- **Southeast Asia / Middle East:** smaller pools than the US (11-13% at later stages vs 18%) and more\n  conservative hiring in 2025 [6]; expect cash-heavier offers and EOR-first hiring across jurisdictions.\n- **LatAm:** cross-border contractor hiring is common; currency risk drives USD/stablecoin pay preferences\n  (Deel) [35]; classification rules vary by country — use an EOR or local counsel."},{"ref":"modern/S03-hiring-and-people.md","label":"Hiring and people (2026 layer)","heading":"AI-era notes","text":"- **What is real:** AI now drafts scorecards, job posts and interview kits, transcribes and summarises\n  interviews, schedules, and writes first-draft reviews — 48% of HR teams use it to write performance reviews\n  and 45% to summarise feedback [54]; 93% of recruiters plan to increase AI use in 2026 [51]. For a founder\n  this collapses the admin cost of *structured* hiring, which was the main reason startups skipped it.\n- **What the evidence does not support:** claims that AI screening removes bias. Only 26% of candidates trust\n  AI to evaluate them fairly [49]; 66% of Americans say they would not apply where AI helps decide (Pew, via\n  [51]); 21% of AI-using US companies let AI reject at every stage with no human review [52] — exactly the\n  practice regulators target. No fetched source shows AI screening out-predicts a structured interview.\n- **The application flood:** ~40% of candidates use AI to write applications [48], 76.9% of recruiters report\n  encountering AI-generated applications (Jan 2026, up from 53% in H1 2024) [52], 6% admit interview fraud and\n  Gartner forecasts 1 in 4 profiles fake by 2028 [48]. Counter-moves: work samples, live problem-solving, in-\n  person or camera-on final rounds (62% of candidates prefer employers who interview in person) [48], identity\n  checks for remote hires, and a small paid trial before the offer.\n- **Law:** EU AI Act — recruitment, selection, promotion, allocation and monitoring AI is high-risk; deployer\n  duties are trained human oversight with override authority, worker/candidate notice, consultation, ≥6-month\n  logs, DPIA; fines up to €35m/7% (prohibited practices) and €15m/3% (high-risk breaches); applies to non-EU\n  companies recruiting EU candidates [42][43][44][45]. NYC LL144 — annual independent bias audit, published\n  summary, candidate notice with opt-out, up to $1,500 per violation per day; a Dec 2025 Comptroller audit found\n  enforcement \"ineffective\" (75% of 311 complaints misrouted; 17 potential non-compliance issues in 32 audits\n  the regulator had cleared) — expect tightening, not relief [47].\n- **New roles:** \"AI trainer\" was the fastest-growing cross-border role on Deel in 2025 (+283%, 70,000+\n  workers, $15-75/hour) [35][36]; Carta reports AI roles among the fastest-rising startup salaries [4].\n- **Agentic recruiting** (autonomous sourcing/outreach/screening agents) is the 2026 vendor pitch; treat any\n  agent that ranks or rejects as a high-risk system under the EU test (\"output heavily influences who\n  advances\") [46], and keep the human decision meeting."},{"ref":"modern/S03-hiring-and-people.md","label":"Hiring and people (2026 layer)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- Splitting founder equity by \"who had the idea\" or on performance metrics; skipping founder vesting [11][12].\n- Hiring a salesperson before founder-led sales works, or an executive sized for five years out [23][19].\n- Unstructured \"culture fit\" chats with six interviewers — lower validity, higher cost [13][16].\n- Treating references as a formality or calling only the candidate's list [22][18].\n- Reserving a 20% pool at seed and letting it dilute the founders while unused (Index) [8].\n- Silent geo discounts on equity that candidates discover later (Pave: 29-36%) [28].\n- Contractors who are employees in substance — back tax, penalties and, in Nigeria, immigration exposure\n  [32][33][34].\n- RTO mandates without a reason or enforcement: required days up, attendance flat, trust down [40].\n- Letting an AI tool reject candidates unreviewed; deploying screening AI on EU or NYC candidates without\n  notice, audit and oversight [43][47][52].\n- No 90-day roadmap: 22% of hires leave within 90 days and a failed hire costs $25-50k [55].\n- Importing the keeper test into a 15-person team as a policy rather than a leadership question [26]."},{"ref":"modern/S03-hiring-and-people.md","label":"Hiring and people (2026 layer)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`scorecard` — one-page definition of a role's mission, measurable outcomes and competencies (Smart & Street).\n`A player` — candidate with a ≥90% chance of achieving outcomes only the top 10% could (Smart & Street).\n`structured interview` — same questions, same rubric, independent scoring; validity r = .42 (Sackett 2022).\n`work sample` — a paid, realistic task used as a predictor (r = .33).\n`backchannel reference` — a reference the hiring team finds, not one the candidate supplies.\n`option pool / ESOP` — shares reserved for employees, sized as % of fully diluted equity by stage.\n`cliff` — the period (usually 12 months) before any equity vests.\n`refresh grant` — additional equity to an existing employee, now typically rating-gated.\n`EMI` — UK Enterprise Management Incentives, tax-advantaged options for companies ≤500 staff (from Apr 2026).\n`BADR` — UK Business Asset Disposal Relief; 18% CGT on qualifying EMI gains from 6 Apr 2026.\n`IR35 / SDS` — UK off-payroll rules; a Status Determination Statement records the client's decision.\n`EOR` — employer of record: a third party legally employs your worker abroad for a monthly fee.\n`AEDT` — automated employment decision tool (NYC Local Law 144 term).\n`high-risk AI system` — EU AI Act class covering recruitment, selection, promotion, allocation, monitoring.\n`keeper test` — Netflix question: would I fight to keep this person? If not, exit them generously.\n`tour of duty` — a defined 2-4 year mission with mutual promises (Hoffman, Casnocha, Yeh).\n`structured hybrid` — fixed in-office days per week set by the employer (Flex Index term)."},{"ref":"modern/S04-operations.md","label":"Operations (2026 layer)","heading":"What changed since 2018","text":"- **Operating systems went mainstream and productised.** EOS/Traction is now claimed by 310,000+\n  companies with 800+ certified implementers in 37 countries [2][1]; Scaling Up got a 22nd\n  anniversary revision in 2024 [4]; Mochary's method is open-sourced on the web and GitHub [8][9];\n  Claire Hughes Johnson turned Stripe's internal cadence into a 2023 book with 100+ pages of\n  templates [10]. A founder no longer invents the rhythm — they pick one.\n- **Cash forecasting moved from monthly to weekly.** The rolling 13-week forecast, once a\n  restructuring tool, is now standard for healthy small companies [19][20]; the median small\n  business holds 27 days of cash buffer [16].\n- **Tax and reporting went digital and continuous.** UK MTD for Income Tax started 6 April 2026 for\n  ~864,000 sole traders and landlords over £50,000 — quarterly digital updates, not one annual\n  return [49][50]; Nigeria's NTA 2025 took effect 1 January 2026 [47][48].\n- **Privacy compliance became a filing, not a policy page.** Nigeria's NDPA + GAID 2025 require an\n  annual audit return, with notices to 1,368 organisations and fines to ₦10m or 2% of gross revenue\n  [44][45][46]; India's DPDP Rules set a May 2027 deadline [51][52]; the EU moved the other way,\n  proposing to lift the GDPR record-keeping exemption from 250 to 750 employees [42][43].\n- **SOC 2 became a sales gate for tiny companies** — $25-50K all-in in year one for a sub-50-person\n  startup, 8-12 months to a Type II report [40][41] — and **cross-border physical operations got\n  taxed**: the US $800 de minimis exemption ended 29 August 2025 for all countries (China/HK\n  2 May 2025), permanent from July 2027, so small parcels now clear at origin tariff rates [37][38].\n- **AI arrived in the back office — unevenly.** 88% of organisations use AI in at least one\n  function but only 39% report any enterprise-level EBIT impact, and for most that impact is under\n  5% of EBIT (McKinsey, 1,993 respondents, 105 nations, Jun-Jul 2025) [22]. Ramp's card data shows\n  paid business AI adoption crossing 50% for the first time in March 2026 [23][24], while the US\n  Census BTOS — which asks *all* firms, not just card-carrying ones — puts actual AI use at\n  17-20% of businesses, and under 20% for firms with four or fewer employees [25].\n- **Services got less billable, not more** — SPI Research puts billable utilisation at 66.4% in\n  2025, the lowest on record against a 75% target [33] — while **support shifted to messaging with\n  an AI first line**: ~45% of interactions are chat and AI deflects a median ~22% of tickets\n  [30][32], well below the 67-76% vendors advertise [31]."},{"ref":"modern/S04-operations.md","label":"Operations (2026 layer)","heading":"Core ideas (current consensus)","text":"- **Pick one operating system and run it badly rather than three well.** Wickman's EOS (*Traction*,\n  2007; still the SMB default), Harnish's Scaling Up / Rockefeller Habits (22nd ed. 2024) and\n  Mochary's method all converge on the same three parts: a short list of priorities, a small set of\n  numbers, and a fixed meeting rhythm [1][3][4][8].\n- **Rhythm beats planning.** Harnish's three habits are Priorities, Data, Rhythm — the rhythm\n  (daily huddle → weekly → monthly → quarterly → annual) keeps the plan alive [3].\n- **Separate the metrics meeting from the strategy meeting.** Amazon's WBR reviews 400-500 metrics\n  in 60 minutes with two-second hand-offs; strategy is banned and happens elsewhere (Bryar & Carr,\n  *Working Backwards*, 2021; mechanics documented by Cedric Chin, 2024/2026) [5][6].\n- **Manage controllable input metrics; report output metrics.** Amazon orders the deck so inputs\n  precede the outputs they drive, ending with financials — you cannot act on revenue, you can act\n  on selection, price and in-stock rate. **Only exceptions get discussed** — routine variation gets\n  \"nothing to see here\", exceptional variation gets an owner explaining or saying \"we don't know and\n  are investigating\" — but no metric may be skipped [5].\n- **Write it down; documents beat decks.** Amazon replaced slides with six-page narratives and\n  starts products from a press release + FAQ written before any code [5][7].\n- **Policy without mechanism is a wish.** Will Larson (*Crafting Engineering Strategy*, 2025): two\n  thirds of operating a policy is avoiding what doesn't work — announcements, one-off training,\n  hoping culture changes; the rest is approval forums, inspection, automation and nudges [11].\n- **Founders should protect a Top Goal block daily** — Mochary: two hours every morning on the\n  single most important thing, and run every level on ACT (Accountability, Coaching, Transparency)\n  [8][9].\n- **Operating cadence is a management artefact, not admin.** Hughes Johnson (Stripe COO 2014-21,\n  ~200 → 7,000 people): foundational documents plus a week/month/quarter cadence make management\n  scalable [10].\n- **Cash, not profit, is the operating constraint** — JPMorgan Chase Institute, 597,000 businesses: median 27 cash buffer days, 25% of firms at 13 or fewer [16]. Hence the 13-week forecast.\n- **OKRs are a communication device, not a measurement system.** Doerr popularised them\n  (*Measure What Matters*, 2018; MIT SMR interview) [12]; practitioners' standing criticism is that\n  his own examples confuse outputs with outcomes and the book is silent on what to do when goals\n  stall [13][14]. Academic work finds OKRs help strategy implementation mainly where leadership\n  commitment and culture already support them [15]. Do not run OKRs *and* EOS Rocks on a 10-person\n  team — both are quarterly priority lists, and two of them halve compliance with either [1][3]."},{"ref":"modern/S04-operations.md","label":"Operations (2026 layer)","heading":"Frameworks & playbooks","text":"| Framework | Author / year | What it says | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| EOS / Traction (Six Key Components; Level 10 Meeting; Scorecard; Rocks) | Gino Wickman, *Traction* (2007); EOS Worldwide | Weekly 90-minute L10 with a fixed 7-part agenda — segue, scorecard, rock review, headlines, to-dos, IDS (Identify-Discuss-Solve), conclude; Scorecard = 5-15 weekly numbers, each with an owner, marked on/off track; quarterly Rocks [1] | 5-250 person owner-led SMBs that want an off-the-shelf rhythm |\n| Scaling Up / Rockefeller Habits (Priorities, Data, Rhythm; One-Page Strategic Plan) | Verne Harnish, *Mastering the Rockefeller Habits* (2002; 22nd anniv. ed. 2024), *Scaling Up* (2014) | One page holds values, vision, annual goals and quarterly priorities; one quarterly theme; daily 10-15 min huddle (good news, numbers, priorities, stucks); weekly = problem-solving not status [3][4] | Companies scaling past ~25 people with multiple teams |\n| Amazon Weekly Business Review (WBR) | Colin Bryar & Bill Carr, *Working Backwards* (2021); mechanics documented by Cedric Chin (2024, upd. 2026) | Fixed weekly meeting on a metrics deck: controllable inputs before outputs, finance last; 6-week + trailing-12-month \"6-12\" charts with prior-year and target overlays; exceptions only; strategy banned; Sun data → Mon owner review → Tue team WBRs → Wed company WBR [5][6] | Any business with enough weekly volume for trends to mean something (e-commerce, marketplaces, support) |\n| Working Backwards / PR-FAQ + six-pager | Bryar & Carr (2021) | Write the launch press release and customer FAQ before building — if the release is boring, the product is; replace decks with a written narrative read silently at the top of the meeting. Metrics flagged as exceptions get the DMAIC loop [5][7] | Deciding what to build next; any decision meeting |\n| Mochary Method (Top Goal, ACT, Goals Review, open curriculum) | Matt Mochary, *The Great CEO Within* (2019) + open curriculum (current) | 2 hours/day on the Top Goal; ACT — Accountability, Coaching, Transparency — at every level; company-wide all-hands weekly-to-monthly sharing the leadership meeting output [8][9] | Founder-CEOs of venture-backed startups; personal operating system |\n| Operating cadence (week / month / quarter) + foundational documents | Claire Hughes Johnson, *Scaling People* (Stripe Press, 2023) | Write the founding docs (operating principles, charters), then design the cadence that reviews them; 100+ pages of templates [10] | 20-500 people; formalising what was tribal knowledge |\n| OKRs | Andy Grove (Intel, 1970s) → John Doerr, *Measure What Matters* (2018) [12] | Objective (qualitative, ambitious) + 3-5 measurable Key Results; quarterly; transparent. Known failure mode: KRs written as outputs/tasks, not outcomes [13][14] | Cross-functional alignment at 30+ people, or where teams must coordinate on one outcome |\n| Mechanisms over intentions | Will Larson, *Crafting Engineering Strategy* (2025) | Explore → diagnose → refine → set policy → operate; operating = approval forums, inspection, automation, nudges — not announcements [11] | Making a new rule actually stick |\n| Rolling 13-week cash-flow forecast | Restructuring/CFO practice; Intuit (2025), FocusCFO, ICAEW guidance for scale-ups [19][20] | Week-by-week receipts and disbursements for 13 weeks; each week drop week 1, add a new week 13, replace forecast with actuals; weeks 1-4 should be 90%+ accurate [19] | Every business, always — mandatory below 3 months' runway |\n| Fire yourself, step by step | Rob Walling, *The SaaS Playbook* (2022); MicroConf hiring tactics [55][56] | Move from task-level to owner-level people; hire the roles that remove you from the critical path first (support, then ops, then marketing) | Bootstrapped founders at $10K-100K MRR |"},{"ref":"modern/S04-operations.md","label":"Operations (2026 layer)","heading":"Benchmarks & numbers (2024-2026)","text":"| Metric | Value | Segment / context | Source | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Median small-business cash buffer | 27 days (25% of firms ≤ 13 days; top quartile ≥ 62) | 597,000 US small businesses, 470M transactions | [16] | 2016 (still the standard cite) |\n| UK small firms hit by late payment | 70% in Q1; 32-day average delay; ~11% of invoices 30+ days overdue; £21.4K owed each | UK SMEs | [17][18] | 2025 |\n| Amazon WBR | 400-500 metrics in 60 min (90 in peak season) | Amazon company-level WBR | [5] | 2024/2026 |\n| Billable utilisation | 66.4% (lowest on record) vs 75% target | SPI Research professional-services benchmark | [33] | 2025 |\n| Utilisation, high performers vs rest | 75.0% vs 64.9% (top maturity 81.2%); targets: consulting 74-84%, creative agencies ~70% | same + secondary [34] | [33] | 2025-26 |\n| 3PL pick-and-pack | ~$2-3 per B2C order (survey avg $3.20; B2B ~$4.80), before shipping | US 3PL market | [35] | 2026 |\n| 3PL storage / receiving / setup | $18-25 per pallet/month; $5-15 per pallet receiving; $250-1,000 setup at ~half of providers | same | [35] | 2026 |\n| 3PL minimums / break-even | at 50 orders/month ~$191 of activity but ~$517 minimum billed; 3PL breaks even ~500-1,000 orders/month | same | [35] | 2026 |\n| Inventory turns by vertical | fashion 4-7x, beauty 4-9x, supplements 8-12x, F&B 12-15x, pet 8-10x, home 3-5x, electronics 4-6x, subscription 12-18x | e-commerce, secondary | [36] | 2026 |\n| Dual-source cost premium | ~5-12% over single source | manufacturing, secondary | [39] | 2026 |\n| SOC 2 first-year all-in / timeline | $25K-50K for a sub-50-person startup; 8-12 months to a Type II report; compliance platform ~$7.5-15K/yr on top | US SaaS startups | [40][41] | 2026 |\n| Accounting software | Xero $15/$42/$78 per month, unlimited users; QuickBooks Online $30/$60/$90/$200, per-seat above 5 | US list pricing, secondary | [21] | 2026 |\n| Organisations using AI in ≥1 function | 88% (from 78%) | 1,993 respondents, 105 nations, Jun-Jul 2025 | [22] | 2025 |\n| Organisations experimenting with AI agents | 62%; ~23% scaling agents in ≥1 function | same | [22] | 2025 |\n| Enterprise-level EBIT impact from AI | 39% report any; for most, <5% of EBIT; ~6% are \"high performers\" | same | [22] | 2025 |\n| GenAI pilots with no measurable P&L impact | 95% | MIT Project NANDA: 52 interviews, 153 leaders, 300 deployments | [27][28] | 2025 |\n| AI pilot success by build model | 67% (internal + external partner) vs 22% (IT-only build) | same | [27] | 2025 |\n| Businesses paying for AI tools | crossed 50% in March 2026 (from ~35% a year earlier) | Ramp card + bill-pay data, 70,000+ firms | [23][24] | 2026 |\n| AI spend per employee | median firm $11.95; top 10% $650; top 1% $7,400 | same | [23] | 2026 |\n| Actual AI *use* across all US firms | 17-20% (Dec 2025-May 2026); <20% for firms with ≤4 employees; 37% for 250+ employees | US Census BTOS | [25][26] | 2026 |\n| Agentic AI projects predicted cancelled | >40% by end-2027; only ~130 of thousands of \"agentic\" vendors judged real | Gartner | [29] | 2025 |\n| AI ticket deflection | median ~22% of support tickets; 80-90% cost reduction on eligible volume | secondary aggregator | [30] | 2026 |\n| Vendor-claimed vs observed AI resolution | 67-76% claimed; 45-53% in production, 38% in one 500-ticket SMB test | secondary | [31] | 2026 |\n| AI vs human CSAT | 4.10/5 vs 4.30/5; gap narrows to 0.05 with hybrid escalation | secondary aggregator | [30] | 2026 |\n| CSAT by intent | password reset 4.41, refund status 4.32; complaint handling 3.34, billing dispute 3.61 | same | [30] | 2026 |\n| SMB self-reported AI benefit | 77% use AI regularly; 41% report revenue increase (self-reported) | 34,000 small businesses, QuickBooks AI Impact Report, reported critically by Forbes | [57] | 2026 |\n| UK MTD for Income Tax | live 6 Apr 2026 at £50K qualifying income (~864,000 taxpayers); £30K in 2027, £20K in 2028; 12-month penalty-point grace | UK sole traders and landlords | [49][50] | 2026 |\n| Nigeria NDPA audit penalty | up to ₦10m or 2% of annual gross revenue, whichever is higher; 1,368 firms served notices | Nigeria DCPMIs | [44][45][46] | 2026 |\n| Privacy deadlines | EU RoPA exemption 250 → 750 employees (**proposal, not law**, 19 Nov 2025); India DPDP full compliance 13 May 2027 | EU / India | [42][43][51][52] | 2025-27 |\n| Nigeria small-company tax | 0% CIT/CGT/4% development levy below the turnover + ≤₦250m fixed-asset test; VAT registration threshold ₦25m; professional services excluded | NTA 2025, effective 1 Jan 2026 | [47][48] | 2026 |\n| First dedicated ops hire | commonly around 10-12 employees; COO much later | VC guidance | [53][54] | 2025-26 |"},{"ref":"modern/S04-operations.md","label":"Operations (2026 layer)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **If you have no weekly number that anyone owns, start there** — 5-15 numbers, one owner each,\n  marked on/off track, before any software purchase (Wickman/EOS) [1]. **If the weekly meeting runs\n  long, you are doing strategy in it** — split them (Amazon WBR discipline) [5][6].\n- **If a metric is an output you cannot act on, demote it** to the financial section and find its\n  controllable input (Bryar & Carr) [5]. **Under 15 people, run one quarterly priority list — not\n  OKRs *and* Rocks** [1][3].\n- **If runway is under 6 months, the 13-week cash forecast is the weekly meeting.** Update it every\n  week with actuals; weeks 1-4 should be 90%+ accurate or your AR/AP data is wrong [19][20].\n- **If 11% of your invoices are 30+ days late you are financing your customers** — the UK average;\n  chase automatically at day 1, 7 and 14, and take card or direct debit up front below a\n  threshold you set [17][18].\n- **If a task has been done the same way three times, write the SOP** — and put it where the work\n  happens, not in a wiki nobody opens. **If a new policy has no forum, no inspection and no\n  automation, assume it will not happen** (Larson) [11].\n- **If you ship fewer than ~500 orders a month, keep fulfilment in-house** — 3PL minimums triple\n  effective cost per order at 50 orders/month [35]. **If billable utilisation is below ~70% in a\n  service business, the problem is pipeline or scoping** — the 2025 average was 66.4% against a 75%\n  target [33][34].\n- **If a single supplier or country is more than ~50% of COGS, price the dual-source premium\n  (~5-12%) against a stock-out** [39]; **if you import into the US, assume duty on every parcel** —\n  de minimis is gone, so re-price or hold US inventory [37][38].\n- **If a prospect asks for SOC 2 before signing, scope it as a $25-50K, 8-12 month project** and\n  decide whether the deal pays for it [40][41]. **If you process personal data in Nigeria, file the\n  annual audit return** via a licensed DPCO — penalty up to ₦10m or 2% of gross revenue [44][45].\n- **If a support intent is structured, automate it; if it is sentiment-heavy (complaints, billing\n  disputes), route it to a human** — CSAT splits 4.3-4.4 vs 3.3-3.6 along that line [30].\n- **If an AI deployment has no measured baseline, it is not a deployment** — 95% of GenAI pilots\n  showed no P&L impact, and those that worked paired internal owners with outside help [27][28].\n- **If the founder is the bottleneck on execution rather than direction, hire an ops lead or chief\n  of staff before a COO** — prove the function at lower cost [53][54]."},{"ref":"modern/S04-operations.md","label":"Operations (2026 layer)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. **Choose one operating system** (EOS if owner-led SMB; Scaling Up if multi-team; Amazon WBR if\n   metrics-rich; Mochary if solo founder-CEO). Write down which one, and stop shopping [1][3][5][8].\n2. **Define the scorecard.** 5-15 weekly numbers. For each: owner, definition, source of truth,\n   target, whether it is a controllable input or an output. Order inputs before outputs [1][5].\n3. **Set the calendar.** Daily huddle 10-15 min (optional under 10 people); weekly 60-90 min\n   metrics + issues; monthly close review; quarterly planning day; annual two days [1][3].\n4. **Run the weekly properly.** Numbers top to bottom, exceptions only, issues captured to a list,\n   IDS the top three, close with owned and dated to-dos [1].\n5. **Close the books monthly on a fixed date.** Bank feeds reconciled, revenue recognised, payroll\n   and taxes accrued, three statements out by working day 10 (`A-financial-statements.md`).\n6. **Build the rolling 13-week cash forecast** — opening cash, receipts by customer, disbursements\n   by category, closing cash; roll it weekly and log each forecast-vs-actual variance [19][20].\n7. **Instrument collections.** Invoice on delivery, terms in writing, automated reminders at\n   day -3/+1/+7/+14, escalation script at +30 [17].\n8. **Document the top 7 processes** — client onboarding, order/fulfilment, complaint handling,\n   invoice approval, new-starter onboarding, weekly reporting, incident response — one page each.\n9. **Automate one process per month**, in this order: data entry → routing/notification →\n   drafting → decision support. Measure hours saved and error rate before and after [27][29].\n10. **Set the security/compliance floor** now: password manager or SSO, 2FA everywhere, least\n    privilege, an offsite backup you have restored from once, a vendor list, a privacy notice, a\n    data-processing record, a named incident contact [40][42][44].\n11. **Review the tool stack and the founder's role quarterly** — per tool: cost, owner, which\n    number it moves, what breaks if cancelled [23]; per founder: what only you can do, delegate or\n    automate the rest, and when the residue is still operations, hire [53][55]."},{"ref":"modern/S04-operations.md","label":"Operations (2026 layer)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Scorecard design (per metric)**\n- What is the number exactly, who owns it, and where does it come from (is that source automated)?\n- Is it an input I can act on this week, or an output I can only report?\n- What is the target, and what counts as an exception worth discussing?\n\n**Weekly review agenda (90 minutes)** — 5 min good news · 10 min scorecard, exceptions only ·\n10 min quarterly priorities on/off track · 10 min headlines · 5 min last week's to-dos (target 90%\ndone) · 45 min identify-discuss-solve the top three issues · 5 min conclude with decisions, the\ncascading message and a rating.\n\n**Cash-health check (monthly)**\n- Days of cash buffer (median small business: 27 [16]); the 13-week low point and which week it is\n- AR over 30 days as % of revenue; largest overdue customer; biggest lumpy payment in the 13 weeks\n- If our top customer paid 30 days late, would we still make payroll?\n\n**Process documentation checklist** — trigger · steps with screenshots · owner · escalation path ·\nSLA · last reviewed · where it lives. Test: could a new hire do it from this page alone?\n\n**Automation candidate scorecard** — frequency × time per run = hours/month · error cost if wrong ·\nis the input structured? · is there a baseline measurement? · who owns the output when AI is wrong?\n\n**Compliance minimum viable checklist**\n- Entity and filings current (`04-legal-structure.md`); tax calendar with an owner (VAT/GST,\n  payroll, corporation, quarterly digital filings [49])\n- Privacy notice, lawful basis, record of processing, DSAR route, breach contact [42][44][51]\n- Quarterly access review; offboarding checklist; annual restore-from-backup test; vendor register\n\n**Ops-lead readiness** — which recurring decisions land on the founder that someone else could\nown? Is the rhythm already repeating, so there is something to hand over [54]? Would a chief of\nstaff or head of ops at half the cost prove the need first [53]?"},{"ref":"modern/S04-operations.md","label":"Operations (2026 layer)","heading":"Regional notes","text":"- **US** — de minimis is gone, so landed cost must be priced per parcel [37][38]; sales-tax\n  economic nexus is per-state, so check every state you ship to; SOC 2 is the de facto B2B security\n  gate at a real $25-50K year-one cost [40][41].\n- **UK** — MTD for Income Tax live from 6 April 2026 at £50,000 qualifying income (quarterly\n  digital updates; threshold falls to £30,000 in 2027 and £20,000 in 2028; no penalty points for\n  12 months) [49][50]. Late payment is the dominant operational tax: 70% affected, 32-day average\n  delay, £21.4K owed [17][18]. UK GDPR is unchanged by the EU's Digital Omnibus [42][43].\n- **EU** — The Digital Omnibus (19 Nov 2025) proposes raising the Article 30(5) record-keeping\n  exemption to under 750 employees with financial criteria and a high-risk carve-out; the EDPB/EDPS\n  welcomed it with caveats. **It is still a proposal — comply with the 250-employee rule** [42][43].\n- **Nigeria** — NTA 2025 in force 1 Jan 2026: a qualifying small company pays 0% CIT, CGT and the\n  4% development levy, subject to a turnover test plus fixed assets ≤ ₦250m; professional services\n  firms are excluded regardless of size; the VAT registration threshold stays at ₦25m [47][48].\n  The two reform statutes have been read as giving different turnover thresholds (₦50m vs ₦100m) —\n  take advice rather than assuming [47]. Separately, NDPA + GAID 2025 require controllers and\n  processors of major importance to file an annual compliance audit return via a licensed DPCO\n  (2026 deadline extended to 30 May); penalties reach ₦10m or 2% of gross revenue and the NDPC has\n  served 1,368 organisations [44][45][46] — a filing no US-shaped ops calendar contains.\n- **India** — DPDP Rules notified 13 Nov 2025; consent-manager provisions from 13 Nov 2026, full\n  substantive compliance from 13 May 2027, with the Data Protection Board already taking complaints\n  [51][52]. Build consent capture into product operations now, not in 2027.\n- **Southeast Asia / LatAm / Middle East** — cadence frameworks travel unchanged; payments and\n  fulfilment do not. Where cash on delivery and marketplace logistics dominate, add weekly \"COD\n  reconciliation\" and \"return rate\" lines to the scorecard and treat the 13-week forecast as\n  mandatory, because receipts are lumpier. **Everywhere**, regionalisation is the 2026 default:\n  dual sourcing, selective nearshoring and regional hubs, at a ~5-12% premium [39]."},{"ref":"modern/S04-operations.md","label":"Operations (2026 layer)","heading":"AI-era notes","text":"- **Evidence, honestly read.** Adoption is near-universal among larger firms and financial impact\n  is not: 88% use AI somewhere, 39% see any enterprise EBIT effect, mostly under 5% of EBIT [22].\n  MIT's NANDA study found 95% of GenAI pilots produced no measurable P&L impact [27][28]. Gartner\n  expects >40% of agentic AI projects to be cancelled by end-2027 and judges only ~130 of thousands\n  of \"agentic\" vendors to be genuinely agentic [29].\n- **The gap between spend and use.** Ramp's card data (50%+ of businesses paying for AI by\n  Mar 2026) measures *purchase*; Census BTOS (17-20%) measures *use* [23][24][25]. Both are true;\n  quote the right one. Micro-firms of ≤4 staff are barely moving [25].\n- **Where returns actually show up:** back-office automation, not sales/marketing pilots — over\n  half of 2025 AI budgets went to the high-visibility, low-ROI end [27]. In a small company that is\n  reconciliation, receipt capture, drafting, ticket triage and meeting follow-ups.\n- **Support is the clearest case, with a clear ceiling.** Median deflection ~22%, cost down 80-90%\n  on eligible volume, CSAT ~0.2 points below human and 0.05 with good escalation [30]; vendor\n  claims of 67-76% land at 45-53% in production [31]. Budget for the escalation path, not the bot.\n- **Build model matters more than model choice** — pilots pairing an internal owner with external\n  expertise succeeded 67% of the time vs 22% for IT-only builds [27] — and **cost discipline is now\n  a thing**: median AI spend is $11.95 per employee, the top 1% $7,400, with businesses visibly\n  refusing premium per-token pricing [23]. Cap and meter each workflow.\n- **Agents belong behind a policy, not in front of the customer's money.** Give an agent read\n  access broadly, write access narrowly, and put anything irreversible (payments, refunds, sending\n  to a customer, deleting data) behind approval with an audit log — Larson's \"approval forums,\n  inspection, automation, nudges\" applied to non-humans [11].\n- **Where hype outruns evidence:** \"autonomous back office\", \"AI CFO\", agent swarms replacing\n  headcount at 10 people. Measured effects are hours saved on structured tasks, not organisations\n  run by agents; self-reported SMB surveys (77% using AI, 41% claiming revenue gains across 34,000\n  businesses) are vendor-collected and were challenged in 2026 — sentiment, not measurement [57]."},{"ref":"modern/S04-operations.md","label":"Operations (2026 layer)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- **Buying the software before defining the numbers.** A scorecard tool with no owned metrics is a\n  subscription, not a system [1].\n- **Turning the weekly metrics meeting into strategy** — the most expensive hour in the company\n  spent on opinion (Amazon forbids it) [5]. **Reporting only outputs**: revenue and churn cannot be\n  acted on this week, the inputs behind them can. **Skipping metrics that look boring** is how\n  drifts go unnoticed until they are quarters old [5].\n- **Running OKRs, Rocks and a personal Top Goal at once** in a 12-person company; and **writing key\n  results as tasks** — the standing critique of *Measure What Matters* [13][14].\n- **Confusing profit with cash.** 27 days of median buffer means a profitable month on 60-day terms\n  can still miss payroll [16]. Related: **not chasing invoices because it feels rude** — 70% of UK\n  small firms were paid late in Q1 2025 [17][18].\n- **Signing a 3PL below break-even volume** and paying minimums that triple effective cost [35];\n  **pricing cross-border product as if de minimis still exists** [37][38]; **single-sourcing a\n  critical input to save 5-12%** [39].\n- **Treating SOC 2 as a checkbox rather than an 8-12 month, $25-50K programme** [40][41].\n- **Assuming EU/UK privacy rules are converging** (the Digital Omnibus relief is an EU *proposal*)\n  and **missing a national filing** — Nigeria's audit return, UK MTD quarters — because the ops\n  calendar was copied from a US template [42][44][49].\n- **Deploying an AI agent with no baseline, no escalation path and no cap on spend** [27][29].\n- **Hiring a COO to fix a problem the founder has not diagnosed.** Prove the function with an ops\n  lead or chief of staff first [53][54]."},{"ref":"modern/S04-operations.md","label":"Operations (2026 layer)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`operating cadence` — the fixed daily/weekly/monthly/quarterly meeting and review rhythm a company runs on.\n`Level 10 Meeting (L10)` — EOS's 90-minute weekly leadership meeting with a fixed seven-part agenda.\n`Scorecard` — 5-15 weekly numbers, each with an owner and a target, reviewed on/off track.\n`Rock` — an EOS quarterly priority with one owner. `IDS` — Identify, Discuss, Solve: its issue-processing method.\n`WBR` — Amazon's Weekly Business Review: a metrics deck read in a fixed order, exceptions only.\n`input metric` — a number you can directly act on this week; `output metric` — a result you can only report.\n`6-12 chart` — trailing six weeks plus trailing twelve months on one graph, with prior-year and target overlays.\n`PR/FAQ` — a press release plus FAQ written before a product is built; `six-pager` — the narrative that replaces a deck.\n`Top Goal` — Mochary's daily protected block for the most important thing; `ACT` — Accountability,\nCoaching, Transparency, what every recurring meeting should deliver.\n`13-week cash-flow forecast` — a rolling week-by-week receipts-and-disbursements forecast for a quarter;\n`cash buffer days` — days of outflows covered by cash on hand with zero inflows.\n`SOP` — a documented step-by-step procedure for a recurring task, with an owner and a review date.\n`mechanism` — Larson/Amazon: the forum, inspection, automation or nudge that makes a policy real.\n`utilisation rate` — billable hours ÷ available hours. `3PL` — outsourced storage, pick/pack and shipping.\n`de minimis` — the customs threshold below which imports entered duty-free; suspended in the US from 29 Aug 2025.\n`DCPMI` / `DPCO` — Nigeria: a controller/processor of major importance, and the licensed body that files its audit return.\n`RoPA` — GDPR record of processing activities. `MTD` — UK Making Tax Digital: digital records + quarterly updates.\n`deflection rate` — share of support contacts resolved without a human."},{"ref":"modern/S05-metrics-and-unit-economics.md","label":"Metrics and unit economics (2026 layer)","heading":"What changed since 2018","text":"- **Efficiency replaced growth as the first screen.** Benchmarkit's 2026 panel shows median Rule of\n  40 jumping 15% → 25% (largest one-year gain in five years) while median private B2B SaaS growth\n  fell to 22%, from 25% in 2024, across 1,000+ companies [15][19].\n- **The burn multiple became the default capital-efficiency metric.** Sacks published it in 2020\n  (net burn ÷ net new ARR) as \"a catch-all metric\"; VCs now quote its bands publicly [4][22].\n  **CAC payback stretched badly** out of the historic 12-14-month range — CRV tells Series A founders\n  ~20 months is typical [22]. Payback, not LTV:CAC, is what investors trust.\n- **Gross retention is eroding.** Median GRR 88% → 84%, top-quartile 95% → 91%; NRR now depends on\n  pricing model — usage-based 108% vs seat-based 98% [15].\n- **T2D3 stopped describing reality.** Agrawal's 2015 \"triple, triple, double, double, double\"\n  remains the aspiration [10]; Battery reframed it around the founder-to-CEO journey [11], SaaStr\n  argues top AI-era companies run \"T3D3\" [12], and Bessemer's Cloud 100 averages 7.5 years to $100M\n  ARR — 5.7 for AI companies [13].\n- **AI broke the 80% gross-margin assumption.** Inference is COGS, not R&D: AI-native margins cluster\n  at 50-60% vs SaaS 70-85%, and only 12% of companies target 80%+ [26][30][27].\n- **Pricing fragmented, so revenue metrics fragmented.** Hybrid pricing overtook everything (37%, up\n  from 25% in a year); seats fell to 18%, outcome-based reached 6%. \"ARR\" now needs a quality note —\n  recurring vs pilot vs services [30][22]. **Rounds also moved further apart**: median time between\n  rounds is near two years against a median seed runway of ~10.8 months [46][47].\n- **Marketplaces moved from GMV to retention and liquidity** — a16z calls GMV retention \"the metric\n  most ignore\" [35]; Tavel calls raw GMV vanity and scores \"happy GMV\" [37][38]. **Attribution\n  broke, so blended metrics won in DTC**; platform-ROAS-only brands over-spend on paid 20-40% [40][55]."},{"ref":"modern/S05-metrics-and-unit-economics.md","label":"Metrics and unit economics (2026 layer)","heading":"Core ideas (current consensus)","text":"- **Unit economics is breakeven at customer level.** Appendix B's contribution margin per unit becomes\n  margin per *customer* over a *lifetime*: does one customer repay the cost of getting them? [1][2]\n- **Payback beats ratios.** Skok pairs LTV:CAC > 3 with CAC recovery under 12 months, best-in-class\n  5-7 [1]. A ratio can be gamed by an optimistic lifetime; payback cannot. And **LTV is a tool, not a\n  strategy** — Gurley (2012, still the definitive critique): the variables are \"interdependent, not\n  independent\" and spending more raises CAC [3].\n- **Every channel decays** — Chen's Law of Shitty Clickthroughs, restated 2025 as \"every marketing\n  channel sucks right now\" [51][52]. Model CAC as rising, not flat. **The burn multiple is the\n  catch-all** — product, churn, pricing and hiring problems all end up as more burn per dollar of net\n  new ARR [4].\n- **Cohorts, not aggregates.** A flattening retention curve is the strongest evidence of PMF;\n  aggregate churn hides whether the product is improving [32][22]. And **read GRR before NRR** —\n  expansion masks an eroding base, exactly Benchmarkit's pattern [15][21].\n- **GMV is a marketplace vanity metric** — measure happy GMV, liquidity and cohort GMV retention\n  instead (Tavel; a16z) [37][38][35]. **Take rate is earned by work done** — Tidemark's layer cake [36].\n- **In DTC only contribution margin after marketing is honest** — the median compressed from ~35%\n  (2021) to ~22% (2025) [40]. **In AI the *direction* of gross margin matters more than the level**\n  (CRV, 2026): tripling revenue can triple compute spend [22][25].\n- **Rule of 40 is a scorecard, not a law** — popularised by Feld and Wilson in 2015 for companies at\n  scale (~$50M+ revenue) [5][6]; ICONIQ applies it from about $25M ARR [16]."},{"ref":"modern/S05-metrics-and-unit-economics.md","label":"Metrics and unit economics (2026 layer)","heading":"Frameworks & playbooks","text":"### A. Acquisition, payback and lifetime value\n\n| Metric | Formula | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| CAC (blended) | Total S&M spend in period ÷ new customers acquired in period | Skok [2]; include salaries, commissions, tools, agency fees. DTC form: total marketing spend ÷ all new customers [40] |\n| CAC (paid) / fully-loaded CAC | Paid acquisition spend ÷ new customers from paid; fully-loaded = (S&M + onboarding/implementation cost) ÷ new customers | Paid CAC is the number that scales [40]; use fully-loaded where onboarding is heavy (services, enterprise) |\n| CAC ratio | S&M spend ÷ net new ARR, same period | \"$ of S&M per $1 of new ARR\"; blended median $1.30 [15] |\n| ARPA / ARPU | Recurring revenue in period ÷ active accounts (or users) | Pick account or user and stay consistent |\n| Customer lifetime (months) | 1 ÷ monthly customer churn rate | 3%/mo churn → 33 months [2] |\n| LTV (simple / gross-margin) | ARPA ÷ monthly churn rate; gross-margin version = (ARPA × gross margin %) ÷ monthly churn rate | **Use the gross-margin version** — the simple one overstates value [2] |\n| LTV (with expansion) | Discounted sum over t of (a + m·t)·(1−c)^t, where a = initial monthly ARPA × GM%, m = monthly ARPA growth per account, c = monthly churn | Skok's \"complex version\"; assumes roughly fixed ARPA growth [2] |\n| LTV:CAC | LTV ÷ CAC | > 3 healthy; best SaaS 7-8x [1]. Meaningless pre-PMF [3] |\n| CAC payback (months) | CAC ÷ (ARPA × gross margin %) | Skok's \"months to recover CAC\" [2] |\n| CAC payback (ARR form) | S&M spend in quarter ÷ (net new ARR × gross margin %) × 12 | The version Benchmarkit and ICONIQ report [15][16] |\n| MER / break-even MER | Total revenue ÷ total marketing spend; break-even MER = 1 ÷ contribution margin % | MER is blended ROAS, nothing double-counted; 30% CM → 3.3x, 40% CM → 2.5x [43] |\n\n### B. Retention, churn and cohorts\n\n| Metric | Formula | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Logo (customer) churn / retention | Customers lost in period ÷ customers at start; retention = 1 − churn | \"World-class\" annual logo retention > 90% (`S01`) [2] |\n| Gross revenue retention (GRR) | (Starting ARR − churned ARR − contraction ARR) ÷ starting ARR | Caps at 100%; the honest base [15][21] |\n| Net revenue retention (NRR/NDR) | (Starting ARR − churn − contraction + expansion) ÷ starting ARR | ICONIQ: 1 + (expansion ARR − gross churn ARR) ÷ average(BOP, EOP ARR) [16] |\n| Net MRR churn | (Churned MRR − expansion MRR) ÷ starting MRR | Negative = expansion exceeds losses [2] |\n| SaaS quick ratio | (New MRR + expansion MRR) ÷ (churned MRR + contraction MRR) | Mamoon Hamid, Social Capital; > 4 was his investable bar [8][9] |\n| Cohort retention (period n) | Cohort members active in period n ÷ cohort size at period 0 | Revenue version: cohort revenue in n ÷ cohort revenue in 0 (can exceed 100%). PMF = the curve flattens [32][22] |\n| GMV retention (marketplace) | Retained cohort GMV ÷ initial cohort GMV × 100 | a16z; supply side typically higher than demand [35] |\n| Repeat purchase rate (DTC) | Customers with ≥ 2 orders ÷ total customers in period | 10-55% by category [41] |\n| 60-day repeat rate (DTC) | Cohort customers placing a 2nd order within 60 days ÷ cohort size | Fastest leading indicator of DTC LTV [40] |\n\n### C. Growth, efficiency, burn and runway\n\n| Metric | Formula | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| ARR | MRR × 12, contracted recurring revenue only | Exclude one-off services and unconverted pilots [22] |\n| Net new ARR | New + expansion − churned − contraction ARR | Numerator of most efficiency metrics |\n| YoY ARR growth | (EOP ARR − prior-year EOP ARR) ÷ prior-year EOP ARR | ICONIQ Enterprise Five #1 [16] |\n| Gross burn / net burn | Total cash operating outflows per month; net burn = cash out − cash in | Net burn is the one runway uses [4] |\n| Runway (months) | Cash on hand ÷ 3-month average monthly net burn | [46] |\n| Default alive / dead | Does projected revenue at current growth cover costs before cash runs out? | Paul Graham's test (2015 classic) |\n| Burn multiple | Net burn ÷ net new ARR, same period | David Sacks, 2020 [4] |\n| Bessemer efficiency score | Net new ARR ÷ net burn | Inverse of burn multiple; < 0.5x good, 0.5-1.5x better, 1.5x+ best [14] |\n| Magic number | (Current-quarter revenue − prior-quarter revenue) × 4 ÷ prior-quarter S&M spend | Scale Venture Partners; < 0.75 inefficient, 0.75-1.0 moderate, > 1.0 efficient [7] |\n| Net magic number | Current-quarter net new ARR ÷ prior-quarter S&M opex | Enterprise Five #4; top quartile > 1.0x. Gross variant uses gross new ARR [16][7] |\n| Rule of 40 | Revenue growth % + profitability margin % ≥ 40 | Margin = FCF (ICONIQ), EBITDA or operating — always state which [16][5][6] |\n| ARR per FTE | EOP ARR ÷ EOP full-time employees | Enterprise Five #5; Benchmarkit median $175K [16][15] |\n| Contribution margin (unit) | Price − variable cost per unit | `B-breakeven.md` definition, applied per customer |\n| Gross margin | (Revenue − COGS) ÷ revenue | Software COGS = hosting, support, third-party APIs, **inference** [27] |\n\n### D. Business-model-specific metrics\n\n| Model | Metric | Formula | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Marketplace | GMV | Total sales value transacted in period | Size, not value; vanity alone [34][37] |\n| Marketplace | Take rate | Net revenue ÷ GMV | Rises with work performed (layer cake) [36] |\n| Marketplace | Liquidity (demand) | Transactions ÷ intent sessions | aka fill rate / search-to-fill [33][39] |\n| Marketplace | Liquidity (supply) | Listings transacted ÷ total active listings | aka utilisation rate; pair with median time to match [39][34] |\n| Marketplace | Concentration | % of GMV from top X buyers or sellers | Fragmented is healthier [34] |\n| DTC | AOV / UPT | Revenue ÷ orders; units ÷ orders | AOV ~$35-$200+; UPT 1.5-2.3 [42][40] |\n| DTC | CM1 / CM2 / CM3 | Revenue − COGS; CM1 − fulfilment, shipping, payment fees, returns; CM2 − marketing | CM3 is the fully-loaded unit margin and the real number [40] |\n[section truncated]"},{"ref":"modern/S05-metrics-and-unit-economics.md","label":"Metrics and unit economics (2026 layer)","heading":"Benchmarks & numbers (2024-2026)","text":"| Metric | Value | Segment / context | Source | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Median growth rate | 22% (from 25% in 2024); bootstrapped 20% vs equity-backed 25%; 7.3% flat or negative | 1,000+ private B2B SaaS | [19] | 2026 |\n| Rule of 40 / CAC ratio / gross margin | median Rule of 40 25% (from 15%), largest 1-year gain in 5 years; $1.30 of S&M per $1 of new ARR; 80% median software GM, stable 4 years | private B2B SaaS, CY22-CY25 | [15] | 2026 |\n| GRR / NRR | GRR 88% → 84% median (top quartile 95% → 91%); NRR usage-based 108% vs seat-based 98% | same | [15] | 2026 |\n| Expansion share / ARR per employee / R&D | expansion 40% of net new ARR (44% in low-growth cohort); ARR/FTE $175K median (+17% YoY, 3.2x P75:P25 spread); R&D 27% of revenue (−8 pts), top quartile 22% | same | [15] | 2026 |\n| Median opex split | S&M 23% (15 sales + 8 marketing), R&D 22%, G&A 15%; at $3-5M ARR S&M 20%, R&D 24%, G&A 15% | 1,000+ private B2B SaaS, Mar 2026 | [20] | 2026 |\n| NRR, larger private / public | 109% at $100M+ ARR private; ~109% public, trending down | ICONIQ Analytics + public data | [18] | 2026 |\n| Burn multiple bands / CAC payback | < 1.0x amazing, 1.0-1.5 great, 1.5-2.0 good, 2.0-3.0 suspect, > 3.0 bad (~3x seed, ~2x Series A); CAC payback ~20 months typical at Series A vs historic 12-14 | Sacks; CRV expects < 2.0x at A | [4][22] | 2020/2026 |\n| Series A ARR bar | $2.5M median (2025), competitive $2-5M+; AI-native ~$3.5M with 120%+ NRR and GM > 60% | CRV; Value Add VC | [22][23][24] | 2026 |\n| Cloud 100: time to $100M / growth / multiple | 7.5 years average (5.7 for AI); 75% average YoY growth (from 55% in 2023); 20x ARR average, AI 24x vs non-AI 19x, public cloud ~8x | Bessemer Cloud 100 (100 private cos) | [13] | 2025 |\n| AI-native gross margin / inference | 50-60% vs SaaS 70-85%; trajectory 45% (2025) → ~53% (2026) → ~59% (2027) projected; inference ~23% of revenue at scaling stage | a16z framing; AI COGS analysis | [26][27] | 2026 |\n| AI gross-margin *target* / pricing mix | median target 50%, only 12% target 80%+; hybrid 37%, flat-fee 28%, seat 18%, usage 11%, outcome 6% | 230 software companies, Apr-May 2026 | [30] | 2026 |\n| Time to $1M / $5M ARR | AI ~11.5 months to $1M; 24 months to $5M vs SaaS 37 | Stripe, top 100 AI cos | [29] | 2026 |\n| Median runway / burn / round gap | seed ~10.8 mo, A 14.6, B 19.4; burn ~$87K/mo seed, ~$380K/mo A; ~696 days between rounds | secondary summary of Carta data | [46][47] | 2025-26 |\n| Monthly churn, GOOD / GREAT | B2C SaaS 3-5% / < 2%; B2B SMB-MM 2.5-5% / < 1.5%; enterprise 1-2% / < 0.5% | 13,000 anonymised SaaS cos (ProfitWell) | [31] | 2022 |\n| Marketplace GMV retention | demand m1 66%, m3 57%, m6 50%, m12 30%; supply m1-m3 80-95%, m12 45-50% (best-in-class 100%+) | a16z marketplace dataset | [35] | 2022 |\n| Marketplace fill rate / take rate | fill < 5% (broad search) to > 80% (bottom-funnel); take 10-30% for match + transaction services, < 1% document/payment processing, ~20bps card networks | Lenny's panel; Tidemark | [33][36] | 2022/— |\n| DTC contribution margin / MER | median CM 35% (2021) → 22% (2025); break-even MER = 1 ÷ CM% (30% CM → 3.3x, 40% → 2.5x), brands target 3-5x | DTC benchmark aggregation; DTC finance practice | [40][43] | 2026 |\n| DTC repeat rate / AOV / returns | repeat 25-30% average (consumables 30-45%, beauty 25-40%, apparel 20-32%, home/durables < 18%), 60-day repeat strong 20-35%; AOV ~$35 (food) to $200+ (furniture); returns apparel 20-35%, consumables 2-6% | same | [41][40][42] | 2026 |\n| Professional services | utilisation 66.4% (lowest in SPI history, target > 70%); project margin 37.7% (target > 35%); EBITDA 9.9% vs 13.8% 5-yr avg; revenue/consultant ~$210K | SPI, 509 orgs, $63B PS revenue | [44][45] | 2026 |\n| African startup capital lost / round size | > $500M across 30+ startups (early 2024 - mid 2026); average round Nigeria ~$1.6M vs Kenya ~$6.9M | Launch Base Africa; ecosystem comparison | [48][50] | 2026 |"},{"ref":"modern/S05-metrics-and-unit-economics.md","label":"Metrics and unit economics (2026 layer)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **If CAC payback exceeds 12 months, fix pricing or CAC before adding S&M spend** (Skok; best\n  recover in 5-7) [1]. The ~20-month Series A tolerance is a market fact, not a target [22].\n- **If LTV:CAC < 3, do not scale acquisition**; above 5-6 with payback under 6 months you are probably\n  under-investing in growth [1]. **Never quote the ratio without gross margin and the churn window** —\n  an LTV assuming a 5-year life for a 2-year-old company is fiction [3][2].\n- **If burn multiple > 3, treat it as a company-wide alarm**, not a finance issue [4]; under 1.5 with\n  growing net new ARR, raising is optional. Series A expects < 2.0x [4][22].\n- **If magic number < 0.75, stop hiring sellers**; 0.75-1.0 hold; above 1.0 fund more S&M [7].\n  **If runway is under 12 months you are already fundraising** — with ~24 months between rounds, plan\n  24 months of cash or a credible default-alive path [46][47].\n- **Apply Rule of 40 only from ~$25-50M ARR** — below that the sum is meaningless; use burn multiple\n  and CAC payback instead, and always state which margin you used [5][6][16].\n- **If NRR > 100% but GRR < 85%, you have a churn problem masked by upsell** [15][21] (see `S01`).\n  **If the retention curve has not flattened, you do not have PMF** — no CAC optimisation will save\n  you, and seed investors look at the curve before the revenue [22][32].\n- **For marketplaces, ignore GMV growth until liquidity improves** — rising GMV with falling fill rate\n  or supply utilisation means you are buying transactions [33][37][39].\n- **For DTC, manage CM3 by channel weekly**; if blended MER falls below 1 ÷ contribution margin, the\n  ad engine loses money whatever platform ROAS says [43][40]. **For services**, fix delivery before\n  selling more if utilisation < ~70% or project margin < ~35% [44].\n- **For AI products, inference above ~25% of revenue is a pricing problem, not a cost problem** —\n  reprice to usage or outcome, or route to cheaper models [27][30].\n- **Model CAC as rising 10-30% a year** beyond 12 months; a 30% CAC rise plus a 30% LTV fall can\n  double time-to-profitability [51]."},{"ref":"modern/S05-metrics-and-unit-economics.md","label":"Metrics and unit economics (2026 layer)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. **Pick the model** — subscription, marketplace, e-commerce, services or AI-native; the metric set\n   in section D follows from that, not from taste. **Define the revenue unit**: what counts as\n   recurring, excluding one-off services and unconverted pilots [22].\n2. **Instrument the customer, not the invoice** — give every customer a cohort (month of first\n   payment), a channel, a segment and a gross margin.\n3. **Compute the base five monthly:** net new ARR (or net revenue), gross margin, net burn, runway,\n   and one retention number (GRR for B2B, cohort retention for consumer/marketplace).\n4. **Add the efficiency layer quarterly:** CAC, CAC payback, burn multiple, magic number, NRR,\n   ARR per FTE; from $25M ARR add Rule of 40 [16][15].\n5. **Build the cohort table** — rows = acquisition month, columns = months since, cells = retained\n   customers, retained revenue and cumulative contribution margin; read down the columns to see\n   whether newer cohorts are better [32][35]. **Find the payback line** where cumulative contribution\n   margin crosses CAC — that is your real payback, not the formula estimate.\n6. **Decompose growth** into new, expansion, contraction and churn each period; this makes quick ratio\n   and NRR diagnosable rather than decorative [8][2]. Then **stress-test** with CAC +30%, churn +50%\n   and (for AI) flat rather than falling inference cost — a plan that only works on the base case is\n   not a plan [51].\n7. **Set the board pack** — five metrics on page one (Enterprise Five is a good default), cohort table\n   on page two, cash and runway on page three [16]. Reconcile everything to the P&L and cash-flow\n   statement in `A-financial-statements.md`; ARR that does not tie to billings is a flag.\n8. **Re-benchmark twice a year** — Benchmarkit, SaaS Capital, ICONIQ and Bessemer publish annually and\n   the medians have moved every year since 2022 [15][19][16][13]."},{"ref":"modern/S05-metrics-and-unit-economics.md","label":"Metrics and unit economics (2026 layer)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Unit-economics one-pager** — What does one customer cost to acquire (blended and paid), and what\ngross margin do they carry? How many months until they repay that cost in gross profit? What lifetime\nassumption are we using and what cohort evidence supports it? What happens if CAC rises 30%? [2][3][51]\n\n**Cohort review (monthly)** — Is the curve flattening, and at what level? Are newer cohorts better than\nolder ones at the same age? What share of this month's revenue came from cohorts older than 12 months?\nWhich channel produces the best month-12 cohort value — not the best day-1 CAC? [32][22]\n\n**Cash & runway review** — Net burn (3-month average), cash, runway. Burn multiple this quarter and the\nprevious three — trend, not level. Are we default alive; if not, what is the smallest change that makes\nus so? If the next round takes 24 months, what must be true? [4][46]\n\n**Investor-readiness checklist** [22][23][24] — Seed: flattening retention curve; ≥ 5 unaffiliated\npaying customers; engagement depth; organic pull. Series A: $2-5M clean recurring ARR; NRR ≥ 100%;\nGRR high-80s+; burn multiple < 2.0x; documented CAC payback; cohort table. Series A (AI): ~$3.5M ARR;\n120%+ NRR; gross margin above 60% and *rising*; falling inference cost per request; pilot-to-production\nconversion. Series B+: Rule of 40, net magic number > 1.0, ARR per FTE, GRR, burn-multiple trend.\n\n**Model-specific quick checks** — Marketplace: fill rate, supply utilisation, time to match, GMV\nretention by side, top-10 concentration, take rate vs work performed [33][34][35][36]. DTC: AOV, UPT,\nCM1/CM2/CM3, 60-day repeat rate, blended MER vs break-even MER, return rate [40][43]. Services:\nutilisation, project margin, effective hourly rate, revenue per consultant, leakage [44][45].\nAI-native: inference cost per request and as % of revenue, margin trend, cost per successful outcome,\nshare of revenue on usage/outcome pricing [27][28][30]."},{"ref":"modern/S05-metrics-and-unit-economics.md","label":"Metrics and unit economics (2026 layer)","heading":"Regional notes","text":"- **US** — every panel above (Benchmarkit, SaaS Capital, ICONIQ, Bessemer, CRV, Carta) is US-weighted.\n  The $2-5M Series A ARR bar and ~20-month CAC payback are US SaaS norms, not global ones [22][15][19].\n- **UK / EU** — no equivalent large open benchmark panel was found in this research; UK and EU founders\n  are generally measured against the same US datasets, which flatters US-scale ACVs. Re-derive CAC\n  payback in local currency and treat growth medians as directional (unverified).\n- **Africa / Nigeria** — the binding constraints are FX and gross margin, not growth. Devaluation and\n  25-30% inflation compress dollar returns while fuel, parts and software licences stay dollar-linked\n  [49]. Launch Base Africa's post-mortem of 30+ collapses (> $500M lost, early 2024 - mid 2026) names a\n  \"full-stack cost trap\" (warehousing, fleets, generators) incompatible with currency volatility,\n  under-capitalised fintech ($1-2M seed rounds against $500-800K/year compliance baselines), and a\n  willingness-to-pay chasm in discretionary categories; one venture was profitable per order (₦43,700\n  basket) yet failed on volume [48]. So: report in both naira and USD, be contribution-margin positive\n  per unit early, measure runway in hard currency, and expect less margin for error — average Nigerian\n  round ~$1.6M vs Kenya ~$6.9M [50].\n- **India / Southeast Asia / LatAm / Middle East** — not researched in this pass; assume lower ACVs,\n  lower absolute CAC and longer payback locally, and re-benchmark rather than importing US medians\n  (unverified)."},{"ref":"modern/S05-metrics-and-unit-economics.md","label":"Metrics and unit economics (2026 layer)","heading":"AI-era notes","text":"- **Inference is COGS, and it changes the shape of the business.** AI-native gross margins sit at\n  50-60% vs 70-85% for SaaS [26], with inference ~23% of revenue at scaling-stage AI B2B firms [27].\n  An LTV built on an 80% margin assumption is simply wrong for an AI product.\n- **Margins are improving, and the trend is the metric** — 45% (2025) → ~53% (2026) → ~59% (2027)\n  projected, credited to model routing, prompt caching (~90% API discounts), batching and context\n  compression, together cutting inference cost 50-70% with no measurable quality loss [27]. CRV:\n  \"the direction of the margin line is more informative than its current level\" [22][25].\n- **AI companies hit milestones far faster** — Stripe's top-100 AI cohort reached $1M ARR in a median\n  ~11.5 months and $5M in 24 months against 37 for SaaS [29]. That compresses the stage gates and\n  raises the bar.\n- **Pricing moved from access to work performed.** Hybrid is the plurality model (37%), outcome-based\n  6% and rising, a third plan AI credits within 6-12 months [30]; seat-based NRR (98%) looks weak\n  beside usage-based (108%) [15]. New metrics follow: cost per successful outcome, credit burn-down,\n  consumption cohort retention. And **\"ARR\" needs a quality footnote** — investors separate recurring\n  from pilot revenue and add pilot-to-production conversion [22].\n- **Dashboards: AI features are table stakes.** ChartMogul, Baremetrics and ProfitWell cover\n  subscription analytics, Mosaic and Drivetrain sit on top as FP&A, and most $5M+ ARR companies run\n  both; building your own re-creates revenue-recognition edge cases vendors already solved [53][54].\n- **Where hype outruns evidence:** claims that AI collapses CAC. Chen's 2025 position is the opposite —\n  channels saturate faster because AI made content and outbound cheap for everyone [52], consistent\n  with `S01`'s cold-email decline. No independent 2024-26 dataset found showing AI lowering blended\n  CAC at company level."},{"ref":"modern/S05-metrics-and-unit-economics.md","label":"Metrics and unit economics (2026 layer)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- **Reporting LTV:CAC without gross margin or a defensible churn window** — the commonest way an early\n  deck overstates unit economics by 2-3x [3][2]; and using LTV at all before the curve flattens [32].\n- **Applying Rule of 40 at $2M ARR** — designed for scale, it rewards slowing down when a young company\n  should invest [5][16]. **Blending self-serve and enterprise into one CAC**, and **treating churn as\n  one number** — split by segment and channel; separate voluntary from involuntary, logo from revenue [31].\n- **Celebrating NRR while GRR erodes** — the sector-wide version of this mistake [15][21]. **Marketplace\n  GMV theatre** — subsidised transactions inflate GMV while liquidity and cohort GMV retention fall\n  [37][35]. **DTC platform-ROAS optimisation** over-spends on paid by 20-40% [40]. **Services firms\n  selling into a delivery bottleneck** — at 66.4% utilisation, more sold work often lowers margin [44].\n- **AI plans that assume SaaS margins** — only 12% of surveyed companies even target 80%+ [30]; and\n  **calling a pilot ARR**, which investors now test explicitly [22].\n- **Raising on a 12-month runway** when the median gap between rounds is near 24 [46][47], or\n  **benchmarking against 2021 medians** — growth, payback and GRR have all moved [15][19]."},{"ref":"modern/S05-metrics-and-unit-economics.md","label":"Metrics and unit economics (2026 layer)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`CAC` — sales & marketing cost to acquire one new customer; `blended CAC` includes organic [2][40].\n`CAC payback` — months of gross profit needed to repay CAC [2]. `CAC ratio` — S&M per $1 of new ARR [15].\n`LTV` — gross-profit value of a customer over their expected life [2].\n`contribution margin (CM1/CM2/CM3)` — margin after COGS / after variable fulfilment / after marketing [40].\n`GRR` / `NRR (NDR)` — gross revenue retention (caps at 100%) / net revenue retention including expansion [15][16].\n`SaaS quick ratio` — (new + expansion MRR) ÷ (churned + contraction MRR) [8][9].\n`cohort` — customers grouped by acquisition period and tracked over time; `retention curve flattening` is the PMF signal [32][22].\n`net burn` — cash out minus cash in per month; `runway` — cash ÷ net burn, in months [4][46].\n`burn multiple` — net burn ÷ net new ARR [4]; `efficiency score` — Bessemer's inverse [14].\n`magic number` — annualised incremental revenue ÷ prior-quarter S&M [7]; `net magic number` uses net new ARR [16].\n`Rule of 40` — growth % + margin % ≥ 40 [5][6]; `T2D3` — triple, triple, double, double, double [10]; `ARR per FTE` — end-of-period ARR ÷ headcount [16].\n`GMV` — value transacted through a marketplace [34]; `take rate` — net revenue ÷ GMV [36]; `GMV retention` — cohort GMV retained over time, per side [35].\n`liquidity` — probability a listing sells or a search converts; `fill rate / search-to-fill` is its demand-side form [33][39].\n`AOV / UPT` — average order value / units per transaction [40][42]; `MER` — revenue ÷ marketing spend, `break-even MER` = 1 ÷ contribution margin % [43].\n`billable utilisation` — billable hours ÷ available hours [44]; `inference efficiency ratio` — revenue relative to inference cost [28].\n`cost per successful outcome` — variable AI cost ÷ outcomes delivered; the unit of outcome pricing [30].\n`default alive` — projected to reach profitability on current cash and growth (Paul Graham, 2015)."},{"ref":"modern/S06-financing-2026.md","label":"Financing (2026 layer)","heading":"What changed since 2018","text":"- **The post-money SAFE ate the early stage.** SAFEs were 93% of pre-priced deals and 89% of\n  pre-priced dollars in Q4 2025, 93%/95% by Q2 2026; post-money SAFEs went from just over 60% of\n  SAFEs in 2021 to almost 90% in 2025 [3][4][6]. The handbook's convertible-note default is now\n  the minority instrument (7% of deals) [3][4].\n- **Capital concentrated into AI and into fewer, larger cheques.** AI took 86% of US venture dollars\n  in H1 2026 ($355.9B of $412.7B) and $100M+ megadeals 87.5% of capital, leaving seed, A and B the\n  remaining 12.5%; three firms raised 48.1% of all new fund capital [10][11][12].\n- **Graduation got much harder, then slightly easier.** Seed→A at 24 months is ~14-17% against ~30%\n  in the boom; the 12-month rate recovered to 10-11% and median seed→A time fell to ~1.9 yrs [7][8].\n- **Headline terms stayed founder-friendly; tail terms hardened.** Q1 2026: 98.2% of rounds 1x\n  liquidation preference, 96.4% non-participating — but pay-to-play 7.3% (from 6.3%) and\n  redemption rights 6.1% (from 1.8% a quarter earlier) [14][15].\n- **SVB's failure rewired venture debt.** SVB held roughly half the market when it failed in March\n  2023; US venture-debt value fell to $27.4B in 2023 then rebounded to $53.3B in 2024 through BDCs\n  and specialist lenders [19], now priced at SOFR + 700-950bps with 3-10% warrant coverage [20].\n- **Revenue-based financing peaked and consolidated.** Pipe, Clearco and Capchase all cut staff and\n  pivoted as banks re-priced recurring-revenue lending; the instrument survives, the 2021 category\n  thesis did not [22][23].\n- **Equity crowdfunding is real but small, capped at $5M/12 months under Reg CF.** Wefunder, the\n  largest portal, closed $109M across 367 deals in 2025; the market fell 28% YoY in Q1 2026 [24][25].\n- **The IPO stopped being the liquidity event; tenders replaced it.** Carta administered 71 tender\n  offers in H1 2026 worth ~$3B — count +34%, value +200% YoY, the highest H1 in six years [17].\n- **Government money moved.** US SBIR/STTR lapsed on 30 Sep 2025 and was reauthorised only on\n  13 Apr 2026 [31][32]; the UK raised EIS company limits to £10M/year and £24M lifetime from\n  6 April 2026 [36]; the EIC Accelerator's 2026 budget is €634M [34][35].\n- **Bank credit stayed hard for small firms.** SBA's June 2025 SOP pushed $350K-$500K loans out of\n  streamlined processing, and approvals of ≤$500K loans fell about 38% by count [27][28]."},{"ref":"modern/S06-financing-2026.md","label":"Financing (2026 layer)","heading":"Core ideas (current consensus)","text":"- **The instrument question is settled; the cap question is not.** Carta's Peter Walker (2026):\n  capped post-money SAFEs are the default pre-seed mechanism, so the only real negotiation is the\n  cap, which scales with the raise — ~$10M on sub-$250K rounds to ~$35M on $2.5M+ in Q2 2026 [6][9].\n- **Post-money SAFEs make dilution certain and additive.** Each fixes the investor's percentage, so\n  stacking three stacks the dilution and the founder absorbs it — Carta: $1-2.4M pre-seed rounds\n  dilute 19-20% at the median, $2.5-4.9M mid-20s [3][4][5].\n- **Raise for a milestone, not a runway number.** Lemkin (SaaStr, 2025-26): the Series A bar is\n  ~$2M ARR ($1M in a hot space growing fast) at 100-150% growth; below that you are raising a seed\n  extension, whatever you call it [65].\n- **The market is bifurcated, not closed.** Lemkin (2025): AI-native companies scaling fast and\n  exceptional SaaS at 100%+ growth get funded; a good company at $30M ARR growing 60-70% is\n  \"walking through the desert\" for VC [64].\n- **Ownership math beats valuation vanity.** Carta cap-table data: median founding teams hold ~56%\n  after seed and ~36% after Series A — SAFEs and pool top-ups, not the round itself, do it [55b].\n- **Speed of execution is the pre-seed signal.** Elizabeth Yin (Hustle Fund): with almost no data,\n  investors underwrite how fast a team learns and ships; she caps fund size so small exits count [57][58].\n- **Most companies should not raise venture at all.** Walling (TinySeed, MicroConf): small product\n  on a platform → income replacement → standalone recurring revenue; TinySeed backs that with\n  $120K-$220K for 10-12% and no 100x pressure [59][60].\n- **Warrant-bearing debt is cheap equity only if you survive it.** 2026 venture debt: SOFR +\n  700-950bps, 3-10% warrants, 2-5% end-of-term fee; it extends runway, it does not replace a round [20][21].\n- **The AI boom is a capital cycle.** Bill Gurley (Benchmark, 2026): AI capex-to-sales heads for 34%\n  in 2026 and 37% in 2028, above the ~32% dot-com peak — \"One day, I just think we trip and run out\n  of money\"; he also warns toxic-high valuations kill good companies [61][62][63].\n- **Angels became organised.** ACA's 2026 Angel Funders Report: group angel investment rose 12% to\n  $491.3M in 2025 — bigger cheques, fewer companies, diligence-led syndicates and community models\n  displacing spray-and-pray [53][54][56]; Canadian angel investment fell to a five-year low [55].\n- **\"Land and expand\" applies to investors too.** Mark Suster (Upfront): you are selling trust and\n  a decade-long working relationship, so build it before you need the cheque [66]."},{"ref":"modern/S06-financing-2026.md","label":"Financing (2026 layer)","heading":"Frameworks & playbooks","text":"| Framework | Author / year | What it says | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Post-money SAFE (2018 revision) | Y Combinator, 2018; still the standard in 2026 | Investor's percentage is fixed at signing; cap is set on the post-money of the SAFE round itself. Variants: cap-only (≈2/3 of SAFEs), cap+discount, discount-only, MFN [3][4][6] | Any pre-seed or seed round under ~$4M raised on a rolling basis |\n| YC Standard Deal | Y Combinator, since 2022 | $125K on an uncapped SAFE for a fixed 7% post-conversion (incl. option pool), plus $375K on an uncapped MFN SAFE that adopts the best terms issued before the next priced round [16] | Benchmarking accelerator terms; understanding MFN mechanics |\n| Venture Deals term-sheet map | Brad Feld & Jason Mendelson, *Venture Deals* (4th ed., 2019) | Separate **economics** (price, liquidation preference, pay-to-play, vesting, pool) from **control** (board, protective provisions, drag-along); negotiate only the terms that matter in both buckets [67] | Reading any priced term sheet |\n| Stair-step bootstrapping | Rob Walling, 2013-2026 | Step 1 a single small product in an existing ecosystem; Step 2 replicate until it replaces your income; Step 3 standalone SaaS with runway and reputation [59] | Founders with no network, no savings and no venture-scale market |\n| TinySeed / calm capital | Rob Walling & Einar Vollset, 2018- | $120K-$220K for 10-12%, remote-first, no 100x mandate; profitability and dividends are acceptable outcomes [60] | B2B SaaS at $10-50K MRR that does not want the venture ladder |\n| Hustle Fund pre-seed underwriting | Elizabeth Yin, 2017- | With no metrics, underwrite execution speed and learning velocity; small fund, small cheques, small exits still work [57][58] | Framing what to show at pre-seed |\n| Milestone-based raise sizing | Common 2026 practice, e.g. Waveup / Eqvista AI playbooks (secondary) | Size the round to reach the *next* funding-grade milestone with 3-6 months of slack: seed AI application rounds of $2-5M targeting $1-2M ARR in 12-18 months [72][73] | Deciding the ask |\n| Tender offer / structured secondary | Carta, 2025-26 | Company-run liquidity event at a set price for employees and early investors; ~70% of H1 2026 tenders were Series C+, but Nasdaq Private Market saw ~50% of its 2025 programs at Series A-C [17][18] | Retention and founder de-risking when IPO is far away |\n| Venture debt sizing | Post-SVB lender practice, 2026 | 25-50% of the last equity round, drawn within 12 months of the round, priced SOFR + 700-950bps, 3-10% warrant coverage, 2-5% end-of-term fee, 2-3 year term [20][21] | Runway extension between priced rounds |\n| Six-week raise sprint | Equidam, 2026 | Weeks 1-2 volume and first meetings (output: a shortlist); weeks 3-6 shortlisted investors in the data room; a round typically fills over 6-16 weeks [70] | Running the process |"},{"ref":"modern/S06-financing-2026.md","label":"Financing (2026 layer)","heading":"Benchmarks & numbers (2024-2026)","text":"| Metric | Value | Segment / context | Source | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Median seed post-money | $24M (from $18M, $16M in prior two years) | Carta primary rounds, Q4 | [1] | 2025 |\n| Median seed round | $24.3M post on $4.1M raised, 18% dilution | Carta, software only, last 6 months, no bridges | [2] | 2026 |\n| Median Series A | $80M post on $14.4M raised, 18% dilution | same | [2] | 2026 |\n| Median Series B | $191M post on $25M raised | same | [2] | 2026 |\n| Median dilution, seed→Series C | fell ~18% → 16% over the year | Carta all rounds | [1] | 2025 |\n| Founder ownership | ~56% after seed; ~36% after Series A | Carta cap-table data (secondary analysis) | [55b] | 2026 |\n| SAFE share of pre-priced deals | 93% of deals / 89% of dollars; 93% / 95% by Q2 2026 | Carta; notes are the rest | [3][4][6] | 2025-26 |\n| Post-money SAFE share of SAFEs | ~60% (2021) → ~90% (2025); cap-only ≈ two-thirds | Carta, 330,000+ SAFEs and notes since 2021 | [3][4][6] | 2025-26 |\n| Median SAFE cap, Q2 2026 | $10M (<$250K) · $12M · $12.5M · $18M ($1-2.4M) · $35M ($2.5M+) | Carta via Finro | [6] | 2026 |\n| Pre-seed dilution | $1-2.4M rounds 19-20% median; $2.5-4.9M mid-20s; <$250K 5-6% | Carta | [3][4] | 2025 |\n| Seed→Series A graduation | 10-11% at 12 months (2025 cohort) vs 4-5% in 2022-23; 14-17% at 24 months vs ~30% in the boom | Carta / Peter Walker | [7][8] | 2026 |\n| US venture, H1 2026 | $412.7B deployed; AI $355.9B = 86% | PitchBook-NVCA Venture Monitor | [10][11][12] | 2026 |\n| Concentration | $100M+ megadeals 87.5% of capital; top 3 firms 48.1% of new fund capital; first-time funds on track for lowest year since 2016 | same | [11] | 2026 |\n| Global venture, Q1 2026 | ~$300B (record); seed $12B, +31% YoY | Crunchbase | [13] | 2026 |\n| Priced-round terms | 1x liq pref 98.2%; non-participating 96.4%; pay-to-play 7.3%; redemption 6.1%; accruing dividends 2.4% | Cooley, 165 deals / $39.9B | [14][15] | Q1 2026 |\n| Round direction | up 86% · flat 2.6% · down 11.4% | Cooley | [14] | Q1 2026 |\n| Bridge rounds | 16.6% of cash raised (Q2 2025) vs 11.8% (Q2 2024) — dollars, not deal count | Carta via secondary | [71] | 2025-26 |\n| Venture debt | US value $53.3B (2024), +94.5% on the $27.4B 2023 trough; SVB was ~50% of the market pre-failure | secondary review | [19] | 2024-26 |\n| Venture debt pricing | SOFR + 700-950bps · 3-10% warrant coverage · 2-5% end-of-term fee · 2-3 year facilities | lender-practice guides (secondary) | [20][21] | 2026 |\n| Reg CF portals, 2025 | Wefunder $109M / 367 deals · StartEngine $89M · DealMaker $66M · Republic $20M | platform league table (secondary) | [24] | 2025 |\n| SBA 7(a) | Small Loan cap cut to $350K (SOP 50 10 8, 1 Jun 2025); ≤$500K approvals -38% by count; $350-500K band -64% | SBA lender analyses | [27][28] | 2025-26 |\n| SBA FY2026 fees | at statutory maximum; guaranty fee waived ≤$950K for small manufacturers (NAICS 31-33), lender flat fee ≤$2,500 | NAGGL | [29] | 2026 |\n| Small-business loan approval | big banks ~13-15% · small banks ~18-20% · alternative lenders 25-30% | Biz2Credit-derived (secondary) | [30] | 2026 |\n| SBIR/STTR | lapsed 30 Sep 2025, reauthorised 13 Apr 2026 (P.L. 119-83); awards fell from a 6,713/yr FY20-24 average to 4,729 in 2025 (~$490M gap); new Phase II \"strategic breakthrough\" awards up to $30M over ≤48 months | Crowell; Federal News Network; Granted AI | [31][32][33] | 2026 |\n| EIC Accelerator | €634M indicative 2026 budget (€414M Open + €220M Challenges); grants up to €2.5M plus equity | European Innovation Council | [34][35] | 2026 |\n| UK SEIS | company £250K (£350K gross); investor £200K/yr; unchanged at Budget 2025 | Farrer; British Business Bank | [36][37] | 2026 |\n| UK EIS from 6 Apr 2026 | annual £10M (£20M knowledge-intensive), lifetime £24M (£40M KI) — up from £5M/£10M and £12M/£20M | Finance Act 2026 | [36][38] | 2026 |\n| Europe | ~$44B raised in 2025 (vs $43B/$41B in 2023/24); ecosystem ~$4T ≈ 15% of GDP; ~40,000 funded tech companies vs 13,000 in 2016; $375B decade funding gap | Atomico State of European Tech via Sifted | [40] | 2025 |\n| Africa 2025 | $4.1B equity+debt (+25%); debt $1.64B (+63% on $1.01B); Kenya $1.04B · South Africa $715M · Egypt $604M · Nigeria $572M (-3%, 102 deals); top four = 72% of capital | Partech | [42] | 2025 |\n| Africa H1 2026 | ~$1.36B (Africa: The Big Deal); Egypt $327M (one Spiro deal) · Nigeria $254M total / $214M equity — largest equity market · Kenya $126M · South Africa $83M | African Business / The Big Deal | [43] | 2026 |\n| Nigeria Q1 2026 | $78.6M, -28% YoY; Africa-wide debt raised >2x equity that quarter | Nairametrics; BusinessDay | [44][45] | 2026 |\n| India | ~$16B VC + growth in 2025, second straight year of growth (from $13.7B in 2024) | Bain India Venture Capital Report 2026 | [48] | 2026 |\n| Southeast Asia | $6.79B / 335 equity rounds in 2025 (+14%); e-Conomy counts $8B, DealStreetAsia $5.37B; 9M-2025 seed $110M, -72% YoY; H1 2025 229 deals, weakest in 6+ years; Singapore ~92% of H1 2025 funding | regional trackers | [49][50] | 2025-26 |\n| MENA | 2025 $3.8B / 688 deals (+74%), international investors 49% of capital; Saudi $1.72B + UAE $1.58B = 86%; AI $858M / 194 deals | MAGNiTT via Arab News | [51] | 2025 |\n| MENA H1 2026 | $1.35B, -22% YoY; 214 deals, -41%; lowest half-year since at least 2022 | MAGNiTT via Arab News | [52] | 2026 |\n| Angels | ACA groups +12% to $491.3M in 2025 (bigger cheques, fewer companies); Canada C$113.79M / 490 deals, a five-year low (-22% capital, -20% deals) | ACA; NACO | [53][54][55] | 2026 |\n| Secondaries | 71 tender offers in H1 2026, ~$3B, count +34% / value +200% YoY; ~70% Series C+; NPM ~50% of 2025 programs at Series A-C (from 30%) | Carta | [17][18] | 2026 |\n| Accelerator terms | YC $500K = $125K uncapped SAFE for 7% + $375K uncapped MFN SAFE | Y Combinator | [16] | 2026 |\n| Series A readiness | ~$2M ARR ($1M in a hot space growing fast), 100-150% growth, strong references | SaaStr / Lemkin | [65] | 2026 |\n[section truncated]"},{"ref":"modern/S06-financing-2026.md","label":"Financing (2026 layer)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **Under ~$4M raised on a rolling basis, use a cap-only post-money SAFE** — 93%+ of the market, so\n  negotiate the cap, not the form [3][4][6].\n- **If you take more than two SAFEs, model the stack before signing the third.** They are additive\n  and the dilution lands on founders and employees; $1-2.4M raised already implies 19-20% [3][4].\n- **If your cap is above what the next round can support, take less money at a lower cap** — you\n  must grow into the cap or face a down round or a structured one (Gurley's \"toxic\" case) [62].\n- **If you are not at ~$2M ARR growing 100%+, do not run a Series A process** — run a seed\n  extension or an insider bridge instead (Lemkin) [65]. Bridges are normal: 16.6% of dollars [71].\n- **If you can reach the next milestone without outside equity, do.** 86% of US venture dollars\n  went to AI, so a non-AI company is competing for a shrinking pool [10][11].\n- **If the business is Main Street or services, the ladder is credit, not equity.** Expect big-bank\n  approval around 13-15% and plan for small banks, CDFIs or fintech lenders [30].\n- **If you are US deep-tech or hardware, chase SBIR/STTR first** — non-dilutive, with a new $30M\n  Phase II tier; budget schedule risk after the six-month lapse [31][32][33].\n- **If you are UK-based, get SEIS/EIS advance assurance before you talk to angels.** SEIS £250K and,\n  from 6 April 2026, EIS annual £10M / lifetime £24M (£20M/£40M knowledge-intensive) [36][37][38].\n- **If you take venture debt, size it at 25-50% of the last equity round and draw it early.** Price\n  warrant coverage and end-of-term fees as real cost; the covenants are what kill you [20][21].\n- **If a term sheet is 1x non-participating with a 2-1-1 board, the economics are market.** Argue\n  pay-to-play, redemption, accruing dividends and pool top-up instead [14][15][67].\n- **If employees are 4+ years in and an IPO is not in sight, plan a tender** — H1 2026 tender value\n  tripled year on year [17].\n- **If choosing between crowdfunding and angels, ask what the cheque brings.** Reg CF caps at\n  $5M/12 months, shrank 28% YoY, and buys a marketing asset and a long register — not advice\n  [24][25][26]."},{"ref":"modern/S06-financing-2026.md","label":"Financing (2026 layer)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. **Decide whether to raise.** Write the milestone the money buys and what happens without it. If\n   the answer is \"grow slower\", price bootstrapping, RBF or debt first [59][60].\n2. **Pick the ladder.** Venture (pre-seed → seed → A → B), calm capital (TinySeed-style), credit\n   (bank/SBA/fintech), grants (SBIR, Innovate UK, EIC), crowd (Reg CF / Crowdcube), or a blend.\n3. **Set the ask from the milestone:** cost to reach the next funding-grade milestone (e.g. $1-2M\n   ARR in 12-18 months for an AI app company) plus 3-6 months of slack [72][73].\n4. **Choose the instrument.** Under ~$4M rolling: post-money SAFE, cap only. Above that, or with a\n   lead setting price: a priced round on NVCA/BVCA documents [3][6][67].\n5. **Model the cap table before the first meeting** — stack every SAFE at its cap, add the pool\n   top-up, check founder ownership against the ~56% / ~36% medians [55b].\n6. **Build the data room one or two quarters early** — round story and use of proceeds, financials\n   and model, fully diluted cap table, stage metrics, core legal [68][69][75].\n7. **Warm the list for 1-2 quarters** with monthly investor updates; convert the warmest into the\n   lead conversation [66].\n8. **Run the process as a sprint.** Weeks 1-2: volume of first meetings, output a shortlist. Weeks\n   3-6: shortlist into the data room, answer in hours. Rounds fill over 6-16 weeks [70].\n9. **Negotiate economics and control separately** (price, preference, pool, vesting | board,\n   protective provisions, drag) [67].\n10. **Close, then keep the discipline:** convert SAFEs, update the cap table, file SEIS/EIS or\n    equivalent, restart the investor-update cadence for the next round.\n11. **Plan liquidity deliberately.** At Series C+ — earlier if retention demands it — run a\n    company tender rather than ad hoc secondary sales [17][18]."},{"ref":"modern/S06-financing-2026.md","label":"Financing (2026 layer)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"- **Should we raise?** What milestone does the money buy? What is the plan without it? What\n  percentage are we selling, at what implied next-round price?\n- **Instrument choice:** rolling or fixed close? Is there a lead willing to set price? How many\n  SAFEs are outstanding, at what caps?\n- **Cap sanity test:** at this cap, what ARR/valuation must we hit in 18-24 months for the next\n  round to be an up round — and is that plan credible?\n- **Dilution model:** founders / employees / investors today → after all SAFEs convert → after the\n  pool top-up → after the priced round. Compare to the 56% / 36% medians [55b].\n- **Data room checklist:** round story and use of proceeds; 24-month model; historical financials;\n  fully diluted cap table with all SAFEs; metrics pack (ARR, growth, retention, burn multiple,\n  payback); incorporation; IP assignments; key contracts; option docs; debt and covenants\n  [68][69][75].\n- **Investor update (monthly):** headline metric, growth, cash and runway, three wins, three\n  misses, one specific ask, one hire needed.\n- **Term-sheet review:** preference multiple and participation; pay-to-play; redemption; accruing\n  dividends; pool size and whether pre- or post-money; board; protective provisions; pro rata;\n  drag-along; founder vesting reset [14][67].\n- **Non-dilutive screen:** do we qualify for SBIR/STTR, Innovate UK, EIC Accelerator, SEIS/EIS\n  relief for our investors, an SBA 7(a) loan, or a revenue-based facility? [27][31][34][36]\n- **Debt covenant stress test:** at 70% of plan do we breach, what is the cure period, and what\n  does the lender control on a breach?"},{"ref":"modern/S06-financing-2026.md","label":"Financing (2026 layer)","heading":"Regional notes","text":"- **US.** Deepest and most concentrated: $412.7B in H1 2026, 86% to AI, 87.5% in $100M+ rounds\n  [10][11][12]. Post-money SAFE + Delaware C-corp is the default stack; Reg CF allows $5M/12 months\n  from the public [24]. Credit is the real channel for non-venture firms — SBA's June 2025 SOP cut\n  ≤$500K approvals ~38% by count [27][28] and big banks approve ~13-15% of applications [30].\n  SBIR/STTR is back after a six-month lapse, with a new $30M Phase II tier [31][33].\n- **UK.** SEIS/EIS is the structural advantage: SEIS £250K per company (£350K gross), £200K per\n  investor per year [36][37]; EIS rose on 6 April 2026 to £10M/year and £24M lifetime (£20M/£40M\n  knowledge-intensive) [36][38]. Get advance assurance first — angels will ask. Crowdcube and\n  Seedrs are mainstream (£100K-£500K raises, ~7.5% commission) under FCA rules shifting toward a\n  formal public-offer regime [39]; the British Business Bank underwrites much of the supply [37].\n- **EU.** Europe raised ~$44B in 2025 and $44.5B in H1 2026 against a $375B decade-long gap and a\n  government-heavy capital base [40][41]. The EIC Accelerator is the flagship non-dilutive route —\n  €634M in 2026, grants to €2.5M plus possible equity [34][35] — but slow: a parallel track, not\n  the plan.\n- **Africa / Nigeria.** 2025 rebounded to $4.1B (+25%), but the growth was **debt**: $1.64B, +63%\n  [42]. Nigeria raised $572M across 102 deals in 2025 (-3%) [42], fell to $78.6M in Q1 2026 (-28%\n  YoY) [44], then reclaimed the top *equity* spot in H1 2026 with $214M equity / $254M total [43];\n  Africa-wide, debt raised more than twice as much as equity in Q1 2026 [45]. FX is the structural\n  term-sheet issue — naira revenue against dollar capital, so investors price devaluation and ask\n  for dollar-linked contracts [44]. The Nigeria Startup Act gives labelled startups three years of\n  tax relief (+2 via NIPC) and mandates ≥₦10bn a year to the Startup Investment Seed Fund, but\n  labelling and access remain bottlenecked [46][47].\n- **India.** ~$16B of VC and growth capital in 2025, a second consecutive year of growth, led by\n  fintech and SaaS with AI cross-cutting, and improving exits on strong public markets [48].\n  Domestic capital plus IPO exits make India less dependent on US risk appetite than most peers.\n- **Southeast Asia.** Barbelled and shrinking at the bottom: 2025 counted between $5.37B and $8B\n  depending on the tracker; seed fell 72% to $110M over the first nine months of 2025 while\n  late-stage rose; H1 2025 had 229 deals, the weakest in six years; Singapore took ~92% of H1 2025\n  dollars [49][50]. Plan to domicile in Singapore and to raise smaller, for longer.\n- **Gulf / MENA.** 2025 was a record: $3.8B across 688 deals (+74%), Saudi ($1.72B) and the UAE\n  ($1.58B) taking 86%, international investors supplying 49% of capital [51]. H1 2026 reversed —\n  $1.35B (-22%) across 214 deals (-41%), the weakest half since at least 2022 [52]. Sovereign-linked\n  funds dominate; a local entity and a regional commitment are usually preconditions for the cheque."},{"ref":"modern/S06-financing-2026.md","label":"Financing (2026 layer)","heading":"AI-era notes","text":"- **AI is the market, not a sector.** 86% of US venture dollars in H1 2026 and 42.5% of Q1 2026\n  deal count [10][11]; globally AI took $212B in 2025, +85% YoY [13]. If you are not AI-native,\n  assume a smaller pool and a longer process.\n- **The concentration is structural, not a cycle.** Deal counts sit at their lowest since 2018 even\n  as dollars hit records [74] — a record headline is not evidence your seed round got easier.\n- **\"AI\" is table stakes; efficiency is the differentiator.** 2026 seed diligence centres on burn\n  multiple, gross margin after inference cost, and a credible $1-2M ARR milestone [72][73].\n- **AI lowers the cost of building, which changes the ask.** Smaller teams reach revenue, so raise\n  what your traction justifies; capital efficiency is a pitch asset, not an apology [72].\n- **AI helps you run the raise.** Deck narrative, data-room assembly and Q&A, monthly investor\n  updates and SAFE-stack modelling are all assistant-tractable — and 2026 data-room guidance\n  assumes near-instant diligence responses [68][70].\n- **Where the hype outruns the evidence.** (a) *Bubble risk is named by insiders*: Gurley projects\n  AI capex-to-sales above dot-com peaks and the classic overinvestment → overcapacity → price\n  collapse sequence [61][62]. (b) *A high cap is not validation* — it is a hurdle for the next\n  round [62]. (c) *Retail access to private AI deals* is being marketed hard; Gurley warns against\n  it [63]. (d) No reliable public evidence yet that AI-assisted diligence raises investor hit\n  rates — treat \"AI-native VC\" claims as marketing."},{"ref":"modern/S06-financing-2026.md","label":"Financing (2026 layer)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- **Stacking post-money SAFEs without modelling them** — percentages add, and founders find out\n  only at the priced round [3][4][55b]. **Optimising the cap instead of the business** is the twin\n  error: a cap you cannot grow into becomes a down round, a pay-to-play or a recap [14][62].\n- **Reading record headline funding as a healthy market.** Dollars are at records while deal counts\n  sit at multi-year lows and 87.5% of capital goes to $100M+ rounds [11][14][74].\n- **Calling a seed extension a Series A** — graduation at 24 months is 14-17%, and investors price\n  the gap to the ~$2M ARR bar precisely [7][65].\n- **Treating venture debt as free**, and concentrating cash plus debt with one venture lender —\n  the covenant, not the coupon, ends companies, and that was the 2023 SVB failure mode [19][20][21].\n- **Launching a Reg CF raise as the fundraising plan.** The channel shrank 28% YoY in Q1 2026 and\n  works best as a community event on top of a lead [24][25].\n- **Ignoring the option pool in the \"pre-money\" number** — a pre-money top-up is a price cut, and\n  it is why founders hold ~36% after a Series A that nominally sold ~20% [55b][67].\n- **Missing SEIS/EIS advance assurance (UK) or startup labelling (Nigeria)** before approaching\n  local investors — both gate the money, and both take weeks [36][46][47].\n- **Building the data room after the first meeting**, and selling secondary shares ad hoc instead\n  of running a tender — both read as disorganisation and set awkward marks [17][18][68][70]."},{"ref":"modern/S06-financing-2026.md","label":"Financing (2026 layer)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`post-money SAFE` — YC's 2018 revision: the investor's percentage is fixed at signing, so SAFEs\ndilute founders additively rather than diluting each other.\n`valuation cap` — the maximum conversion valuation on a SAFE or note; the main negotiated term.\n`MFN (most-favoured-nation)` — an uncapped, undiscounted SAFE that adopts the best terms issued\nbefore the next priced round; the form of YC's $375K tranche.\n`pay-to-play` — forces existing investors to join a new round or lose preferred rights; 7.3% [14].\n`redemption right` — lets an investor make the company buy its shares back; 6.1% of deals [14].\n`participating preferred` — preference plus a share of remaining proceeds (\"double dip\"); rare,\nsince 96.4% of Q1 2026 rounds were non-participating [14].\n`bridge / extension` — capital between priced rounds, usually a SAFE or note at or near the last cap.\n`warrant coverage` — the equity warrants a venture lender takes, as a percentage of the drawn loan.\n`revenue-based financing (RBF)` — repaid as a fixed percentage of monthly revenue to a cap multiple;\nnon-dilutive, expensive, and now a consolidated market.\n`tender offer` — a company-run liquidity event letting employees and early investors sell a portion\nof their shares at a set price.\n`Reg CF` — US Regulation Crowdfunding: up to $5M per issuer per 12 months from the public via a\nregistered portal.\n`SEIS / EIS` — UK tax-relief schemes that make early angel investment much cheaper for the investor.\n`graduation rate` — the share of a seed cohort raising a Series A within a given window.\n`burn multiple` — net burn divided by net new ARR; the efficiency metric 2026 investors lead with."},{"ref":"modern/S07-ai-native-company.md","label":"Building with AI (2026 layer)","heading":"What changed since 2018","text":"- **Software got cheap to write and expensive to trust.** Karpathy named \"vibe coding\" in Feb 2025 —\n  describe intent, accept code you barely read — then declared it superseded by \"agentic\n  engineering\" as tools got reliable around Dec 2025 [20][21].\n- **The headcount-to-revenue curve broke at the top end.** Cursor/Anysphere ~$2B ARR on 300+ staff\n  (~$6.7M/employee) [16]; Lovable $100M ARR at 45 FTE → $400M at 146 [14][15]. Median private SaaS\n  is ~$130K/employee [14].\n- **Adoption is wide but shallow.** 88% organisational adoption [8][9] and 50.4% of US businesses\n  paying for AI by Mar 2026 [3] — yet only 17-20% of US firms tell the Census they actually *use* it\n  in producing goods or services [4][5], and McKinsey finds 80% reporting productivity gains against\n  37% reporting any EBIT impact, with \"high performers\" still 6% [6][7].\n- **Inference became a real line of COGS.** AI-native gross margins ~52% in 2026 (from 41% in 2024)\n  vs 75-85% for SaaS; inference is 20-23% of product cost [32][33].\n- **Agents got a plumbing standard** — MCP went to the Linux Foundation's Agentic AI Foundation in\n  Dec 2025, with native support from Anthropic, OpenAI, Google and Microsoft [39][40] — and\n  **capital concentrated violently**: H1 2026 global venture hit a record $510B, with OpenAI and\n  Anthropic alone taking $217B, 43% of *all* startup funding [42].\n- **Law arrived, then slipped.** The EU Digital Omnibus (in force 27 Jul 2026) pushed high-risk AI\n  duties to Dec 2027, but Art. 50 transparency still applies from 2 Aug 2026 [44][45].\n- **Copyright got a price tag.** Anthropic's $1.5B author settlement was approved in July 2026 —\n  training on lawfully obtained books was fair use; keeping pirated copies was not [50][51].\n- **The backlash is evidenced.** MIT NANDA put 95% of enterprise genAI pilots at zero measurable\n  P&L return [11]; METR's RCT found devs 19% *slower* while believing they were 20% faster [10]."},{"ref":"modern/S07-ai-native-company.md","label":"Building with AI (2026 layer)","heading":"Core ideas (current consensus)","text":"- **AI-native is an architecture claim, not a feature claim.** CRV's remove test (Mar 2026): turn the\n  AI off — degrade = AI-enabled, stop = AI-native. The harder commercial test is the second: does a\n  better foundation model make you more valuable, or replace you? [1]\n- **It is an operating-model choice.** McKinsey's high performers are defined by workflow *redesign*\n  (~75% vs ~25%) [6][7]; bolting a chatbot onto a human-shaped process is the modal failure.\n- **Agents, not chat, are the 2026 unit.** Grady & Huang (Sequoia, Jan 2026): 2023-24 apps were\n  \"talkers\", 2026-27 apps are \"doers\" — moving the market from software to services budgets [31].\n- **Agentic workflows beat waiting for the next model.** Ng (AI Fund / YC): outline → search → draft\n  → critique → revise gives a bigger quality jump than a model upgrade, and execution speed is the\n  best predictor of startup success [56].\n- **AI raises the floor and substitutes for a teammate** (randomised P&G experiment, 776\n  professionals: individuals with AI matched two-person teams without it, at 16.4% less time\n  [24][25]) — but **it also amplifies whatever your engineering system already is**: DORA finds\n  adoption now correlates with throughput (a reversal) yet still negatively with stability [22][23].\n- **The \"GPT wrapper\" sneer aged badly; so did \"data moat\".** Casado & Wang (a16z, 2026): value is\n  accruing at the application layer and brand is becoming a real moat [28]; a16z argued in 2019 most\n  data moats are empty [29]. Durable defensibility is still Currier's list — network effects,\n  embedding, data loops [30].\n- **Model choice is a routing decision, not an identity:** 81% of enterprises run 3+ model families\n  in production (from 68%) [35][36] and open weights went 11% → 29% of gateway tokens between April\n  and June 2026 [38].\n- **Evals are the product discipline** — Husain & Shankar: 60-80% of production AI dev time is error\n  analysis and evaluation; read real traces by hand first, keep judges binary, and treat a 100% pass\n  rate as evals that are too easy [54][55]. **Agent security is likewise architectural**: Willison's\n  lethal trifecta (private data + untrusted content + an outbound channel) is only fixed by removing\n  a leg, not by a better system prompt [41].\n- **Small is a strategy, not a phase.** YC's W26 batch: 3x as many companies at $1M annualised as\n  W25, record 14% average week-on-week growth, 22 solo founders (11%) [18]; Tan says teams under\n  ten reach ~$10M revenue [19].\n\n### Evidence vs hype (read this before quoting any number above)\n- **EVIDENCE (randomised / large-sample):** METR's RCT — 16 experienced devs, 246 real tasks, 19%\n  slowdown against a self-reported 20% speedup [10]. The P&G field experiment — 776 professionals,\n  randomised, 16.4% time saving and a team-equivalence effect [24]. DORA's throughput-up /\n  stability-down pattern [22][23]. Census BTOS — nationally representative, and it says AI *use* is\n  17-20%, not 80% [4][5].\n- **EVIDENCE (credible survey, self-reported):** McKinsey 80%/37% [6][7]; ICONIQ's 305 AI-product\n  executives on margins and inference [32][33]; Ramp's card panel [2][3]; a16z's CIO survey [35][36].\n- **HYPE, or at least unaudited:** vendor resolution rates. Fin publishes 67-76%, its own case\n  studies cluster at 42-50%, an independent 500-ticket test landed at 38%; Decagon self-reports 80%\n  deflection against 49% in a head-to-head. Gartner's honest frame: ~45% deflected, ~14% fully\n  self-resolved [52][53].\n- **HYPE with a real kernel — \"95% of AI pilots fail.\"** MIT NANDA rests on 52 interviews, 153 survey\n  responses and 300 public deployments: directional, not a census, and challenged within weeks\n  [11][12]. Its defensible core (only ~5% of *custom* tools reach production; failure is workflow\n  fit, not model quality) matches McKinsey's 80/37 gap [6][11].\n- **HYPE — the one-person billion-dollar company.** Altman's betting-pool remark is an anecdote; as\n  of September 2026 no verified one-person $1B company exists. The strongest adjacent datapoint is\n  a solo-built open-source agent (OpenClaw) acquired by OpenAI in Feb 2026 [17].\n- **Hype in the *other* direction:** \"AI does nothing\" is also unsupported — 53% population adoption\n  in three years and $172B/year of estimated US consumer value [8][9]. And watch the self-report\n  gap: METR's participants were wrong about their own speed by ~39 points [10], so any \"AI saves us X\n  hours\" without a control is a feeling, not a finding."},{"ref":"modern/S07-ai-native-company.md","label":"Building with AI (2026 layer)","heading":"Frameworks & playbooks","text":"| Framework | Author / year | What it says | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| The remove test / model-improvement test | CRV, *What Is AI-Native?* (Mar 2026) | Turn the AI off: degrade = AI-enabled, stop = AI-native. Then ask whether better base models make you more valuable or redundant, and what remains valuable if the model were swapped tomorrow [1] | Positioning; deciding whether to claim \"AI-native\" to investors |\n| Software 1.0 / 2.0 / 3.0 | Andrej Karpathy (2017, 2025) | 1.0 = written code, 2.0 = trained weights, 3.0 = prompts to a model as the program; English becomes a programming surface [21] | Explaining to a team why the build process changed |\n| Vibe coding → agentic engineering | Karpathy (Feb 2025; Dec 2025 onward) | Vibe coding = accept AI output without reading it (fine for prototypes, throwaway tools); agentic engineering = specs, tests, review and agents run as a managed system [20] | Deciding what may be vibe-coded and what may not |\n| Agentic workflow loop | Andrew Ng, AI Fund / YC (2024-26) | Outline → search → draft → self-critique → revise beats single-shot prompting; slower per call, much higher quality; pick *concrete* ideas over vague ones and optimise for speed of iteration [56] | Designing any AI feature that must be reliable |\n| Lethal trifecta / Agents Rule of Two | Simon Willison (Jun 2025); Meta's formulation | An agent with private data **and** untrusted input **and** an outbound channel can be made to exfiltrate. Allow at most two; sandbox and log if you truly need all three [41] | Before giving any agent write access or tool access |\n| Error-analysis-first evals | Hamel Husain & Shreya Shankar (2025-26) | Read real production traces by hand, open/axial-code the failures, *then* build a binary LLM-judge for the top failure modes; generate synthetic tests from explicit dimensions, not \"make me test cases\" [54][55] | The moment an AI feature has users |\n| Defensibility trio | James Currier, NFX | Network effects, embedding (deep integration you can't rip out) and data loops are the durable moats; AI alone is not one [30] | The \"what's your moat\" question in any pitch |\n| Value-accrual thesis | Martin Casado & Sarah Wang, a16z (2026) | Value is accruing at the application layer; brand is becoming a real moat; foundation labs keep moving up into applications, so pick a layer you can defend [28][29] | Deciding build-on-top vs build-the-model |\n| Talkers → doers / services TAM | Pat Grady & Sonya Huang, Sequoia (Jan 2026) | Long-horizon agents make *services* addressable by software; price the outcome, not the seat [31] | Pricing and TAM slides for an agent product |\n| AI-amplifier model | DORA (2025-26) | AI magnifies existing engineering strength or dysfunction; the ROI runs through code review, testing, version control and fast feedback [22][23] | Before scaling AI-written code volume |"},{"ref":"modern/S07-ai-native-company.md","label":"Building with AI (2026 layer)","heading":"Benchmarks & numbers (2024-2026)","text":"| Metric | Value | Segment / context | Source | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| US businesses paying for AI | 50.4% (first crossing of 50%); by vendor Anthropic 43.8%, OpenAI 39.8%, 16% pay both (vs 8% a year earlier) | Ramp panel, 70,000+ firms | [2][3] | 2026 |\n| Firms paying for open-weight models | 6.4% of AI-spending firms, 3.6% of all; top-1% AI spend $7,205/employee/month | same | [2] | Sep 2026 |\n| Token price and mix | blended $0.68/M, −41% from a Mar 2026 peak of $1.15; frontier models 45% of token share, down from 53% | same | [2] | Sep 2026 |\n| US firms *using* AI in operations | 17-20%; 20-23% expect to within 6 months; by size 37% (250+ staff); 32% (100-249); <20% (≤4 staff) | Census BTOS, nationally representative | [4][5] | Dec 2025-May 2026 |\n| Global adoption | 88% organisational; 53% of population within 3 years (faster than PC or internet) | Stanford AI Index | [8][9] | 2026 |\n| Productivity vs EBIT impact | 80% see individual gains, 37% report EBIT impact; \"high performers\" (≥5% EBIT from AI) are 6%, flat YoY, and ~75% of them redesigned workflows vs ~25% of others | McKinsey, 1,719 respondents | [6][7] | Aug 2026 |\n| Enterprise LLM spend and model mix | $4.5M → $7M over two years, $11.6M expected by end-2026; growing ~75% YoY; innovation-budget share 25% → 7%; 81% run 3+ model families in production (from 68%) | a16z CIO survey | [35][36] | 2026 |\n| Open-weight share of tokens | 11% (Apr) → 29% (Jun) on Vercel AI Gateway; ~61% of OpenRouter tokens were Chinese open-weight models by May | Vercel / OpenRouter, reported | [38] | 2026 |\n| AI-native gross margin and cost mix | ~52% (2026) from 41% (2024), vs 75-85% for SaaS; trajectory 45% → 53% → 59% (2025-27); inference is 20-23%, talent 26-28% at GA/scaling; VC view 50-60% typical, fastest-scaling cohort ~25% and often negative | ICONIQ (305 execs) / Bessemer | [32][33][34] | 2026 |\n| Revenue per employee | AI leaders: Cursor ~$6.7M, Lovable ~$2.77M ($100M ARR at 45 FTE → $400M at 146); earlier Midjourney $2M, OpenAI $1.5M. Median private SaaS ~$130K | company-reported / Dealroom | [13][14][15][16] | 2025-26 |\n| YC W26 batch | 3x more companies at $1M annualised vs W25; 14% avg week-on-week growth (record); 22 solo founders (11%); ~25% of YC startups had 95% of code AI-written | Y Combinator / Garry Tan | [18][19] | 2026 |\n| Venture funding, AI share, concentration | $510B in H1 2026 (vs $440B for all of 2025); >70% of global Q2 capital to AI (from ~50%); OpenAI + Anthropic $217B = 43% of all H1 funding; 3 deals = 67% of Q1 AI funding | Crunchbase / PitchBook | [42][43] | 2026 |\n| Developer RCT and team equivalence | −19% actual speed vs +20% self-reported; 16 devs, 246 tasks; individual + AI ≈ two-person team without AI, −16.4% time | METR; 776 P&G professionals, randomised | [10][24] | 2025 |\n| Trust and stability | ~30% little/no trust in AI code (\"somewhat+\" fell to ~70% from 87.9%); throughput ↑, stability still ↓ | DORA | [22][23] | 2025-26 |\n| GenAI pilots with no P&L return | 95%; only ~5% of custom tools reach production | MIT NANDA (52 interviews, 153 surveys, 300 deployments) | [11] | 2025 |\n| Workslop | 40% of workers hit it; ~2 hrs each; ~$186/worker/month; >$9M/yr at 10,000 staff | Stanford/BetterUp via HBR | [26][27] | 2025 |\n| AI support resolution | vendor 67-80%, own case studies 42-50%, independent tests 38-73%; Gartner: ~45% deflected but only ~14% fully self-resolved | Fin/Decagon, independent, Gartner | [52][53] | 2026 |\n| MCP ecosystem | ~97M monthly SDK downloads; 9,400+ public servers; 41% of software orgs in limited/broad production | MCP / Stacklok | [39][40] | 2026 |"},{"ref":"modern/S07-ai-native-company.md","label":"Building with AI (2026 layer)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **If removing the AI only degrades the product, don't call it AI-native** — or price it as if it\n  were. **If a better base model makes you redundant, you are a feature:** name what survives a model\n  swap — workflow, distribution, brand, data loops, integrations [1][28][30].\n- **If you have fewer than ~50 real production traces, don't build an LLM-judge** — hand error\n  analysis first; budget 60-80% of AI engineering time for evaluation [54][55].\n- **If an agent touches private data, untrusted input and an outbound channel, redesign it:** cut a\n  leg, or sandbox + log + human approval on writes [41].\n- **If gross margin is under ~50% and inference over ~25% of revenue, fix routing before you raise**\n  [32][33]; **default to a cheap or open-weight model and escalate**, reserving frontier models for\n  the hard minority, with a second provider wired for failover [2][37][38].\n- **If AI writes a large share of your code, fix review, tests and rollback first** — throughput\n  rises, stability falls, and the ROI runs through the control system [22][23]. **Vibe-code\n  prototypes and internal tools; never vibe-code money, auth or personal data** [20].\n- **If you can't measure it against a control, don't claim a productivity gain** — METR's\n  participants were wrong about their own speed by ~39 points [10].\n- **If output is generated faster than it can be consumed, you are manufacturing workslop** —\n  measure the receiver's remediation time (~2 hrs per instance) [26][27].\n- **If you sell a support agent, publish resolution (closed without escalation), not deflection** —\n  buyers discount vendor numbers by 17-25 points [52][53].\n- **If you have EU users and a bot or synthetic media, ship the disclosure now** (Art. 50, from\n  2 Aug 2026), and **if you fine-tuned on scraped content, document provenance** — the Anthropic\n  settlement turned on acquisition, not training [44][45][50][51].\n- **If you are raising and you are not AI, assume a harder market**: >70% of global Q2 2026 venture\n  dollars went to AI, and 43% of H1 funding to two companies [42].\n- **Keep a human in the loop where being wrong is asymmetric** (refunds, pricing, contracts, anything\n  irreversible); automate where errors are cheap and visible [11][41]."},{"ref":"modern/S07-ai-native-company.md","label":"Building with AI (2026 layer)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. **Map workflows before buying anything** — the 10 processes eating the most team hours; high\n   performers are separated by redesign, not tool count [6][7].\n2. **Classify each: automate, augment, leave alone.** Automate where errors are cheap and reversible;\n   augment where judgement matters; leave alone where the relationship is the product.\n3. **Instrument the baseline** (time, volume, error rate, cost) *before* the AI goes in — otherwise\n   you will report a feeling, as METR's developers did [10].\n4. **Build the thinnest agentic loop that works** — outline, retrieve, draft, critique, revise —\n   rather than one mega-prompt [56].\n5. **Collect 50-200 real traces, read them, code the failures** into named modes; then write binary\n   judges for the top 2-3 [54][55].\n6. **Do the security pass:** private data + untrusted content + outbound channel? Remove a leg, or\n   sandbox, require approval on writes, log every tool call [41].\n7. **Cost-engineer:** measure tokens per *successful task*; route to the cheapest model that passes\n   evals; cache; set per-tenant spend caps [2][33][37].\n8. **Set the reliability gate before launch** (target pass rate, escalation path, behaviour when the\n   model is down or wrong) [54][55], then **publish the honest metric** — resolution not deflection,\n   tasks completed not tokens processed [52][53].\n9. **Re-run the redesign quarterly** — blended token price fell 41% in six months, so last quarter's\n   architecture is often last quarter's cost [2].\n10. **Write the compliance one-pager once** (jurisdictions, disclosures, data flows, training stance)\n    [44][45][46][48], and **re-decide build vs buy every two quarters** — custom enterprise tools\n    rarely reach production, so buy the commodity and build the differentiated slice [11]."},{"ref":"modern/S07-ai-native-company.md","label":"Building with AI (2026 layer)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**AI-native self-assessment** [1][6]\n- Switch the AI off today: does the product degrade or stop? If the base model got 2x better, are we\n  more valuable or less necessary? If it were swapped tomorrow, what of ours is still unique?\n- Which workflows did we *redesign* (not just accelerate) in the last two quarters? What share of\n  our cost base is inference, and is it rising or falling per successful task?\n\n**Moat audit** [28][29][30]\n- Network effects: does each new user measurably improve the product for the others? Embedding: what\n  would a customer have to rip out to leave — how many integrations, how deep?\n- Data loop: what do we accumulate that a rival with the same model can't buy or scrape in a quarter?\n  Brand: who thinks of us first, in which category, and why?\n- If all four are weak: is the plan speed, price, or a niche too small to interest the labs?\n\n**Function-by-function AI plan** (per function: task, tool, human checkpoint, metric)\n- Product/engineering: what may be vibe-coded; what needs specs + tests + review [20][22].\n- Sales: research and drafting yes, the argument and the close human (`S01-sales.md`). Marketing:\n  production scale is free, distinctiveness is not (`S02-marketing.md`).\n- Support: publish resolution rate; define escalation and the \"I don't know\" path [52][53].\n- Ops/finance: reconciliation, categorisation, drafting; approvals human and logged. Hiring: what did\n  we tool instead of hiring this quarter? (`S03-hiring-and-people.md`)\n\n**Agent safety checklist** [41]\n- Private data? Untrusted content? Outbound channel? (All three: stop and redesign.)\n- Least privilege per tool; read-only agents separated from write agents; human approval on\n  irreversible actions; full tool-call audit log; kill switch; per-run spend cap; test with\n  adversarial content planted in the documents or emails the agent will read.\n\n**Eval readiness** [54][55]\n- A labelled trace set, hand-read by a named person, with a date? Top three failure modes and the\n  pass rate on each? Judges binary, pass rate under 100%? Do evals re-run on every prompt or model\n  change, and what does production do when one fails?\n\n**Unit-economics sheet** [2][32][33]\n- Tokens and cost per *successful task*; gross margin per plan; margin if usage doubles.\n- Model mix by volume and cost; share of calls downgradable without failing evals; cache hit rate;\n  retry rate; the 5% of users generating most of the cost.\n\n**Regulatory one-pager** [44][45][46][48][50]\n- Which jurisdictions do our users sit in? Does Art. 50 disclosure apply to any surface? Are we a\n  deployer or provider of anything classifiable high-risk (Annex III)? Which US state laws apply\n  (CA SB 53 thresholds; CO SB 26-189 from Jan 2027)?\n- What did we train or fine-tune on, and can we prove lawful acquisition? What customer data goes to\n  which provider, under what retention and training terms?"},{"ref":"modern/S07-ai-native-company.md","label":"Building with AI (2026 layer)","heading":"Regional notes","text":"- **US** — loudest adoption numbers, most fragmented law. No federal AI statute; California's SB 53\n  (frontier transparency above 10^26 FLOPs, up to $1M per violation for firms over $500M revenue)\n  took effect 1 Jan 2026, and Colorado repealed its 2024 AI Act for the narrower SB 26-189 (duties\n  from 1 Jan 2027). A Dec 2025 executive order and a DOJ litigation task force push preemption, but\n  as of Aug 2026 no state AI law had been preempted [46][47].\n- **EU** — the Digital Omnibus on AI (Reg (EU) 2026/1744, in force 27 Jul 2026) deferred Annex III\n  high-risk duties to 2 Dec 2027 and Annex I to 2 Aug 2028. Still live for a small company: GPAI\n  obligations (since Aug 2025) if you are a model provider, and Art. 50 transparency from 2 Aug\n  2026. A new EU-level sandbox gives SMEs priority access; national ones are due 2 Aug 2027 [44][45].\n- **UK** — deliberately no AI bill. The government dropped the proposed TDM exception with\n  rights-holder opt-out; its 18 Mar 2026 Report on Copyright and AI changed no law and signalled\n  wait-and-see plus industry licensing [48][49]. Effect: licence, or document provenance.\n- **Africa / Nigeria** — the opportunity is applied AI on thin margins, not frontier models. African\n  startups raised $3.42B in 2025 (+~45%) and $705M in Q1 2026 across 59 deals [57]; Nigeria (50),\n  South Africa (49) and Kenya (31) host ~63% of tracked African AI startups [58], while Nigeria's Q1\n  2026 funding fell 28% YoY to $78.6M [61]; ~40% of African corporates are experimenting with genAI\n  [57]. Design constraints: power and connectivity costs, dollar-denominated inference against local\n  revenue, data residency. Practical rule: price in local currency, meter inference hard, prefer\n  small or open-weight models for routine calls [2][38].\n- **India** — an MSME-scale wave: ~55% of Indian MSMEs are looking at AI adoption, 78% rank\n  operational efficiency top, and government estimates put AI's contribution at up to $1.7T by 2035\n  [60]; PwC India's MSME playbook is the local reference [59].\n- **Middle East / Southeast Asia / LatAm** — not researched in this pass (unverified). Note too that\n  the Ramp, Census, McKinsey and a16z figures above are US-weighted panels; Stanford's AI Index is\n  the closest thing to a global baseline [8][9]."},{"ref":"modern/S07-ai-native-company.md","label":"Building with AI (2026 layer)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- **Calling yourself AI-native because you shipped a chatbot** — investors now apply the remove and\n  model-swap tests [1] — and **buying tools instead of redesigning work**, the behaviour separating\n  the 6% who see EBIT impact from everyone else [6][7].\n- **Building custom internal AI tools that never reach production**; buy the commodity, build only\n  the differentiated slice [11].\n- **Shipping an agent before shipping evals** — without hand-read traces you can't tell a regression\n  from a bad day [54][55] — or **calling a system prompt a control** against the lethal trifecta [41].\n- **Letting AI-written code outrun review and tests** — throughput up, stability down [22][23].\n- **Pricing per seat while paying per token** — how AI-native margins land at 25% instead of 60%\n  [33][34]. **Believing vendor resolution rates** (independent tests run 17-25 points lower) and\n  **over-automating the relationship** — deflection is not resolution, 45% vs 14% [52][53].\n- **Producing workslop:** volume that shifts cost to the reader (~2 hrs, ~$186/worker/month) [26][27].\n- **Assuming the EU delay means nothing applies** — Art. 50 still bites from Aug 2026 [45] — and\n  **treating training data as free**; $1.5B says provenance matters [50][51].\n- **Planning a raise on \"AI adjacency\"** in a market where two companies took 43% of H1 2026 startup\n  funding [42].\n- **Reading tiny-team revenue-per-employee figures as a plan** — Cursor, Lovable and ElevenLabs are\n  outliers in a once-a-decade demand wave [13][14][15][16]; most small firms use no AI at all [4]."},{"ref":"modern/S07-ai-native-company.md","label":"Building with AI (2026 layer)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`AI-native` / `AI-enabled` — AI is the architecture and removing it stops the product / AI enhances a\nproduct whose value predates it; the `remove test` distinguishes them [1].\n`vibe coding` / `agentic engineering` — accepting generated code without reading it (Karpathy, 2025)\n/ running coding agents under specs, tests and review as a managed system [20].\n`Software 3.0` — prompting a model as the program; English as a programming surface [21].\n`agentic workflow` — plan → retrieve → draft → critique → revise, rather than one prompt [56].\n`long-horizon agent` — an agent pursuing a multi-step goal over time with its own tool calls [31].\n`MCP (Model Context Protocol)` — open standard connecting models to tools and data; a Linux\nFoundation project since Dec 2025 [39][40]. `computer use` — an agent driving a GUI directly.\n`lethal trifecta` — private data + untrusted content + outbound channel = exfiltration risk, via\n`prompt injection`: instructions planted in content the agent reads [41].\n`eval` / `LLM-as-judge` / `error analysis` — a repeatable output-quality test against labelled cases\n/ a model scoring another's output, best kept binary / hand-reading production traces and coding\nfailure modes before building any metric [54][55].\n`inference COGS` — variable compute cost of serving a request; 20-23% of AI product cost [33].\n`cost per successful task` — compute divided by tasks that actually passed, not by calls made.\n`model routing` — sending each request to the cheapest model that passes its evals [2][37].\n`open-weight` / `frontier model` — published weights you can self-host (Llama, Qwen, DeepSeek) / top-\ncapability proprietary models, now 45% of paid token share and falling [2][37][38].\n`deflection vs resolution` — conversations kept from a human vs problems actually solved [53].\n`workslop` — AI output that looks like work and moves the real work to the receiver [26][27].\n`GenAI Divide` — MIT NANDA's framing: high adoption, low transformation [11].\n`revenue per employee` — the tiny-team scoreboard; ~$130K median SaaS vs $2-7M at AI leaders [14][16].\n`Annex III high-risk` / `Art. 50 transparency` / `GPAI obligations` — EU AI Act duties starting\n2 Dec 2027 / 2 Aug 2026 / already in force since Aug 2025 [44][45]."},{"ref":"modern/S08-global-lens.md","label":"Country and region (2026 layer)","heading":"What changed since 2018","text":"- **Capital de-globalised, then re-concentrated on AI.** Global venture hit a record $510B in H1 2026\n  [8], but ~50% of 2025 global VC went to AI [10] and Q1 2026 sent ~80% of all venture dollars there\n  [9] — with ~88% of AI capital landing in US-based companies [8]. A non-US, non-AI founder competes\n  for a shrinking slice of a record pot.\n- **Tariffs are a first-order business variable again.** The US average effective tariff rate is ~11%,\n  the highest since 1943, projected ~11.8% by end-2026 [16]; the IMF frames its 2026 outlook (growth\n  3.0%) around trade-policy uncertainty [14][15].\n- **The de minimis era ended.** The US suspended duty-free treatment for sub-$800 parcels from China\n  (2 May 2025) and all countries (29 Aug 2025, EO 14324), permanent from 1 Jul 2027; the EU dropped\n  its €150 threshold on 1 Jul 2026 [21][22]. Parcels under $800 into the US fell 54% [21].\n- **Company formation is being re-papered.** The UK made identity verification mandatory at Companies\n  House on 18 Nov 2025 and doubled the digital incorporation fee to £100 on 1 Feb 2026 [23][24]. The\n  EU proposed \"EU Inc\" — an EU-wide 28th form, online in 48 hours for ≤€100, no minimum capital — on\n  18 March 2026 [25][26][27].\n- **State-run instant rails beat cards in the biggest emerging markets.** UPI did 23.2bn transactions\n  worth ₹29.9tn in May 2026 alone [38]; Pix ran ~80bn transactions worth BRL 35tn in 2025 and is ~29%\n  of Brazilian retail payments [39][40]. Neither existed in the handbook's world."},{"ref":"modern/S08-global-lens.md","label":"Country and region (2026 layer)","heading":"Core ideas (current consensus)","text":"- **Ecosystem rank is a supply map, not a verdict.** Startup Genome's 2026 ranking scores six success\n  factors — Performance, Funding, Market Reach, Talent & Experience, AI-Native Cluster, R&D Engine —\n  with Silicon Valley, New York and London unchanged at 1-2-3 [1][2]. It tells you where talent and\n  capital sit, not whether your company can win.\n- **Different indices measure different things.** StartupBlink ranks 110+ countries and 1,400 cities\n  on quantity, quality and business environment across 40+ parameters [3][4]; Dealroom values\n  ecosystems by the combined enterprise value of their companies (London $715B, 161 unicorns) [6].\n  Always ask what is being counted before quoting a rank.\n- **Domicile is now an explicit, contested decision.** UK Ltd, Delaware C corp, Nevada/Texas, Estonian\n  OÜ and (possibly from 2027) EU Inc are genuinely different products on cost, investor familiarity\n  and disclosure [23][27][32][34].\n- **Diaspora is structural, not sentimental.** ~66% of US billion-dollar companies were founded or\n  co-founded by immigrants or their children, and ~24% had a founder who arrived as an international\n  student [54]; remittances to low- and middle-income countries run ~$690B, Nigeria $19.5B = 35% of\n  sub-Saharan Africa [75].\n- **The funding gap for women did not close.** Female-only teams took 2.3% of $289B of global VC in\n  2024, up from 2.1% [51]; none of the 124 new US unicorns of 2025 had an all-female founding team\n  [53], even as companies with *at least one* female founder took 27.7% of US deal value [52]."},{"ref":"modern/S08-global-lens.md","label":"Country and region (2026 layer)","heading":"Frameworks & playbooks","text":"| Framework | Author / year | What it says | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GSER Success Factors | Startup Genome, GSER 2026 | Score an ecosystem on Performance, Funding, Market Reach, Talent & Experience, AI-Native Cluster, R&D Engine (1-10 each); the 2025 edition drew on 5m startups across 350+ ecosystems [1][2] | Choosing where to base, hire or open a second office |\n| Ecosystem enterprise value | Dealroom, 2025-26 | Value an ecosystem by combined EV, unicorn counts and VC invested (UK raised $23.7B in 2025; Europe $63.8B) [5][6][7] | Sizing the investor pool actually reachable from a city |\n| The Multiplier Effect™ | Endeavor (Linda Rottenberg et al.), 2024-26 | Scaled founders recycle capital, talent and mentorship; ecosystems compound through alumni, not programmes [73][74] | Deciding where to build a network, or who to put on a cap table |\n| B-READY: 3 pillars × 10 topics | World Bank, 2024 / interim 2025 | Regulatory framework, public services, operational efficiency across the firm life cycle in 101 economies; average readiness only ~60%, with the widest gaps between written rules and practice [61] | Country due diligence before entering or incorporating |\n| Domicile ladder | Practitioner convention, evidenced by 2025-26 law changes | Local opco for operations → holdco in an investor-familiar jurisdiction when raising institutional money; price the flip before you need it [23][27][32] | Before a first institutional round with cross-border investors |\n| Local-rail-first checkout | Stripe market guides, 2025-26 | Default to the dominant domestic instant rail (Pix, UPI, mobile money); cards as fallback; recurring billing now supported via Pix Automático [39][40] | Any storefront or SaaS selling into BR / IN / KE / NG |\n| Search fund / ETA | Stanford GSB & IESE, 2024 biennial study | Raise search capital → buy one profitable SME → operate it; international ROI 1.9x, IRR 19.4%, 79% acquisition rate; Spain 67, Mexico 50, UK 35 first-time funds [55][56] | A founder who wants ownership and cash flow without a VC-scale market |\n| Employee Ownership Trust exit | UK (Finance Act 2014; restricted Nov 2025) | Sell control to a trust for employees; CGT relief now 50%, not 100%, from 26 Nov 2025; ~2,500 UK employee-owned businesses by mid-2025 [57][58] | UK founder wanting succession without a trade sale |\n| EOR-first market entry | EOR vendor practice, 2026 | Hire through an employer of record (~$599/employee/month, 100+ countries); incorporate only when headcount or tax presence justifies an entity [36][37] | Testing a market with 1-5 people |\n| Market-creating innovation | Ojomo & Christensen, *The Prosperity Paradox* (2019) | Growth in poor markets comes from making non-consumption consumable; infrastructure follows demand, not the reverse | Building for a low-income or \"no existing market\" segment |"},{"ref":"modern/S08-global-lens.md","label":"Country and region (2026 layer)","heading":"Benchmarks & numbers (2024-2026)","text":"| Metric | Value | Segment / context | Source | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Global venture funding | $510B in H1 2026 (record half-year) | all sectors, global | [8] | 2026 |\n| AI share of global VC | ~50% of 2025 ($202B); ~80% in Q1 2026; ~88% of AI capital to US firms | global | [10][9][8] | 2025-26 |\n| US average effective tariff | ~11.0% (highest since 1943); ~11.8% projected end-2026 | Yale Budget Lab | [16] | 2026 |\n| UK Bank Rate / CPI | 3.75%; CPI 2.6% (Jun 2026), peaking ~3.2% Q4 2026 | Bank of England | [17] | 2026 |\n| US Fed funds target | 3.50-3.75% | Commons Library indicators | [18] | 2026 |\n| Female-only founding teams | 2.3% of $289B global VC (2.1% in 2023); mixed teams 14.1% | global | [51] | 2024 |\n| Ecommerce share of retail | ~20.5% of global retail; >$8tn retail ecommerce | global | [70] | 2026 |\n| Creator economy | 207m+ creators; >50% earn <$15k/yr; 4% earn >$150k | global | [72] | 2026 |\n| Remittances to LMICs / Nigeria | ~$690B; Nigeria $19.5B = 35% of sub-Saharan Africa | World Bank | [75] | 2023-25 |\n| AI usage vs income | +1% GDP per capita → ≈+0.7% AI usage; no country-level catch-up | Anthropic Economic Index | [47][48] | 2026 |"},{"ref":"modern/S08-global-lens.md","label":"Country and region (2026 layer)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **If your buyers and investors are US institutions, hold a US (or UK) holdco; otherwise don't pay\n  for one.** Delaware is no longer automatic — ~62% of US IPO incorporations — but it is still the\n  majority default, and Nevada/Texas carry their own governance trade-offs [32][33].\n- **If you are UK-based, verify identity at Companies House before you need to file.** Existing\n  directors must be verified by 18 Nov 2026 or the confirmation statement becomes a criminal-offence\n  problem for the individual *and* the company [23].\n- **If you are Nigerian and tech, get the NITDA Startup Label early**: CAC-registered LLC, under 10\n  years old, ≥1/3 shareholding held by Nigerian founder(s), digital-innovation objects [28][29].\n- **If your Nigerian company is near the small-company threshold, check *which* threshold** — the\n  Nigeria Tax Act 2025 (₦50m) and the Tax Administration Act (₦100m) disagree, and professional\n  services are excluded from the exemption entirely [30][31].\n- **If you sell physical goods into the US, re-price before you re-market.** Every parcel now needs an\n  HTS code, a formal entry and duty regardless of value [21][22]; model landed cost, not list price.\n- **If you hold revenue in a soft currency, hedge by billing, not by trading.** The naira's forecast\n  2026 range (₦1,350-1,520/$) is wide enough to erase a services margin in a quarter [20].\n- **If you are hiring your first 1-5 people abroad, use an EOR**; incorporate when an entity costs\n  less than ~$599/employee/month or a tax presence is triggered anyway [36][37]."},{"ref":"modern/S08-global-lens.md","label":"Country and region (2026 layer)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. **Name the three geographies separately**: where the customers are, where the company is\n   incorporated, where the people sit. They need not match; conflating them is the commonest error.\n2. **Score the customer geography**: GSER factors for talent and capital [1], B-READY for operating\n   friction [61], and the dominant local payment rail [38][39].\n3. **Check the macro three**: policy rate, inflation and currency regime for both the revenue currency\n   and the cost currency [14][17][19][20].\n4. **Map trade exposure**: does anything cross a customs border? If so, price tariffs and the loss of\n   de minimis into unit economics before pricing [16][21][22].\n5. **Choose the domicile** against investor expectations, disclosure burden, formation cost and time\n   (UK £100 digital [24]; Estonian OÜ via e-Residency [34]; Delaware/Nevada/Texas [32]; EU Inc later\n   [27]), plus any local incentive forfeited by not incorporating locally [28][68].\n6. **Register for the local incentive while you still qualify** — Nigeria's Startup Label [29],\n   India's DPIIT recognition and 80-IAC holiday [68][69], UK SEIS/EIS advance assurance [59][60].\n7. **Wire payments in the local order**: dominant instant rail → wallet/mobile money → cards; add a\n   stablecoin corridor only for cross-border settlement, with a compliant on/off-ramp [39][41].\n8. **Hire through an EOR first**, converting to an entity when entity cost < EOR cost or a permanent\n   establishment is triggered [36][37].\n9. **Build the funding stack region-first**: grants and sovereign programmes → debt or revenue-based\n   finance → equity (in Africa, debt was 41% of all 2025 capital [11]), then re-test annually — every\n   input above moved at least once in the last 18 months."},{"ref":"modern/S08-global-lens.md","label":"Country and region (2026 layer)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Domicile questionnaire**\n- Who writes the first institutional cheque, and what form do they require on a term sheet?\n- What does incorporation cost and take — fees *and* verification time — in each candidate?\n- What is the exit route from this form — trade sale, IPO venue, EOT, ESOP — and is it plausible here?\n- If you flip later, what does that cost in tax and time?\n\n**Country-entry checklist (per market)**\n- Dominant payment rail, settlement time, and whether recurring billing is supported.\n- Cost of accepting money and of getting it out (FX spread, capital controls, remittance cost).\n- Employment: EOR available? Notice periods, 13th month, mandatory benefits?\n- Tax: corporate rate, small-company exemptions, VAT/GST registration threshold, digital-services tax.\n- Customs: is anything physical crossing a border, and at what duty?\n- One named local counsel and one named local accountant before the first invoice.\n- Quarterly review: policy rate, FX range versus your pricing indexation, tariff-schedule changes on\n  any HS code you touch, and filing-rule changes in your domicile."},{"ref":"modern/S08-global-lens.md","label":"Country and region (2026 layer)","heading":"Regional notes","text":"Weighted for a London-based founder with Nigerian and US ties; every figure is a starting point for\nlocal advice, never a substitute.\n\n### United Kingdom\n- **Formation.** A Ltd is cheap and fast, but the rules tightened: identity verification became\n  mandatory at Companies House on 18 Nov 2025, existing directors and PSCs must verify by 18 Nov 2026,\n  and the statutory registers moved to Companies House itself [23]; digital incorporation went from\n  £50 to £100 on 1 Feb 2026 [24].\n- **Funding.** The deepest market in Europe: $23.7B into UK startups in 2025, on a ~$34.2B pace for\n  2026 (+45%), against $63.8B for all of Europe [5][7]. London carries $715B of combined enterprise\n  value and 161 unicorns [6], ranks 3rd globally in GSER [1], and the UK was among the\n  fastest-growing top-20 countries (+26.3%) [4].\n- **Payments.** Cards plus Faster Payments and open-banking pay-by-bank; there is no dominant instant\n  consumer rail of the Pix/UPI kind, so checkout stays card-led.\n- **Hiring.** Protective employment law by US standards (notice, statutory sick pay, pension\n  auto-enrolment); for a first overseas hire, EOR pricing (~$599/mo) is the benchmark to beat [37].\n- **Tax & incentives.** SEIS/EIS are the distinctive advantage: 50% (SEIS) or 30% (EIS) income-tax\n  relief for investors, and from 6 Apr 2026 EIS company limits rise to £10m/year and £24m lifetime\n  with the gross-asset test doubled to £30m [60]. HMRC recorded SEIS at £276m (+14%) and EIS at\n  £1,575m across 3,735 companies in 2024-25 [59].\n- **Exit.** EOTs are a live succession route (~2,500 employee-owned businesses by mid-2025) but CGT\n  relief fell from 100% to 50% on 26 Nov 2025 [57][58]; the UK is also the third-largest search-fund\n  market, with 35 first-time funds [55].\n\n### Nigeria and Africa\n- **Formation.** Register the LLC with the CAC; for tech, add the NITDA Startup Label under the\n  Startup Act 2022 — CAC-registered, under 10 years old, digital-innovation objects, and at least\n  one-third of shares held by Nigerian founder(s) [28][29].\n- **Funding.** Partech counted $4.1B into African tech in 2025 (+25%): equity $2.4B and debt $1.64B —\n  41% of all capital, up from 17% in 2019 [11]. The narrower Africa: The Big Deal count was >$3B, +33%\n  on 2024, fintech ≈40% ($1.37B) [13]. The Big Four — Kenya ($1.04B), South Africa ($715M), Egypt\n  ($604M), Nigeria ($572M) — took >70% of the continent, and Nigeria was the only one to fall year on\n  year [11][12]. Trackers disagree by hundreds of millions: quote the source, not \"the number\".\n- **Payments.** Mobile money and agent networks, not cards: M-Pesa moved KES 41.68tn (~$322B) across\n  46.41bn transactions in the year to March 2026 with 40.99m monthly actives [44], Kenyan\n  mobile-money accounts passed 53m [43], Moniepoint processed ~14bn transactions worth ~$294B in\n  Nigeria [46], and the CBN now ties every PoS agent to one principal institution [45].\n- **Cross-border.** Stablecoins are a mainstream corridor: the IMF published on their Nigerian use in\n  June 2026 [41] and Nigeria accounts for ~60% of sub-Saharan African stablecoin inflows [42] —\n  against ~9% average cost to send $200 to the region conventionally [41].\n- **Macro.** The CBN cut the MPR to 26.5% in February 2026 with inflation at 15.10% and falling for a\n  tenth month [19]; the naira trades near ₦1,430/$ in a forecast 2026 range of ₦1,350-1,520 [20].\n- **Hiring.** Deep, affordable senior engineering talent, but payroll, pensions and PAYE are manual;\n  EOR coverage exists across the major markets [37].\n- **Tax & incentives.** The Nigeria Tax Act 2025 took effect 1 Jan 2026: small companies are exempt\n  from companies income tax, capital gains tax and the development levy — but the threshold differs\n  between the Tax Act (₦50m) and the Tax Administration Act (₦100m), and professional services are\n  excluded [30][31].\n- **Diaspora.** Remittances are macro-scale — Nigeria received $19.5B, 35% of sub-Saharan Africa's\n  total [75]. Diaspora customers, angels and credibility are an under-used distribution asset.\n\n### United States\n- **Formation.** Delaware remains the majority default but is contested: ~62% of US IPO\n  incorporations in 2025, Nevada ~17%, Texas ~4%; of 18 companies proposing to leave, 13 chose\n  Nevada, and SB 21 was Delaware's response [32][33].\n- **Funding.** The deepest pool by far and increasingly AI-only: ~$510B globally in H1 2026 with ~88%\n  of AI capital going to US companies [8][9]. Female-founded companies (≥1 female founder) took 27.7%\n  of US deal value, a record $73.6B — yet none of 2025's 124 new unicorns had an all-female founding\n  team [52][53].\n- **Payments.** Card-dominant with ACH/RTP behind it; the US is the outlier among large markets in\n  *not* having a consumer-default instant rail.\n- **Hiring & tax.** At-will employment with state-by-state payroll and franchise-tax registration;\n  non-US founders normally hire via EOR before opening state entities [36][37].\n- **Trade.** The decisive change for anyone selling in: de minimis is gone (China May 2025, all\n  countries Aug 2025, permanent July 2027) and the effective tariff rate is ~11% [16][21][22].\n- **Immigration.** ~66% of US billion-dollar companies were founded or co-founded by immigrants or\n  their children, and ~24% had a founder who came as an international student [54].\n\n### European Union\n- **Formation.** 27 national regimes today; \"EU Inc\", proposed 18 March 2026 (COM(2026) 321), would\n  add a 28th — fully digital, online in 48 hours for ≤€100, no minimum capital, open to any limited\n  company in a member state, with agreement sought by end-2026 [25][26][27]. Estonia is the practical\n  route now: e-residents formed 5,556 companies in 2025, generating €125m of state revenue [34].\n- **Funding.** $63.8B across Europe in 2025, on an ~$89.1B pace for 2026 [7], the UK ~37% of it [5].\n  Climate tech, long a European strength, fell 19% in H1 2025 as AI absorbed capital [50].\n[section truncated]"},{"ref":"modern/S08-global-lens.md","label":"Country and region (2026 layer)","heading":"AI-era notes","text":"- **AI adoption tracks income, not enthusiasm.** The Anthropic Economic Index finds Claude use per\n  capita strongly correlated with GDP per capita (≈0.7% more usage per 1% higher income), with the\n  gaps stable and no sign of low-use countries catching up; Singapore and Canada are among the most\n  intensive users per capita while Indonesia, India and Nigeria lag [47][48]. Usage skews to\n  augmentation (57%) over automation (43%) [47].\n- **Within-country diffusion is fast; between-country diffusion is not.** Lower-usage US states are\n  converging ~10x faster than historical technology diffusion [48] — do not generalise a fast domestic\n  curve to a cross-border one.\n- **Capital concentration is the sharpest AI-era global fact**: ~80% of Q1 2026 global venture went to\n  AI [9] and ~88% of AI capital to US companies [8]. For a London or Lagos founder, \"we're an AI\n  company\" is not a fundraising strategy; distribution and revenue are.\n- **Where the hype outruns the evidence:** the claim that AI \"flattens\" the global playing field. The\n  measured direction is the opposite — usage, capital and AI-native clusters (now an explicit GSER\n  success factor [1]) are all concentrating. AI *has* genuinely cut the cost of operating across\n  borders (translation, localisation, contract review, multi-jurisdiction research), which is an\n  input-cost effect, not a demand one. Treat it as leverage on an existing advantage, and budget\n  per-market legal review, since AI disclosure and human-review duties differ by jurisdiction."},{"ref":"modern/S08-global-lens.md","label":"Country and region (2026 layer)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- **Ranking tourism** — choosing a city because it moved up a table, without checking that the\n  factor which moved (R&D, AI cluster) is the one you need.\n- **Copying a US cap table into a country whose investors have never seen it.** SAFEs, US-specific tax\n  reliefs and S-corp logic do not travel; local counsel first [30][31][59].\n- **Assuming the DTC arbitrage still works.** The sub-$800 and sub-€150 duty-free windows are closed;\n  models built on them in 2022-2024 are structurally unprofitable now [21][22].\n- **Leaving a local incentive on the table** — Startup Label, DPIIT recognition and SEIS/EIS advance\n  assurance are all age- or size-gated [29][59][68].\n- **Treating an EOR as permanent** past the point an entity is cheaper, or assuming it removes\n  permanent-establishment risk; and **restructuring on a proposal** — EU Inc is not yet law [25][27].\n- **Believing AI erases distance** — diffusion and capital are both concentrating [8][47][48].\n- **Reading one African funding number as *the* number** — trackers differ by hundreds of millions\n  depending on whether debt, exits and undisclosed rounds are counted [11][12][13]."},{"ref":"modern/S08-global-lens.md","label":"Country and region (2026 layer)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`GSER` — Startup Genome's Global Startup Ecosystem Report; the top-40 city ranking on six success factors.\n`B-READY` — the World Bank's successor to Doing Business; scores economies on regulation, public services and operational efficiency.\n`de minimis` — the customs threshold below which imports entered duty-free; suspended in the US (2025), removed in the EU (2026).\n`28th regime / EU Inc` — a proposed EU-wide company form usable alongside the 27 national regimes.\n`DExit` — US companies reincorporating out of Delaware, mainly to Nevada and Texas.\n`Startup Label` — NITDA certification under Nigeria's Startup Act 2022 that unlocks incentives.\n`DPIIT recognition` — India's startup registration that gates the 80-IAC tax holiday and other benefits.\n`UPI` / `Pix` — India's and Brazil's dominant real-time retail payment rails.\n`EOR` — employer of record: a third party that legally employs your staff where you have no entity.\n`EOT` — UK employee ownership trust; a succession route with (now reduced) CGT relief.\n`ETA / search fund` — entrepreneurship through acquisition: raise search capital, buy and run one SME."},{"ref":"modern/S09-legal-and-structure-2026.md","label":"Legal structure (2026 layer)","heading":"What changed since 2018","text":"- **Delaware is still the VC default, but it is no longer unchallenged.** Delaware's share of new IPO\n  incorporations fell from 80-93% over the prior decade to **62% in 2025**, with Nevada ~17% and\n  Texas ~4% [1]. Between Jan 2024 and Mar 2026, **49 Delaware public companies** filed re-domestication\n  proposals; 26 of those in 2025 alone [1].\n- **Delaware fought back with SB 21 (signed March 2025)**, narrowing entire-fairness exposure for\n  conflicted and controller transactions (§144) and curbing books-and-records demands (§220); the\n  Delaware Supreme Court upheld it on **27 February 2026** [1][7]. Texas (SB 29, signed 14 May 2025)\n  and Nevada (AB 239, signed 30 May 2025) answered with codified business-judgment rules, derivative\n  ownership thresholds, exclusive Texas/Nevada venue and **charter-level jury-trial waivers** [4][5][6][7].\n- **QSBS got materially better — and tiered.** The One Big Beautiful Bill Act (enacted **4 July 2025**)\n  created a 50%/75%/100% exclusion at **3/4/5 years**, raised the per-issuer cap from $10M to **$15M**\n  (now inflation-indexed) and the issuer's gross-assets ceiling from $50M to **$75M** — all only for\n  stock **acquired after 4 July 2025** [9][10][11]. This changed the C-corp-vs-LLC calculus more than\n  anything since 2018. Two details that change the arithmetic: the cap is the **greater of $15M or\n  10x the taxpayer's basis** (the 10x alternative survived unchanged), and gain *not* excluded on a\n  3-to-4-year hold is taxed at **28%**, not the usual 20% [11].\n- **The 83(b) election became a real IRS form.** Form 15620 was posted 7 Nov 2024 (Rev. 4-2025), and\n  since **mid-2025 it can be filed online** through an IRS account instead of by certified mail\n  [12][13][14]. The 30-day deadline did not move and still has essentially no exceptions [13].\n- **The Corporate Transparency Act effectively stopped applying to US companies.** FinCEN's interim\n  final rule of **26 March 2025** redefined \"reporting company\" to cover only foreign entities\n  registered to do business in the US; a **final rule on 11 August 2026** made that permanent [15][16].\n- **UK founders now have to prove who they are.** Companies House identity verification became\n  mandatory on **18 November 2025** for directors, LLP members and PSCs under ECCTA 2023 [21][22][64].\n- **UK employee equity got much wider.** Autumn Budget 2025 raised EMI's employee limit to 500, gross\n  assets to £120M and the company option pool to £6M, and extended option life to 15 years, from\n  **6 April 2026** [23][24][25][63].\n- **Europe is building a 29th option.** The Commission proposed the \"28th regime\" / **EU Inc** on\n  **18 March 2026** — an opt-in, digital-by-default EU company form, targeted at online formation in\n  48 hours for ≤ €100 with no minimum capital — with agreement hoped for by end-2026 [32][33][34].\n- **Formation is now a $400-500, two-day commodity in the US**, while Germany still requires a notary and\n  €25,000 nominal capital for a GmbH [17][18][38][39]. The legal work that matters has moved from\n  *forming* the company to *keeping it clean*. AI now drafts documents — and hallucinates in them:\n  court AI-fabrication incidents rose from ~640 in late 2024 to **over 1,590 by mid-2026** [57][58]."},{"ref":"modern/S09-legal-and-structure-2026.md","label":"Legal structure (2026 layer)","heading":"Core ideas (current consensus)","text":"- **Entity choice is a financing decision first, a tax decision second.** Cooley's position is\n  unchanged: Delaware because \"corporate structures are familiar to many investors\" and franchise tax\n  is comparatively low — and if you operate in your home state you will register there anyway, so an\n  exotic domicile buys little [8].\n- **The 2018 rule \"VCs won't hold S-corp stock\" survives; the reason is unchanged.** Institutional\n  funds are partnerships or LLCs and are ineligible S-corp shareholders. The book's \"35 shareholders\"\n  figure is stale — the statutory cap is 100 (families may elect to count as one) (verify with counsel).\n- **\"LLC now, convert later\" is a real but priced option, and QSBS is why.** Conversion costs time and\n  money, and the **QSBS clock (5 years, or the new 3/4-year tiers) only starts when C-corp stock is\n  issued** [9][11] — up to $15M per shareholder per issuer, stackable across founders [9][10][11].\n- **Vesting is not negotiable, even for founders.** Carta's guidance and practitioner consensus is\n  4 years with a 1-year cliff; advisors 2 years; early option pools 10-20%. Investors prefer no\n  acceleration or double-trigger — single-trigger reads as a poison pill to acquirers [59].\n- **The two things that stall a seed close are cap-table errors and missing IP assignments** — both\n  fixable cheaply before you raise, expensive after [61].\n- **A flip is a capital-markets move, not a tax trick — and it is close to one-way.** The Delaware flip\n  makes a non-US company a wholly-owned subsidiary of a new Delaware C-corp so US investors can buy\n  familiar preferred stock [54][56]; **IRC §7874 makes flipping back out very hard** [55]. If customers,\n  revenue and investors are all local, a US holdco buys nothing and costs filings and a tax event [52][56].\n- **Compliance is now identity-based.** Companies House IDV, NDPC/GAID registration in Nigeria and the\n  EU Business Wallet all point one way: the state wants a verified human behind the entity [21][35][45]."},{"ref":"modern/S09-legal-and-structure-2026.md","label":"Legal structure (2026 layer)","heading":"Frameworks & playbooks","text":"| Framework / playbook | Author / source, year | What it says | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Delaware C-corp default stack | Cooley GO Incorporation Package (reviewed Jul 2025) | DE C-corp + bylaws + founder restricted stock purchase agreements + CIIAA (IP assignment) + 83(b) — the documents Series A diligence expects [8][62] | Any venture-track US company |\n| Domicile comparison (DE / NV / TX) | Sullivan & Cromwell (Jun 2025); Alston & Bird (Jan 2026) | Compare litigation forum, jury waiver, derivative thresholds, franchise cost; the three states have converged on defendant-friendly governance [7] | Reincorporation questions, controller-heavy cap tables |\n| QSBS clock planning | Holland & Knight / Mintz / DWT (Jul 2025) | Date-stamp every issuance; pre- and post-4 Jul 2025 stock lives under different regimes; track the $15M per-issuer cap and $75M gross-asset test at issuance [9][10][11] | Incorporating, converting, or issuing new stock |\n| 83(b) 30-day drill | IRS Form 15620; Goodwin (Jul 2025) | On any grant of unvested stock: file within 30 days, online or by mail (never both), give a copy to the company, keep proof [12][13][14] | Every founder and early restricted-stock grant |\n| Founder vesting standard | Carta / practitioner consensus (2025-26) | 4-year vest, 1-year cliff, monthly thereafter; ~70% of employee grants carry a cliff vs ~half of management grants; double-trigger only [59] | Co-founder agreements, first hires |\n| Cap-table hygiene before the raise | YC-ecosystem diligence practice (2025-26) | Clean ledger, every share and SAFE modelled to conversion, all IP assigned, all 83(b)s filed — before Demo Day, not after [61] | 60-90 days before any priced round |\n| Delaware flip | LathamDrive; Wilson Sonsini (UK tax) | New DE C-corp issues mirror-image shares; local company becomes a 100% subsidiary; IP and contracts migrate on agreed terms [53][54] | Raising from US funds, US-centric revenue |\n| Reverse-flip caution | Vircon Legal | §7874 anti-inversion effectively bars unwinding a US holdco; treat the flip as one-way [55] | Before flipping \"just in case\" |\n| SEIS/EIS advance assurance | HMRC; Farrer & Co (Finance Act 2026) | Get HMRC's pre-investment view before the raise; approval is not automatic — 76% SEIS / 72% EIS approved in 2025-26 [26][27] | Any UK pre-seed/seed raise from UK angels |\n| Startup Label (Nigeria) / DPIIT (India) | Nigeria Startup Act 2022, NITDA; Startup India/DPIIT | CAC-registered Ltd ≤ 10 yrs → NITDA label → tax and duty incentives [41][42][44]. Pvt Ltd or LLP ≤ 10 yrs, turnover ≤ ₹200 crore → free recognition, then a *separate* IMB application for §80-IAC [48][49] | Post-registration, before claiming any incentive |\n| Singapore holdco | ACRA/IRAS rules (2026) | Pte Ltd, ≥ 1 resident director, ≥ S$1 capital; SUTE gives 75% / 50% exemption on first two S$100k slices for 3 years — but **pure investment-holding companies are excluded** [50][51] | Asian regional holdco, but not a shell |"},{"ref":"modern/S09-legal-and-structure-2026.md","label":"Legal structure (2026 layer)","heading":"Benchmarks & numbers (2024-2026)","text":"| Metric | Value | Segment / context | Source | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Delaware share of new IPO incorporations | 62% (was 80-93% prior decade) | US public markets | [1] | 2025 |\n| Nevada / Texas share of new IPO incorporations | ~17% / ~4% | same | [1] | 2025 |\n| Delaware re-domestication proposals | 49 total Jan 2024-Mar 2026; 26 in 2025 | US public companies; 13 of 18 2025 proposals chose Nevada | [1][2][3] | 2026 |\n| Coinbase reincorporation to Texas | approved by ~78% of voting power, 15 Dec 2025 | largest single DExit | [1] | 2025 |\n| QSBS per-issuer exclusion cap | $15M (from $10M), inflation-indexed | stock acquired after 4 Jul 2025 | [9][10] | 2025 |\n| QSBS issuer gross-assets ceiling | $75M (from $50M), inflation-indexed | same | [9][10] | 2025 |\n| QSBS tiered exclusion | 50% @ 3 yrs, 75% @ 4 yrs, 100% @ 5 yrs | same; pre-4 Jul 2025 stock keeps the 5-year rule | [9][11] | 2025 |\n| 83(b) filing deadline | 30 days from transfer, no practical exception | US restricted stock | [13] | 2025 |\n| BOI reporting population | US-formed entities exempt; only foreign registrants report | final rule 11 Aug 2026 | [15][16] | 2026 |\n| Stripe Atlas formation | $500 one-time incl. first-year registered agent; ~2 business days; EIN, founder stock, 83(b) filing included | DE C-corp or LLC | [17] | 2026 |\n| Clerky / Firstbase / Delaware ongoing | ~$425 + $99/yr / ~$399 / ~$300 franchise tax + ~$100 agent per year | secondary comparison (verify current rates) | [18] | 2026 |\n| Companies House IDV | mandatory from 18 Nov 2025; free via GOV.UK One Login or via an ACSP | UK directors, LLP members, PSCs | [21][22] | 2025 |\n| EMI limits from 6 Apr 2026 | employees 500 (from 250); gross assets £120M (from £30M); company pool £6M (from £3M); option life 15 yrs (from 10, retrospective); individual limit unchanged at £250k | UK | [23][24][25] | 2026 |\n| EIS company limits from 6 Apr 2026 | annual £10M (from £5M), £20M knowledge-intensive; lifetime £24M, £40M KIC; gross assets £30M pre-investment | UK (verify with counsel) | [27][28] | 2026 |\n| SEIS limits | unchanged: £250k company lifetime; £200k per investor per year; 50% relief | UK | [26][27] | 2026 |\n| EIS/VCT sunset | shares issued before 6 April 2035; SEIS has no sunset | UK | [27] | 2024-26 |\n| Advance assurance approval rate | 76% SEIS / 72% EIS (from 85% / 76% prior year) | HMRC statistics | [26] | 2025-26 |\n| Making Tax Digital for Income Tax | from 6 Apr 2026 at £50k qualifying income; £30k in 2027, £20k in 2028 | UK sole traders and landlords | [29] | 2026 |\n| MTD population in scope at launch | ~864,000 sole traders and landlords | UK | [29] | 2026 |\n| EOT rules from 30 Oct 2024 | clawback window extended to end of the 4th tax year after disposal; trustees must be UK resident as a body; former owners/connected persons < 50% of trustees | UK | [30][31] | 2024 |\n| EU Inc target | online formation in 48 hours, ≤ €100, no minimum capital | proposal of 18 Mar 2026 | [32][33][34] | 2026 |\n| Estonian e-Residency | 5,556 companies founded by e-residents in 2025 (+15%); 13,828 new e-residents (+20%); €125M state revenue | Estonia | [36][37] | 2025 |\n| Estonian OÜ cost | €150 e-Residency fee; €265 online registration; €0.01 minimum capital | Estonia | [36] | 2026 |\n| German GmbH / UG | €25,000 nominal capital (half payable at formation) / €1 for a UG, profits retained until €25,000; notary ~€350-850 incl. tax for standard articles | Germany, notarial deed mandatory | [38][39] | 2026 |\n| French SAS capital | €1 minimum; no notarial deed required for SAS/SARL | France | [40] | 2026 |\n| Nigeria CAC | business name ~₦10,500; Ltd from ~₦60,000 at ₦1M share capital; ₦20,000 per ₦1M of capital; single director/shareholder permitted under CAMA 2020 | Nigeria | [47][65] | 2026 |\n| Nigeria Startup Label | CAC-registered Ltd, ≤ 10 years old; unlocks PSI tax holiday (3 + 2 years), R&D deduction (120% under the Nigeria Tax Act 2025), import duty/VAT relief | Nigeria | [41][43][44] | 2025-26 |\n| NDPC registration trigger | personal data of 200+ individuals in any six months; GAID 2025 replaced the NDPR in Sept 2025, three-tier classification | Nigeria (verify threshold with counsel) | [45] | 2026 |\n| DPIIT recognition | free, 1-3 days; ≤ 10 years (20 deep tech); turnover ≤ ₹200 crore (₹300 crore deep tech); angel tax repealed from FY 2025-26 | India | [48][49] | 2026 |\n| India §80-IAC | 100% profit deduction for any 3 consecutive years in the first 10; separate IMB application, 3-12 months | India | [48][49] | 2026 |\n| Singapore Pte Ltd | ACRA fee S$315 (S$15 name + S$300); ≥ 1 resident director; ≥ S$1 capital; nominee director S$1,800-3,500/yr and individuals barred from acting \"by way of business\" since 9 Jun 2025 (CSP Act 2024) | Singapore | [50] | 2026 |\n| Singapore SUTE | 75% exemption on first S$100k, 50% on next S$100k, first 3 YAs; investment-holding companies excluded | Singapore | [51] | 2026 |\n| India-Singapore treaty LOB | ≥ SGD 200,000 annual operating spend in Singapore to claim benefits | India/Singapore (verify with counsel) | [52] | 2026 |\n| Median founder ownership | ~56% at seed; ~36% at Series A | Carta cap tables, rounds 2021-2025 | [60] | 2026 |\n| Founder vesting norm | 4 years / 1-year cliff; ~70% of employee grants carry a cliff vs ~half of management grants; early pool 10-20%, advisors 0.1-1.0% over 2 years | Carta guidance | [59] | 2025 |\n| Legal-AI hallucination rates | Lexis+ AI 17%, Westlaw AI-AR 33%, GPT-4 43%; >1,590 court incidents by mid-2026 (from ~640 late 2024) | Stanford RegLab study; tracker | [57][58] | 2025-26 |"},{"ref":"modern/S09-legal-and-structure-2026.md","label":"Legal structure (2026 layer)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **If you intend to raise institutional venture capital, incorporate as a Delaware C-corp on day one.**\n  Investor familiarity and standard documents, not tax, are the reason [8][62].\n- **If you are a US public company with a controlling stockholder and heavy litigation exposure,\n  Nevada or Texas is now a genuine alternative** — jury waivers, codified business-judgment rules,\n  derivative thresholds [4][5][6][7]. For a private venture-backed startup it is still a distraction [1][8].\n- **If founder economics matter and you are US-based, the QSBS clock argues for a C-corp now, not later**\n  — post-4 Jul 2025 stock reaches 50% exclusion at three years; an LLC issues no qualifying stock [9][11].\n- **If the business is a cash-generating services firm with no outside equity, an LLC (often with an\n  S election) still wins** — one level of tax plus, since OBBBA made §199A permanent, up to a 20% QBI\n  deduction; but the owner must take a *reasonable salary* before distributions or the IRS recharacterises\n  them [19][20].\n- **If you receive stock subject to vesting, file the 83(b) within 30 days.** File online *or* by mail,\n  never both; send a copy to the company; keep proof [12][13][14].\n- **If you are US-formed, you no longer file BOI** — but check whether any *foreign* entity in your\n  group registered to do business in a US state still does [15][16].\n- **If you are a UK director, LLP member or PSC, verify your identity now** — new appointments require it\n  before incorporation, existing directors at the next confirmation statement [21][22].\n- **If you are a UK company issuing options, re-run EMI eligibility after 6 April 2026** — companies\n  that failed the 250-employee or £30M gross-assets tests may now qualify [23][24][25]. If you are a UK\n  sole trader over £50k of qualifying income, you are in MTD from the same date [29].\n- **If you are selling a UK business to an EOT, budget for a four-year tail**: the CGT relief can be\n  clawed back until the end of the fourth tax year after disposal, and the trustee board must be UK\n  resident with former owners in a minority [30][31].\n- **If you are a Nigerian tech company, do CAC first, then the Startup Label, then NDPC** — the label\n  requires a CAC-registered limited company under 10 years old, and NDPC registration bites once you\n  process personal data at scale [41][44][45].\n- **If you are an Indian startup, DPIIT recognition is free and fast but is not the tax holiday** —\n  §80-IAC needs a separate IMB application that takes months [48][49].\n- **If you use a Singapore holdco purely to hold shares, expect no SUTE and expect substance questions.**\n  Investment-holding companies are excluded from the startup exemption, and treaty benefits need real\n  local spend [51][52].\n- **Do not flip to Delaware until a US investor requires it.** The share transfer can be a taxable event\n  at home, and §7874 makes flipping back out impractical [52][55][56].\n- **Never leave IP in a founder's personal name, a prior employer's scope or an unpaid contractor's\n  hands** — assign everything in writing, including pre-incorporation work [61]."},{"ref":"modern/S09-legal-and-structure-2026.md","label":"Legal structure (2026 layer)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. **Decide the financing path first.** Venture-track, bootstrapped-services, or family business? That\n   answer, not the tax rate, picks the form (Ch 4's point, still true) [8].\n2. **Pick the jurisdiction where your investors live**, not where your tax adviser dreams. US venture →\n   Delaware. UK angels → a UK Ltd, kept SEIS/EIS-clean. EU-only → a national form today, EU Inc if and\n   when it is adopted [8][32].\n3. **Form the entity.** US: Atlas/Clerky/Firstbase, ~$400-500, ~2 days [17][18]. UK: Companies House, but\n   complete identity verification first [21]. Nigeria: CAC [46][47]. India: MCA/SPICe+ then DPIIT [48].\n   Singapore: ACRA Bizfile with a resident director [50].\n4. **Issue founder stock immediately, at a nominal price, subject to vesting** — 4 years, 1-year cliff —\n   and record it on a real cap table, not a spreadsheet you forget to update [59].\n5. **File 83(b) within 30 days** of each unvested issuance; store the confirmation with the stock\n   certificate [12][13].\n6. **Sign IP assignment and confidentiality agreements with every founder, employee and contractor**,\n   scoped (not dated) to cover pre-incorporation work [61].\n7. **Set up the option pool and the plan documents** before the first offer letter promises \"0.5%\".\n   UK: check EMI eligibility under the new 6 Apr 2026 limits and get a valuation agreed [23][24].\n8. **Get the regional stamp that unlocks incentives**: SEIS/EIS advance assurance (UK) [26][27]; Startup\n   Label (Nigeria) [44]; DPIIT then IMB (India) [48][49].\n9. **Register for the compliance regimes that now attach to the entity**: Companies House IDV [21],\n   MTD for Income Tax if you also trade personally [29], NDPC/GAID if you process personal data [45],\n   BOI only if a foreign entity registered in a US state [15].\n10. **Diligence yourself 90 days before any raise** [61], and **revisit the structure at each financing,\n    at 5 years (QSBS) and before any exit** — the moments when the wrong form becomes expensive [9][11]."},{"ref":"modern/S09-legal-and-structure-2026.md","label":"Legal structure (2026 layer)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Entity-choice questions (2026 version of the book's 14)**\n- Are you raising institutional venture capital in the next 24 months, and in which country do your\n  first serious investors sit?\n- Will US founders or US investors want QSBS? When would the clock start?\n- Do you want flow-through losses, or will you reinvest everything?\n- Will you give equity to employees, and under which country's tax-advantaged scheme?\n- Do you have co-founders, and is every one of them on the same vesting terms?\n- Is any IP sitting outside the company today?\n- Do you process personal data at a scale that triggers registration where you operate, or have a\n  foreign entity registered to do business in a US state?\n- Is there an exit shape you already believe in (trade sale, EOT, IPO, none)?\n\n**Founder hygiene checklist (the \"will this round close\" list)**\n- [ ] Entity formed in the jurisdiction investors expect; charter and bylaws on file\n- [ ] Founder stock issued, priced, and vesting (4 yrs / 1-yr cliff), double-trigger if any\n- [ ] 83(b) filed within 30 days, receipt stored (US)\n- [ ] IP assignment + confidentiality signed by every founder, employee and contractor; no\n      prior-employer claim over the core IP; no open-source licence contamination\n- [ ] Cap table in a real system, reconciled to the stock ledger, SAFEs modelled to conversion\n- [ ] Option plan adopted, pool sized, valuation current (409A / EMI valuation)\n- [ ] Board and stockholder consents signed for every issuance\n- [ ] Identity verification complete (UK: Companies House/One Login)\n- [ ] Regional stamp obtained (advance assurance / Startup Label / DPIIT)\n- [ ] Registered agent, franchise tax, annual return and data-protection filings diarised\n\n**Flip decision worksheet** — Who is actually asking for the flip, a term sheet or a blog post? What is\nthe local tax cost of transferring shares to the new parent? Where will IP, employees and customers sit\nafterwards? Can you live with §7874 making it one-way? Would a US *subsidiary* solve the same problem?"},{"ref":"modern/S09-legal-and-structure-2026.md","label":"Legal structure (2026 layer)","heading":"Regional notes","text":"- **US.** Delaware remains the venture default [8], but the domicile market is genuinely competitive for\n  the first time since the handbook was written [1][7]. QSBS is the dominant founder-tax consideration\n  [9][10][11]; the CTA/BOI burden on US-formed entities is gone [15][16]; for non-venture small business\n  the LLC + S election + reasonable salary + §199A stack is the mainstream answer [19][20].\n- **UK.** The Ltd is the default; sole trader is fine until you need limited liability, staff or\n  investors — but MTD for Income Tax now imposes quarterly digital reporting above £50k from 6 Apr 2026\n  [29]. LLPs remain a professional-firm form. SEIS/EIS advance assurance shapes almost every UK seed\n  round and approval rates are falling [26]; EMI just got much wider [23][24][25]; EOTs are a real\n  alternative exit with a tougher four-year tail since Oct 2024 [30][31]; IDV is unavoidable [21][22].\n- **EU.** No single startup form yet. EU Inc (the 28th regime) was proposed 18 Mar 2026 and is targeted\n  at 48-hour, ≤ €100, zero-capital online formation, with agreement hoped for by end-2026 [32][33][34];\n  the European Business Wallet is the complementary identity/document rail, Council position adopted\n  9 Jun 2026 [35]. Until then: Estonia's OÜ via e-Residency is the cheapest EU entry (€150 + €265,\n  €0.01 capital; 5,556 e-resident companies in 2025) [36][37]; France's **SAS** is flexible, €1 capital\n  and needs no notary [40]; Germany's **GmbH** needs €25,000 nominal (half paid in) and a notary, with\n  the **UG** as a €1 bootstrap that must retain profits until it reaches €25,000 [38][39].\n- **Nigeria.** Register a private limited company at CAC under CAMA 2020 — a single director and single\n  shareholder are permitted; fees scale with share capital (₦20,000 per ₦1M) [47][65]. The Nigeria\n  Startup Act 2022 Startup Label (NITDA) unlocks pioneer status, R&D deductions enhanced by the Nigeria\n  Tax Act 2025, and import duty/VAT relief [41][43][44]. NDPA compliance moved to the **GAID 2025**\n  directive (replacing the NDPR in Sept 2025) with tiered NDPC registration [45]. Flipping to Delaware is\n  common among funded Nigerian startups — and worth resisting until a US investor requires it [56].\n- **India.** Pvt Ltd (not OPC, not proprietorship) is the recognised startup form; DPIIT recognition is\n  free and quick, §80-IAC needs a separate IMB approval, and angel tax is gone from FY 2025-26\n  [48][49]. Singapore holdcos are heavily scrutinised under GAAR and the treaty LOB clause [52].\n- **Singapore.** Pte Ltd via ACRA for S$315 with at least one resident director; SUTE gives three years\n  of partial exemption but **not** to investment-holding companies [50][51]. Nominee-director services\n  are now regulated under the CSP Act 2024 (individuals barred from acting by way of business since\n  9 Jun 2025) [50]."},{"ref":"modern/S09-legal-and-structure-2026.md","label":"Legal structure (2026 layer)","heading":"AI-era notes","text":"- **What AI genuinely does well in 2026:** first-draft NDAs, contractor agreements and board consents\n  from a known template; summarising a data room; flagging unassigned IP or missing signatures across\n  a folder; explaining a term sheet clause; translating a foreign filing requirement into a checklist.\n  The formation platforms themselves are the real automation story — Atlas files the entity, gets the\n  EIN, issues founder stock and files the 83(b) for $500 in two days [17].\n- **Where it fails, with evidence.** The Stanford RegLab study found hallucination rates of **17%\n  (Lexis+ AI), 33% (Westlaw AI-Assisted Research) and 43% (GPT-4)** on legal research, including\n  \"sycophancy\" — generating support for a wrong premise rather than correcting it [57]. Tracked\n  AI-fabrication incidents in court passed **1,590 by mid-2026**, up from ~640 in late 2024, with\n  six-figure sanctions and a first indefinite licence suspension in the US [58].\n- **The practical rule:** use AI to *draft and to find questions*, never to *decide or to cite*. Any\n  statute, case, threshold or deadline an assistant gives you — including everything in this file —\n  must be checked against the primary source before you act on it.\n- **Hype to discount:** \"AI replaces your startup lawyer.\" It does not replace the two things that\n  actually cost money — judgement about which structure fits your financing path, and a signature that\n  carries professional liability. RAG-based legal tools reduce but do not eliminate fabrication [57].\n- **A real new risk:** AI-generated code and content raise IP-provenance questions (training data,\n  open-source licence contamination, ownership of purely machine-generated output) that diligence teams\n  now ask about. Keep a record of what was generated under which tool's terms (verify with counsel)."},{"ref":"modern/S09-legal-and-structure-2026.md","label":"Legal structure (2026 layer)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- **Forming an LLC \"for now\" while planning to raise venture money** — you pay for the conversion and\n  you restart the QSBS clock [9][11].\n- **Missing the 83(b) 30-day window.** There is no cure, and the tax cost lands as the stock vests [13].\n- **Assuming the book's S-corp numbers.** The 35-shareholder figure is stale (the cap is 100), and the\n  passive-income and foreign-revenue tests have moved (verify with counsel).\n- **Reincorporating out of Delaware because of headlines.** DExit is real but concentrated in public\n  companies with controllers; for a private startup it fights investors' standard documents [1][8].\n- **Believing the CTA is back on.** US-formed entities are exempt under the Aug 2026 final rule — but a\n  foreign entity registered in a US state still reports [15][16].\n- **Skipping Companies House IDV** and finding the next appointment or confirmation statement rejected [21].\n- **Assuming EMI still fails you** on the old 250-employee / £30M gross-assets tests after 6 Apr 2026 [23][24].\n- **Treating SEIS/EIS advance assurance as a formality** — about a quarter were refused in 2025-26 [26].\n- **Selling to an EOT and spending the proceeds** before the four-year clawback window closes [30][31].\n- **Flipping to Delaware to look serious**, triggering a local tax event and a one-way §7874 door [52][55].\n- **Standing up a Singapore or Delaware holdco with no substance** — treaty benefits and GAAR challenges\n  turn on real spend and real people [52].\n- **Leaving contractor IP unassigned, or the cap table in a spreadsheet** [61].\n- **Citing an AI-generated statute, threshold or case** without opening the primary source [57][58]."},{"ref":"modern/S09-legal-and-structure-2026.md","label":"Legal structure (2026 layer)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`QSBS` — qualified small business stock under IRC §1202; C-corp stock whose gain can be excluded, now 50/75/100% at 3/4/5 years up to $15M per issuer.\n`OBBBA` — the One Big Beautiful Bill Act, enacted 4 July 2025; rewrote §1202 and made §199A permanent.\n`Form 15620` — the IRS's official form for a §83(b) election, filable online since 2025.\n`BOI report` — beneficial ownership information filed with FinCEN under the Corporate Transparency Act; now required only of foreign registrants.\n`SB 21 / DExit` — Delaware's March 2025 DGCL amendments, and the 2024-26 movement of US companies reincorporating to Nevada or Texas.\n`ECCTA identity verification` — the UK requirement (from 18 Nov 2025) that directors, LLP members and PSCs prove their identity.\n`EMI` — Enterprise Management Incentives, the UK's tax-advantaged share option scheme; limits widened from 6 Apr 2026.\n`SEIS / EIS advance assurance` — HMRC's pre-investment indication that a UK company's shares should qualify for investor relief.\n`EOT` — Employee Ownership Trust; a UK exit route giving the seller CGT relief, with a four-year clawback tail.\n`MTD for Income Tax` — HMRC's quarterly digital reporting regime for sole traders and landlords, from 6 Apr 2026 above £50k.\n`28th regime / EU Inc` — the proposed opt-in, EU-wide, digital-by-default company form (proposed 18 Mar 2026).\n`OÜ / UG / SAS` — the Estonian private limited company (€265 online, €0.01 capital); the German €1 \"mini-GmbH\" that retains profits until it reaches €25,000; France's flexible €1-capital SAS.\n`Startup Label` — NITDA's certificate under the Nigeria Startup Act 2022 that unlocks tax and duty incentives.\n`GAID 2025` — Nigeria's General Application and Implementation Directive, the operative data-protection instrument since Sept 2025.\n`DPIIT recognition` — India's free startup certification; a precondition for, but not the same as, the §80-IAC tax holiday.\n`SUTE` — Singapore's Start-Up Tax Exemption; three years of partial exemption, not available to investment-holding companies.\n`Delaware flip / §7874` — reorganising a non-US company under a new Delaware C-corp parent; the US anti-inversion rule that makes reversing it impractical.\n`Double-trigger acceleration` — vesting accelerates only on both a change of control and a qualifying termination.\n`CIIAA` — confidential information and invention assignment agreement; the document that puts IP in the company."},{"ref":"modern/S10-launch-method-2026.md","label":"Product-market fit and strategy (2026 layer)","heading":"What changed since 2018","text":"- **PMF stopped being binary.** First Round (Todd Jackson, Apr 2024) publishes four *levels* —\n  Nascent, Developing, Strong, Extreme — each with its own ARR band, retention, margin and burn\n  thresholds; extreme PMF takes 2-6 years, and PMF can be lost again [1].\n- **The 40% survey became the industry default *and* acquired critics.** Rahul Vohra's Superhuman\n  engine (First Round Review, 2018-19) turned Sean Ellis's single question into a quarterly\n  operating loop [4]; practitioners now warn the 40% line was never published with vertical or\n  geography and is noise below ~100 responses [7].\n- **Discovery became a weekly habit, not a project phase.** Teresa Torres's *Continuous Discovery\n  Habits* (2021) — one customer interview a week by the product trio, mapped onto an\n  opportunity-solution tree — displaced the one-off \"customer development\" sprint [9].\n- **Building an MVP got ~free; learning did not.** Lovable, Bolt, v0 and Replit Agent compress a\n  prototype to hours, but audits find 45% of AI-generated code carries an OWASP Top 10 flaw and\n  ~70% of sampled Lovable apps shipped with row-level security off [35][36].\n- **Pricing moved off the seat.** In Kyle Poyar's 2026 survey of 230 B2B software/AI companies,\n  hybrid pricing (subscription + consumption) rose from 25% to 37% in twelve months and 29% now\n  sell AI credits [16].\n- **Strategy shifted from position to power, and from goals to problems.** Helmer's *7 Powers*\n  (2016), Rumelt's *The Crux* (2022) and McGrath's transient advantage now sit on top of the\n  Porter material in chapter 3 [29][30][33]; NFX attributes ~70% of tech value created since 1994\n  to network effects and catalogues 16 types [23][24].\n- **\"Why now\" became the highest-weighted slide.** Sequoia's template always had it; in 2024-26\n  practice a \"why now\" that fails to name a specific recent enabler is treated as a red flag [52].\n- **Capital now demands evidence of fit before seed.** Africa's H1 2026 funding rose 73% to $3.3bn\n  while deal count fell ~10% — fewer, more-validated companies [41]; Southeast Asia's 2025 total\n  fell 33.9% to $5.37bn across 461 deals [45][46]."},{"ref":"modern/S10-launch-method-2026.md","label":"Product-market fit and strategy (2026 layer)","heading":"Core ideas (current consensus)","text":"- **PMF is a dynamic equilibrium across three dimensions** — satisfaction, demand, efficiency —\n  and you move between levels by adjusting the 4Ps: persona, problem, promise, product\n  (First Round / Todd Jackson, 2024) [1].\n- **Level 1 is about satisfaction only.** Churn, gross margin and burn multiple are irrelevant at\n  3-5 customers; the only question is whether you have moved out of the \"friend zone\" into genuine\n  need (First Round, 2024) [1].\n- **Make PMF a number and a quarterly OKR.** Vohra (Superhuman, 2018-19): segment to the users who\n  would be \"very disappointed\", write a high-expectation-customer profile, ignore the\n  not-disappointed, and split the roadmap ~50/50 between deepening love and removing blockers [4].\n- **Retention that flattens is the real fit signal**; the survey is a leading indicator of it\n  (Rachitsky, 2020; Winters) [5].\n- **Talk about their life, not your idea** — Fitzpatrick, *The Mom Test* (2013): specific past\n  behaviour, never hypothetical future purchases. Moesta (*Demand-Side Sales 101*, 2020) adds the\n  instrument: reconstruct the switch on a timeline and read the four forces — push, pull, habit,\n  anxiety — instead of pitching features [10].\n- **Look for a well, not a puddle.** Paul Graham (2012): the best ideas are organic (you wanted\n  it), you can build them, and few others see the value; a small group that desperately needs the\n  thing beats a large group that mildly wants it [11]. His companion rule (2013): recruit users\n  manually and over-serve them — the unscalable phase is a stage, not a failure [12][13].\n- **Monopoly, not competition.** Peter Thiel, *Zero to One* (2014): dominate a small market first,\n  then expand concentrically; \"what important truth do few people agree with you on\" is the\n  idea-generating question.\n- **Design the product around the price.** Ramanujam & Tacke, *Monetizing Innovation* (2016):\n  have the willingness-to-pay conversation *before* you build; 72% of innovations miss their\n  financial targets, mostly for pricing reasons, not product reasons [17][18].\n- **Power beats position.** Helmer (2016): a business is worth owning only if it holds one of seven\n  powers (scale economies, network economies, counter-positioning, switching costs, branding,\n  cornered resource, process power); Porter sizes the opportunity, Helmer says whether you can keep\n  the value [33].\n- **Strategy is a diagnosis of the crux, not a list of goals.** Rumelt (2011, 2022): find the one\n  addressable challenge that unlocks the most progress and concentrate action on it [29]. And\n  assume it expires — McGrath (2013 onward): hold a portfolio of arenas and disengage from eroding\n  advantages early rather than defending them [30][31][32].\n- **AI has made the functional layer copyable.** 2026 commentary converges on workflow depth,\n  proprietary data and distribution as what survives commoditisation; inference prices fell >280x\n  between Nov 2022 and Oct 2024, so \"clever model call\" is not a business [53][54][55]."},{"ref":"modern/S10-launch-method-2026.md","label":"Product-market fit and strategy (2026 layer)","heading":"Frameworks & playbooks","text":"| Framework | Author / year | What it says | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Levels of PMF (4 levels, 3 dimensions, 4Ps) | Todd Jackson / First Round Capital, Apr 2024 | Nascent → Developing → Strong → Extreme, each with thresholds; score satisfaction, demand, efficiency; move levels by changing persona, problem, promise or product [1][2] | Diagnosing where you actually are and what to ignore at this level |\n| The Sean Ellis test | Sean Ellis, ~2009 (classic) | \"How would you feel if you could no longer use this product?\" ≥40% \"very disappointed\" ≈ PMF, benchmarked on ~100 startups [4][6] | A quick, repeatable fit reading once you have real users |\n| The PMF Engine | Rahul Vohra / Superhuman, 2018-19 | Segment to supporters → build the HXC profile → analyse the \"somewhat disappointed\" who value the main benefit → 50/50 roadmap → re-run quarterly as the top OKR [4] | You scored below 40% and need a route upward, not a verdict |\n| Continuous Discovery Habits + opportunity-solution tree | Teresa Torres, 2021 | Weekly customer touchpoints by the product trio; map outcome → opportunities → solutions → assumption tests; test assumptions before building [8][9] | Turning discovery into a cadence the team sustains |\n| The Mom Test | Rob Fitzpatrick, 2013 (classic) | Past behaviour, specifics, commitments; never ask if they like your idea | Every interview in stages 1-3 |\n| Jobs to Be Done / Four Forces / Switch interview | Clayton Christensen (2016 *Competing Against Luck*); Bob Moesta, 2020 | Push of the situation, pull of the new, habit of the present, anxiety of the new; timeline-reconstruct the moment of switch [10] | Understanding why people actually bought or didn't |\n| Do things that don't scale | Paul Graham / YC, 2013 | Recruit manually, delight insanely, do the work by hand; startups take off because founders push [12][13] | First 10-100 users; concierge-style delivery |\n| How to get startup ideas / Zero to One | Paul Graham, Nov 2012; Peter Thiel, 2014 | Notice, don't brainstorm; live in the future; organic > made-up; prefer a well to a puddle; drop the schlep and unsexy filters [11]. Thiel adds: secrets, monopoly, dominate a small market first, and the seven questions | Idea generation and screening (extends Timmons in ch 2) |\n| Smoke test / fake door | Practitioner canon; current guidance 2025-26 | Landing page or in-product tile measures intent before build; a smoke test validates a concept, a fake door validates one feature; disclose after the click and offer a waitlist [50][51] | Cheapest demand signal; pre-build |\n| Concierge & Wizard of Oz MVP | Lean-startup canon; CRV MVP guide 2025 | Deliver the service manually (concierge) or fake the automation behind a real interface (Wizard of Oz) to test value before engineering it [50] | Service-shaped or AI-shaped products |\n| 7 Powers | Hamilton Helmer, 2016 | Seven durable powers; each needs a benefit *and* a barrier; scale economies and switching costs are the most common [33] | Deciding whether a win is keepable (layer onto Porter, ch 3) |\n| Playing to Win — strategy choice cascade | A.G. Lafley & Roger Martin, 2013; essays 2024-25 | Winning aspiration → where to play → how to win → capabilities → management systems; where-to-play and how-to-win are inseparable [27][28] | Writing a one-page strategy a team can act on |\n| Good Strategy/Bad Strategy kernel + The Crux | Richard Rumelt, 2011 / 2022 | Kernel = diagnosis, guiding policy, coherent action; the crux is the surmountable challenge with the biggest payoff; run a \"strategy foundry\" [29] | When the plan is a list of goals rather than a strategy |\n| Transient advantage / arenas | Rita McGrath, 2013-2025 | Compete in arenas not industries; launch, ramp, exploit, reconfigure, disengage; watch for inflection points [30][31][32] | Fast-moving or AI-exposed categories |\n| Cold Start Problem / atomic network | Andrew Chen (a16z), 2021 | Build the smallest self-sustaining network, solve for the hard side first, then copy the network city-by-city or team-by-team [25][26] | Marketplaces, social, multi-sided models |\n| Network Effects Manual (16 types) | NFX, 2019-2026 | Direct, 2-sided, data, tech-performance and social network effects, ranked by strength; network effects ≈70% of tech value since 1994 [23][24] | Choosing which network effect you are actually building |\n| Monetizing Innovation (9 rules) | Madhavan Ramanujam & Georg Tacke, 2016 | WTP conversation first; segment by value not demographics; choose model, then packaging, then price; build the business case on price [17][18] | Before the first line of code and at every packaging change |\n| Van Westendorp Price Sensitivity Meter | Peter van Westendorp, 1976 (classic) | Four price questions → acceptable range; cheap and fast, but stated-preference, single-product, no competitive context [19][20] | Early range-finding only; pair with Gabor-Granger, conjoint or a live A/B test |\n| Hybrid pricing / AI credits | Kyle Poyar, Growth Unhinged, 2026 | Subscription base + consumption (usually credits) is now the dominant transition state; sell the work, not the seat [16] | Any product where AI does variable-cost work |\n| Sequoia \"why now\" | Sequoia Capital business-plan template (popularised ~2015) | Name the specific technological, regulatory or behavioural change that makes this the moment; vague macro trends are penalised [52] | Pitch decks and go/no-go on timing |"},{"ref":"modern/S10-launch-method-2026.md","label":"Product-market fit and strategy (2026 layer)","heading":"Benchmarks & numbers (2024-2026)","text":"| Metric | Value | Segment / context | Source | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Level 1 (Nascent) | $0-500K ARR, 3-5 customers, <10 staff, 1 in 10-20 warm intros converts | B2B SaaS | [1] | 2024 |\n| Level 2 (Developing) | $500K-5M ARR, 5-25 customers, NRR ≥100%, magic number 0.5-0.75, GM ≥50%, burn multiple ≤5x, first-call-to-close ~10%, regretted churn 10-20% | B2B SaaS | [1] | 2024 |\n| Level 3 (Strong) | $5-25M ARR, NRR >110%, magic >0.75, CAC payback <18 mo, GM 60-70%+, burn multiple 1-3x, word of mouth >10% of inbound | B2B SaaS | [1] | 2024 |\n| Level 4 (Extreme) | $25M+ ARR, NRR >120%, GM 80%+, burn multiple 0-1x, CAC payback <12 mo; 2-6 years to reach, 12-18 months spent at Level 1 | B2B SaaS | [1][3] | 2024 |\n| Sean Ellis threshold | ≥40% \"very disappointed\"; benchmarked on ~100 startups | cross-sector | [4][6] | classic |\n| Superhuman's own trajectory | 22% → 33% (one quarter) → 47% | single company | [4] | 2017-19 |\n| Survey reliability floor | below ~100 responses the 40% line is too noisy | practitioner guidance (secondary) | [7] | 2026 |\n| User retention, month 6 — good/great | consumer social 25/45%; consumer transactional 30/50%; consumer SaaS 40/70%; SMB-MM SaaS 60/80%; enterprise SaaS 70/90% | 20 growth practitioners + public data | [5] | 2020 |\n| NRR, month 12 — good/great | consumer SaaS 55/80%; bottom-up SaaS 100/120%; SMB-MM land-and-expand 90/110%; enterprise 110/130% | same study | [5] | 2020 |\n| Consumer AI retention | D30 >30% for top apps (Sora sub-8%); ChatGPT DAU/MAU 36%, month-12 desktop 50%, paid month-12 68% (Gemini 21/25/57%); only 9% pay for two AI subscriptions | app analytics + consumer panel | [39] | 2025 |\n| B2B SaaS churn | 3.5% avg (2.6% voluntary, 0.8% involuntary); fixing involuntary lifts revenue 8.6% in yr 1; 52% of consumers cancelled a subscription for non-use | 76M subscribers, 2,200 merchants | [40] | 2025-26 |\n| Pricing model mix | hybrid 37% (from 25% YoY); 29% offer multiple models (from 21%); per-seat 29% among >$150M ARR. Secondary aggregate: seat-based 21%→15%, Gartner sees ≥40% of enterprise SaaS spend usage/agent/outcome by 2030 | 230 B2B software/AI cos, Apr-May 2026 | [16][22] | 2026 |\n| AI credits | 29% use them; 33% plan to within 6-12 months; ~50% of >$50M ARR planning; +126% adoption in 2025 | same survey | [16] | 2026 |\n| Investor-preferred AI model / margin target | hybrid 35%, outcome 26%, usage 24%, flat 10%, per-seat 5%; median AI gross-margin target 50%, only 12% aim at 80%+ | same survey | [16] | 2026 |\n| Innovations missing financial targets | 72% | Simon-Kucher research behind *Monetizing Innovation* | [17][18] | 2016 |\n| Why startups fail | lack of market need 42% (postmortems); of 431 shutdowns since 2023: poor PMF 43%, bad timing 29%, unit economics 19%, ran out of cash 70% (proximate) | CB Insights; second figure via secondary summary | [14] | 2021-2026 |\n| Network effects share of tech value | ~70% of value created since 1994; 16 catalogued types | NFX multi-year study | [23][24] | 2024-26 |\n| AI-generated code defects | 45% of samples introduce an OWASP Top 10 vulnerability | 100+ LLMs tested (Veracode) | [35][36] | 2025-26 |\n| Vibe-coded app exposure | ~70% of 1,645 sampled Lovable apps had RLS disabled; 2,000+ vulns, 400 exposed secrets, 175 PII leaks across 5,600 apps. Inference cost fell >280x (Nov 22→Oct 24), so thin wrappers trade at 3-8x revenue vs 25-40x for data+IP | Escape.tech / Tenzai scans; practitioner analysis | [35][53][54] | 2026 |\n| AI coding productivity | experienced OSS devs were **19% slower** with AI; they predicted 24% faster and believed 20% faster | RCT, 16 devs, 246 real tasks | [34] | 2025 |\n| Africa funding | $3.3bn in H1 2026, +73% YoY, deal count −10%; Nigeria $214M equity (ahead of Egypt's $183M), $254M incl. debt | ecosystem trackers | [41][42] | 2026 |\n| Southeast Asia / India | SEA 2025: $5.37bn across 461 deals, −33.9% YoY; India early-stage valuations 30-50% below mature markets | ecosystem trackers | [45][46][47] | 2025-26 |\n| Creator economy | $313.7bn (2025) → $387.8bn (2026), ~23.6% CAGR; Substack >5M paid subscriptions and >$500M processed; Patreon >8M paying members | market research + platform disclosures | [48][49] | 2025-26 |\n| Nigeria WhatsApp economics | per-message charging from 1 Oct 2026; ~$0.0101 (≈₦14) per chargeable utility message | Meta pricing change coverage | [43][44] | 2026 |"},{"ref":"modern/S10-launch-method-2026.md","label":"Product-market fit and strategy (2026 layer)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **If you have fewer than ~100 real users, do not run the 40% survey** — read retention curves\n  and interviews instead; below that the score is noise [7]. And if you score under 40%, do not\n  conclude \"no PMF\": segment to the sub-population that *is* very disappointed and reposition [4].\n- **If you are at Level 1, ignore churn, gross margin and burn multiple.** The only metric is\n  whether 3-5 customers genuinely need you; optimise satisfaction, not efficiency (First Round) [1].\n- **If retention has not flattened, do not spend on acquisition** — paid growth leaks out the\n  bottom [5]; and if NRR is under 100% at $500K-5M ARR you are below Level 2 and should not be\n  raising on a PMF story [1]. If a customer will not pay, commit time or refer a colleague, you\n  have interest, not demand (Fitzpatrick, 2013).\n- **If the \"why now\" answer is a macro trend (\"AI is big\"), rewrite it.** Name a specific\n  technological, regulatory or behavioural change dated in the last 24 months [52].\n- **If you cannot name which of Helmer's seven powers you are building, you have a product, not a\n  business** [33]. If your advantage is a prompt plus an API call, assume replication within a\n  quarter and stack two moats — workflow depth, data, compliance or distribution [53][54].\n- **If you are building a network business, get one atomic network self-sustaining before opening\n  the second** (Chen, 2021) [25][26].\n- **Price before you build.** Have the willingness-to-pay conversation in discovery; if nobody\n  states a number you can live with, change the product, not the pitch [17][18]. Use van Westendorp\n  for a *range*, never a price — stated preference overstates willingness to pay [19][20].\n- **If AI does variable-cost work in your product, price hybrid** (base + credits) rather than\n  per-seat; per-seat is the model most associated with an expansion-revenue problem, and AI gross\n  margin below ~50% is a pricing bug, not a cost of doing business [16].\n- **If you ship an AI-built MVP to real users, run a security pass first** — RLS/row policies,\n  secrets out of client code, auth flows, webhook idempotency [35][36].\n- **Do not scale headcount or spend before Level 2.** Premature scaling is Startup Genome's\n  headline failure mode for high-growth startups [15]."},{"ref":"modern/S10-launch-method-2026.md","label":"Product-market fit and strategy (2026 layer)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. **Generate organically.** List problems you have personally hit in the last 12 months, plus the\n   schleps others avoid. Score each against Timmons (ch 2) and Graham's well-vs-puddle test [11].\n2. **Write the \"why now\".** One paragraph naming the specific enabler (technology, regulation,\n   price curve, behaviour) dated within ~24 months. If you cannot, park the idea [52].\n3. **Pick a beachhead you could dominate.** Smallest market where you could plausibly be the\n   obvious choice (Thiel, 2014); make it a persona, not a vertical.\n4. **Interview before building.** 15-25 switch interviews; past behaviour only; capture push, pull,\n   habit and anxiety; record their words for the problem [10]. Map the results on an\n   opportunity-solution tree: one outcome → opportunities → solutions → assumption tests [8][9].\n5. **Have the money conversation early.** Willingness to pay per bundle; ask what they pay today\n   for the workaround (Ramanujam) [17][18].\n6. **Run the cheapest test that can kill the idea.** Smoke test or fake door for demand; concierge\n   or Wizard-of-Oz for value; pre-order or paid pilot for commitment. Disclose after the click and\n   offer a waitlist [50][51].\n7. **Deliver manually to the first 10.** Over-serve; do the work by hand; instrument every step\n   (Graham, 2013) [12][13].\n8. **Instrument retention from day one.** Cohort curves by week/month; watch for the flattening\n   point, not the absolute number [5].\n9. **Run the PMF survey at ~100+ users**, quarterly, segmented; set the score as a company OKR and\n   split the roadmap 50/50 (Vohra) [4].\n10. **Locate your level.** Score satisfaction, demand and efficiency against First Round's\n    thresholds; fix only what that level says matters [1].\n11. **Write the strategy on one page.** Rumelt's kernel (diagnosis / guiding policy / coherent\n    action) or Martin's cascade; name the crux, then name which of the 7 powers holds the value and\n    what the barrier is [27][28][29][33].\n12. **Choose the model and the meter** (subscription / usage / hybrid / outcome), the unit you\n    meter, the tiers and the expansion path — then re-test the whole loop quarterly, because PMF is\n    an equilibrium, not a trophy [16][22][1][30]."},{"ref":"modern/S10-launch-method-2026.md","label":"Product-market fit and strategy (2026 layer)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Idea screen (extends Timmons, ch 2)**\n- What did I personally struggle with in the last year that others also struggle with?\n- Is demand a well (few people, desperate) or a puddle (many, mildly)? Which schlep or unsexy\n  filter is keeping competitors out? [11]\n- What important truth about this market do few people agree with me on? (Thiel, 2014)\n- What changed in the last 24 months that makes this possible now? [52]\n\n**Switch interview script (Moesta)** [10]\n- Walk me back to when you first realised the old way wasn't working. What happened that day?\n- What did you try first? What did you search for? Who else was involved?\n- What almost stopped you? What did you give up, and what did you keep doing anyway?\n- What would have to be true for you to go back?\n\n**Demand-evidence ladder (weakest → strongest)**\n1. Said they liked it → 2. Gave an email → 3. Booked a call → 4. Spent real time in a pilot →\n5. Introduced a colleague → 6. Paid → 7. Renewed / expanded. Only 6-7 count as demand.\n\n**PMF survey block**\n- How would you feel if you could no longer use [product]? (very disappointed / somewhat / not)\n- What is the main benefit you get? (verbatim) What type of person would benefit most?\n- How can we improve it? — then: % very disappointed by persona and use case; target ≥40% within\n  your best segment before broadening [4][6].\n\n**Level check (First Round)** [1] — ARR band, customer count, NRR, regretted churn, gross margin,\nburn multiple, magic number, first-call-to-close, % inbound from word of mouth → which level, and\nwhat this level tells me to *ignore*.\n\n**Strategy one-pager**\n- Diagnosis / crux (the one surmountable challenge) / guiding policy / 3-5 coherent actions [29].\n- Where to play and how to win — are they inseparable? [27][28] Which of the 7 powers, and what is\n  the barrier that stops a competitor copying it? [33]\n\n**Pricing worksheet**\n- What do they pay today for the workaround? What grows as they get more value (value metric)?\n- Four van Westendorp prices → acceptable range → then a live test [19].\n- Model: flat / per-seat / usage / hybrid / outcome — where does expansion revenue come from? If\n  AI does the work: cost per unit, target gross margin ≥50%, credit-pack sizing [16]."},{"ref":"modern/S10-launch-method-2026.md","label":"Product-market fit and strategy (2026 layer)","heading":"Regional notes","text":"- **US** — the benchmark set above (First Round levels, Poyar's pricing survey, a16z consumer\n  retention) is US-heavy; treat the thresholds as US B2B SaaS norms, not universal [1][16][39].\n- **UK / EU** — the same discovery and PMF instruments apply, but consumer testing must respect\n  GDPR consent for interview recordings and behavioural tracking; fake-door tests that collect\n  emails need a lawful basis and a clear disclosure after the click [51]. EU B2B buyers'\n  procurement adds cycle time, which distorts early conversion benchmarks.\n- **Africa / Nigeria** — capital is concentrating: H1 2026 raised $3.3bn (+73%) on ~10% fewer\n  deals, so investors ask for evidence of fit earlier; Nigeria led equity funding with $214M\n  in H1 2026 [41][42]. Distribution is WhatsApp-first: it is storefront, catalogue and support\n  desk in one, and from 1 Oct 2026 Meta charges per delivered message (~$0.0101 / ≈₦14 for a\n  chargeable utility message), so unit economics now carry a messaging line item [43][44]. Launch\n  method: manual WhatsApp onboarding, spreadsheets and short paid pilots before software; localise\n  price points, payment rails and language (Pidgin/Hausa/Yoruba/Igbo for promos, English for OTPs\n  and receipts); validate per-city, not per-country [43].\n- **India** — early-stage valuations run 30-50% below mature markets, and tier-II/III city\n  demand is a distinct segment from metro demand; validate willingness to pay in the tier you\n  intend to serve, not the tier you live in [47].\n- **Southeast Asia** — funding fell 33.9% in 2025 to $5.37bn across 461 deals, the lowest in six\n  years; assume a longer pre-seed runway, and treat SEA as six atomic networks, not one [45][46].\n- **Middle East / LatAm** — not separately sourced; the 2025-26 pattern (fewer deals, larger\n  cheques, PMF evidence demanded pre-seed) is reported across emerging ecosystems [41][46]."},{"ref":"modern/S10-launch-method-2026.md","label":"Product-market fit and strategy (2026 layer)","heading":"AI-era notes","text":"- **Building is cheap; the discovery bottleneck moved.** Prompt-to-app tools (Lovable, Bolt, v0,\n  Replit Agent) can produce a working prototype in hours, which makes the smoke-test/concierge\n  sequence *cheaper*, not obsolete — the constraint is now customer access, not engineering [36][35].\n- **The documented pitfalls are real and specific.** 45% of AI-generated code samples introduce an\n  OWASP Top 10 vulnerability; ~70% of 1,645 sampled Lovable apps had row-level security disabled;\n  a scan of 5,600 vibe-coded apps found 2,000+ vulnerabilities, 400 exposed secrets and 175 PII\n  leaks. AI tools ship happy-path code — unhandled API errors, missing retries, non-idempotent\n  webhooks, half-built auth [35][36]. Ship the prototype to *design partners*, not the public,\n  until it has had a security pass.\n- **Speed claims outrun the evidence.** METR's randomised trial found experienced open-source\n  developers were 19% *slower* with early-2025 AI tools while believing they were 20% faster — a\n  strong caution against planning your launch schedule on perceived AI speed-up [34].\n- **Synthetic users are a rehearsal, not a substitute.** 2025 studies find LLM personas produce\n  plausible narratives but wrong magnitudes, poor minority representation and no contextual\n  friction; use them to sharpen the guide and pre-test surveys [37][38]. Where AI does help is\n  the analysis layer — transcribing, tagging and clustering interviews, drafting the\n  opportunity-solution tree, holding the weekly cadence; the interview itself stays human [8].\n- **Monetisation has been rebuilt around AI work.** Hybrid pricing is at 37% and rising, 29% sell\n  AI credits, and investors prefer hybrid (35%) and outcome-based (26%) over per-seat (5%);\n  outcome pricing brings genuine accounting complexity (when is revenue recognised if you are paid\n  per resolved ticket?) that Deloitte now publishes guidance on [16][21].\n- **PMF is harder to *hold*.** Inference costs fell >280x in two years, so feature-level advantage\n  commoditises fast; the surviving moats are workflow depth, data flywheels, compliance and\n  distribution, stacked at least two deep [53][54][55]. Consumer AI shows the novelty gap: top apps\n  clear 30% D30, Sora sat under 8%, and the market is winner-concentrated [39].\n- **Hype watch:** \"AI found our PMF\", \"we replaced user research with synthetic personas\" and\n  \"we shipped in a weekend\" are the three claims the 2025-26 evidence does not support [34][37][35]."},{"ref":"modern/S10-launch-method-2026.md","label":"Product-market fit and strategy (2026 layer)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- **Treating PMF as a binary switch** and declaring victory at the first good quarter; it is an\n  equilibrium that competitors, markets and model releases can break [1]. Its twin is premature\n  scaling — hiring or buying growth before Level 2, Startup Genome's signature failure [15].\n- **Running the 40% survey on too few, or too broad, a sample** — then averaging away the one\n  segment that loves you [7][4]. Related: reading NRR as health while gross churn erodes the base [1].\n- **Fake doors without disclosure** — implying availability you cannot deliver burns the audience\n  you are recruiting; disclose after the click and offer a waitlist [51].\n- **Shipping an AI-generated MVP straight to the public** with client-side keys, disabled RLS and\n  half-built auth [35][36] — and planning the schedule on perceived, not measured, AI speed [34].\n- **Substituting synthetic respondents for customers** at the validation stage [37][38].\n- **Pricing last**, or per-seat on an AI product with no expansion path: building for two years\n  then finding willingness to pay is a third of the assumption sits behind the 72% figure [17][18][16].\n- **Calling a strategy \"grow 3x\" ** — a goal, not a diagnosis; Rumelt's definition of bad\n  strategy [29].\n- **Claiming a moat you cannot name.** If it is not one of the seven powers plus a barrier, assume\n  it is a head start [33] — and do not open five networks before one is self-sustaining [25]."},{"ref":"modern/S10-launch-method-2026.md","label":"Product-market fit and strategy (2026 layer)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`levels of PMF` — First Round's four-stage model (Nascent, Developing, Strong, Extreme) with\nper-level thresholds; the `4Ps` (persona, problem, promise, product) are the levers between them.\n`Sean Ellis score` — % of users \"very disappointed\" to lose the product; ≥40% is the fit line.\n`HXC (high-expectation customer)` — the most discerning person who would still be delighted.\n`opportunity-solution tree` — Torres's map: outcome → opportunities → solutions → assumption tests.\n`four forces of progress` — push, pull, habit, anxiety: the forces acting on a switch decision,\nsurfaced by a `switch interview` (timeline reconstruction of the moment a customer changed).\n`smoke test` — a landing page or ad that measures intent for a concept that does not exist yet.\n`fake door` — the same test for a single feature inside a live product.\n`concierge MVP` — delivering the service manually to test value before automating it; a\n`Wizard of Oz MVP` is a real interface with humans behind it.\n`atomic network` — the smallest group that makes a network product useful on its own; the\n`hard side` is its scarcer, harder-to-recruit side (drivers, sellers, creators).\n`7 powers` — Helmer's durable advantages: scale economies, network economies, counter-positioning,\nswitching costs, branding, cornered resource, process power.\n`crux` — Rumelt's term for the surmountable challenge whose solution unlocks the most progress.\n`value metric` — the unit you charge for that grows with delivered value.\n`hybrid pricing` — subscription base plus consumption (often AI credits).\n`outcome-based pricing` — charging per achieved result rather than per seat or unit consumed;\n`van Westendorp PSM` — four-question survey producing an acceptable price range. `transient\nadvantage` — McGrath's view that advantages must be launched, exploited and exited in cycles.\n`burn multiple` — net burn ÷ net new ARR; `magic number` — net new ARR ÷ prior-quarter S&M.\n`why now` — the dated, specific change that makes this the right moment for this solution."},{"ref":"modern/S11-leadership-and-scaling-2026.md","label":"Leadership and scaling (2026 layer)","heading":"What changed since 2018","text":"- **\"Founder mode\" reopened the delegation question.** Paul Graham's essay (Sept 2024), written\n  after a Brian Chesky talk at Y Combinator, argues the standard advice — \"hire good people and\n  give them room to do their jobs\" — is manager-mode advice that leaves founders feeling \"gaslit\"\n  [1]. Chesky himself says he never used the phrase and frames it as being in the details without\n  micromanaging [2][3]. The book's chapter 11 prescribes almost exactly the trajectory Graham\n  attacks; both readings are now live.\n- **Org charts are flattening and spans are widening.** Amazon directed every senior leadership\n  team to raise the ratio of individual contributors to managers by at least 15% by end of Q1 2025\n  [6]; Gallup's 2025 analysis puts the average span at 12.1 direct reports [8]; Gartner's 2025 CEO\n  survey frames AI explicitly as a delayering tool [9].\n- **Remote work stopped being opinion and became evidence.** Bloom et al.'s randomised trial\n  (Nature, June 2024, n=1,612) found hybrid cut quit rates about a third with no measurable\n  performance cost [12][13]; a University of Pittsburgh study of 3M+ workers found RTO mandates\n  raised time-to-fill 23% and cut hire rates 17%, attrition concentrated in senior, skilled and\n  female employees [14]. The four-day week also got its largest trial — 141 organisations, ~2,900\n  employees, six countries, six months: burnout down, satisfaction and mental health up [10][11].\n- **Blitzscaling lost its default status.** Hoffman & Yeh's 2018 thesis — speed over efficiency\n  under uncertainty [50][51] — was repriced after 2022 toward capital efficiency, runway and\n  \"default alive\". (No formal recantation by Hoffman found: a shift in practice, not a revision.)\n- **Founder mental health became a measured category.** Startup Snapshot's *Untold Toll* series\n  puts founder anxiety at 37% and burnout at 36%, with startup *employees* worse on both [15].\n- **Governance caught up with growth outside the US.** Africa's 2024-25 shutdown wave and India's\n  BharatPe/Byju's/GoMechanic cases turned \"founder-controlled, board-light\" into a red flag\n  [44][45][47][48].\n- **AI entered the operating model, not just the product.** Shopify made \"reflexive AI usage\" a\n  baseline expectation and required teams to show why AI cannot do the work before asking for\n  headcount (April 2025) [26][27]; ICONIQ finds AI-revenue leaders run flatter than peers [25].\n- **CEO turnover hit records.** 446 public-company CEO exits in 2025, the highest on record, and\n  18% of incoming CEOs appointed on an interim basis versus 7% a year earlier [21][22]."},{"ref":"modern/S11-leadership-and-scaling-2026.md","label":"Leadership and scaling (2026 layer)","heading":"Core ideas (current consensus)","text":"- **The founder's unique asset is context, not control** — Graham (2024): founder mode abandons the\n  rule that a CEO engages only through direct reports; Jobs's annual retreat for Apple's \"100 most\n  important people\" regardless of title is the archetype [1]. Graham concedes it is undescribed:\n  \"There are as far as I know no books specifically about founder mode\" [1].\n- **Founder mode is a licence that can be abused** — Chris Yeh (2024): in weak hands it becomes \"a\n  solipsistic system of governance where anything and anyone might be overruled\"; his fix is\n  *evidence mode* — the founder may overrule, but must do it on evidence and in the company's\n  interest [4].\n- **Depth is task-specific, not a personality** — the best reconciliation in the 2024-26 debate\n  borrows Grove's *task-relevant maturity*: go deep where the team is new to the problem, back off\n  where it is not [5] — Roberts' mode-mixing from chapter 11, restated at task level.\n- **Companies need an operating system, written down** — Claire Hughes Johnson, *Scaling People*\n  (Stripe Press, 2023): founding documents (mission, long-term goals, operating principles, team\n  charters), operating cadences (business reviews, planning) and feedback/performance mechanisms;\n  her four principles are build self-awareness, say the thing you think you cannot say, distinguish\n  management from leadership, and come back to your operating system [34][55].\n- **Most companies underperform on standards, not strategy** — Slootman, *Amp It Up* (2022): raise\n  standards, increase urgency, narrow focus, align people; the enemy is tolerated mediocrity [35].\n- **Written narrative beats slides for decisions** — Bryar & Carr, *Working Backwards* (2021):\n  Amazon banned PowerPoint in 2004 for six-page narratives read silently for the first ~20 minutes\n  of a meeting; the discipline is in the writing [38][39][52].\n- **Match decision speed to reversibility** — Bezos (2015-16 shareholder letters): two-way-door\n  decisions go fast, made by individuals or small groups; only one-way doors deserve heavyweight\n  process. Big organisations apply heavyweight process to everything, which is what makes them\n  slow [36][37].\n- **Candour is a manager's job, respect is the precondition** — Kim Scott: *Radical Candor* (2017)\n  = care personally + challenge directly (failure modes: obnoxious aggression, ruinous empathy);\n  *Radical Respect* (2024) is its prequel — candour fails without respect for the person [40][41].\n- **Transparency can substitute for hierarchy at the top** — Jensen Huang runs ~60 direct reports\n  and no standing 1:1s, so that \"there is no privileged access to information\" [33]. It removes\n  layers — and the private channel people use to raise problems.\n- **Founder wellbeing is an organisational variable.** Startup Snapshot (Nov 2025) finds employees\n  more burnt out than founders: a founder who cannot show vulnerability propagates that downward\n  [15][17].\n- **Flattening is not free.** Gallup finds no single optimal team size; what predicts engagement is\n  manager quality, manager time on IC work (best under 40%) and weekly meaningful feedback — about\n  seven in ten are engaged at *any* team size when the feedback is there [8]."},{"ref":"modern/S11-leadership-and-scaling-2026.md","label":"Leadership and scaling (2026 layer)","heading":"Frameworks & playbooks","text":"| Framework | Author / year | What it says | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Founder mode vs manager mode | Paul Graham, essay, Sept 2024 [1] | Founders may engage below the org chart (skip-level, \"top 100\" style forums); manager mode = delegate through direct reports only and trust the black box. Explicitly undescribed and easy to abuse as micromanagement cover | Deciding how deep to go as headcount passes ~50-150 |\n| Evidence mode | Chris Yeh, Sept 2024 [4] | Founder retains override authority but must exercise it on evidence and in the whole company's interest — guardrails on founder mode | Writing down when the founder may overrule a team |\n| Company operating system | Claire Hughes Johnson, *Scaling People* (2023) [34][55] | Founding documents + operating cadence + team development + feedback/performance mechanisms; 100+ pages of templates | Getting from ~20 to ~200 people |\n| Amp It Up (five moves) | Frank Slootman, 2022 [35] | Raise standards → align people → sharpen focus → pick up the pace → transform strategy | A company that is busy but not winning |\n| Working Backwards / PR-FAQ + six-pager | Colin Bryar & Bill Carr, 2021 (practices from 2004 on) [38][39][52] | Write the press release and FAQ before building; replace decks with six-page narratives and silent reading | Product decisions, exec reviews, board packs |\n| Type 1 / Type 2 (one-way / two-way doors) + disagree and commit | Jeff Bezos, 2015-16 shareholder letters [36][37] | Irreversible decisions get heavyweight process; reversible ones get speed and a named decider. \"Disagree and commit\" ends stalemates without consensus | Any decision backlog; delegating authority |\n| DACI / RAPID | Atlassian (DACI) and Bain (RAPID), pre-2018 (classics) | Name one Driver, one Approver, Contributors, Informed (DACI); or Recommend, Agree, Perform, Input, Decide (RAPID). One accountable name per decision | Cross-functional decisions with no clear owner |\n| Radical Candor / Radical Respect | Kim Scott, 2017 / 2024 [40][41] | Care personally **and** challenge directly; obnoxious aggression, ruinous empathy and manipulative insincerity are the three failure quadrants. Respect for the person is the precondition | Manager training; feedback norms |\n| Blitzscaling | Reid Hoffman & Chris Yeh, 2018 [50][51] | Prioritise speed over efficiency while a winner-take-most market is being decided; accept inefficiency as the price | Only in genuinely land-grab markets — read with the post-2022 efficiency correction |\n\n**The OKR critique (2020s).** OKRs (Grove; Doerr, *Measure What Matters*, 2018) have no\npeer-reviewed evidence base as implemented; the closest support is Locke & Latham's goal-setting\ntheory, which backs specific difficult goals *with regular feedback*, not this ritual [42].\nRecurring failure modes: key results written as outputs (\"launch feature X\"); quarterly cycles that\nfreeze while the market moves; no data infrastructure to measure the KRs [42][43]. Treat OKRs as one\noption among several (SMART, North Star + inputs, WhyGo) and pick on measurability [43]."},{"ref":"modern/S11-leadership-and-scaling-2026.md","label":"Leadership and scaling (2026 layer)","heading":"Benchmarks & numbers (2024-2026)","text":"| Metric | Value | Segment / context | Source | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Average span of control | 12.1 direct reports; median 5-6 | 92,252 teams, 104 orgs, 26 industries, 46 countries | [8] | 2025 |\n| Manager IC-work threshold | best under 40% of manager time | Gallup meta-analysis | [8] | 2025 |\n| Amazon IC:manager directive | +15% ratio of ICs to managers by end Q1 2025 | company-wide memo | [6] | Sept 2024 |\n| Delayering via AI | Gartner CEO survey framed as a delayering opportunity; widely reported prediction that 20% of orgs use AI to flatten and cut more than half of middle-management roles through 2026 | Gartner research note (abstract; prediction cited secondarily) | [9][54] | 2025 |\n| Hybrid trial: quit rates | non-manager attrition 2.4% vs 7.2% control (about one-third fewer quits) | RCT, 1,612 employees, Trip.com | [12][13] | 2024 |\n| RTO mandates: hiring cost | time-to-fill +23%; hire rate -17% | 3M+ tech & finance workers, LinkedIn histories, S&P 500 | [14] | Dec 2024 |\n| RTO mandates: attrition | \"abnormally high\" turnover, concentrated in senior, skilled and female employees | same | [14] | Dec 2024 |\n| Four-day week trial | burnout down; job satisfaction, mental health and (least) physical health up | 141 orgs, ~2,900 employees, 6 months, 6 countries; ~290 control | [10][11][53] | Jul 2025 |\n| Founder anxiety / burnout | 37% / 36% | Startup Snapshot, *Untold Toll* Part 2 | [15] | Nov 2025 |\n| Founder-CEO replacement | ~25% replaced by Series A; >50% by Series C; <40% still on founder-CEO at Series D | Wasserman, 10,000 startups (classic dataset) | [18][19][20] | 2003-08 |\n| Public-company CEO exits | 446 — highest on record (373 in 2024) | Challenger, Gray & Christmas (2,032 US CEO exits in all) | [21] | 2025 |\n| Interim CEO appointments | 18% of incoming CEOs vs 7% a year earlier | same | [21][22] | 2025 |\n| eNPS, tech (\"New Tech\") | median 18; 72% of employees engaged | ~11M responses, ~800 orgs, Jul 2025-Jun 2026 | [23] | 2026 |\n| Management layers at AI leaders | 72% have 4 or fewer layers CEO to junior, vs 56% of peers | ~300 software execs, 87% North America | [25] | Q2 2026 |\n| Workforce-plan rethink | 78% rethinking workforce planning; 45% expect a different role mix; 33% expect smaller teams | same | [25] | 2026 |\n| Agentic AI project failure | >40% of agentic AI projects to be cancelled by end-2027 | Gartner prediction; Jan 2025 poll n=3,412 | [31] | 2025 |\n| Enterprise AI pilots with no P&L impact | 95% | MIT NANDA: 52 interviews, 153 surveys, 300 deployments | [32] | Aug 2025 |\n| Workforce AI survey | 31,000 professionals, 31 countries; 46% of leaders say their org is fully automating some workflows with agents | Microsoft Work Trend Index | [29] | 2025 |\n| Nvidia CEO span | ~60 direct reports, no standing 1:1s | reported management practice | [33] | 2024-25 |"},{"ref":"modern/S11-leadership-and-scaling-2026.md","label":"Leadership and scaling (2026 layer)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **If you are the bottleneck on *everything*, that is manager-mode failure; if you are the\n  bottleneck on *nothing*, that is founder-mode failure** [1][4][5] — decide per decision.\n- **If a decision is a two-way door, name one decider and give them a deadline.** Only one-way\n  doors get a heavyweight process (Bezos) [36]. Keep a written list of which is which.\n- **If you are about to delayer, check manager IC time first.** Gallup: keep managers under ~40% IC\n  work and give weekly meaningful feedback; team size matters far less than either [8].\n- **If you mandate full-time office, budget for a hiring penalty** — +23% time-to-fill, -17% hire\n  rate, disproportionate loss of senior and skilled staff [14]. Hybrid is the evidence-backed\n  default: about a third fewer quits, no performance cost [12].\n- **If you want a wellbeing intervention with trial evidence, a four-day week beats most perks**\n  [10]. Pilot six months with a control group and pre/post measures, as the trial did.\n- **If more than one of the book's seven professional-management signals is true (chapter 11,\n  p. 192), act now** — boards are increasingly reaching for interim CEOs (18% of appointments)\n  rather than planning succession [21].\n- **If the founder is the only person with full context, the company is one burnout away from a\n  crisis** (37% anxiety, 36% burnout, half report stigma about getting help) [15] — a coach, a peer\n  group and a written decision log are continuity infrastructure, not perks.\n- **If your board has no independent director by Series B, fix it before you need it** (seed 2:1\n  founders:investors, 2:2:1 from Series A/B) [49]; Africa's and India's 2024-25 failures are mostly\n  board-absence failures [44][45][47].\n- **If AI is your reason for a smaller team, write down which work it removes.** Shopify's test —\n  \"demonstrate why you cannot get this done using AI\" — is answerable, and judgement, relationships\n  and accountability are legitimate answers [26][27]. Key results still need a weekly data source."},{"ref":"modern/S11-leadership-and-scaling-2026.md","label":"Leadership and scaling (2026 layer)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. **Diagnose the mode per surface, not per company.** List your 10 largest recurring decisions.\n   Mark each content / behaviors / results / context (chapter 11) and each one-way / two-way door\n   [36]. Where the marks disagree with reality, that is your delegation backlog.\n2. **Write the founding documents** — mission, 3-year goals, operating principles, team charters\n   (Hughes Johnson) [34]. One page each. Undocumented culture does not survive doubling.\n3. **Set the operating cadence.** Weekly leadership meeting with a written pre-read; monthly\n   business review against `S05-metrics-and-unit-economics.md`; quarterly planning; annual\n   strategy. Put the six-pager and silent-reading rule in the calendar invite [38][39].\n4. **Publish the decision-rights map.** Per recurring decision: DACI or RAPID roles, reversibility\n   class, and whether the founder may override (evidence mode: on what evidence?) [4][36].\n5. **Choose a goal system and its measurement plan together.** If OKRs, write outcome key results\n   and confirm each has a live data source before the quarter starts [42][43].\n6. **Design spans deliberately.** Manager IC time under 40%, a weekly feedback ritual, and a layer\n   count you can state out loud (AI-revenue leaders cluster at 4 or fewer) [8][25].\n7. **Fix the work model with evidence.** Pick hybrid days, RTO or a four-day pilot; run six months\n   with pre/post engagement, attrition and output measures [10][12].\n8. **Measure the culture quarterly.** Engagement plus eNPS against the tech benchmark (median 17-18;\n   72% engaged) [23][24]; act on two items and publish what you changed — 13% of tech employees say\n   they see no change after surveys [23].\n9. **Build the board and the founder's support before you need them.** Seed 2:1, independent by\n   Series A/B [49]; advisory board per chapter 11 (p. 190-191); a coach, a peer CEO group and a\n   written succession note [15][21]. If the professional-management checklist trips and you cannot\n   adapt, move to chair and hire a CEO — with the handover defined in writing, or you produce what\n   chapter 11 calls the illusion of letting go."},{"ref":"modern/S11-leadership-and-scaling-2026.md","label":"Leadership and scaling (2026 layer)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Founder-mode audit (adapted from [1][4][5])**\n- Which three areas will I stay deep in — permanently, by choice, and known to everyone?\n- Which three have I delegated *with the authority to be wrong*?\n- When I overrule someone, what evidence do I show, and where is it written?\n- What is my skip-level mechanism (all-hands Q&A, \"top 100\" forum, customer calls), and how often?\n- Has anyone told me in the last 90 days that I am too deep somewhere? If not, why not?\n\n**Operating-system checklist (Hughes Johnson) [34]**\n- Mission, 3-year goals, operating principles, team charters — written and shared?\n- Weekly/monthly/quarterly/annual cadence on the calendar, with owners and pre-reads?\n- One documented feedback mechanism and one documented performance mechanism?\n- A planning process that allocates people and money at the same time?\n\n**Decision hygiene**\n- One-way or two-way door? [36] Who is the single Approver / Decider (DACI / RAPID)? What is the\n  deadline, and what happens if it passes? Who disagreed and committed, and is that on the record?\n\n**Org design review**\n- How many layers between me and a junior contributor? (AI leaders: 4 or fewer) [25]\n- What share of each manager's week is IC work? (target under 40%) [8]\n- Which managers do not give weekly meaningful feedback, and if we delayer, who inherits the\n  coaching they were doing? [8]\n\n**Culture measurement**\n- Engagement % favourable and eNPS versus the benchmark (tech median 17-18) [23]\n- The two lowest-scoring items — and the one change we will ship this quarter\n- Tech-sector chronic gaps: growth/progression, social connection, manager support [23]\n- Do people believe surveys change anything? (13% say no in tech) [23]\n\n**Founder wellbeing**\n- Hours slept, days fully off in the last month, and who else can run the weekly meeting\n- Do I have a coach, a peer group, or neither? (77% of founders get no professional help) [16]\n- Who on the board knows how I am actually doing? If I were unavailable for 30 days, what breaks,\n  and is that written down?"},{"ref":"modern/S11-leadership-and-scaling-2026.md","label":"Leadership and scaling (2026 layer)","heading":"Regional notes","text":"- **US.** The flattening story is loudest here: Amazon's IC:manager directive and five-day RTO\n  [6][7], Meta and others cutting layers [54], record public-company CEO exits and a jump in interim\n  appointments [21][22]. Founder-friendly governance (dual-class, founder-controlled boards) is\n  still available but drew more scrutiny after 2022. At-will employment makes delayering fast and\n  legally cheap relative to Europe.\n- **UK and EU.** Delayering is slower and costlier: collective-consultation thresholds, works\n  councils in Germany, the Netherlands and France, statutory notice — a \"15% ratio\" memo is not\n  executable the same way. The UK ran the largest four-day-week pilots and supplied part of the 2025\n  trial dataset [10][11]. The EU AI Act's high-risk classification for employment-related AI (see\n  `S03-hiring-and-people.md`) also covers AI used in promotion, task allocation and monitoring —\n  relevant the moment you use AI to manage people.\n- **Africa (Nigeria especially).** 2024-25 was a governance reckoning: the 2024 Startup Graveyard\n  analysis attributes 58% of failures to financial difficulty, 27% to operational issues and 17% to\n  regulatory ones, with weak governance as the connective tissue [44]; Launch Base Africa tracked\n  30+ high-profile crises in 2025, Nigeria the largest single share [45]; Thepeer's public\n  post-mortem put roughly $1.2M unaccounted for [46]. For a Lagos-based founder: appoint a\n  non-executive director before Series A, separate founder and company bank accounts formally, keep\n  board minutes, and expect governance diligence as heavy as traction diligence. Dual-currency pay,\n  power and connectivity costs also make \"office days\" a different calculation than in the OECD.\n- **India.** BharatPe, Byju's, GoMechanic and BluSmart made governance the dominant investor theme;\n  the structural gap is that listed-company rules (SEBI LODR) are strict while unlisted startups are\n  largely unregulated [47][48]. CII has published a voluntary governance charter for startups [47].\n  Expect audit-committee, related-party and revenue-recognition questions early.\n- **Southeast Asia, the Middle East and LatAm.** Family- and conglomerate-linked capital makes board\n  seats relational rather than purely financial; plan for a chair who is also an investor-operator.\n  Cross-time-zone and cross-border teams (normal at seed stage across Argentina, Brazil, Colombia\n  and Mexico) make written asynchronous cadences — six-pagers, decision logs — more valuable than\n  the US default of synchronous meetings, and make the hybrid evidence [12] matter more than the\n  RTO debate."},{"ref":"modern/S11-leadership-and-scaling-2026.md","label":"Leadership and scaling (2026 layer)","heading":"AI-era notes","text":"- **AI is now an argument in org design, not just tooling.** Gartner's 2025 CEO survey frames AI as\n  a delayering opportunity [9]; ICONIQ finds companies earning most revenue from AI run flatter —\n  72% at four or fewer layers versus 56% of peers [25]. Whether flat *causes* fast, or fast\n  companies simply end up flat, is not established by that survey.\n- **Headcount gates moved.** Shopify's April 2025 memo made \"reflexive AI usage\" a baseline\n  expectation, added AI usage to performance and peer reviews, and required teams to demonstrate\n  why AI cannot do the work before requesting headcount [26][27][28]. Copying the rule without the\n  enablement (tools, training, worked examples) produces theatre.\n- **A new managerial skill: supervising agents.** Microsoft's 2025 Work Trend Index (31,000\n  professionals, 31 countries) names the \"agent boss\" role and the human-agent ratio as a planning\n  question; 46% of leaders said their org is fully automating some workflows with agents [29][30].\n- **The evidence is thinner than the rhetoric.** MIT's NANDA study found 95% of enterprise GenAI\n  pilots delivered no measurable P&L impact [32]; Gartner expects over 40% of agentic AI projects to\n  be cancelled by end-2027 on cost, unclear value or weak risk controls, and estimates only ~130 of\n  thousands of self-described agentic vendors are real [31]. ICONIQ's own data shows agent\n  deployments averaging under 30% productivity impact across revenue bands [25].\n- **Where AI does show up in leadership work today:** drafting and critiquing six-pagers and\n  PR-FAQs; summarising customer calls and support threads so the founder keeps context at scale\n  (the thing founder mode is really about); synthesising engagement-survey free text; preparing\n  board packs; rehearsing hard conversations. None of these need an agent framework.\n- **Risks specific to leaders.** (1) Widening spans with AI without adding feedback capacity —\n  feedback, not headcount arithmetic, drives engagement [8]. (2) Letting AI write the values and\n  the review text, producing culture documents nobody owns [23]. (3) AI-assisted monitoring crossing\n  into EU AI Act high-risk territory. (4) Banking an AI-productivity claim you have not measured —\n  MIT and Gartner both say most do not survive contact with the P&L [31][32].\n- **A defensible 2026 stance:** use AI to remove the reporting and summarising work that made middle\n  management expensive, keep coaching and feedback human, and measure the substitution before you\n  put it in a plan."},{"ref":"modern/S11-leadership-and-scaling-2026.md","label":"Leadership and scaling (2026 layer)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- **Using \"founder mode\" as cover for micromanagement.** Graham predicted it himself: once named, it\n  becomes an excuse [1]; Yeh calls the failure state autocracy without evidence [4].\n- **Copying a practice without its preconditions.** Huang's no-1:1s works with radical information\n  symmetry and a specific kind of company [33]; Netflix-style candour works with Netflix-level\n  severance; Amazon's six-pagers work because writing is a promotion criterion.\n- **Delayering to a target number.** Cutting managers without redistributing coaching produces\n  directionless teams; feedback frequency, not span, is the lever [8].\n- **Mandating RTO as a performance intervention.** No performance gain, a measurable hiring and\n  retention cost, and researchers characterising some mandates as blame-shifting [14].\n- **Running OKRs as a reporting ritual.** Output-shaped key results, frozen quarters and\n  unmeasurable KRs are the three documented failure modes [42][43].\n- **Letting the board be a formality.** Africa's and India's 2024-25 failures are mostly governance\n  failures — no independent director, no minutes, no separation of funds [44][45][47].\n- **Treating founder burnout as a private matter.** It propagates: employees report higher anxiety\n  and burnout than founders, and most founders never seek help [15][16].\n- **Banking AI productivity before measuring it** (95% of pilots showed no P&L impact) [31][32], or\n  **blitzscaling a market that is not being decided** — a conditional bet, not a strategy [50][51].\n- **Hiring a professional CEO and not actually handing over** — chapter 11's \"illusion of turning\n  the organization over\" is still the most common failure of this transition."},{"ref":"modern/S11-leadership-and-scaling-2026.md","label":"Leadership and scaling (2026 layer)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`founder mode` — Graham's (2024) label for a CEO engaging below the org chart, including skip-level\nforums, rather than only through direct reports.\n`manager mode` — the delegate-through-direct-reports default that founder mode is defined against.\n`evidence mode` — Yeh's (2024) guardrail: founder override is legitimate only on evidence and in the\nwhole company's interest.\n`task-relevant maturity` — Grove's rule that management depth varies with the team's experience of\nthe specific task.\n`span of control` — direct reports per manager; average 12.1, median 5-6 (Gallup, 2025).\n`delayering / flattening` — deliberately removing management layers to speed decisions and cut cost.\n`one-way / two-way door` — irreversible vs reversible decision; sets how much process is justified.\n`disagree and commit` — explicit agreement to back a decision you argued against, without consensus.\n`DACI / RAPID` — decision-rights notations naming one accountable approver or decider per decision.\n`six-pager` — a narrative memo replacing slides, read silently at the start of the meeting.\n`operating system (company)` — founding documents + cadence + feedback/performance mechanisms\n(Hughes Johnson, 2023).\n`eNPS` — employee net promoter score; tech median 17-18 (Culture Amp, 2026).\n`agent boss` — Microsoft's (2025) term for an employee who directs and supervises AI agents.\n`human-agent ratio` — how many agents per role, and how many humans are needed to supervise them.\n`default alive` — Graham's earlier test: would you reach profitability on current growth and spend\nwithout raising again? The post-2022 counterweight to blitzscaling."},{"ref":"modern/S12-exit-and-liquidity-2026.md","label":"Exit, liquidity and valuation (2026 layer)","heading":"What changed since 2018","text":"- **The IPO window reopened, then punished the late buyers.** 202 US IPOs raised $44.0B in 2025,\n  +35% on count and +49% on proceeds — the busiest year since 2021 [1]; Renaissance projects 200-230\n  deals and $40-60B for 2026 [2]. The 2025 class split violently: CoreWeave +130% from its debut and\n  Circle +62%, against Klarna −26% from its $40 IPO price and Chime −25%; Figma popped ~250% on day\n  one then fell from a $143 peak to $73 by late December [3].\n- **Exit is now an AI story.** 40% of 2025 US VC exit value traced to AI, across a record 317 AI\n  exits [4]. Q2 2026 produced the most billion-dollar venture-backed exits since 2021 and the\n  largest venture-backed exit ever (SpaceX) [6].\n- **M&A, not IPO, is still the harvest — and it got enormous.** Announced global M&A hit a record\n  $2.8T in H1 2026, +48% YoY and the highest first half since LSEG began tracking in 1980;\n  technology led with $649B [8][9].\n- **Time to exit roughly doubled.** Median time to IPO for tech companies is now ~11.5 years and 45%\n  of unicorns have sat in portfolios nine years or more [5]. The book's Figure 9-1 trend never\n  flattened.\n- **Liquidity decoupled from exit.** A record 16,538 employees sold shares in Carta-administered\n  tender offers in 2025 [11], and Carta ran 71 tenders worth ~$3B in H1 2026 alone — count +34% and\n  value +200% YoY [10]. Secondary tenders totalled ~$35B in 2025 against ~$45B of IPO proceeds [13].\n- **Continuation funds became a real exit route.** Global secondaries reached $240B in 2025 (+48%),\n  of which GP-led was $116B [15][16]; continuation vehicles were 77-89% of GP-led volume [15][16],\n  and forecasters expect 30-40% of all PE exits to run through them in 2026-27 [17].\n- **The \"reverse acquihire\" was invented to sidestep merger review.** Between March 2024 and January\n  2026 Google, Microsoft, Amazon and Meta spent over $20B hiring founding teams and licensing models\n  without buying the companies [19].\n- **Buying a business became a mainstream entrepreneurial path.** The Stanford search-fund study\n  reports 33.9% aggregate IRR and 4.75x ROI across US/Canada funds since 1984 [35][36], and the\n  non-US universe grew from 83 funds in 2018 to 503 by end-2025 [37]."},{"ref":"modern/S12-exit-and-liquidity-2026.md","label":"Exit, liquidity and valuation (2026 layer)","heading":"Core ideas (current consensus)","text":"- **Sell at a local maximum, not a global one** — Jason Lemkin, SaaStr: you cannot time the peak, so\n  sell when your own metrics and the category are both momentarily favourable [60].\n- **The old SaaS exit ladder is broken.** Lemkin (Oct 2025): build to $20-50M ARR and a strategic or\n  PE buyer appears — \"not anymore\"; software clears nearer 15x EBITDA than 25x, and 46% of Q2 2025\n  SaaS M&A was vertical SaaS with embedded workflows [59].\n- **Staying private is a gift and a curse** — Bill Gurley (2025): long private lives concentrate\n  founder and employee wealth in illiquid paper while no one in the chain has an incentive to mark\n  it accurately [70].\n- **Valuation is triangulation, and the multiple comes from the market, not the model** — the\n  handbook's rule survives, but the modern spread is enormous: the SaaS Capital Index median read\n  3.8x ARR in July 2026 while the market-cap-weighted BVP Emerging Cloud Index read 6.3x [47][48].\n- **Discount rates must be built, not borrowed** — Damodaran (2026): the forward-looking US equity\n  risk premium was 4.23% at the start of 2026, while historical averages would say anywhere from\n  5.5% to 14.5% depending on window and method [52][53].\n- **Hold forever is a strategy** — Brent Beshore, Permanent Equity: ~30-year funds, little debt, no\n  intention to sell, so the question becomes \"who should own this next\" [71]. Andrew Wilkinson built\n  Tiny to 35+ businesses on the same decentralised holdco logic [73].\n- **Employee ownership is the exit nobody pitches you.** 6,609 US ESOPs cover 15.1M participants and\n  $2.1T of assets [41][42]; the UK reached 2,824 employee-owned businesses by March 2026, ~500 of\n  them transitioning in 2025 alone [43]."},{"ref":"modern/S12-exit-and-liquidity-2026.md","label":"Exit, liquidity and valuation (2026 layer)","heading":"Frameworks & playbooks","text":"| Framework | Author / year | What it says | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Local-maximum selling | Jason Lemkin, SaaStr (2024-25) | You will not catch the top; sell when growth, category sentiment and a willing buyer coincide, not when you feel finished [60] | Any inbound offer; deciding whether to run a process |\n| \"Who will buy the SaaS companies?\" screen | Lemkin, SaaStr (Oct 2025) | Strategics tuck in less; PE buys only category leaders with AI attach and a path to 40%+ EBITDA; vertical SaaS with switching costs is the bid [59] | Positioning software for sale 12-24 months out |\n| Tender offer / structured secondary | Carta practice (2025-26) | Company-sanctioned, capped, priced sale window; median third-party cap 20% of holdings for employees and ex-employees [11][12] | Giving the team liquidity without an exit |\n| Continuation vehicle (GP-led) | Secondaries practice; Lazard, Kroll (2025-26) | The fund sells the asset to a new vehicle it also manages; LPs choose cash or roll [15][17] | Investor-driven \"we need liquidity but not a sale\" |\n| Reverse acquihire / licence-and-hire | Big Tech 2024-26 (Inflection, Character.AI, Windsurf, Scale) | Non-exclusive licence + hiring the founding team; the shell survives with a payout to holders [19][23] | Understanding a non-acquisition offer and its regulatory and fairness risk |\n| Search fund / ETA | Stanford GSB (Kelly, Zenios, Ng, 2026); Deibel (2018) | Raise search capital, buy one profitable SMB, operate it; aggregate IRR 33.9%, ROI 4.75x since 1984 [35][36][72] | The buyer across the table in an SMB sale; or your own next act |\n| Holdco / permanent capital | Brent Beshore (Permanent Equity), Andrew Wilkinson (Tiny) | Buy durable cash-generating businesses, minimal leverage, no forced exit, decentralised management [71][73] | Sellers who want continuity over maximum price |\n| AI-enabled roll-up | General Catalyst, Thrive, Elad Gil et al. (2023-26) | Buy fragmented labour-intensive services at 5-15% margins, automate 30-70% of workflows, compound via acquisition [61][62] | Services owners; also a warning about future competitors |\n| Berkus method | Dave Berkus (1990s, still standard) | Up to $500K each for prototype, team, customer, financial performance, advisors — ~$2M cap pre-revenue [55] | Pre-revenue with no comparable |\n| Scorecard / Bill Payne method | Bill Payne | Weight against regional comparables: team 25%, opportunity 20%, product/tech 18%, sales/marketing 15%, funding need 10%, other 10% [55] | Angel-stage priced rounds |\n| VC method | Classic (Sahlman); still taught 2026 | Terminal value ÷ required return, discounted at 30-70% by stage, adjusted for expected dilution [54] | Seed to Series A where an exit value is imaginable |\n| DCF for young firms | Damodaran, *Valuing Young or Start-Up Firms* + annual data updates | Forecast to a stable state, build the discount rate from a current ERP, decay the cost of capital toward the mature-market number [52][54] | Growing firms a current-earnings multiple would undervalue |\n| Deal-points benchmarking | ABA Private Target M&A Deal Points Study (2025 ed.) | Earnouts 18%, RWI 63%, no-survival 41% in middle-market deals — negotiate against the distribution, not anecdote [57][58] | Reading an LOI or SPA |\n| Structured wind-down | SimpleClosure (2024-26); Carta dissolution guides | Board/stockholder approval → employee and creditor notice → asset sale → dissolution filings → final tax returns [45][46] | When the answer is \"close it\" |"},{"ref":"modern/S12-exit-and-liquidity-2026.md","label":"Exit, liquidity and valuation (2026 layer)","heading":"Benchmarks & numbers (2024-2026)","text":"| Metric | Value | Segment / context | Source | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Small-business cash-flow (SDE) multiple** | **2.7x** (2.61x in 2025, +1%) | US Main Street, BizBuySell closed deals | [33][31] | 2026 / 2025 |\n| **Small-business revenue multiple** | **0.7x** (0.69x in 2025) | same | [33][31] | 2026 / 2025 |\n| Small-business multiples by sector | earnings 2.0-3.3x (avg 2.58x); revenue 0.42-1.2x (avg 0.67x) | BizBuySell sector tables | [32] | 2025-26 |\n| Median small-business sale price | $349,250 (−1% YoY); $350,000 in 2025, at 94% of asking | BizBuySell | [33][31] | 2026 / 2025 |\n| **Public SaaS revenue multiple (equal-weighted median)** | **3.8x ARR** (3.2x in June 2026, a decade low) | SaaS Capital Index | [47] | Jul 2026 |\n| **Public cloud multiple (market-cap weighted)** | **6.3x** | BVP Nasdaq Emerging Cloud Index | [48] | 2026 |\n| Private SaaS multiple | median 4.5x; equity-backed 5.3x vs bootstrapped 4.8x | SaaS Capital survey | [47] | 2026 |\n| **AI M&A EV/Revenue** | **mean 24.5x, median 13.1x** | AI acquisitions dataset | [49] | 2026 |\n| AI multiples by type | foundation models ~37.5x; AI-native SaaS 25-30x; applications 8-20x | secondary aggregation of public and private comps | [49][50] | 2026 |\n| AI sale reality check | defensible 8-12x revenue; narrative-only 3-5x | advisor observation | [51] | 2026 |\n| Micro-SaaS / online business | ~2.85-6.13x annual profit; Empire Flippers ~40-50x monthly profit (≈3.3-4.2x annual) | Acquire.com / Flippa / Empire Flippers, secondary summary | [63][64] | 2025-26 |\n| **VC-method discount rate** | **30-70%**, highest at pre-seed/seed, falling by stage | standard practice, secondary summary | [54] | 2026 |\n| **Equity risk premium (forward-looking)** | **4.23%** at 1 Jan 2026; 4.45% US / 4.17% mature-market in July 2026 | Damodaran | [52][53] | 2026 |\n| 409A common-vs-preferred | common ~10-30% of preferred at seed → 45-70% late stage; DLOM 20-35% at seed | 409A practice summary | [56] | 2026 |\n| Earnouts in private-target deals | 18% (from 26% in the 2023 study) | ABA, deals $25M-$900M, CY2024-Q1 2025 | [57][58] | 2025 |\n| Rep & warranty insurance / no-survival | RWI 63% (55% in 2023, 29% in 2016-17); no survival of reps 41% (from 30%) | same | [57][58] | 2025 |\n| **Median time to IPO (tech)** | **11.5 years**; 45% of unicorns held ≥9 years | PitchBook | [5] | 2025 |\n| US IPOs | 202 raising $44.0B (+35% count, +49% proceeds); 2026 forecast 200-230 deals, $40-60B | Renaissance Capital | [1][2] | 2025-26 |\n| IPO lockup | 180 days standard; some issuers hard-wire shorter, early releases increasingly negotiated | Cooley | [18] | 2025 |\n| Global announced M&A | record $2.8T in H1 2026, +48% YoY; technology $649B | LSEG data in mid-year reviews | [8][9] | 2026 |\n| Unicorn M&A | 36 deals, $67B (all-time high); largest Wiz-Alphabet $32B | Crunchbase | [7] | 2025 |\n| **Employees selling in tender offers** | **16,538** (record); 20% were former employees | Carta-administered tenders | [11] | 2025 |\n| Tender offers administered | 71 in H1 2026, ~$3B; count +34%, value +200% YoY | Carta | [10] | 2026 |\n| Tender participation / subscription | median participation 36.6% → 56%; median subscription 99.9% | Carta | [12] | 2025 |\n| Secondary tenders vs IPO proceeds; pricing | ~$35B tenders vs ~$45B IPOs; venture secondaries average ~78% of NAV | Nasdaq Private Market | [13] | 2025 |\n| Tracked private company value | $421B (2015) → $4.1T (Q3 2025) | Forge Global | [14] | 2025 |\n| Secondaries market | $240B total (+48%); GP-led $116B; dry powder $315B | Lazard / PitchBook / Kroll | [15][16][17] | 2025 |\n| Continuation vehicles | 77% of GP-led deals (Lazard: 86-89%); forecast 30-40% of PE exits 2026-27 | same | [15][16][17] | 2025-26 |\n| **Search fund aggregate return** | **33.9% IRR, 4.75x ROI, 2.88 PME** (2024 study: 35.1%, 4.5x) | Stanford GSB, US/Canada funds since 1984, data to 31 Dec 2025 | [35][36] | 2026 |\n| Search fund outcome distribution | 31% return <1x; 18.5% 1-2x; 25.5% 2-5x; 25% ≥5x (mean MOIC 2.80x) | Yale investor-level study | [37] | 2025 |\n| International search funds | 320 funds, 18.1% IRR, 2.0x MOIC; universe 83 (2018) → 503 (end-2025) | IESE international study | [37] | 2024-26 |\n| SBA 7(a) acquisition equity | ≥10% of total project cost; seller note counts only on full standby for the loan term | SOP 50 10 8, effective 1 Jun 2025 | [38] | 2025 |\n| SBA 7(a) from 1 Oct 2026 | DSCR floor 1.25x on trailing (not projected) results; lender-ordered QoE at ≥$3M price; no 7(a) Small underwriting for change of ownership | SOP 50 10 8.1 | [39][40] | 2026 |\n| **HSR size-of-transaction threshold** | **$133.9M** (from $126.4M); alt threshold $535.5M; size-of-person $267.8M / $26.8M | FTC, effective 17 Feb 2026 | [24][25] | 2026 |\n| UK merger thresholds (DMCCA) | turnover threshold £70M → £100M; one party UK turnover > £10M | Bird & Bird / CMA | [27][28] | 2025-26 |\n| US ESOPs | 6,609 plans at 6,411 companies; 15.1M participants; $2.1T assets; ~270 new/yr | NCEO | [41][42] | 2026 |\n| UK employee-owned businesses | 2,824 (≈500 transitioned in 2025); ~547,971 employee owners | Employee Ownership Association, March 2026 | [43] | 2026 |\n| Startup shutdowns | 966 (2024) vs 769 (2023), +25.6%; Series A shutdowns 2.5x YoY in 2025; Q1 2026 2.6x Q1 2025 | Carta / SimpleClosure | [45][46] | 2024-26 |"},{"ref":"modern/S12-exit-and-liquidity-2026.md","label":"Exit, liquidity and valuation (2026 layer)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **If you want liquidity but not an exit, run a tender before you run a process.** Tenders clear\n  (99.9% median subscription) and participation is now a majority of eligible holders [12].\n- **If your ARR multiple conversation starts above 6x, ask which index.** Equal-weighted private-SaaS\n  medians (3.8-4.5x) and market-cap-weighted public cloud (6.3x) are not the same number [47][48].\n- **If you are a services business, expect ~2.5-3x SDE, not a software multiple** [32][33].\n- **If you are pre-revenue, do not run a DCF.** Use Berkus (five $500K milestones, ~$2M ceiling) or\n  Scorecard against regional comparables, then sanity-check with the VC method [55].\n- **If you use the VC method, pick the discount rate by stage (30-70%) and say it out loud** — the\n  rate, not the terminal value, is where the argument actually is [54].\n- **Build the discount rate from a current ERP, not a historical average** (4.23% forward vs a\n  5.5-14.5% historical range at the start of 2026) [52][53]. Keep terminal growth below long-run\n  nominal GDP — Damodaran's growth cap [54].\n- **If a fast-growing firm is offered a multiple of current earnings, insist on DCF** — the\n  handbook's own rule, still routinely ignored (`C-valuation.md`).\n- **If the deal price is $133.9M or more (US, 2026), assume an HSR filing** — and assume that being\n  below it is not safety, because the 2023 Guidelines reach serial acquisitions and labour effects\n  [24][26][29].\n- **If a buyer proposes \"we hire your team and license the IP\", treat it as a sale with none of the\n  protections** — regulators now call these reportable-shaped, and shareholders left in the shell may\n  have claims [19][20][21].\n- **Buying with SBA 7(a): the seller note only counts as equity on full standby**, and from 1 Oct\n  2026 the deal must clear 1.25x DSCR on trailing results, with a QoE above $3M [38][39][40].\n- **Sell into optimism** — the handbook's timing rule, refined by Lemkin: aim for a local maximum you\n  can actually recognise, not a top you cannot [60]."},{"ref":"modern/S12-exit-and-liquidity-2026.md","label":"Exit, liquidity and valuation (2026 layer)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"**A. Deciding the route (2-4 weeks)**\n1. Name the motivation using the handbook's four: diversify / end of the line / begin anew / retire.\n2. Separate **liquidity** from **exit** — if you need cash but the business compounds, price a\n   tender, a shearing plan or a debt recap first [10][12].\n3. Screen the routes against your facts: IPO (≥$100M revenue, profitable quarters, 11.5-year median\n   clock [5]); strategic M&A; PE or roll-up; search-fund/individual buyer; ESOP or EOT; management\n   buyout; holdco; wind-down.\n4. Identify the *live* buyer classes in your category now — in 2026 that includes AI roll-up holdcos\n   for services businesses [61][62] and vertical-SaaS consolidators [59].\n\n**B. Getting valuation-ready (1-3 months)**\n5. Normalise earnings: add back owner compensation and one-offs; produce SDE and EBITDA side by side.\n6. Pull **recent comparable transactions**, not index levels — date, size, sector, and whether the\n   multiple was on revenue, SDE or EBITDA.\n7. Run three methods (the appendix's rule): a market multiple, a DCF with a built-up discount rate,\n   and an asset/liquidation floor. Reconcile the spread in writing.\n8. Fix the cap table and 409A history; \"cheap stock\" findings move price or delay closing [56].\n9. Commission a quality-of-earnings report if the likely price is ≥$3M and SBA financing is\n   plausible — from 1 Oct 2026 the lender requires one anyway [39][40].\n\n**C. Running the process (3-9 months)**\n10. Appoint advisers before you talk price: M&A lawyer, accountant, broker or banker sized to the\n    deal (brokers still cluster under ~$20M, the handbook's threshold).\n11. Solicit more than one bidder. A single bidder sets the price; two set the market.\n12. Negotiate the LOI against benchmarks — earnouts 18%, RWI 63%, no-survival 41% [57][58] — and\n    decide asset vs stock sale (handbook rule: stock sale favours the seller).\n13. Clear regulatory: HSR if ≥$133.9M [24]; UK if target turnover exceeds £100M or the share-of-\n    supply test bites [27][28].\n14. Fix the post-close role and its authority explicitly — the handbook's third M&A issue, still the\n    one founders concede for free.\n\n**D. Liquidity without exit (6-10 weeks per event)**\n15. Set eligibility (tenure, employee vs ex-employee), a cap (~20% median) and a price basis.\n16. Get board and investor consent; check ROFR and transfer restrictions.\n17. Price off the last round or a fresh 409A and disclose the basis; run the window — expect\n    near-full subscription and majority participation [12]. Repeat on a cadence so it is policy,\n    not a favour.\n\n**E. Winding down (4-12 weeks)**\n18. Board resolution and stockholder approval; stop incurring new obligations.\n19. Notify employees (WARN where applicable) and pay final wages and benefits.\n20. Sell or license assets — IP, domains, customer lists — before they decay [45].\n21. Notice creditors, settle or reserve, file dissolution and final tax returns, close payroll and\n    state registrations; then write the post-mortem and return remaining cash."},{"ref":"modern/S12-exit-and-liquidity-2026.md","label":"Exit, liquidity and valuation (2026 layer)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Liquidity-vs-exit triage**\n- How much cash do I personally need, and by when?\n- Would selling 10-20% of my holdings solve it? Would the board approve a tender?\n\n**Valuation prep (extends the handbook's checklist)**\n- Normalised SDE and EBITDA for three years, with add-backs listed and defensible.\n- Three to five comparable transactions with date, size and multiple basis.\n- Discount-rate build-up: risk-free + current ERP [52][53] + size/illiquidity premium; state the\n  stage discount if using the VC method (30-70%) [54]. Terminal growth below long-run nominal GDP.\n- Customer concentration, contracted vs non-contracted revenue, NRR/GRR, churn.\n- 409A history and current common/preferred ratio [56].\n\n**Buyer-readiness (what diligence will find)**\n- Are IP assignments signed by every contributor, including contractors and AI-tool output?\n- Is there a single source of truth for the cap table, including SAFEs and option grants?\n- Do the top-10 customer contracts assign on change of control?\n- What happens to the business if you leave in six months?\n\n**Offer evaluation**\n- Headline price vs actual cash at close: escrow, earnout, holdback, rollover equity, seller note.\n- If stock: is it liquid, and would you buy it with cash today? (The handbook's test, unchanged.)\n- If earnout: who controls the metric, and what covenant binds the buyer? [57]\n- If licence-and-hire: what do remaining shareholders receive, and who signs the fairness opinion?\n  [19][20]\n\n**Employee ownership and wind-down**\n- Do I want continuity of culture more than maximum headline price? Can we afford an annual\n  independent valuation? US: does an ESOP fit S-corp treatment [41][42]? UK: EOT, and have I checked\n  the current Budget-year rules [43][44]?\n- Wind-down: cash runway to close properly; assets worth selling (IP, brand, domains, data);\n  personal guarantees, director liabilities and unpaid payroll taxes — the three that follow you."},{"ref":"modern/S12-exit-and-liquidity-2026.md","label":"Exit, liquidity and valuation (2026 layer)","heading":"Regional notes","text":"- **US.** HSR size-of-transaction $133.9M from 17 Feb 2026 [24][25]; the 2023 Merger Guidelines reach\n  serial acquisitions, labour markets and partial interests [26]. SBA 7(a) is the dominant SMB\n  acquisition financing and tightened twice (SOP 50 10 8 from Jun 2025, 50 10 8.1 from Oct 2026)\n  [38][39][40]. ESOPs are a mature, tax-advantaged exit at ~270 new plans a year [42].\n- **UK.** The DMCCA raised the merger turnover threshold from £70M to £100M with a £10M floor for one\n  party [27]; CMA jurisdiction and procedure guidance was reissued in October 2025 [28]. **Employee\n  Ownership Trusts are the fastest-growing owner exit**: 2,824 employee-owned businesses by March\n  2026, ~500 transitioning in 2025, ~548,000 employee owners [43]; check the Budget 2025 changes to\n  EOT relief before modelling the tax [44]. The listing route is the LSE Main Market or AIM, not the\n  SEC process in chapter 9.\n- **EU / Germany.** The Bundeskartellamt found Microsoft's hire-and-licence of Inflection staff was a\n  **reportable concentration** in substance, declining only for lack of local nexus [21] — a European\n  acquirer cannot assume the acquihire structure escapes merger control.\n- **India.** A genuine domestic IPO exit market: 18 startups listed in 2025 raising a record ₹41,248\n  crore, with 29 DRHPs already filed for 2026 [65]. Indian founders can plan a listing exit at a\n  scale the handbook reserves for US companies.\n- **Africa (Nigeria especially).** IPOs remain rare; M&A is the route and rose ~72% in 2025, but the\n  buyer mix localised — foreign acquirers fell from 56% of exits in 2020 to 33% in 2025 [66]. 2025\n  funding: Kenya $1.04B (+72%), South Africa $715M, Egypt $604M, Nigeria $572M (−3%) [67]. Plan for a\n  local or pan-African strategic buyer, longer holds, and secondaries as realistic liquidity.\n- **Southeast Asia.** The hardest exit market here: PE deal value fell ~10% to ~$14B across 84 deals\n  in 2025, Singapore recorded four exits and Vietnam zero, with exit difficulty cited as investors'\n  top concern [68]; the Golden Gate Ventures × INSEAD exit study is the regional reference [69].\n- **Everywhere.** Rule 144, lockups, ESOP qualification and the US broker associations named in\n  chapter 13 are US-only. The economic logic transfers; the rulebook does not."},{"ref":"modern/S12-exit-and-liquidity-2026.md","label":"Exit, liquidity and valuation (2026 layer)","heading":"AI-era notes","text":"- **AI is the exit market.** 40% of 2025 US VC exit value and a record 317 AI exits [4]; technology\n  led H1 2026 M&A with $649B of announced volume [8].\n- **AI multiples are a different asset class — and widely dispersed.** AI M&A averaged 24.5x\n  EV/Revenue against a 13.1x median, so a handful of strategic deals carry the average [49].\n  Foundation models ~37.5x, AI-native SaaS 25-30x, applications 8-20x [49][50] — yet advisors report\n  that in an actual sale defensible AI companies clear 8-12x revenue and narrative-only ones 3-5x\n  [51]. **Where hype outruns evidence:** the headline multiple is a public-comp and mega-deal\n  artefact; price your own company off closed deals in your band.\n- **The reverse acquihire is an AI-era invention and a founder trap.** Inflection (~$650M: $620M\n  licence + $33M for employee claims) [21], Character.AI ($2.7B) [19], Windsurf ($2.4B, July 2025)\n  [23] and Scale-Meta set the pattern: the team and the model leave, the company is not bought, and\n  investors and non-joining employees can be left holding a shell.\n- **Regulators are closing the gap.** The FTC chair said the agency will examine whether acquihire\n  structures trigger HSR and the DOJ Antitrust Division's acting head called them a \"red flag\" for\n  merger-review avoidance [20]; the FTC opened an investigation into Microsoft-Inflection [22];\n  Germany's FCO held the structure was a concentration in substance [21].\n- **AI roll-ups are a new buyer for unglamorous businesses.** General Catalyst's $1.5B allocation, its\n  70-category map and 10 automatable categories; Titan ($74M, Aug 2025, acquired MSP RFA) and Long\n  Lake (~$670M raised, $100M EBITDA in under two years) [61]; >$3B committed across GC, Thrive,\n  Khosla, Elad Gil, Bessemer and GV [62]. A services firm on 5-15% margins now has a bidder class\n  that did not exist in 2018 — and a competitor class too.\n- **AI in the deal process** speeds diligence (document review, QoE prep, data-room Q&A) but does not\n  change what diligence finds; BizBuySell's own 2026 framing is stabilisation plus \"stricter\n  underwriting, deeper financial scrutiny\" [30][33]. Clean books still beat an AI story."},{"ref":"modern/S12-exit-and-liquidity-2026.md","label":"Exit, liquidity and valuation (2026 layer)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- **Quoting a public index multiple as your own.** The gap between an equal-weighted private median\n  and a market-cap-weighted public index is not negotiating room [47][48].\n- **Anchoring on AI headline multiples.** A 24.5x mean built from a few strategic deals will not be\n  offered to a $3M-ARR application company [49][51].\n- **Treating \"we'll license your model and hire your team\" as flattering.** It is an exit for the\n  founders, a shell for everyone else, and live regulatory risk [19][20][21].\n- **Assuming a sub-threshold deal is invisible.** Below $133.9M there is no HSR filing but there is\n  still enforcement interest, especially for serial acquirers [24][26][29].\n- **Accepting an earnout without an operating covenant** — only 14% of deals require the buyer to run\n  the business as before [57]; the metric you negotiated is the buyer's to move.\n- **Waiting for the \"right\" market.** Median time to tech IPO is 11.5 years [5]; the option you hold\n  for free is expensive in founder years and employee illiquidity.\n- **Shutting down slowly.** The 2025-26 cohort dies later with more capital and more obligations;\n  every month of drift adds liabilities and destroys salvageable asset value [45]."},{"ref":"modern/S12-exit-and-liquidity-2026.md","label":"Exit, liquidity and valuation (2026 layer)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`tender offer` — a company-sanctioned window in which holders may sell a capped share of their equity at a set price.\n`continuation vehicle (CV)` — a new fund, managed by the same GP, that buys assets from an older fund so LPs can take cash or roll.\n`reverse acquihire` — hiring a startup's founding team plus a non-exclusive technology licence, instead of acquiring the company.\n`SDE (seller's discretionary earnings)` — small-business earnings with owner compensation and discretionary items added back; the base for Main Street multiples.\n`quality of earnings (QoE)` — an independent review testing whether reported earnings are real, recurring and transferable.\n`representations and warranties insurance (RWI)` — a policy backstopping breaches of a seller's reps, replacing escrow and indemnity.\n`earnout` — deferred purchase price contingent on post-close performance.\n`seller note / standby` — vendor financing paid over time; \"standby\" means no principal or interest is paid, which is what makes it count as SBA equity injection.\n`HSR filing` — the US pre-merger notification required above the annually adjusted size-of-transaction threshold.\n`409A valuation` — an independent appraisal of common stock fixing option strike prices under US tax rules; `DLOM` is its discount for lack of marketability.\n`ETA / search fund` — entrepreneurship through acquisition: raising capital to find, buy and personally run one company.\n`EOT (Employee Ownership Trust)` — UK structure in which a trust buys a controlling stake on behalf of all employees.\n`local maximum` — a temporarily favourable moment for your own metrics and your category, as distinct from the market peak."},{"ref":"modern/S13-founder-evidence-and-pitching-2026.md","label":"Founder evidence and pitching (2026 layer)","heading":"What changed since 2018","text":"- **The founder-age myth was settled by administrative data, then mis-summarised.** Azoulay, Jones,\n  Kim & Miranda (AER: Insights, 2020) put the mean age at founding of the 1-in-1,000 fastest-growing\n  US new ventures at **45.0**, with similar results in high-tech sectors, entrepreneurial hubs and\n  successful exits [1][2]. It is a *tail* statistic about US employer firms, not a prescription.\n- **Solo founding went mainstream.** On Carta, solo-founded new incorporations rose from 23.7% (2019)\n  to 36.3% (H1 2025) [3][4][5] — but solo founders took only 14.7% of cash raised in priced rounds\n  [3][4]. The norm moved; the capital did not follow at the same rate.\n- **The seed bar rose to what Series A used to be.** CRV (2026) targets **$500K-$1.5M ARR** for a\n  strong B2B SaaS seed [20]; median seed on Carta is ~$4M at ~$20M post, and median seed→Series A\n  time stretched to **2.1 years** from 1.5 in 2019 [6].\n- **Failure got measured, not just anecdotal.** Carta counted **966** customer shutdowns in 2024 vs\n  769 in 2023 [27]; from Q1 2024, *funded* companies began shutting down more often than unfunded\n  ones [26]. BLS puts 5-year establishment survival in the low-to-mid 50s across cohorts [28][29][30].\n- **The 40-page plan lost its investor audience; the *reading* artefacts multiplied.** Decks, memos,\n  one-pagers and data rooms now tell one story at four depths [31][45][47] — while banks, SBA lenders\n  and grant bodies still want the long document (see Regional notes).\n- **Deck reading is now instrumented.** Papermark's 2026 report covers **24,541 decks / 358,672\n  investor views / 15.2M page-level data points**: median **18 minutes** total attention per deck,\n  **4.0 min** per view, 16% of views over within 10 seconds, fewer than 50% reach the last slide [31].\n- **Acquisition entrepreneurship stopped being exotic.** Tracked US/Canada search funds grew from\n  ~20 in the mid-1990s to **800+** by 2026, and SBA 7(a) acquisition lending hit **$8.29B in FY2025**,\n  up ~35% YoY across ~7,000 deals [23][24].\n- **AI made the deck cheap and the *insight* expensive.** Generic AI-drafted decks are now\n  pattern-recognised by VCs, some of whom pre-screen with AI themselves [48][49][60]."},{"ref":"modern/S13-founder-evidence-and-pitching-2026.md","label":"Founder evidence and pitching (2026 layer)","heading":"Core ideas (current consensus)","text":"- **Experience, not youth, is the measurable edge.** The Azoulay et al. result is specifically that\n  *prior experience in the specific industry* predicts much greater rates of entrepreneurial success,\n  and that youth-centric hypotheses are \"strongly rejected\" [1]. Read it as an argument for\n  founder-market fit, not as \"wait until you are 45\".\n- **Founder-market fit is the earliest legible signal.** Before revenue exists, investors substitute\n  the founder's lived exposure to the problem, domain network and credibility with buyers [54][61];\n  CRV states it as \"authentic problem insight over market trends\" and \"coachability over complete\n  answers\" [20].\n- **There is no single founder personality.** McCarthy et al. (2023, 21,000+ startups) find founders\n  differ from the general population on Big Five facets (openness to adventure, activity level, lower\n  modesty) but cluster into **six** types, not one; personality-*diverse* teams do better, and the\n  \"Hipster, Hacker, Hustler\" combination was about **twice as likely to succeed** [17][18] (cf. PNAS\n  2023 on founder personality and outcomes [19]). This updates the handbook's single trait checklist.\n- **Co-founding is a portfolio decision, not a virtue.** Carta: two-founder teams are the modal\n  venture-funded shape, yet ~1 in 4 co-founder teams sees a departure by year four [6]; median\n  ownership at exit was **75% greater** for solo founders — which is mostly arithmetic (no split),\n  not proof of better outcomes [3][4].\n- **Repeat founders are advantaged but not linearly.** Gompers, Kovner, Lerner & Scharfstein (JFE,\n  2010) measured success as taking a VC-backed company public: **30%** for previously successful\n  founders vs **18%** for first-timers; returns to further repetition decline [8].\n- **Most \"founder myths\" are base-rate errors.** Tamaseb's *Super Founders* (2021) compared ~200\n  billion-dollar companies with a random control of startups that had raised ≥$3M and found no\n  penalty for solo founding or a non-technical CEO, that most had strong incumbent competitors, and\n  that <15% went through an accelerator [9]. It is descriptive, not causal.\n- **Being a founder is an occupational-health risk.** 44% high stress, 37% anxiety, 36% burnout, 13%\n  depression, 10% panic attacks; only 10% talk to investors about it [15]. Brad Feld's 2025 work\n  extends the finding to teams [16].\n- **The plan is dead; the *thinking* is not.** No serious early-stage investor reads 40 pages, but\n  the same five questions — opportunity, solution, market, model, team — survive in Sequoia's ten\n  prompts [38] and YC's demo-day skeleton [39]. The handbook's core elements list is still correct;\n  its *format* is not.\n- **Clarity beats sophistication.** Kevin Hale (YC): make the idea *legible* — understandable by\n  someone who knows nothing about the business — because a legible idea is what gets retold [40];\n  YC works a company's two-sentence answer to \"what do you do?\" every week of the batch [41]."},{"ref":"modern/S13-founder-evidence-and-pitching-2026.md","label":"Founder evidence and pitching (2026 layer)","heading":"Frameworks & playbooks","text":"| Framework | Author / year | What it says | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Age & high-growth entrepreneurship | Azoulay, Jones, Kim & Miranda — *AER: Insights* 2(1), 2020 [1][2] | Mean founding age 45.0 for the top 0.1% fastest-growing US ventures; prior same-industry experience is the strong predictor; youth hypotheses rejected | Countering \"too old to start\"; arguing for domain choice |\n| Founder-market fit | VC practice, formalised 2022-2026 [54][61][20] | Domain exposure + network + authentic obsession as a pre-traction predictor; \"have you lived this problem?\" | Idea selection; pre-seed narrative; a fit score in onboarding |\n| Six founder types (FOALED) + team personality diversity | McCarthy, Gong, Stephany, Braesemann, Rizoiu & Kern, 2023 [17][18] | Fighters, Operators, Accomplishers, Leaders, Engineers, Developers; diverse combinations (e.g. Hipster/Hacker/Hustler) roughly 2x success odds | Co-founder search; replacing a single trait checklist |\n| Super Founders base rates | Ali Tamaseb, 2021 [9] | 30,000 data points across unicorns vs a ≥$3M-raised control; debunks solo-founder, first-mover, accelerator and industry-experience \"requirements\" | Sanity-checking founder folklore before it becomes a rule |\n| Founder Mode vs Manager Mode | Paul Graham, Sept 2024, after a Brian Chesky talk [50][51] | Scaling need not mean delegating into a management layer; founders can stay hands-on with skip-level detail | Leadership transition at 30-150 people; contested — see Pitfalls |\n| Earnestness / \"ambitious misfits\" | Garry Tan, YC, 2024-2026 [52][53] | YC screens for earnestness, shipping velocity and being other-focused (\"make something people want\") over résumé polish | Accelerator applications; founder self-assessment |\n| Entrepreneurship through acquisition (ETA) | Ruback & Yudkoff, *HBR Guide to Buying a Small Business* (2017; updated ed. 2024) [25] | Buy a dull, profitable, enduringly-demanded small business rather than start one; self-funded search or search fund; seller-friendly deal mechanics | User has operating skill + capital access but no novel idea |\n| Lean Canvas (one-page plan) | Ash Maurya, adapted from Osterwalder's BMC [59] | Nine boxes centred on problem, solution, key metrics and unfair advantage; designed to be tested, not defended | Replacing the handbook's chapter-5 long-form first draft |\n| Sequoia business-plan prompts | Sequoia Capital, page updated Sept 2026 [38] | Company purpose (one declarative sentence) → problem → solution → why now → market potential → competition → business model → team → financials → **vision** (the popular template swaps Vision for Product) | The default deck skeleton for a growth-equity-style story |\n| YC demo-day structure | Geoff Ralston / YC [39] | Clarity, excitement, informativeness, memorability; pick 3-4 \"vertebrae\"; ≤7 words per slide; bottoms-up market sizing; practise until smooth | 2-3 minute live pitch; demo day; investor speed meetings |\n| Legibility pitch | Kevin Hale, YC Startup Library [40] | Problem + solution + unique insight, stated so a non-expert can repeat it; \"why\" and \"how\" often obstruct clarity | Writing the one-liner and the first two slides |\n| Strategic narrative | Andy Raskin, 2016-2026 [42][43] | Name the big change → name the enemy (an old mindset) → promised land → \"magic gifts\" (capabilities) → evidence you can deliver | Category creation; a deck that must change the reader's frame |\n| Deck / memo / one-pager / data room stack | 2025-26 practice [31][45][47] | Deck for screening, 2-4 page memo for asynchronous depth, one-pager for intros, data room for diligence — same story at four depths | Any raise; the app should generate all four from one source |"},{"ref":"modern/S13-founder-evidence-and-pitching-2026.md","label":"Founder evidence and pitching (2026 layer)","heading":"Benchmarks & numbers (2024-2026)","text":"| Metric | Value | Segment / context | Source | Year |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mean founding age, top 0.1% fastest-growing ventures | **45.0** | US administrative firm/worker/owner data | [1][2] | 2020 (pub.) |\n| Solo-founded share of new incorporations | 23.7% → 36.3% (~36% of the 2025 cohort) | Carta platform, 2019 → H1 2025 | [3][4][5] | 2026 |\n| Solo founders' share of priced-round cash | 14.7% | Carta, 2024-25 rounds | [3][4] | 2025 |\n| Median ownership at exit, solo vs lead founder | **75% greater** for solo | Carta 2019-H1 2025 (arithmetic of not splitting) | [3][4] | 2025 |\n| Founder ownership by round (median) | 56.2% post-seed → 36.1% post-A → 23.0% post-B | Carta | [5][6] | 2026 |\n| Co-founder departures | ~1 in 4 teams by year 4 | Carta | [6] | 2025 |\n| Median seed round / post-money | ~$4M / ~$20M | Carta | [6] | 2025 |\n| Seed → Series A graduation | ~50% by Q16 (4 yrs); median gap **2.1 yrs** (1.5 in 2019) | Carta, 2015-2024 incorporations | [6] | 2025 |\n| Seed ARR expectation (B2B SaaS) | $500K-$1.5M for a strong round | CRV | [20] | 2026 |\n| Repeat-founder IPO success | 30% vs 18% first-timers | VC-backed US founders (JFE 2010, classic) | [8] | 2010 |\n| YC repeat founders | 454 repeat founders, 935 founder-company records, 2005-2026; peak 65 in 2025; mean 5.1-yr gap | Y Combinator via Crunchbase | [7] | 2026 |\n| Startup shutdowns (Carta customers) | 966 (2024) vs 769 (2023), +25.6%; enterprise SaaS 32% of them | US Carta companies exiting via bankruptcy/dissolution | [27] | 2025 |\n| Funded vs unfunded shutdowns | 136 of 254 Q1-2024 shutdowns had raised a priced round — first such quarter in five | Carta | [26] | 2024 |\n| US establishment survival, 5 years | 49.8-57.3% depending on cohort (2006 low, 2018 high) | BLS Business Employment Dynamics, *establishments* not firms | [28] | 2024 |\n| US establishment survival, 1 year | ~77.9% (i.e. 22.1% close within a year) | BLS BED, year to March 2025 | [29][30] | 2025 |\n| Search funds tracked (US/Canada) | ~20 (mid-1990s) → 800+ | search-fund census | [23][24] | 2026 |\n| Search-fund aggregate return | 35.1% IRR / 4.5x ROI across 681 funds to 31 Dec 2023; exited companies 42.9% IRR | Stanford GSB 2024 study — dollar-weighted, tail-driven; ~26% of acquisitions show a loss | [21][22][23] | 2024 |\n| SBA 7(a) acquisition lending | $8.29B in FY2025, +~35% YoY, ~7,000 deals | US SBA via ETA trackers | [24] | 2026 |\n| Founder mental health | 72% report an impact; 44% high stress, 37% anxiety, 36% burnout, 13% depression, 10% panic attacks; 81% not open about it, 77% never access therapy, 10% tell investors | Startup Snapshot, 400+ founders | [15] | 2024 |\n| Deck attention (largest dataset) | median **18 min** total; **4.0 min** per view; 11 investors per deck; 16% of views <10 s; <50% reach the last slide (biggest drop slide 1 → 2); median 59 days first→last view | Papermark: 24,541 decks, 358,672 views, 184 countries | [31][33] | 2026 |\n| Optimal deck length | 9-16 pages (46% of founders); 12 slides gets most views (34 avg, 68% return rate); completion falls to ~40% at 16-18 pages | same | [31] | 2026 |\n| Slide-level | Team slide most-read (5.7 s/view, in 71% of decks); Traction +20% views if in the first three slides; Financials above-median attention but in only 40% of decks | same | [31] | 2026 |\n| Closed vs unclosed rounds | decks that closed got **37%** more time per page in the back half | same | [31] | 2026 |\n| Seed deck review time | ~3 min 44 s (seed); ~4 min 10 s (pre-seed); ~58% viewed to completion; 20 slides in successful seed decks | DocSend Startup Index | [34][36] | 2024-25 |\n| Team-slide attention shift | +40% (seed) / +30% (pre-seed) vs 2023; Competition -48%, pre-seed Market Size -19% | Dropbox DocSend, 400+ startups | [35] | 2024 |\n| Female-founded share of US VC deal value | record **$73.6B / 27.7%**; ~25% of US exit count | PitchBook *All In* (includes AI megadeals: >$30B from two companies) | [10] | 2026 |\n| All-female teams, Europe | 1% of deal value (from 1.3%); mixed teams 16.3% | PitchBook | [10] | 2026 |\n| Black-founded share of US VC | ~$942M = **0.32%** of $290B (1.4% in 2021, 1.1% in 2022) | Crunchbase; cf. DocSend funding-divide [37] | [11] | 2026 |\n| UK: at-least-one-female-founder share | 32% of VC from Investing in Women Code signatories (27% in 2024) vs **15%** of the wider market; all-female 6% vs 2% | British Business Bank | [14] | 2026 |\n| Africa: female-founded share | all-female 0.9% of $3.2B ($28.8M); female-CEO-led 2.2%; mixed teams 8% | Africa: The Big Deal | [12][13] | 2026 |\n| India | ~$10.5-11B total, deals -39% to 1,518; seed -30% to $1.1B; first-time-funded companies 459 (from 678) | Tracxn / TechCrunch | [55][56] | 2026 |\n| Southeast Asia | $5.4B across 461 deals; seed $110M in 9M-2025 (-72% YoY); Singapore 91% of capital | DealStreetAsia | [57][58] | 2026 |\n| Investor-update effect | \"40% higher follow-on odds\" / \"2.3x faster follow-on\" circulate widely | **Vendor/secondary sources; no primary study located — unverified** | [44] | 2025 |"},{"ref":"modern/S13-founder-evidence-and-pitching-2026.md","label":"Founder evidence and pitching (2026 layer)","heading":"Decision rules & rules of thumb","text":"- **If you have 5+ years in the industry you are entering, weight that above age, pedigree or\n  passion** — it is the variable the strongest founder-age evidence actually isolates [1].\n- **If you are over 40, delete \"too old\" from the analysis.** The strongest US tail-growth evidence\n  puts the mean at 45.0 [1]. If you are under 25, the same evidence says your edge must come from\n  somewhere other than age.\n- **If you cannot name three people who will take your call in this industry, you do not yet have\n  founder-market fit** — fix that before the deck [54][61][20].\n- **If you are choosing a co-founder, optimise for personality and skill complementarity, not\n  friendship** — diverse-type teams outperform, and ~1 in 4 co-founder pairs breaks by year four\n  [17][18][6]. Vest everything, 4 years / 1-year cliff, before you write a line of code.\n- **If you go solo, expect the funding penalty, not a performance penalty.** 36% of new companies but\n  14.7% of priced-round cash [3][4]; plan a bootstrap or angel path and a stronger traction story at\n  the same stage.\n- **If you need capital to hire a technical co-founder, you are not seed-ready** — CRV calls it an\n  immediate rejection [20].\n- **If your B2B SaaS has under ~$300-500K ARR, treat \"seed\" as pre-seed** and price/plan accordingly\n  [20]; median seed→A is now 2.1 years, so raise 30 months of runway, not 18 [6].\n- **If you are building a deck, target 9-16 pages and put Traction in the first three slides**\n  (+20% views) [31]. Below 8 or above 16 you lose completion.\n- **If the first slide does not land, nothing else will.** The biggest drop-off is slide 1 → 2 and\n  16% of views end within 10 seconds [31]; open with what you do in one legible sentence [40][41].\n- **If you are pre-seed/seed, keep the data room to roughly five documents** (deck, model, cap table,\n  founders'/IP agreements, summary) and stage deeper access [45][47].\n- **If any past contributor has not signed an IP assignment, fix it before you open a data room** —\n  it is the most commonly flagged seed diligence defect [47].\n- **If you are raising, send a monthly update whether or not anyone replies**, including to\n  prospective investors: the causal evidence is weak but the cost is an hour a month [44].\n- **If you have operating skill, capital access and no novel idea, price the ETA path against the\n  startup path** — but read search-fund returns as dollar-weighted and tail-driven, with ~26% of\n  acquisitions showing a loss [21][22][23].\n- **If a bank, SBA lender, grant body or visa route is in your funding mix, write the long plan\n  anyway** — the handbook's chapter-5 format is what those readers expect (Regional notes).\n- **If you show signs from the Startup Snapshot list (chronic anxiety, burnout, panic), treat it as\n  an operating risk with a plan**, not a character flaw: 77% of founders never see a therapist [15][16]."},{"ref":"modern/S13-founder-evidence-and-pitching-2026.md","label":"Founder evidence and pitching (2026 layer)","heading":"Process / steps","text":"1. **Founder-market fit audit (before the idea).** Write, in one page: the problem you have lived,\n   how long you were exposed to it, who you can call in that market tomorrow, what you know that\n   outsiders do not, and what you would still be working on in seven years [54][61][20].\n2. **Base-rate check.** Test each assumption about \"what founders must have\" against published base\n   rates (age [1], solo founding [3][4][9], accelerators [9], competitors [9]). Delete the folklore.\n3. **Team shape decision.** Solo, pair or trio; map complementary types and the capability gap list;\n   if co-founding, sign vesting, roles and IP assignment on day one [17][18][6][47].\n4. **Choose the entry path.** Start vs buy: capital required, time to cash, personal risk appetite\n   and skill fit (ETA vs startup) [21][24][25].\n5. **Write the one-liner.** Company name + what you do, in language a non-expert can repeat. Iterate\n   it weekly; it is the linchpin of everything downstream [40][41].\n6. **Build the narrative spine.** Big change in the world → old mindset (the enemy) → promised land →\n   your capabilities → proof [42][43].\n7. **Draft the Lean Canvas**, not the 40-page plan: problem, customer segments, UVP, solution,\n   channels, revenue, costs, key metrics, unfair advantage [59].\n8. **Convert the canvas into a 9-16 page deck** on the Sequoia/YC skeleton: purpose → problem →\n   solution → why now → market (bottoms-up) → traction (early!) → product → model → competition →\n   team → ask [38][39][31].\n9. **Write the 2-4 page memo** — the same story with the reasoning and numbers a deck cannot carry,\n   for asynchronous readers and partner meetings [31].\n10. **Instrument distribution.** Send tracked links (not attachments), watch time-per-page and\n    completion, and re-cut the slides where readers stall [31][32][33].\n11. **Stage the data room.** Level 1 on interest, Level 2 on term-sheet track: clean cap table, IP\n    assignments, contracts, model [45][46][47].\n12. **Run the monthly update from day one** — metrics, wins, lowlights, asks — to existing and\n    prospective investors [44]; and put a founder-health routine (peer group, named professional,\n    recovery block, co-founder check-in) in the operating plan [15][16]."},{"ref":"modern/S13-founder-evidence-and-pitching-2026.md","label":"Founder evidence and pitching (2026 layer)","heading":"Worksheets, checklists & questions","text":"**Founder-market fit score (0-5 each, replaces the trait quiz as the primary screen):**\n- Years of direct exposure to this problem or industry.\n- Number of potential customers who would take your call this week.\n- A non-obvious insight you hold that most people in the market do not.\n- Depth of your hiring network in this domain.\n- Would you still choose this problem if it took ten years?\n- Do you have a distribution advantage (audience, channel, relationships)?\n\n**Team-shape checklist:** complementary types identified? vesting + cliff signed? IP assignments from\nevery contributor? roles and final-decision rights written down? a documented way to break a tie?\nco-founder \"pre-nup\" for departure? [6][17][47]\n\n**Founder wellbeing check (monthly, private):** stress level 1-10; sleep; one thing you are hiding\nfrom your team or investors; who you last spoke to honestly; when you last took two consecutive days\noff; is there a professional you could call today? [15][16]\n\n**Deck QA (run before every send):**\n- Is the one-liner on slide 1, readable in five seconds? [40][41]\n- Is traction in the first three slides, and the deck 9-16 pages? [31]\n- ≤7 words on any slide that will be presented live; bottoms-up market sizing? [39]\n- Team slide present (71% include it; it is the most-read slide)? [31][35]\n- Financials included (only 40% do, and they are read above median)? [31]\n- Does every slide carry a specific fact an AI could not have produced? [48][60]\n\n**Diligence-readiness checklist (Level 1 / Level 2):** deck; 2-4 page memo; financial model; cap\ntable; incorporation docs; founders' agreement and vesting; IP assignments (every contributor);\ncustomer contracts; key metrics export; org chart; regulatory approvals [45][46][47].\n\n**Monthly investor update template:** headline metric + delta; 3 wins; 3 lowlights; cash and runway;\nhiring; 2 specific asks; one-line ask-for-intros list [44]."},{"ref":"modern/S13-founder-evidence-and-pitching-2026.md","label":"Founder evidence and pitching (2026 layer)","heading":"Regional notes","text":"- **US.** Deepest seed market and the source of nearly all the founder data above — remember the\n  Carta/DocSend/Papermark samples are venture-track and US-heavy [3][6][31]. SBA 7(a) makes the\n  buy-a-business path uniquely financeable: $8.29B of acquisition lending in FY2025 [24]. Black\n  founders received 0.32% of US VC in 2025 [11]; female-founded companies a record 27.7% of deal\n  value, flattered by two AI megadeals [10].\n- **UK.** The long-form plan survives for bank lending, Innovate UK grants and Innovator Founder\n  visas. Investing in Women Code signatories put 32% of VC into teams with at least one female\n  founder vs 15% in the wider market; all-female teams 6% vs 2% [14]. SEIS/EIS shape deck and\n  cap-table structure from the first round (see `S06-financing-2026.md`).\n- **EU.** Grant and public co-investment programmes (EIC, national agencies) still require full\n  written plans, work packages and budgets — the handbook's chapter-5 format is *live* here, not\n  obsolete. All-female European teams took 1% of deal value in 2026-to-date, down from 1.3% [10].\n- **Africa (Nigeria especially).** Total 2025 funding was ~$3.2B; all-female-founded teams got 0.9%\n  ($28.8M), female-CEO-led 2.2%, mixed teams 8% [12][13]. Assume a longer raise, build a\n  revenue-first plan, and expect diligence to focus on governance, FX exposure and customer\n  concentration. Founder mental-health support networks are thin and under-reported [62].\n- **India.** ~$10.5-11B in 2025 with deals down 39% and seed down 30%; first-time-funded companies\n  fell from 678 to 459, while \"operator-founders\" (ex-senior operators at scaled startups) attracted\n  ~11% of total funding despite being <1% of companies founded 2023-25 [55][56]. Founder pedigree is\n  doing unusually heavy lifting in this market.\n- **Southeast Asia.** $5.4B across 461 deals in 2025; seed collapsed to $110M in the first nine\n  months (-72% YoY) and Singapore captured 91% of regional capital [57][58]. Plan for a Singapore\n  holding structure and a longer pre-seed bridge.\n- **Middle East / LatAm.** Sovereign and corporate-backed funds dominate, pushing founders back\n  toward formal written plans and board-grade models; no 2024-26 primary dataset verified here —\n  treat regional benchmarks as unestablished."},{"ref":"modern/S13-founder-evidence-and-pitching-2026.md","label":"Founder evidence and pitching (2026 layer)","heading":"AI-era notes","text":"- **The deck is now free to produce, so it no longer signals effort.** VCs report pattern-recognising\n  AI-generated decks on sight — identical visual language, boilerplate competition slides, templated\n  market narratives — and firms including EQT and Moonfire are pre-screening decks with AI before a\n  partner opens them [48][49]. The scarce input is a specific, earned fact per slide.\n- **Practical rule:** use AI for the first draft, the structure and the tightening; supply it with\n  your own raw material (call transcripts, usage data, customer quotes) and hand-write every line\n  that contains an insight or a number [48][60].\n- **Investor attention is measured, so optimise against measurement, not taste.** Tracked-link\n  analytics (Papermark, DocSend) tell you exactly where readers stop; re-cut those slides [31][32].\n  Do not confuse an investor's AI summariser reading your deck with an investor reading it.\n- **\"Team\" is rising in attention precisely because AI made everything else easy to fake** — the\n  2024 DocSend shift (+40% seed team-slide time, -48% competition) is consistent with investors\n  re-weighting toward the least-copyable signal [35].\n- **Solo founding and \"tiny teams\" are the AI-era story**, and the Carta trend line is real (23.7% →\n  36.3% solo) [3][4]. The viral revenue-per-employee anecdotes circulating in 2026 are *not* from\n  audited datasets; treat one-person-unicorn claims as marketing until a primary source exists.\n- **Founder-fit self-assessment is a good AI use case; diagnosis is not.** An AI advisor can score\n  founder-market fit and run a base-rate check; it must not be positioned as mental-health care —\n  route to a human professional, since 77% of founders never reach one [15][16].\n- **Where the hype outruns the evidence:** AI plan/deck generators claim fundraising uplift with no\n  controlled evidence; the only well-powered public datasets measure *reading behaviour*, not\n  conversion [31][32]. Nothing in the 2024-26 literature shows an AI-written narrative beating a\n  founder-written one."},{"ref":"modern/S13-founder-evidence-and-pitching-2026.md","label":"Founder evidence and pitching (2026 layer)","heading":"Pitfalls","text":"- **Quoting \"45\" as a target age.** It is a mean for the top 0.1% of US growth firms, not an optimum,\n  a median, or a claim about the odds of any individual founder [1].\n- **Reading Carta as \"all startups\".** Carta measures venture-track US companies on one cap-table\n  platform; shutdown and ownership figures are not economy-wide [3][6][26].\n- **Confusing solo founders' higher exit ownership with better outcomes.** Not splitting equity\n  mechanically raises your share; it says nothing about the size of the exit [3][4].\n- **Treating repeat-founder data as personal destiny.** The 30% vs 18% finding was measured on\n  *previously successful* VC-backed founders taking companies public, in a 2010 paper [8].\n- **Building the 40-page plan for a VC.** No dataset in 2024-26 shows an investor reading one; the\n  measured median attention is 18 minutes across an entire deck [31]. Write it only for lenders,\n  grant bodies and your own budget.\n- **Shipping an AI-default deck.** Generic phrasing now reads as an absence of insight, not polish\n  [48][49][60].\n- **Taking \"founder mode\" as licence to micromanage.** Graham's essay is an observation about a\n  poorly-studied way of running a company, contested since publication; it is not a management method\n  with evidence behind it [50][51].\n- **Treating the investor-update multipliers as established fact.** The widely-quoted \"40% higher\n  follow-on\" and \"2.3x faster\" figures come from vendor content, not a primary study [44].\n- **Hiding founder distress.** 81% of founders are not open about it and only 10% tell investors —\n  which is exactly why it shows up as a company-level failure rather than a personal one [15]."},{"ref":"modern/S13-founder-evidence-and-pitching-2026.md","label":"Founder evidence and pitching (2026 layer)","heading":"Key terms","text":"`founder-market fit` — the alignment of a founder's lived exposure, network and obsession with the\nmarket they are entering; used as a pre-traction investment screen.\n`FOALED types` — the six founder personality clusters (Fighters, Operators, Accomplishers, Leaders,\nEngineers, Developers) identified by McCarthy et al. (2023).\n`ETA (entrepreneurship through acquisition)` — becoming an owner-operator by buying an existing small\nbusiness rather than starting one.\n`search fund` — a vehicle raising \"search capital\" for an entrepreneur to find and acquire one\ncompany, then operate and exit it.\n`investment memo` — a 2-4 page prose document carrying the reasoning, numbers and risks a deck cannot.\n`staged data room` — a diligence room with access tiers released as an investor's interest deepens.\n`strategic narrative` — Raskin's structure: name the change, the enemy (an old mindset), the promised\nland, your capabilities, and the evidence."}];

export const CORPUS_BUILT = "2026-09-14";
